= Signaling feedback
{parent=Signal transduction}

<Signaling feedback> returns the effect of a downstream event to an earlier stage of <signal transduction>, closing a loop analogous to <feedback control>. Trace the causal arrows: if an earlier relay activates a later relay and the later relay changes the earlier relay's activity, the return path closes the loop. <Negative feedback> opposes the initiating change; <positive feedback> reinforces it. <Signaling feedback> is a possible property of <signal transduction>, not a requirement of every pathway.

For a schematic example, let relay $A$ activate relay $B$. If $B$ inhibits $A$, increased $A$ eventually produces an opposing influence: <negative feedback>. If $B$ activates $A$, increased $A$ produces a reinforcing influence: <positive feedback>. These signs describe effects on activity, rather than the direction in which <molecules> travel. A closed loop containing two inhibitions can have <positive feedback>, because inhibiting an inhibitor reinforces the original change. The sign belongs to the whole loop.

\Image[signaling-feedback-loops.png]
{title=<Negative feedback>, <positive feedback> and an <incoherent feedforward loop>}
{description=Original causal schematics. Arrows activate; flat-ended lines inhibit. The upper loops return from downstream relay $B$ to earlier relay $A$. In the lower <incoherent feedforward loop>, input $s$ drives both a fast activating branch $x$ and a slower inhibitory branch $a$; their contributions meet at readout $r$. There is no return arrow from $r$ to either branch or to $s$. Shapes and connections illustrate the distinction, rather than a measured pathway.}

<Negative feedback> can restrain excessive activation and help preserve a usable range of responses. <Positive feedback> can support a decisive transition: Alberts and colleagues describe an <enzyme> whose product increases its own activity. Such reinforcement can contribute to switchlike behavior, but a <positive feedback> arrow alone does not prove <bistability>. A different structure, an <incoherent feedforward loop>, sends opposing influences forward from the same input; inhibition there is not a downstream return. <Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/General Principles of Cell Communication>{ref} https://cqb.pku.edu.cn/tanglab/pdf/78.pdf[Ma et al., Defining Network Topologies that Can Achieve Biochemical Adaptation, Cell 138 (2009), pp. 760–773: definitions on p. 764]{ref}

A worked <mathematical model> shows why <negative feedback> does not automatically produce complete <signaling adaptation>. Let $s$ be a fixed normalized input, $r$ a normalized response, $g\geq0$ a dimensionless <feedback gain>, and $\tau>0$ a <time constant>. Suppose response removal contributes $-r$ and a return proportional to the response contributes $-gr$. The <ordinary differential equation> is
$$
\tau\frac{dr}{dt}=s-r-gr=s-(1+g)r.
$$
Setting the <derivative> to zero gives the <equilibrium point> $r_*=s/(1+g)$. A displacement $\delta r=r-r_*$ obeys $\tau\,d(\delta r)/dt=-(1+g)\delta r$, so its decaying <exponential function> establishes <asymptotic stability>. For a unit input switched on at $t=0$, <initial condition> $r(0)=0$, $g=2$ and $\tau=1$ second,
$$
r(t)=\frac{1-e^{-3t/\tau}}{3},\qquad r(1\,\mathrm{s})\simeq0.316737,\qquad \lim_{t\to\infty}r(t)=\frac13.
$$
The <negative feedback> reduces the eventual response from $1$ to $1/3$, but this response rises monotonically and never returns to its zero baseline. Attenuation and complete <signaling adaptation> are different properties. This deliberately simple <mathematical model> specifies no molecular pathway; <integral action> provides a contrasting way to remove a lasting offset in a stable <feedback control> system. https://www.fbswiki.org/wiki/index.php/Feedback_Principles[Åström and Murray, Feedback Systems, Chapter 2: proportional and integral feedback]{ref}

= Negative feedback
{parent=Signaling feedback}

<Negative feedback> closes a causal loop whose net effect opposes an initiating change. In <signal transduction>, activation followed by a downstream inhibition of the earlier activating stage is one example of <negative feedback>. Loop sign alone does not establish <asymptotic stability> or complete <signaling adaptation>; see the worked <signaling feedback> example.

A useful biological example is <chemotaxis feedback regulation>: an <Escherichia coli> <biological cell> adjusts its response to a sustained attractant instead of remaining at its immediate response. Increased <CheA> activity promotes <CheB> <phosphorylation>; <CheB> reverses <receptor methylation>, opposing their activation. After attractant addition, reduced activity instead favors <receptor methylation> by <CheR>, restoring activity. This is a return from <receptor> activity to the machinery regulating that activity. The response need not return exactly at every dose. <Precision and Kinetics of Adaptation in Bacterial Chemotaxis by Yigal Meir and others>{ref}

A motivating measurement is the <chemotaxis FRET assay>. <CheY> and <CheZ> carrying yellow and cyan fluorescent tags were observed in immobilized <Escherichia coli> <biological cells> on a coverslip in a flow chamber. Adding and removing attractant changed the yellow-to-cyan emission <ratio>, reporting the association of phosphorylated <CheY> with <CheZ>. This measures a molecular readout upstream of motor behavior. Comparisons with <biological cells> lacking <CheR>, <CheB>, or both tested the dependence on <receptor> modification; these perturbations also change starting activity and sensitivity, so they are not pure changes of one <feedback gain>. <Receptor sensitivity in bacterial chemotaxis by Victor Sourjik and others>{ref}

The later <chemotaxis feedback regulation> experiment used only the <Tar receptor> and nonmetabolizable methylaspartate, supplied and removed by flow. Long recordings separated slow recovery from incomplete <signaling adaptation>; correction for bleaching addressed drift in the optical readout. Activity recovered incompletely after sufficiently large additions, with detectable imprecision already at $10\,\mathrm{\mu M}$ in that preparation. Restricting the <receptor> type and preventing consumption of the stimulus help interpret the perturbation, but limit transfer to other <receptors> and conditions. The readout infers <CheA> activity through <CheY>-<CheZ> association, rather than directly measuring every molecular step. <Precision and Kinetics of Adaptation in Bacterial Chemotaxis by Yigal Meir and others>{ref}

<Negative feedback> can also overshoot or sustain <oscillations> if its return arrives too late. The worked <signaling feedback> example gives the contrasting instantaneous case; the <delayed negative feedback model> below isolates the effect of timing. Loop sign describes causation, while <asymptotic stability> describes what happens to displacements over time.

\Image[negative-feedback-delay-modes.png]
{title=<Negative feedback> with an instantaneous or delayed return}
{description=Original synthetic <delay differential equation> modes, not measured <chemotaxis> responses. All variables and time are normalized. The same negative return coefficient is used throughout; only its delay changes. Each curve has its own matching history. A growing <oscillation> signals instability of this linear <mathematical model>, not a bounded biological rhythm.}

= Delayed negative feedback model
{parent=Negative feedback}
{title2=$d$}

A <feedback delay> is a time interval between an event and its returning influence. Multiple biochemical steps can introduce an effective lag; a fixed delay is an idealization, not a universal description of <signal transduction>. To isolate timing, let $x$ be a signed displacement from an <equilibrium point> and use normalized <dimensionless variables>:
$$
\frac{dx}{dt}=-x(t)-2x(t-d),\qquad d\geq0.
$$
The local removal is $-x$ and the returning influence is negative at every delay. At $d=0$ the <ordinary differential equation> reduces to the decay already illustrated under <signaling feedback>. For $d>0$ a <delay differential equation> requires a history on $[-d,0]$, not just an <initial condition> at one instant. Negative values of $x$ are displacements, not negative chemical <concentrations>.

An <exponential function> $x=e^{\lambda t}$ gives the <characteristic equation of a delay differential equation>
$$
\lambda+1+2e^{-\lambda d}=0.
$$
Putting $\lambda=i\omega$ and equating real and imaginary parts gives $\cos(\omega d)=-1/2$ and $\omega=2\sin(\omega d)$. The first crossing with $\omega>0$ therefore occurs at
$$
\omega=\sqrt3,\qquad d_c=\frac{2\pi}{3\sqrt3}\simeq1.20920.
$$
For this particular <mathematical model>, the <equilibrium point> is <asymptotically stable> for $0\leq d<d_c$. No nonnegative real <delayed feedback characteristic root> can satisfy the equation; the first loss of <asymptotic stability> is the indicated pair of imaginary <delayed feedback characteristic roots>. Differentiating the <characteristic equation of a delay differential equation> along a root yields
$$
\frac{d\lambda}{dd}=-\frac{\lambda(\lambda+1)}{1+d(\lambda+1)},\qquad \operatorname{Re}\left(\frac{d\lambda}{dd}\right)_{d=d_c}=\frac{3}{(1+d_c)^2+3d_c^2}>0.
$$
Thus the pair moves into growth as the delay increases through $d_c$. The neutral mode at $d_c$ is a <periodic function>, but its amplitude depends on history: this linear <mathematical model> does not select an attracting <limit cycle>.

The figure plots $x(t)=e^{at}\cos(bt)$ using a <delayed feedback characteristic root> $\lambda=a+ib$ and that same formula on the history interval. At $d=0.7$ the displayed mode decays; at $d=1.5$ it grows. These are exact modes up to numerical root accuracy, not step responses with identical histories. Molecular bounds and a changed return law would be needed to interpret what happens after an unstable biological response becomes large.

= Delayed feedback characteristic root
{parent=Delayed negative feedback model}
{title2=$\lambda$}

A <delayed feedback characteristic root> solves the <characteristic equation of a delay differential equation> and supplies an <exponential function> mode. Its <real part> determines decay or growth of that mode.

= Chemotaxis feedback regulation
{parent=Negative feedback}

In <Escherichia coli>, <chemotaxis feedback regulation> couples <receptor> activity to modification of the <receptors> by <CheR> and <CheB>. It is a molecular example of <negative feedback> supporting <signaling adaptation>, with precision and kinetics assessed by the <chemotaxis FRET assay> above.

= Receptor methylation
{parent=Chemotaxis feedback regulation}

= Tar receptor
{parent=Chemotaxis feedback regulation}
{c}

= Chemotaxis feedback proteins
{parent=Chemotaxis feedback regulation}

= CheA
{parent=Chemotaxis feedback proteins}
{c}

<CheA> is the signaling <enzyme> that promotes <phosphorylation> of <CheY> and <CheB> in the <chemotaxis feedback regulation> pathway.

= CheB
{parent=Chemotaxis feedback proteins}
{c}

<CheB> is an <enzyme> that reverses <receptor methylation> in <chemotaxis>; its regulation contributes to <negative feedback>.

= CheR
{parent=Chemotaxis feedback proteins}
{c}

<CheR> is an <enzyme> that catalyzes <receptor methylation> in <chemotaxis>, contributing to <signaling adaptation>.

= CheY
{parent=Chemotaxis feedback proteins}
{c}

The <phosphorylation> state of the <protein> <CheY> connects <receptor> signaling to motor behavior in <Escherichia coli>.

= CheZ
{parent=Chemotaxis feedback proteins}
{c}

<CheZ> is an <enzyme> that binds phosphorylated <CheY> and accelerates removal of its <phosphate>.

= Chemotaxis FRET assay
{parent=Chemotaxis feedback regulation}

The <chemotaxis FRET assay> uses <Förster resonance energy transfer> between fluorescent tags on <CheY> and <CheZ> to report their association. Interpreting this emission <ratio> as <CheA> activity requires the coupling between <CheY> <phosphorylation> and <CheZ> binding described in the experimental sources above.

= Positive feedback
{parent=Signaling feedback}

<Positive feedback> closes a causal loop whose net effect reinforces an initiating change. A loop with two inhibitory links can therefore have <positive feedback>. In <signal transduction>, a product that increases the activity of the <enzyme> making it illustrates reinforcement; the consequences depend on the full <mathematical model>, not just the loop sign. See <signaling feedback>.

A biological example is <cell-cycle activation feedback>. <Cyclin B> binds <Cdc2>, whose activity promotes activation of <Cdc25>; <Cdc25> removes inhibitory <phosphorylation> from <Cdc2>, permitting further activation. <Cdc2> also inhibits <Wee1>, an <enzyme> that inhibits <Cdc2> through <phosphorylation>. That second return contains two inhibitions and therefore also has <positive feedback>. These are regulatory paths, not a claim that every connection is a single direct molecular encounter. <Multisite phosphoregulation of Cdc25 activity refines the mitotic entrance and exit switches by Lucy X. Lu and others>{ref}

Reinforcement can preserve a decision after its initiating stimulus weakens: returning from the high state may require a lower input than entering it. The <cell-cycle hysteresis experiment> below tests this history dependence at matched <cyclin B> <concentrations>. A steep response alone is insufficient evidence of <bistability>, and finite observation times can make slow relaxation look like memory. A proposed switch must specify its input, readout and observation interval.

\Image[positive-feedback-switch-hysteresis.png]
{title=A synthetic <positive feedback> switch and its <hysteresis> thresholds}
{description=Original computed <equilibrium points> of a normalized saturating <mathematical model>, not <cyclin B> measurements or a fit to <Cdc2>. Filled intersections are <asymptotically stable>; the open intersection is unstable. With $\beta=1.8$, a slow sweep follows stable branches until their folds; with $\beta=1$, the same positive return has only one stable <equilibrium point>.}

= Saturating positive feedback
{parent=Positive feedback}

<Saturating positive feedback> limits the reinforcing rate as activity increases. Here is an explicitly synthetic <ordinary differential equation> for normalized nonnegative input $u$, response $x$, and return strength $\beta$:
$$
\frac{dx}{dt}=f(x;u,\beta)=u+\frac{\beta x^2}{1+x^2}-x,\qquad u\geq0,\quad\beta\geq0.
$$
The return is an increasing, nonproportional function of $x$, while removal is proportional to $x$. Time and activities are <dimensionless variables>. At $x=0$ the <vector field> points into the nonnegative domain, and $dx/dt\leq u+\beta-x$ prevents unbounded growth there. This makes the toy useful for asking about a bounded switch; it supplies no molecular mechanism for the chosen rate law.

At an <equilibrium point>, $f=0$. For a simple <equilibrium point>, the sign of the <derivative>
$$
f_x=\frac{2\beta x}{(1+x^2)^2}-1
$$
determines local <asymptotic stability>. If $\beta=1$, the maximum reinforcing slope is $3\sqrt3/8<1$, so $f$ decreases strictly: every $u\geq0$ has one <asymptotically stable> <equilibrium point> despite <positive feedback>.

Three <equilibrium points> become possible only if the reinforcing slope exceeds removal's slope somewhere. Its maximum is $3\sqrt3\beta/8$, at $x=1/\sqrt3$. Consequently this toy requires
$$
\beta>\frac{8}{3\sqrt3}\simeq1.53960
$$
for two distinct folds. This is a necessary threshold for a <bistability> interval in this toy, not a universal biochemical number. At a fold, both $f=0$ and $f_x=0$, giving
$$
(1+x^2)^2=2\beta x,\qquad u=x-\frac{\beta x^2}{1+x^2}.
$$
For $\beta=1.8$, the folds are at $(x,u)\simeq(0.350003,0.153563)$ and $(0.887240,0.094405)$. Between these input values there are two <asymptotically stable> <equilibrium points> separated by an unstable one. At $u=0.12$, the three activities are approximately $0.171331$, $0.621230$ and $1.127439$; the low and high ones are stable.

A sufficiently slow increasing sweep follows the low branch to $u_{\mathrm{on}}\simeq0.153563$ and then jumps upward. A decreasing sweep follows the high branch to $u_{\mathrm{off}}\simeq0.094405$ and then jumps downward: <hysteresis>. These are limiting quasistatic thresholds, with relaxation between input changes and negligible perturbations that could cause earlier switching. Finite sweep rates and fluctuations can alter observed thresholds. A switchlike response with only one stable <equilibrium point> does not have this equilibrium <hysteresis> mechanism.

= Cell-cycle activation feedback
{parent=Positive feedback}

<Cell-cycle activation feedback> is the reinforcement of <Cdc2> activation through <Cdc25> and suppression of <Wee1>. Its role is to help make transitions in the <cell cycle> decisive; the <cell-cycle hysteresis experiment> tests the resulting states rather than inferring them solely from circuit arrows.

= Cell-cycle hysteresis experiment
{parent=Cell-cycle activation feedback}

A 2003 study split a <Xenopus laevis egg extract> into portions with different histories. One received <calcium> to destroy endogenous <cyclin B> before receiving nondegradable <cyclin B>; the other received nondegradable <cyclin B> first, then <calcium>. The same sustained <cyclin B> dose was approached from low or high <Cdc2> activity. A <phosphorimager> quantified <histone H1> <phosphorylation>, sampling every $15$–$30$ minutes until an apparent stationary response. At $45$–$60\,\mathrm{nM}$, the dose maintained high activity but failed to induce it from the low state.<Building a cell cycle oscillator: hysteresis and bistability in the activation of Cdc2 by Joseph R. Pomerening and others>[pp. 3–4, Figure 3a–c: history protocols and H1 readout; p. 6, Methods: phosphorimaging]{ref}

Controls tracked labeled endogenous <cyclin B> disappearance after <calcium> treatment and persistence of nondegradable <cyclin B>, addressing unequal remaining input. The comparison supports <hysteresis> under extract conditions; finite observations cannot establish indefinite persistence. It does not isolate each reinforcing path or establish thresholds in intact <biological cells>.<Building a cell cycle oscillator: hysteresis and bistability in the activation of Cdc2 by Joseph R. Pomerening and others>[p. 3, control discussion; p. 4, Figure 3h; reported 60–90 minute approach to apparent steady state]{ref}

= Xenopus laevis egg extract
{parent=Cell-cycle hysteresis experiment}
{c}

A <Xenopus laevis egg extract> is a preparation of egg contents retaining biochemical machinery of the <cell cycle> outside intact <biological cells>.

= Histone H1
{parent=Cell-cycle hysteresis experiment}

<Histone H1> is the <protein> used as a substrate for the <Cdc2> activity readout in the <cell-cycle hysteresis experiment>.

= Phosphorimager
{parent=Cell-cycle hysteresis experiment}

= Cyclin-dependent kinase 1
{parent=Cell-cycle activation feedback}

= Cdc2
{synonym}
{c}

<Cdc2> is a <protein> <enzyme> regulated by binding <cyclin B> and by <phosphorylation>. The experimental source uses the historical name <Cdc2>.

= Cyclin B
{parent=Cell-cycle activation feedback}

<Cyclin B> is the regulatory <protein> binding <Cdc2> in the <cell-cycle activation feedback> example.

= Cdc25
{parent=Cell-cycle activation feedback}
{c}

<Cdc25> is an <enzyme> removing inhibitory <phosphorylation> from <Cdc2>.

= Wee1
{parent=Cell-cycle activation feedback}
{c}

<Wee1> is an <enzyme> inhibiting <Cdc2> through <phosphorylation>.

= Signaling adaptation
{parent=Signal transduction}

<Signaling adaptation> is a decline of a response toward its earlier level while the initiating <signal> remains present. Specify which response is measured and its baseline: complete <signaling adaptation> means eventual return to that baseline; partial <signaling adaptation> leaves a lasting displacement. The continuing <signal> distinguishes <signaling adaptation> from simply switching off the stimulus. <Signaling adaptation> describes behavior, while <signaling feedback> describes a causal arrangement, so neither implies the other.

By reducing a persistent response to background <ligand> <concentration>, <signaling adaptation> can make a <biological cell> sensitive to changes rather than only to absolute levels. Resetting the response helps leave room for another change over a useful range of <ligand> <concentrations>. This is valuable in <chemotaxis>: swimming <bacteria> encounter changing chemical conditions, and their <signal transduction> influences whether a run continues or a tumble occurs. <Signaling adaptation> helps separate a change encountered during motion from a lasting background. This usefulness does not imply exact return or equal sensitivity at every possible <concentration>. <Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/General Principles of Cell Communication>{ref} https://groups.csail.mit.edu/mac/projects/amorphous/6.978/papers/doyle-PNAS-2000.pdf[Yi, Huang, Simon and Doyle, PNAS 97 (2000), pp. 4649–4653: chemotaxis and adaptation]{ref}

Here is a solvable <incoherent feedforward loop> toy that exhibits complete <signaling adaptation> without <signaling feedback>. The input $s(t)$ jumps from $0$ to $1$ at $t=0$ and remains $1$, a <Heaviside step function>. Let $x$ be a fast activating branch, $a$ a slower inhibitory branch, and $r=x-a$ a readout. The activities $s,x,a,r$ are <dimensionless variables>; physical time is in seconds. Choose <time constants> $\tau_x=1$ second and $\tau_a=5$ seconds, with <initial conditions> $x(0)=a(0)=0$:
$$
\tau_x\frac{dx}{dt}=s-x,\qquad \tau_a\frac{da}{dt}=s-a,\qquad r=x-a.
$$
Both branches read $s$ directly. Neither branch depends on $r$, and $a$ does not change $x$: the subtraction only defines the readout. Thus the slow opposing influence is feedforward, with no closed <feedback control> loop.

For $t\geq0$, solving the two <ordinary differential equations> gives
$$
x(t)=1-e^{-t/\tau_x},\qquad a(t)=1-e^{-t/\tau_a},\qquad r(t)=e^{-t/\tau_a}-e^{-t/\tau_x}.
$$
Writing $t$ for the numerical time in seconds, these are $x=1-e^{-t}$, $a=1-e^{-t/5}$ and $r=e^{-t/5}-e^{-t}$. Initially $r=0$, then $x$ rises faster than $a$, producing a pulse. At long times $x$ and $a$ both approach $1$, so $r$ returns to $0$ despite sustained $s=1$. For this upward step $e^{-t/5}\geq e^{-t}$, hence $r(t)\geq0$. The striking feature of this <signaling adaptation> toy is that both branches remain active while the readout becomes quiet: the continuing background and the response to its arrival are separable.

\Image[signaling-adaptation-step-response.png]
{title=A sustained unit input and a transient readout in an <incoherent feedforward loop> toy}
{description=Original computed curves for <time constants> of $1$ and $5$ seconds, zero <initial conditions>, and normalized <dimensionless variables>. The upper panel shows the sustained input and the two branches; the lower panel shows the readout $r=x-a$, its maximum near $2.0118$ seconds, and its approach to the zero baseline. Values and time scales are chosen for illustration, not fitted to a measured <signal transduction> pathway.}

Find the pulse maximum by differentiating the <exponential functions>. With numerical time in seconds, the <derivative> is
$$
r'(t)=-\frac15e^{-t/5}+e^{-t}.
$$
The stationary condition is $e^{-4t/5}=1/5$, so the <natural logarithm> gives
$$
t_{\mathrm{peak}}=\frac54\ln5\simeq2.0118\ \text{seconds},\qquad r_{\mathrm{peak}}=\frac45\,5^{-1/4}\simeq0.53499.
$$
Before this time the <derivative> is positive; afterwards it is negative. Together with $r(0)=0$ and $\lim_{t\to\infty}r(t)=0$, this proves that it is the unique pulse maximum. As another numerical check, at $t=10$ seconds $x\simeq0.999955$, $a\simeq0.864665$ and $r\simeq0.135290$: the slow branch is still catching up.

The subtraction $r=x-a$ is a mathematical readout, not a claim that an actual chemical <concentration> equals this difference for arbitrary inputs. Nonnegativity was established only for the specified upward step; other input histories can give a negative signed readout. Complete <signaling adaptation> here also relies on equal eventual branch gains: changing the readout to $r=x-0.9a$ leaves $r_*=0.1$ for $s=1$. This toy illustrates cancellation and different <time constants>; it does not establish molecular implementation or robustness to arbitrary parameter changes. Ma and colleagues' 2009 study separately analyzed biochemical <mathematical models> with <negative feedback> and <incoherent feedforward loops>, identifying additional conditions for <signaling adaptation> in those models. https://cqb.pku.edu.cn/tanglab/pdf/78.pdf[Ma et al., Cell 138 (2009): adaptive architectures and their kinetic conditions]{ref}

A historical connection to <feedback control> comes from Yi, Huang, Simon and Doyle's 2000 analysis of a bacterial <chemotaxis> model. They identified <integral action> in its regulation of <receptor> activity: accumulated deviations alter a slower adjustment, which in turn changes the measured activity. At a stationary state, an adjustment that integrates a nonzero error cannot remain constant, explaining how zero error can follow. Their conclusion depended on stated biochemical assumptions and stable behavior; it was not a claim that every <biological cell> uses that mechanism. A transient pulse alone, as the <incoherent feedforward loop> toy shows, cannot identify the underlying <signaling feedback> architecture. https://groups.csail.mit.edu/mac/projects/amorphous/6.978/papers/doyle-PNAS-2000.pdf[Yi et al., Robust perfect adaptation in bacterial chemotaxis through integral feedback control, PNAS 97 (2000): Results and Discussion]{ref}

= Incoherent feedforward loop
{parent=Signal transduction}

An <incoherent feedforward loop> carries influences from a shared input to a shared output along routes with opposite net signs. In the <signaling adaptation> toy, $s$ drives fast $x$ and slow $a$, which contribute with opposite signs to $r=x-a$. Despite the conventional word "loop", those routes do not return the output to an earlier stage: this arrangement is distinct from <negative feedback>. Opposing routes alone do not guarantee complete <signaling adaptation>. https://cqb.pku.edu.cn/tanglab/pdf/78.pdf[Ma et al., Cell 138 (2009), p. 764: definition of an incoherent feedforward loop]{ref}

= Centrifugation
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

<Centrifugation> separates suspended material by its motion relative to a rotating liquid. A <centrifuge rotor> creates an outward <centrifugal acceleration> in the <rotating reference frame>; material denser than its surroundings tends to move outward, opposed by <drag force>. Collected material can form a <centrifugation pellet>, leaving a <supernatant>, or remain in separated zones within a <density gradient>. The useful question is which physical difference the separation exploits: a difference in <sedimentation coefficient> or a difference in <buoyant density>.

\Image[centrifuge-rotor-and-separated-fractions.png]
{title=<Centrifuge rotor> geometry and the <centrifugation pellet>–<supernatant> distinction}
{description=Original schematic of one swung-out tube during rotation and a conceptual pelleting comparison. <Rotor radius> increases toward the tube's outer end. Colors and particle counts illustrate incomplete separation and trapping; they are not measured recovery fractions. The tube remains horizontal in this depiction of the rotating geometry.}

<Centrifugation> makes otherwise slow settling useful for studying <biological cells>, <organelles>, <proteins> and <DNA>. Separation and identification are different steps: a <centrifugation pellet> is a collected fraction, not proof that it contains only one kind of <organelle>. The <lysozyme wall-removal experiment> illustrates a complementary readout: testing material released into the separated liquid can be more informative than inspecting the retained solids.

= Centrifuge rotor
{parent=Centrifugation}

A <centrifuge rotor> holds sample vessels around the rotation axis. A fixed-angle <centrifuge rotor> holds tubes at a fixed inclination; a swinging-bucket <centrifuge rotor> lets them swing outward during rotation. Material moves away from the axis, so a <centrifugation pellet> can first collect against an outer tube wall rather than travel along the entire tube axis. Geometry sets both the <rotor radius> and the travel distance.<Basics: Centrifugal Force & Centrifuge Type by Eppendorf>[Basics: Centrifugal Force & Centrifuge Type, Fixed-angle or swing-bucket rotors? and Fixed-angle rotor: tube orientation and pellet position]{ref}

= Rotor radius
{parent=Centrifuge rotor}
{title2=$r$}

The <rotor radius> $r$ is the perpendicular distance from the rotation axis to a specified sample position, not the tube's length. A filled tube spans a range $r_{\min}\leq r\leq r_{\max}$. Consequently, one rotational speed produces different local <centrifugal accelerations> along that range. A reported <relative centrifugal force> needs a stated reference position, such as the outermost sample radius.

= Relative centrifugal force
{parent=Centrifuge rotor}
{title2=$\mathrm{RCF}=a/g_0$}

<Relative centrifugal force> is the <dimensionless> ratio of the local <centrifugal acceleration> magnitude $a$ to the conventional gravitational reference $g_0=9.80665\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. Despite its name, it is an acceleration ratio: the actual <force> on a specified <mass> $m$ is $ma$. With <angular speed> $\omega$ and <rotor radius> $r$, $a=\omega^2r$. If $n$ is the numerical rotational speed in revolutions per minute and $r_{\mathrm{cm}}$ the numerical <rotor radius> in centimetres, unit conversion gives
$$
\omega=\frac{2\pi n}{60}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}},\qquad
\mathrm{RCF}=\frac{(r_{\mathrm{cm}}/100)(2\pi n/60)^2}{9.80665}
\simeq1.118\times10^{-5}r_{\mathrm{cm}}n^2.
$$
Thus rpm alone does not specify the <centrifugal acceleration>.<Basics: Centrifugal Force & Centrifuge Type by Eppendorf>[Important definitions, Rcm vs rpm, and How to calculate relative centrifugal force: radius dependence and acceleration ratio]{ref}

\Image[centrifuge-radius-speed-relative-acceleration.png]
{title=<Relative centrifugal force> for stipulated <rotor radii> and rotational speeds}
{description=Original theoretical curves calculated from $a=\omega^2r$ with $g_0=9.80665\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. The <rotor radii> of $5$, $10$ and $15\,\mathrm{cm}$ are chosen examples, not specifications of real <centrifuge rotors>. The plotted range is not an instrument operating limit or a recommendation.}

For a stipulated $3000\,\mathrm{rpm}$, <rotor radii> of $5$ and $10\,\mathrm{cm}$ give approximately $503$ and $1006$ times $g_0$. Doubling the <rotor radius> doubles <relative centrifugal force>; doubling rpm quadruples it. Even equal <relative centrifugal forces> do not guarantee equal separation: travel distance, elapsed time, <viscosity>, sample <density> and particle shape still matter. These are comparisons of a <mathematical model>, not instructions for running an instrument.

= Centrifugation pellet
{parent=Centrifugation}

A <centrifugation pellet> is material accumulated against the vessel wall by <centrifugation>. Its existence establishes collection, not purity; other suspended material can be trapped or co-sediment.

= Supernatant
{parent=Centrifugation}

A <supernatant> is the liquid remaining above settled or pelleted material. It can contain dissolved <molecules> and particles that have not reached the <centrifugation pellet>; a visually clear <supernatant> need not be chemically empty.

= Sedimentation coefficient
{parent=Centrifugation}
{title2=$s=v/(\omega^2r)$}

The <sedimentation coefficient> $s$ is radial drift <velocity> divided by local <centrifugal acceleration>. It has units of time and combines particle properties with those of the surrounding liquid. In a dilute, deterministic <mathematical model> with negligible particle <inertia>, an effective displaced volume $V$, particle <mass> $m$, local liquid <density> $\rho_f$ and <linear friction coefficient> $f>0$ give
$$
f v=(m-\rho_f V)\omega^2r,\qquad s=\frac{v}{\omega^2r}=\frac{m-\rho_f V}{f}.
$$
The subtraction represents <buoyancy>. Equal <mass> does not imply equal $s$: changing shape changes <drag force>, and changing the medium changes both <buoyancy> and <viscosity>.https://www.beckman.com/resources/technologies/centrifugation/ultracentrifugation/[An Introduction to Ultracentrifugation, The Physics Behind the Technology: mass, size, shape, medium density and viscosity]{ref}

For an isolated <sphere> of radius $b$ and uniform <density> $\rho_p$, <Stokes's law> supplies $f=6\pi\eta b$, where $\eta$ is dynamic <viscosity>, and $m=(4\pi/3)b^3\rho_p$. Therefore
$$
s=\frac{2b^2(\rho_p-\rho_f)}{9\eta}.
$$
This original force-balance check predicts fourfold $s$ on doubling $b$ with everything else fixed, and zero drift when $\rho_p=\rho_f$. It applies to dilute isolated <spheres> in <Stokes flow>, not arbitrary folded <proteins> or crowded suspensions. If $s$ and $\omega$ remain constant in a uniform medium, the <ordinary differential equation> $dr/dt=s\omega^2r$ has the <exponential function> solution $r(t)=r(0)e^{s\omega^2t}$. Arrival at an outer boundary therefore depends on starting position as well as $s$; a separation has no universal size cutoff.

= Differential centrifugation
{parent=Centrifugation}
{wiki}

<Differential centrifugation> successively separates <centrifugation pellets> from <supernatants> by differences in settling rate. A first <centrifugation pellet> can enrich faster-settling material; a later <centrifugation pellet> from its <supernatant> can enrich slower-settling material. The fractions overlap because particles have different starting positions and can become trapped. <Differential centrifugation> is consequently an enrichment method, not automatic identification or complete purification.https://www.beckman.com/resources/technologies/centrifugation/principles/particle-separation/[Particle Separation: differential pelleting, smaller particles initially near the tube bottom, and incomplete separation]{ref}

= Density-gradient centrifugation
{parent=Centrifugation}

<Density-gradient centrifugation> uses a liquid whose <density> varies along the separation direction. A band does not by itself establish which mechanism produced it: <rate-zonal centrifugation> separates by migration rate before equilibrium, whereas <isopycnic centrifugation> uses equilibrium positions set by <buoyant density>.

= Isopycnic centrifugation
{parent=Density-gradient centrifugation}

= Equilibrium density-gradient centrifugation
{synonym}

In <isopycnic centrifugation>, a species concentrates near the position where the liquid's <density> matches its <buoyant density>. In a <density gradient> increasing outward, material displaced inward encounters less-dense liquid and tends to move outward; material displaced outward encounters denser liquid and tends to move inward. <Diffusion> broadens the resulting band. If the <density gradient> never reaches the species' <buoyant density>, a matching equilibrium position need not exist within the vessel.https://www.beckman.com/resources/technologies/centrifugation/ultracentrifugation/[An Introduction to Ultracentrifugation, Isopycnic Centrifugation: matching buoyant density and diffusion in equilibrium gradients]{ref}

The 1958 paper <The replication of DNA in Escherichia coli by Matthew Meselson and Franklin W. Stahl> (pp. 672–676, Figures 1–4) makes the distinction concrete. Its separation apparatus was a Spinco Model E ultracentrifuge with an <Escherichia coli> lysate in a <cesium chloride density gradient>. Ultraviolet <wave absorption> photographs of the rotating sample were scanned with a recording microdensitometer: peak position reported <buoyant density>, while signal above baseline reported <DNA> <concentration>.<The replication of DNA in Escherichia coli by Matthew Meselson and Franklin W. Stahl>[p. 676, Experimental: Spinco Model E and recording microdensitometer; p. 675, Figure 4: peak position and baseline]{ref}

Mixed lysates labeled fully with the <nitrogen> <isotope> $^{15}\mathrm{N}$ and unlabeled lysates supplied reference bands; a mixture including intermediate material checked its position relative to those references. Figure 1 followed band formation over time, helping distinguish a migrating distribution from an approximately stationary one. These controls address resolution, calibration and approach to equilibrium. They do not make every ultraviolet-absorbing component identifiable as <DNA>, or turn a band into a direct image of individual strands. The <DNA replication> article develops the copying inference; here the key result is a separation by <buoyant density>, not a claim that heavier-labeled <DNA> is physically larger.<The replication of DNA in Escherichia coli by Matthew Meselson and Franklin W. Stahl>[pp. 672–673, Figures 1–2; p. 675, Figure 4 density references and microdensitometer baseline; p. 676, Experimental: Spinco Model E and ultraviolet photographs]{ref}

= Buoyant density
{parent=Isopycnic centrifugation}
{title2=$\rho_b$}

<Buoyant density> is the surrounding liquid <density> at which a species has no net centrifugal drift under the stated conditions. It depends on interactions with the medium, including associated solvent, rather than necessarily equaling the dry substance's <density>. Equal <buoyant densities> can give overlapping bands even for differently sized <proteins> or <DNA> fragments.https://www.beckman.com/resources/technologies/centrifugation/ultracentrifugation/[An Introduction to Ultracentrifugation, Isopycnic Centrifugation: buoyant density depends on the surrounding medium]{ref}

= Cesium chloride density gradient
{parent=Isopycnic centrifugation}

A <cesium chloride density gradient> forms when the <salt> $\mathrm{CsCl}$ redistributes in a rotating <chemical solution>. It can supply matching-density positions for <DNA> during <isopycnic centrifugation>.https://www.beckman.com/resources/technologies/centrifugation/density-gradient-centrifugation/[Types of Density Gradients and Types of Gradient Material: self-forming CsCl gradients and nucleic-acid separation]{ref}

= Rate-zonal centrifugation
{parent=Density-gradient centrifugation}

<Rate-zonal centrifugation> resolves an initially narrow sample zone by differences in <sedimentation coefficient> during a finite interval. Its bands remain transient: extending the interval can carry material into a <centrifugation pellet>. Thus a <density gradient> need not imply an equilibrium <buoyant density> measurement.https://www.beckman.com/resources/technologies/centrifugation/density-gradient-centrifugation/[Types of Density Gradients, Rate Zonal Centrifugation and Table 1: migration rates before equilibrium or complete sedimentation]{ref}

= Biophysical measurement
{parent=Biology}

= Förster resonance energy transfer
{parent=Biophysical measurement}
{c}

= FRET
{synonym}
{c}

<Förster resonance energy transfer> is a nonradiative transfer of <energy> from an <excited state> of a <FRET donor> to a nearby <FRET acceptor>, through coupling of their <electric dipoles>. The transfer step does not emit a <photon> that the <FRET acceptor> subsequently absorbs. It competes with the <FRET donor>'s own <fluorescence> and other routes out of its <excited state>. A fluorescent <FRET acceptor> can then emit <fluorescence>; an absorbing but nonfluorescent <FRET acceptor> can still receive the transferred <energy>.<Advanced Fluorescence Microscopy Techniques—FRAP, FLIP, FLAP, FRET and FLIM by Hellen C. Ishikawa-Ankerhold and others>[§3.1: nonradiative dipole coupling and a potentially nonfluorescent acceptor]{ref}

The striking use of <FRET> is to turn a change in molecular proximity into an optical readout. <Proteins> carrying a <FRET donor> and a <FRET acceptor> may change the readout when they associate, as in the <chemotaxis FRET assay>; two labels on one <protein> may report a change in its shape. Interpretation depends on the <FRET spectral overlap>, <FRET dipole orientation factor> and labeling geometry, as well as separation. A change in <FRET> is therefore evidence about proximity under specified conditions, rather than a direct measurement of an arbitrary distance.<Advanced Fluorescence Microscopy Techniques—FRAP, FLIP, FLAP, FRET and FLIM by Hellen C. Ishikawa-Ankerhold and others>[§3.1 and §3.3: dependence on distance and orientation; inter- and intramolecular applications]{ref}

For a single pair with fixed <Förster distance> $R_0$ and label separation $r$, the <FRET efficiency> is
$$
E=\frac{1}{1+(r/R_0)^6}.
$$
At $r=R_0$, half the <FRET donor> excitations transfer <energy>. At $r=R_0/2$, the model gives $E=64/65\approx98.46\%$; at $r=2R_0$, it gives $E=1/65\approx1.54\%$. The sixth power makes <FRET> especially useful near the <Förster distance>, with little sensitivity to further separation once transfer is already negligible. These are model calculations, not measurements of a particular pair.

\Image[forster-distance-transfer-efficiency.png]
{title=<FRET efficiency> versus separation relative to the <Förster distance>}
{description=Original single-pair <mathematical model> with fixed <Förster distance>. The shaded interval is $0.2\leq E\leq0.8$. The curve does not establish a universal working range or justify the <electric dipole> approximation for overlapping labels.}

= FRET donor
{parent=Förster resonance energy transfer}
{c}

A <FRET donor> supplies the initial <excited state> <energy>. Its <fluorescence> provides both an emission channel and a reference <FRET donor lifetime> when no <FRET acceptor> is present. Calling a label a <FRET donor> describes its role in a chosen pair, rather than a permanent classification of the <molecule>.

= FRET acceptor
{parent=Förster resonance energy transfer}
{c}

A <FRET acceptor> receives the <excited state> <energy>. Its absorption must support <FRET spectral overlap> with the <FRET donor>. <FRET sensitized emission> requires a fluorescent <FRET acceptor>; measuring a shortened <FRET donor lifetime> does not.

= FRET efficiency
{parent=Förster resonance energy transfer}
{c}
{title2=$E$}

<FRET efficiency> is the <probability> that an excitation of the <FRET donor> ends in transfer to the <FRET acceptor>. Write $k_D=k_{\rm r}+k_{\rm nr}$ for the total decay rate without a <FRET acceptor>, including radiative <fluorescence> and other decay, and $k_T$ for the transfer rate. For constant competing rates,
$$
k_T=k_D\left(\frac{R_0}{r}\right)^6,\qquad E=\frac{k_T}{k_D+k_T}=\int_0^\infty k_T e^{-(k_D+k_T)t}\,dt.
$$
The <integral> counts transfer events among all excitations in a population with <exponential decay>. Substitution gives the separation law above. This short rate argument assumes one <FRET donor>, one <FRET acceptor>, weak coupling, an available <FRET acceptor>, and fixed geometry during the decay; it is not a model for arbitrary interacting labels.<Advanced Fluorescence Microscopy Techniques—FRAP, FLIP, FLAP, FRET and FLIM by Hellen C. Ishikawa-Ankerhold and others>[§3.1, equations 14–16 and discussion before §3.3: single-pair assumptions and transfer efficiency]{ref}

= Förster distance
{parent=FRET efficiency}
{c}
{title2=$R_0$}

= Förster radius
{synonym}
{c}

The <Förster distance> is the separation giving <FRET efficiency> $E=1/2$ for the specified pair and conditions. It is a property of the optical pair and its environment, rather than a universal length. In the usual pair model,
$$
R_0^6\propto \kappa^2 Q_D J n^{-4},
$$
where $\kappa^2$ is the <FRET dipole orientation factor>, $Q_D$ the <FRET donor quantum yield>, $J$ the <FRET spectral overlap> <integral>, and $n$ the medium's <refractive index>. The omitted proportionality constant depends on the unit convention for $J$.<Advanced Fluorescence Microscopy Techniques—FRAP, FLIP, FLAP, FRET and FLIM by Hellen C. Ishikawa-Ankerhold and others>[§3.1, equations 16–18: factors controlling Förster distance]{ref}

If <FRET efficiency> is known and those assumptions hold, inversion gives
$$
r=R_0\left(E^{-1}-1\right)^{1/6},\qquad \frac{d\log r}{dE}=-\frac{1}{6E(1-E)}.
$$
The <derivative> explains why errors become troublesome near $E=0$ or $E=1$. An illustrative pair with $R_0=4$ nanometres and another with $R_0=6$ nanometres both give $E=1/2$ at their different <Förster distances>: knowing <FRET efficiency> alone does not determine $r$.

A heterogeneous population adds another ambiguity. In a synthetic example with equal weights per <FRET donor> excitation, efficiencies $0.1$ and $0.9$ average to $0.5$, but their separations are $9^{1/6}R_0$ and $9^{-1/6}R_0$. Their average separation is approximately $1.068R_0$, whereas inverting the average <FRET efficiency> gives $R_0$. A measured emission <ratio> has additional detection weights; it need not equal even this excitation-weighted average. The transferable lesson is that an average readout does not uniquely specify a distribution of molecular separations.

= FRET spectral overlap
{parent=Förster distance}
{c}
{title2=$J$}

<FRET spectral overlap> quantifies overlap between the <FRET donor>'s emission and the <FRET acceptor>'s absorption as functions of <wavelength> $\lambda$. With normalized donor emission $f_D$ and acceptor molar absorption coefficient $\varepsilon_A$, one convention is
$$
\int_0^\infty f_D(\lambda)\,d\lambda=1,\qquad J=\int_0^\infty f_D(\lambda)\varepsilon_A(\lambda)\lambda^4\,d\lambda.
$$
Matching two emission colors is insufficient: the receiving absorption matters.<Advanced Fluorescence Microscopy Techniques—FRAP, FLIP, FLAP, FRET and FLIM by Hellen C. Ishikawa-Ankerhold and others>[§3.1, equation 17 and Figure 19: normalized donor emission and acceptor absorption overlap]{ref}

= FRET dipole orientation factor
{parent=Förster distance}
{c}
{title2=$\kappa^2$}

The <FRET dipole orientation factor> describes the geometry of the two transition <electric dipoles> relative to their separation. Let $\widehat{\boldsymbol\mu}_D$, $\widehat{\boldsymbol\mu}_A$ and $\widehat{\mathbf r}$ be their <unit vectors>. Then
$$
\kappa^2=\left[\widehat{\boldsymbol\mu}_D\cdot\widehat{\boldsymbol\mu}_A-3(\widehat{\boldsymbol\mu}_D\cdot\widehat{\mathbf r})(\widehat{\boldsymbol\mu}_A\cdot\widehat{\mathbf r})\right]^2,\qquad 0\leq\kappa^2\leq4.
$$
For a separation along $z$, parallel <electric dipoles> along $z$ give $\kappa^2=4$, parallel ones along $x$ give $1$, and one along $x$ with the other along $y$ gives $0$. Thus rotating a pair can change <FRET efficiency> without changing its separation.

The familiar value $\kappa^2=2/3$ requires suitable rapid, independent orientation averaging during the <FRET donor lifetime>. Random orientations across a population of slowly rotating fluorescent <proteins> do not establish that dynamic condition. <Quantitative time domain analysis of lifetime-based Förster resonant energy transfer measurements with fluorescent proteins: Static random isotropic fluorophore orientation distributions by Yuriy Alexandrov and others>{ref}

= FRET donor quantum yield
{parent=Förster distance}
{c}
{title2=$Q_D$}

The <FRET donor quantum yield> is the emitted <fluorescence> <photon> fraction per absorbed excitation <photon>, measured without a <FRET acceptor>. In the constant-rate model used for <FRET efficiency>, $Q_D=k_{\rm r}/(k_{\rm r}+k_{\rm nr})$. Changing the <FRET donor quantum yield> changes the <Förster distance>, even if label positions remain fixed.

= FRET donor lifetime
{parent=FRET efficiency}
{c}
{title2=$\tau_D,\tau_{DA}$}

The <FRET donor lifetime> is the mean time spent in the <excited state>. For the constant-rate model of <FRET efficiency>,
$$
\tau_D=\frac1{k_D},\qquad \tau_{DA}=\frac1{k_D+k_T},\qquad E=1-\frac{\tau_{DA}}{\tau_D}.
$$
After a sufficiently brief excitation pulse, the <FRET donor>'s <fluorescence> follows <exponential decay>, $I(t)=I(0)e^{-t/\tau}$. With the illustrative values $\tau_D=4$ nanoseconds and $\tau_{DA}=2$ nanoseconds, the inferred <FRET efficiency> is $1/2$. Multiplying $I(0)$ changes brightness without changing this decay time.

The lifetime comparison requires the same nontransfer decay rates in the reference and paired samples. Changes in the <FRET donor>'s environment or a different nonradiative loss can also shorten the <FRET donor lifetime>. Heterogeneous <FRET efficiencies> or slowly varying <FRET dipole orientation factors> need an appropriate decay model; substituting any fitted average lifetime into the single-pair formula can mislead. <Quantitative time domain analysis of lifetime-based Förster resonant energy transfer measurements with fluorescent proteins: Static random isotropic fluorophore orientation distributions by Yuriy Alexandrov and others>{ref}

\Image[fret-fluorescence-lifetime-readouts.png]
{title=<FRET sensitized emission>, optical controls and the <FRET donor lifetime>}
{description=Original schematic and synthetic curves. The spectral panel assumes equal <fluorescence> yields and collection efficiencies for the two labels; its amplitudes are illustrative, not a calibration or measured cyan/yellow spectra. The lifetime panel uses $\tau_D=4$ and $\tau_{DA}=2$ nanoseconds. Dashed optical paths require <FRET optical crosstalk> controls; they do not represent the nonradiative transfer mechanism.}

= FRET sensitized emission
{parent=Förster resonance energy transfer}
{c}

<FRET sensitized emission> is <FRET acceptor> <fluorescence> caused by transfer from an excited <FRET donor>. Excite predominantly the <FRET donor> and collect both emission bands. Reduced <FRET donor> emission and increased <FRET acceptor> emission are useful together, but an acceptor-to-donor emission <ratio> is not automatically the <FRET efficiency>: brightness, label abundance and channel collection also enter.<Advanced Fluorescence Microscopy Techniques—FRAP, FLIP, FLAP, FRET and FLIM by Hellen C. Ishikawa-Ankerhold and others>[§3.4.2: sensitized emission and dependence on optical correction and label concentrations]{ref}

= FRET optical crosstalk
{parent=FRET sensitized emission}
{c}

<FRET optical crosstalk> includes <FRET donor> emission leaking into the <FRET acceptor> detection band and direct excitation of the <FRET acceptor> by the nominal <FRET donor> excitation light. Measure donor-only, acceptor-only and unlabeled preparations with the same <fluorescence microscope> settings to separate these contributions and background.<Advanced Fluorescence Microscopy Techniques—FRAP, FLIP, FLAP, FRET and FLIM by Hellen C. Ishikawa-Ankerhold and others>[§3.4.2, samples i–iii and equations 20–21: spectral correction controls]{ref}

An explicit linear measurement model makes the controls' purpose clear. Let $D$ be background-subtracted donor-band counts under donor excitation, $A$ acceptor-band counts under acceptor excitation, and $F$ acceptor-band counts under donor excitation. Donor-only and acceptor-only preparations determine leakage coefficients $a$ and $b$. Then
$$
S=F-aD-bA
$$
is a corrected <FRET sensitized emission> signal under the assumed linear, fixed optical response. In a synthetic example $D=100$, $A=80$, $a=0.10$, $b=0.05$ and $F=34$ give $S=20$. Even a nontransferring sample would give $F=14$ from these two contaminants. Correcting that signal still does not convert $S$ into <FRET efficiency> without a calibration of excitation, yields and detection.

A biological comparison also needs a pairing-negative preparation and a known transferring positive preparation. Match label abundance and localization where possible; changing <concentration> can change encounter frequency as well as brightness. Check that attaching the labels preserves the <protein> function being investigated. Otherwise the measurement can faithfully report a perturbation introduced by the labels themselves.

= FRET photobleaching controls
{parent=Förster resonance energy transfer}
{c}

In <FRET photobleaching controls>, selectively destroy the <FRET acceptor>'s ability to absorb and test whether <FRET donor> <fluorescence> increases. Under unchanged excitation and detection, complete loss of transfer, and an intact <FRET donor>,
$$
E=1-\frac{I_{D,\rm before}}{I_{D,\rm after}}.
$$
This is a destructive comparison. Monitor acceptor loss, donor-only samples exposed to the bleaching illumination, and an unbleached reference region or time trace. Incomplete bleaching, donor damage, movement or label replenishment violate the comparison. During ordinary recordings, gradual unequal bleaching can instead produce an apparent change of <FRET> without a biological change.<Advanced Fluorescence Microscopy Techniques—FRAP, FLIP, FLAP, FRET and FLIM by Hellen C. Ishikawa-Ankerhold and others>[§3.4.1.1–3.4.1.2: donor recovery, sample damage, acceptor replenishment and photoconversion artifacts]{ref}

= FRET proximity readout experiment
{parent=Förster resonance energy transfer}
{c}

In <Receptor sensitivity in bacterial chemotaxis by Victor Sourjik and others>, <Victor Sourjik> and <Howard C. Berg> used a cyan fluorescent tag on <CheZ> as <FRET donor> and a yellow tag on <CheY> as <FRET acceptor>. <Escherichia coli> <biological cells> adhered to a polylysine-coated coverslip in a continuously perfused chamber. A <fluorescence microscope> excited them with a $442$-nanometre <laser>; split emission bands fed two <photon>-counting detectors. The yellow/cyan emission <ratio> fell when attractant was added and rose when it was removed.<Receptor sensitivity in bacterial chemotaxis by Victor Sourjik and others>[Introduction, Materials and Methods: Preparation of Cells and Data Acquisition; Results and Figure 1]{ref}

Saturating attractant increased cyan emission by an amount matching selective bleaching of the yellow tag: removing the <FRET acceptor> supported relief of <FRET donor> transfer. Strains carrying the tagged pair but lacking other <chemotaxis> machinery showed no stimulus-dependent emission <ratio> change. Weak illumination reduced bleaching drift.<Receptor sensitivity in bacterial chemotaxis by Victor Sourjik and others>[Data Analysis and Results: acceptor bleaching comparison, low illumination, and flhC negative control]{ref}

The inference is a change in the population of nearby tagged <CheY>/<CheZ> pairs associated with <CheY> <phosphorylation>. It neither measures each pair's separation nor directly observes motor motion. The readout also averages many <biological cells>, and perfusion takes finite time. The pathway interpretation belongs to the <chemotaxis FRET assay>; the general measurement lesson is that molecular association can alter <FRET> while the geometry of an individual associated pair stays the same.

= Model organism
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

A <model organism> is an <organism> used to investigate biological processes through accessible, repeatable experiments. Its value combines <experimental tractability> with a carefully delimited relationship to other <organisms>. For example, a <gene deletion> in <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> can test whether a <gene> is required for growth under specified conditions; adding another <gene> can test <functional complementation>. Such interventions support <causal inference> more directly than merely noticing that a <gene> and a <phenotype> occur together. <Budding Yeast for Budding Geneticists: A Primer on the Saccharomyces cerevisiae Model System by Andrea A. Duina and others>[Duina, Miller and Keeney (2014), introduction and Experimental Toolkit and Related Resources: manipulation of genes and phenotypic effects]{ref}

The striking opportunity is to ask two experimentally separate questions: what does this component do here, and which part of that function survives a change of <species> or conditions? The <yeast cross-species complementation experiment> makes both questions concrete. <Model organism evidence transfer> requires specifying the function being compared; a useful <model organism> need not reproduce every feature of another <organism>.

= Experimental tractability
{parent=Model organism}

<Experimental tractability> is the practical ability to cultivate a system, manipulate the variables of interest, and obtain an interpretable readout with appropriate <scientific controls>. In <Saccharomyces cerevisiae>, inexpensive cultivation, accessible <haploid> and <diploid> states, and tools for introducing <DNA> make controlled changes of <genotype> feasible. A <haploid> <biological strain> exposes a changed <allele> without a second copy at that <genetic locus>, while crosses and <meiosis> permit tests of inheritance. <Budding Yeast for Budding Geneticists: A Primer on the Saccharomyces cerevisiae Model System by Andrea A. Duina and others>[Duina, Miller and Keeney (2014), introduction, Figure 2 and Experimental Toolkit and Related Resources]{ref}

An accessible readout is not necessarily a direct measurement of the mechanism. Growth of a <biological colony> integrates many cellular functions. Failure to grow could reflect the intended <gene deletion>, unsuitable conditions, failed <genetic transformation>, or another <mutation>. Matching the <genetic background> and using <scientific controls> narrows these alternatives. Ease of doing an experiment and strength of its inference are distinct advantages.

= Yeast cross-species complementation experiment
{parent=Model organism}

Can a <gene> from <Schizosaccharomyces pombe> replace a missing function in <Saccharomyces cerevisiae>? A modern laboratory project tested <functional complementation> of <methionine> synthesis. For each tested <gene deletion>, the investigators compared <plasmids> carrying the corresponding <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> <gene>, a candidate <Schizosaccharomyces pombe> <gene>, and an unrelated <gene>. The first was a positive <scientific control>; the last was a negative <scientific control>. The paper describes this design rather than publishing a complete set of individual growth measurements. <Pathways over Time: Functional Genomics Research in an Introductory Laboratory Course by Todd D. Reeves and others>[Reeves, Warner, Ludlow and O’Connor (2018), Course Activities, pp. 5–6 and Table 1: three plasmids, replica plating and protein-expression checks]{ref}

The recipient <biological strains> also lacked the ability to make <uracil>. The pYES2.1/V5-His <plasmids> carried URA3, a <gene> restoring that ability, and used the GAL1 <gene promoter> to control the inserted <gene>. Initial plates lacked <uracil> but supplied <methionine>: this selected <biological colonies> containing <plasmids> without requiring successful <methionine> synthesis. Subsequent plates lacking <methionine> tested the missing function, comparing <galactose induction> with <glucose> conditions. Thus selecting for a <plasmid> and testing its functional contribution were separate operations. <Pathways Over Time Plasmids Engage Students in Functional Genomics Research by Clare O’Connor>[O’Connor (2018), Plasmid features enable multiple student experiments and Complementation reveals conservation of function]{ref}

The apparatus can be simple and informative. In the associated protocol, <replica plating> uses a block covered with velveteen to transfer small samples of <biological colonies> from a master plate onto plates with different nutrient conditions. Orientation marks preserve correspondence between locations; gentle contact limits the transferred material. The specified replicas omit <uracil> and <methionine> with either inducing <sugar> or <glucose>, and include a fresh <glucose> plate supplying <methionine>. The readout is growth at corresponding locations, not a direct measurement of <enzyme> activity. <Investigations in Molecular Cell Biology by Clare M. O’Connor/12.6: Exercise 2 - Replica plating and complementation>[O’Connor, Investigations in Molecular Cell Biology, §12.6, introductory paragraphs and steps 1–3: velveteen block, orientation marks and three replica media]{ref}

\Image[yeast-gene-complementation-experimental-design.png]
{title=Separating <plasmid> selection from a <yeast> <functional complementation> test}
{description=Original schematic of the reported experimental design, not copied growth data. All three constructs enter the same <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> <gene deletion> background. Plate labels identify distinct comparisons; arrows show sample transfer, not observed rescue. The candidate <Schizosaccharomyces pombe> outcome must be measured.}

The <scientific controls> answer different objections. Growth on the <methionine>-supplied replica checks survival and transfer. Rescue by the native <gene> checks that this <biological strain> and assay can reveal <functional complementation>. The unrelated <gene> tests whether carrying a <plasmid> alone is sufficient. Comparing induction conditions checks dependence on <gene expression>, although changing the <sugar> also changes <metabolism>: it is not an isolated change of <gene expression> in every other respect.

If the candidate <gene> restores growth while those comparisons behave as intended, it supplies a function sufficient for the tested growth requirement in this recipient. Failure to rescue is less decisive: the candidate <protein> might not be produced, might be altered by its attached tag, or might not work in this cellular context. The investigators therefore also used <Western blots> to examine <protein> production. A detected <protein> still does not establish correct <protein folding> or catalytic activity. <Pathways over Time: Functional Genomics Research in an Introductory Laboratory Course by Todd D. Reeves and others>[Reeves et al. (2018), Course Activities, pp. 5–6: functional conservation, tag interference and Western-blot follow-up]{ref}

= Model organism evidence transfer
{parent=Model organism}

Evidence transfers at a stated level. <Sequence alignment> can suggest a candidate relationship between <genes>; <functional complementation> tests whether a candidate can supply a particular function in a chosen recipient. Neither alone establishes identical native <gene expression>, identical <signal transduction>, or identical consequences in every <species>. The <yeast cross-species complementation experiment> is especially useful because it tests a conserved function by intervention rather than assuming it from resemblance. <Pathways over Time: Functional Genomics Research in an Introductory Laboratory Course by Todd D. Reeves and others>[Reeves et al. (2018), project design, pp. 3–4, and Course Activities, pp. 5–6]{ref}

\Image[model-organism-evidence-transfer-boundaries.png]
{title=What a <model organism> experiment establishes, and what needs another test}
{description=Original evidence map using the <yeast cross-species complementation experiment>. Solid arrows indicate an experimental question that can be addressed in the recipient; dashed arrows mark additional claims requiring evidence. This is a schematic of inference boundaries, not a <phylogenetic tree>, a measured pathway, or a claim about medical outcomes.}

Even successful <functional complementation> occurs within a specific <genetic background>, nutrient supply and imposed <gene expression>. The <enzyme> may work with the recipient’s available <substrates> and partner <proteins> at an experimentally imposed abundance. Establishing the same role in the donor <species> calls for experiments there; establishing behavior under another condition calls for experiments under that condition. A conserved component can therefore coexist with context-dependent behavior of an entire pathway. This separation makes <model organisms> powerful: an accessible experiment supplies a precise mechanistic foothold and a better question for the next system.

= Organism
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

An <organism> is an individual living system, made of one <biological cell> or an integrated assembly of <biological cells>. An <Escherichia coli> cell is an entire <organism>; a <neuron> is normally a specialized part of an <animal>. <Metabolism>, regulated exchange through <cell membranes>, and inherited information connect these very different scales. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-2-themes-and-concepts-of-biology[OpenStax Biology 2e, 1.2: living systems and levels of organization].

Keep two questions separate: how many <biological cells> make the <organism>, and how those <biological cells> are organized. A <yeast> and an <Escherichia coli> are both <unicellular organisms>, but the <yeast> is a <eukaryote> and the <Escherichia coli> a <prokaryote>. A <plant> and an <animal> are <multicellular organisms> with <eukaryotic> cellular organization. Neither distinction ranks <organisms> as better or worse: <natural selection> acts on reproduction in particular environments.

= Unicellular organism
{parent=Organism}
{wiki}

A <unicellular organism> is an <organism> whose living body consists of one <biological cell>. That <biological cell> maintains itself, grows and participates in <reproduction>; it is not merely one specialized component of a larger body. <Escherichia coli> and ordinary free-living <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> illustrate <prokaryotic> and <eukaryotic> examples respectively. Cellular organization and the number of <biological cells> are separate questions. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-1-studying-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 4.1: cells as units of organisms].

How can one <biological cell> do enough to sustain an <organism>? Its <cell membrane> retains useful material while <membrane transport> allows exchange; <metabolism> transforms nutrients and supplies usable <energy>; <gene expression> makes <proteins> needed to maintain and rebuild the machinery. These functions operate together inside the same boundary. In <Saccharomyces cerevisiae>, for example, <glucose> can support <alcoholic fermentation> and production of <ATP>, while the <cell cycle> and <budding> produce descendants. A single <biological cell> therefore combines chemical processing, regulated exchange and inherited organization. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26864/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell (2002): universal cellular machinery] and the experimental accounts under <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> and <yeast>.

\Image[unicellular-multicellular-organization.png]
{title=One <biological cell>, a <biological colony>, <clonal development> and <developmental integration>}
{description=Original conceptual schematic, not micrographs or an evolutionary ladder. Colors mark illustrative lineages in the middle panels and illustrative functions in the right panel; they are not measured traits. A <biological colony> can also be clonal, and <clonal development> need not produce specialized <biological cells>. The integrated panel illustrates a division of functions, not a particular <species>.}

Being a <unicellular organism> does not mean isolated or independent of all other life. <Unicellular organisms> can interact and cooperate in groups. A <biological colony> describes association; <clonal development> describes descent; <developmental integration> describes coordination of the developing whole. Neither shared ancestry nor a visible clump alone establishes the latter. This distinction makes single-cell experiments easier to interpret: measuring the growth of one <biological cell>, the expansion of a group and the <reproductive success> of a reproducing cluster can answer different questions. See https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2781869/[Queller and Strassmann, Beyond society: the evolution of organismality (2009): cooperation and the limits of simple organism criteria].

= Multicellular organism
{parent=Organism}
{wiki}

A <multicellular organism> has a body of multiple <biological cells> whose growth and activities are integrated into the life of the whole <organism>. Extensive <cell differentiation> is common in complex examples, but specialized <biological cells> are not a prerequisite for every simple form. Ask how the body develops, how its parts cooperate and how it produces descendants, rather than counting attached <biological cells> alone. The boundary with a <biological colony> depends on the degree of integration and the criteria being investigated; there is no universal cell-count threshold. See the Queller and Strassmann account cited under <unicellular organism>.

Specialization makes the whole capable of combinations that one general-purpose <biological cell> cannot achieve in the same way. In an <animal>, a <neuron> transmits signals while a <red blood cell> carries <oxygen>; each still requires a working <cell membrane> and <metabolism>. A mature mammalian <red blood cell> lacks a <cell nucleus> and <mitochondria>, illustrating how a useful part need not retain the equipment of a self-reproducing <organism>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/40-2-components-of-the-blood[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 40.2: red blood cell specialization]. <Cell differentiation> generally involves different <gene expression>, rather than a different inherited <genome> for each function. <Biological development> must coordinate growth, position, interactions and specialization: increasing the number of <biological cells> is only one component. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21046/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell, chapter 21 (2002): construction of multicellular bodies].

\b[A motivating experiment: select for a property of groups.] Ratcliff, Denison, Borrello and Travisano's 2012 study began with <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> growing as <unicellular organisms>. Daily transfer favored rapidly settling material. Their initial protocol allowed a $10\,\mathrm{mL}$ culture to settle for $45\,\mathrm{min}$, discarded the upper $9.9\,\mathrm{mL}$, and transferred the bottom $100\,\mathrm{\mu L}$ into fresh medium. After seven days, they instead sampled $1.5\,\mathrm{mL}$ and centrifuged it at $100$ times gravitational acceleration for $10\,\mathrm{s}$, again transferring the bottom $100\,\mathrm{\mu L}$. Faster settling made it more likely that a lineage would seed tomorrow's culture; the procedure selected a physical outcome without specifying how it must arise. These protocol details are explicitly recounted in https://academic.oup.com/evolut/article/73/5/1012/6727309[Gulli, Herron and Ratcliff, Evolution of altruistic cooperation among nascent multicellular organisms (2019): original settling protocol].

\Image[unicellular-multicellular-settling-selection.png]
{title=Settling selection and an explicitly stipulated enrichment example}
{description=Original qualitative transfer schematic with toy numerical bars. The workflow represents the initial settling protocol described above. The lower panel assumes equally weighted tracked units and transfer probabilities $0.8$ and $0.1$; these values and the resulting frequencies are invented for explanation, not fitted to <snowflake yeast> measurements. The equal-transfer comparison is a model control, distinct from the experimental comparisons described below.}

The primary 2012 paper reports <snowflake yeast> dominating all ten initial replicate populations by 60 transfers. To distinguish the joining of previously separate <biological cells> from <clonal development>, the researchers enzymatically separated clusters and followed individual <biological cells> for $16\,\mathrm{h}$: attached descendants rebuilt clusters, with no such joining observed. Staining localized attachments at bud scars. Time-lapse observations also showed clusters releasing smaller multicellular offspring, called propagules; small clusters grew before they reproduced. See https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3277146/[Ratcliff et al., Experimental evolution of multicellularity (2012), figures 1–3: emergence, development and reproduction].

The experimental comparisons ask different questions. Competition with the <unicellular organism> ancestor showed a reported $34\%$ advantage under settling selection. Without settling selection, the estimated $10\%$ disadvantage had a one-sided $P=0.06$, so it was suggestive rather than decisive evidence of a cost. Separately, three populations derived from one evolved isolate retained the cluster form through 35 transfers without settling selection. That persistence tests stability, not whether clusters always outperform single <biological cells>. See Ratcliff et al. (2012), figure 2 and the cluster-formation results cited above.

A 2015 follow-up added a causal test: deleting the <gene> ACE2 in the <unicellular organism> ancestor produced <snowflake yeast>, while supplying functional ACE2 to one evolved isolate restored separation into single <biological cells>. Sequence changes altering the encoded <protein> were found in ACE2 in five of ten examined lineages; the study did not identify that same sequence-change mechanism in every lineage. The deletion and restoration comparisons support a mechanism beyond the observation that clusters settle quickly. See https://www.nature.com/articles/ncomms7102[Ratcliff, Fankhauser, Rogers, Greig and Travisano, Origins of multicellular evolvability in snowflake yeast (2015), figure 1].

\b[What follows, and what does not.] The evidence supports inherited cluster formation, <clonal development> and a reproducing collective whose properties can respond to <natural selection>. Calling these simple <multicellular organisms> recognizes those demonstrated properties; it does not turn every clump into an <animal> or establish animal-like <cell differentiation>. This laboratory route also does not reconstruct the actual ancestry of <animals> or show that complex <developmental integration> must follow. The interesting achievement is narrower and testable: a change in how descendants remain connected creates a new scale at which growth and <reproductive success> can be measured.

For the toy figure, let $p$ be the fraction of tracked units of type C before transfer and let $s_C,s_U$ be the respective <probabilities> of transfer for C and U. Assume equally weighted units and equal multiplication between transfers. When overall transfer <probability> is nonzero, conditioning on transfer gives
$$
p_{\mathrm{after}}=\frac{p s_C}{p s_C+(1-p)s_U}.
$$
With $p=1/5$, $s_C=4/5$ and $s_U=1/10$, the numerator is $4/25$ and the denominator $6/25$, giving $p_{\mathrm{after}}=2/3$. If both types have the same nonzero transfer <probability>, the fraction stays $1/5$. This is <conditional probability>, not a model of the full experimental life cycle: real cluster sizes, growth and fragmentation affect which quantity a frequency measures. In particular, a fraction of clusters is not automatically a fraction of <biological cells>.

= Clonal development
{parent=Multicellular organism}

<Clonal development> builds a group through successive divisions of founding <biological cells>, rather than by gathering previously separate lineages. In <snowflake yeast>, daughter <biological cells> remain attached after <budding>. Common ancestry does not imply that every descendant remains genetically identical forever: new <mutations> can arise. See the 2015 study cited under <multicellular organism>.

= Snowflake yeast
{parent=Clonal development}

<Snowflake yeast> are cluster-forming <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> with branching connections between mothers and attached daughter <biological cells>. They provide an experimental system for studying early transitions to simple <multicellular organisms>; see the protocol, observations and limits under <multicellular organism>.

= Developmental integration
{parent=Multicellular organism}

<Developmental integration> here means coordination of cellular growth, organization and function into a developing whole. It describes what the parts do together, whereas <clonal development> describes how they descend from founders. Interactions through <signal transduction> can help coordinate <cell differentiation>; see the Alberts et al. development chapter cited under <multicellular organism>.

= Cellular differentiation
{parent=Developmental integration}
{wiki}

= Cell differentiation
{synonym}

<Cell differentiation> is the acquisition of a specialized <cell state>, involving changes in structure, function and <gene expression>. A <neuron> and a <red blood cell> illustrate different outcomes: signaling and <oxygen> transport. Different functions usually do not require a different inherited <DNA> sequence for each function. The shared-<genome> principle concerns nucleated precursors and most nucleated descendants: a mature mammalian <red blood cell> has lost its <cell nucleus> and <mitochondria>, so it is not an example of two mature <biological cells> both retaining the same nuclear <DNA>. <Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others>[Biology 2e, §40.2, Red Blood Cells: specialization and absence of a nucleus and mitochondria at mammalian maturity]{ref}

The puzzle is how a shared set of <genes> supports different jobs. <Gene expression> changes which <RNA> and <proteins> a <biological cell> produces, and in what amounts. A <transcription factor> can regulate several <genes>; different combinations of regulators can interpret the same signal differently. Stable regulatory patterns can persist through the <cell cycle>, providing memory of developmental history. Specialization therefore usually changes use of inherited information, rather than replacing the <genome> wholesale. <Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others>[Alberts et al., The Molecular Genetic Mechanisms That Create Specialized Cell Types, subsections The Expression of a Set of Genes Can Be Coordinated by a Single Protein and Stable Patterns of Gene Expression Can Be Transmitted to Daughter Cells, and Summary]{ref}

\Image[cell-differentiation-shared-genome-expression.png]
{title=Shared <genes>, different <gene expression> and specialized <cell states>}
{description=Original schematic with three invented <genes> H, A and B. Both nucleated <biological cells> retain all three <genes>; colored outputs indicate different illustrative expression patterns. These are not measured <RNA> or <protein> amounts, named real <genes>, or a claim that real <gene expression> is simply on or off. The separate mammalian <red blood cell> note marks an important limit to the shared nuclear <DNA> comparison.}

A small counting example separates sequence from activity. Suppose the same three toy <genes> are present in every nucleated <biological cell>, and independently assign each <gene> an on/off state. There are $2^3=8$ possible expression profiles with no <DNA> replacement. The figure shows only two: H and A on versus H and B on. Real <gene expression> is graded and coordinated, so this count illustrates possible descriptions, not a prediction of eight actual cell types. The interesting consequence is that a stable <cell state> can depend on how a shared record is used.

<Stem cells> combine self-renewal with production of differentiated descendants; <progenitor cells> generally have more restricted developmental options and renewal capacity. Neither term means that a <biological cell> must give rise to every cell type. <Terminal differentiation> denotes advanced specialization within a lineage, often accompanied by exit from the <cell cycle>. <Cell fate> concerns the developmental outcome of a <biological cell> or its descendants, whereas <cell state> describes its current properties. A temporary change in <gene expression> after <signal transduction> is not, by itself, proof of a new <cell fate>. <Anatomy and Physiology 2e by J. Gordon Betts and others>[Anatomy and Physiology 2e, §3.6, Stem Cells and Differentiation: renewal, restricted differentiation potential and regulation of gene expression]{ref}

= Cell fate
{parent=Cellular differentiation}

<Cell fate> is the developmental outcome of a <biological cell> or its descendants in a specified setting. What it normally becomes and what it can become after an experimental intervention are different questions. The <intestinal nucleus transfer experiment> tests potential after <cellular reprogramming>, rather than directly tracing the normal <cell fate> of the donor.

= Terminal differentiation
{parent=Cellular differentiation}

<Terminal differentiation> is advanced specialization at the end of a developmental lineage. It often includes withdrawal from the <cell cycle>; the term does not mean that every nuclear <gene> needed for another <cell fate> has been deleted. A mature mammalian <red blood cell> is an especially restrictive example because it lacks a <cell nucleus>; the <intestinal nucleus transfer experiment> used nucleated donors.

= Cellular reprogramming
{parent=Cellular differentiation}

<Cellular reprogramming> changes an established <cell state> so that a different developmental program becomes accessible. Experimentally induced <cellular reprogramming> must be distinguished from the ordinary progression of <cell differentiation>. An <ovum>'s <cytoplasm> can reset aspects of a transferred <cell nucleus>; this does not mean that a specialized donor normally returns to an early developmental state in its original surroundings. <Mature cells can be reprogrammed to become pluripotent by Nobel Assembly at Karolinska Institutet>[Nobel Assembly, Mature cells can be reprogrammed to become pluripotent, Scientific background: Gurdon nuclear transfer and distinction between differentiation and reprogramming]{ref}

= Somatic cell nuclear transfer
{parent=Cellular reprogramming}
{wiki}

<Somatic cell nuclear transfer> introduces a body-cell <cell nucleus> into an <ovum> whose own nuclear contribution has been removed or inactivated. The donor nuclear <genome> and recipient <cytoplasm> have different origins; successful development does not show that the donor <cell nucleus> acts independently of the recipient's machinery.

= Intestinal nucleus transfer experiment
{parent=Somatic cell nuclear transfer}

\b[Can a specialized donor still supply information for other cell types?] The 1962 https://doi.org/10.1242/dev.10.4.622[The Developmental Capacity of Nuclei taken from Intestinal Epithelium Cells of Feeding Tadpoles by J. B. Gurdon] used mid-intestinal cells of feeding <Xenopus laevis> tadpoles. Donors were recognized by columnar shape, basal <cell nuclei> and a striated absorptive border. Dissociation used Versene solution; this made individual donors accessible. The readout was subsequent development, including normal feeding tadpoles, rather than just division or survival. <The Developmental Capacity of Nuclei taken from Intestinal Epithelium Cells of Feeding Tadpoles by J. B. Gurdon>[Gurdon (1962), Material and Methods and Normal tadpoles; inspected primary text reproduced in the indexed Paperity preview]{ref}

\b[Apparatus and intervention.] A microscope with <ultraviolet> illumination directed light at the surface region containing the unfertilized <ovum>'s <chromosomes>, inactivating the recipient nuclear contribution. Irradiation also softened the surrounding jelly so a glass micropipette could enter. The pipette delivered donor nuclear material into recipient <cytoplasm>. In the technique's validation, fertilizing irradiated eggs produced <haploid> embryos, supporting destruction of the maternal nuclear contribution. This is inactivation, not a diagram of surgically removing an intact <cell nucleus>. <The Egg and the Nucleus: A Battle for Supremacy by J. B. Gurdon>[Gurdon, The Egg and the Nucleus: A Battle for Supremacy, The Technique of Nuclear Transfer in Xenopus; indexed authored Nobel lecture text inspected]{ref}

\Image[frog-intestinal-nucleus-transfer-controls.png]
{title=<Somatic cell nuclear transfer>: intervention, developmental readout and distinct controls}
{description=Original apparatus-and-inference schematic, not a tracing of an experimental photograph. The <ultraviolet> beam indicates inactivation of recipient nuclear material; the injection pipette supplies a donor <cell nucleus>. Donor nuclear markers test origin; interspersed embryonic-donor transfers test recipient quality. Successful development is a possible outcome, not an asserted success rate. The figure omits exact dimensions, light dose and many technical steps.}

\b[Controls ask different questions.] Donor <cell nuclei> carried a marker absent from recipients; post-<blastula> embryos carried that marker. The marker distinguished diploid donors with one <nucleolus> per <cell nucleus> from the usual two-<nucleolus> recipient background, rather than mistaking one <nucleolus> for proof of a <haploid> state. <The Egg and the Nucleus: A Battle for Supremacy by J. B. Gurdon>[Gurdon, Nobel lecture, The Technique of Nuclear Transfer in Xenopus, p. 233: one-nucleolus diploid marker and its use to establish donor nuclear origin]{ref} Interspersed transfers of <blastula> or later embryonic <cell nuclei> checked variable recipient quality. A positive embryonic-donor control thus tests the preparation, while the marker tests donor origin. Abnormal development remained common. Neither control establishes universal transfer competence. <The Developmental Capacity of Nuclei taken from Intestinal Epithelium Cells of Feeding Tadpoles by J. B. Gurdon>[Gurdon (1962), Material and Methods (nuclear marker), Results (interspersed embryonic controls), and Normal tadpoles (abnormal outcomes); primary text inspected through the indexed preview]{ref}

\b[Inference and limits.] A successful marked donor demonstrates that specialization had not irreversibly removed all information required to build other cell types. Failure is less decisive: transfer damage, recipient condition and incomplete <cellular reprogramming> can also prevent development. The marker identifies nuclear origin; it is not a complete <DNA> sequence comparison. The experiment tests donor nuclear potential in an altered <cytoplasm>, rather than normal donor <cell fate>, a universal capacity of every donor, or development from a mature mammalian <red blood cell>. The Nobel account also describes serial transfers through embryonic intermediates; these must not be silently combined with direct transfers into one success rate. <Mature cells can be reprogrammed to become pluripotent by Nobel Assembly at Karolinska Institutet>[Nobel Assembly, Scientific background, Gurdon's nuclear-transfer experiments and serial transplantation]{ref}

= Colony
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Organism}
{wiki}

= Biological colony
{synonym}

A <biological colony> is an associated group of <organisms> or <biological cells>. The name alone specifies neither <cell differentiation> nor a particular mode of <biological development>. A colony can arise from related descendants or from previously separate members; see <clonal development> and the Queller and Strassmann discussion cited under <unicellular organism>.

= Prokaryote
{parent=Organism}
{wiki}

= Prokaryotic
{synonym}

A <prokaryote> is an <organism> whose <biological cells> lack a membrane-bounded <cell nucleus>. The <DNA> of its <chromosomes> occupies a <nucleoid> within the <cytoplasm>. <Bacteria> and <archaea> have this cellular organization. Both contain <ribosomes> that perform <biological translation> and a selective <cell membrane>; neither is a bag of unorganized chemicals. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells[OpenStax Biology 2e, 4.2: prokaryotic organization].

The word <prokaryotic> describes organization, not a single exclusive <clade>. Grouping <bacteria> and <archaea> by the absence of a <cell nucleus> is useful for comparing <biological cells>, but does not establish that they share a more recent ancestor with one another than either does with <eukaryotes>. See <three-domain system> and <two-domain system> for the different ancestry models.

<Prokaryotic> does not mean membrane-free. <Cyanobacteria> contain internal <thylakoid> membranes for <oxygenic photosynthesis>, despite lacking <chloroplasts> and a <cell nucleus>. Many <prokaryotes> have circular <chromosomes>, but circular shape and a single <chromosome> are not defining requirements. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/22-2-structure-of-prokaryotes-bacteria-and-archaea[OpenStax Biology 2e, 22.2: bacterial and archaeal structures].

\Image[prokaryote-eukaryote-cell-organization.png]
{title=Cell organization: an <Escherichia coli> example beside a nucleated <animal> cell}
{description=Original schematic cross-sections, not micrographs and not at exact relative scale. Both show a <cell membrane>, <cytoplasm>, <DNA> and <ribosomes>. The <Escherichia coli> envelope is simplified: its outer membrane is omitted. The <animal> cell additionally shows a <nuclear envelope> and <mitochondria>; many other structures are omitted. The outlined <nucleoid> is a region, not a membrane.}

= Bacteria
{parent=Prokaryote}
{wiki}

<Bacteria> are a major evolutionary lineage of <organisms> with <prokaryotic> cellular organization. <Escherichia coli> and <cyanobacteria> illustrate its variety: the same broad cellular plan supports different kinds of <metabolism>. Most <bacteria> have a <cell wall> containing <peptidoglycan>; <archaea> do not have <peptidoglycan> walls. A <cell wall> and a <cell membrane> are distinct structures, and some <bacteria> lack a <cell wall>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/22-2-structure-of-prokaryotes-bacteria-and-archaea[OpenStax Biology 2e, 22.2: bacterial envelopes].

The usefulness of recognizing <bacteria> extends beyond naming: it identifies a shared evolutionary background for comparing <genes>, <proteins> and cellular mechanisms. A familiar shape or the absence of a <cell nucleus> alone cannot distinguish <bacteria> from <archaea>; molecular comparisons, including <ribosomal RNA>, supply additional evidence.

= Alphaproteobacteria
{c}
{parent=Bacteria}
{wiki}

<Alphaproteobacteria> are a bacterial group implicated in the ancestry of <mitochondria>. This relationship concerns <common descent>, not descent from a named modern member of <Alphaproteobacteria>.

= Escherichia coli
{c}
{parent=Bacteria}
{wiki}

= E. coli
{c}
{synonym}

<Escherichia coli>, often abbreviated <E. coli>, is a bacterial <species> widely used to study <DNA replication> and <gene expression>. Its <chromosome> is in a <nucleoid>, and its <ribosomes> work in the <cytoplasm>, outside any <cell nucleus>. A result from one laboratory strain should not automatically be generalized to every member of the <species>.

The original 1997 report by Blattner and colleagues presented the K-12 MG1655 <genome> sequence as a circular <chromosome> of $4{,}639{,}221$ <base pairs>. See https://www.genome.wisc.edu/sequencing/U00096.1.htm[Blattner laboratory: original K-12 sequence and 1997 paper]. As a bookkeeping example, one complete round of <DNA replication> from one such <chromosome> produces two daughter sequence copies totaling $2\times4{,}639{,}221=9{,}278{,}442$ <base pairs>. This is twice the copied material, not twice the length of the reference <genome>; it does not specify the instantaneous <DNA> content of a rapidly growing <E. coli> cell.

= Cyanobacteria
{parent=Bacteria}
{wiki}

<Cyanobacteria> are <bacteria> that perform <oxygenic photosynthesis>: <light-dependent reactions> release <oxygen> from <water>. Their photosynthetic machinery operates on <thylakoid> membranes without a <chloroplast> or a <cell nucleus>. This is a particularly useful counterexample to equating <photosynthesis> with being a <plant>. <Chloroplasts> descend from an ancestral cyanobacterial partner through <endosymbiosis>; present-day <cyanobacteria> are independent descendants, rather than <chloroplasts> waiting to become <plants>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26819/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: chloroplasts and cyanobacteria].

= Gram-positive bacteria
{c}
{parent=Bacteria}
{wiki}

Typical <Gram-positive bacteria> have a relatively thick <peptidoglycan> layer outside the <cell membrane>, without the <bacterial outer membrane> of typical <Gram-negative bacteria>. This is a representative <cell envelope> pattern, not a complete description of every member of <bacteria>.

= Gram-negative bacteria
{c}
{parent=Bacteria}
{wiki}

Typical <Gram-negative bacteria> have a relatively thin <peptidoglycan> layer between the <cell membrane> and a <bacterial outer membrane>.

= Bacillus megaterium
{c}
{parent=Bacteria}

<Bacillus megaterium> is the historical species name used for strain KM in the <lysozyme wall-removal experiment>.

= Archaea
{parent=Prokaryote}
{wiki}

<Archaea> are a major evolutionary lineage distinct from <bacteria>. The cellular comparison here concerns the <prokaryotic> members of <archaea>; see <two-domain system> for broader <clade> usage. Their <biological cells> have a <nucleoid> rather than a membrane-bounded <cell nucleus>, yet aspects of <DNA replication>, <transcription> and <biological translation> resemble those of <eukaryotes>. Their characteristic <lipids> differ chemically from the usual bacterial membrane <lipids>, and their <cell walls>, when present, lack <peptidoglycan>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/22-2-structure-of-prokaryotes-bacteria-and-archaea[OpenStax Biology 2e, 22.2: archaeal distinctions].

Some <archaea> perform <methanogenesis>, producing <methane> as part of their <metabolism>. <Archaea> also inhabit ordinary soils and oceans: extreme habitats do not define the lineage. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/22-1-prokaryotic-diversity[OpenStax Biology 2e, 22.1: moderate and extreme environments]. Woese and Fox's 1977 analysis of <ribosomal RNA> distinguished the methanogenic group they called archaebacteria from typical <bacteria>. Their proposed three primary lines of descent were a historical interpretation, rather than the final resolution of deep <phylogenetics>. See https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.74.11.5088[Woese and Fox, 1977: Phylogenetic structure of the prokaryotic domain].

= Asgard archaea
{c}
{parent=Archaea}
{wiki}

<Asgard archaea> are an archaeal group important in studies of <eukaryotic> origins. Molecular comparisons connect their <genes> to the nuclear lineage of <eukaryotes>, rather than making modern <Asgard archaea> the direct ancestors of modern <animals> or <plants>. See the original analysis cited under <two-domain system>.

= Eukaryote
{parent=Organism}
{wiki}

= Eukaryotic
{synonym}

A <eukaryote> is an <organism> belonging to the lineage characterized by a membrane-bounded <cell nucleus> and extensive cellular compartmentalization. A <nuclear envelope> surrounds the nuclear <genome>; nuclear <chromosomes> are typically linear. <Ribosomes> perform <biological translation> outside the <cell nucleus>. See https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells[OpenStax Microbiology, 3.4: nuclear organization and protein synthesis]. Specialized <biological cells> can lose their <cell nucleus>, as mature mammalian <red blood cells> do, while remaining parts of <eukaryotic> <organisms>; see https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/40-2-components-of-the-blood[OpenStax Biology 2e, 40.2: mature red blood cells].

<Yeasts>, <plants> and <animals> are all <eukaryotes>. Typical cells in these groups have <mitochondria>, but <chloroplasts> belong to photosynthetic <plant> and <algae> lineages, not to every <eukaryote>. A <cell wall> occurs in typical <plant> and <fungus> cells but is absent from <animal> cells. These are comparisons of representative cells, not a checklist every specialized cell must satisfy. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells[OpenStax Biology 2e, 4.3: nucleus, organelles and representative cells].

The interesting feature is controlled separation: <cell membranes> and <organelle> membranes let different cellular processes occupy distinct compartments while <membrane transport> connects them. The <evolution> of <eukaryotes> also involved partnership: <mitochondria> descend from a bacterial <endosymbiosis>. Modern <phylogenetics> supports an archaeal ancestry for the nuclear lineage, alongside this bacterial contribution; see <two-domain system>. Thus the history of a whole <eukaryote> cannot be read as the history of just one of its <genes>.

\Image[cell-lineages-and-shared-machinery.png]
{title=Shared cellular machinery and distinguishing organization across <bacteria>, <archaea> and <eukaryotes>}
{description=Original comparison table, not a <phylogenetic tree>. The shared functions do not require identical <molecules>. Entries describe representative cellular organization and explicitly qualify exceptions; column positions imply neither ancestry nor evolutionary rank.}

= Fungus
{parent=Eukaryote}
{wiki}

A <fungus> is a <eukaryote> that obtains nutrients by absorption rather than <photosynthesis>. <Fungi> include unicellular <yeasts> and multicellular forms. Their <cell walls> differ from bacterial <peptidoglycan> walls; having a <cell wall> does not make a <fungus> a <plant>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/24-1-characteristics-of-fungi[OpenStax Biology 2e, 24.1: fungal nutrition and organization].

= Yeast
{parent=Fungus}
{wiki}

<Yeasts> are <fungi> whose growth is predominantly unicellular, commonly through <budding> or division into two cells. The word describes a growth form across different fungal lineages, rather than one <species> or an exclusive <clade>; some <fungi> can switch between unicellular and filamentous growth. Thus <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> is a <yeast>, but <yeast> is not another name for that one <species>. See https://booksite.elsevier.com/samplechapters/9780444521491/9780444521491.pdf[Kurtzman, Fell and Boekhout, The Yeasts, chapter 1 (2011): definition and classification].

A <yeast> cell has <eukaryotic> organization, including a <cell nucleus>, <mitochondria> and a <cell wall>. Small size and a unicellular lifestyle do not make it a member of <bacteria>. These compartments let an experimentally accessible <biological cell> retain much of the organization shared with other <eukaryotes>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/24-1-characteristics-of-fungi[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 24.1: fungal cells and yeast growth].

\b[A motivating experiment: can <fermentation> continue without intact <yeast> cells?] In 1897, Eduard Buchner ground brewers' <yeast> with quartz sand and diatomite, added <water>, and pressed out a liquid extract. Adding concentrated cane <sugar> produced <carbon dioxide> after 15–60 minutes. A cell-retaining filter still yielded an active filtrate: with sterilized cane <sugar> in an icebox, <fermentation> began after about a day. Saturated lactose gave no observed <fermentation>. These are reported observations, not invented control results. See https://web.lemoyne.edu/~giunta/buchner.html[Buchner, Alcoholic fermentation without yeast cells (1897), translated excerpt hosted by Carmen Giunta].

\Image[yeast-cellfree-fermentation-evidence.png]
{title=Removing intact <yeast> cells while retaining <fermentation> activity}
{description=Original qualitative evidence schematic based on Buchner's report and Harden's later summary cited here. No measured rates are plotted; the preparations had different conditions and are not a matched quantitative comparison.}

The filtration comparison addresses the objection that surviving intact <yeast> cells caused the activity. The lactose comparison tests <substrate> dependence; it is not a no-<sugar> blank, nor a comparison at matched <sugar> <concentrations>. A proposed modern follow-up would also compare the same extract with and without added <sugar>, accounting for material already in the extract. That is an experimental suggestion, not a result attributed to Buchner. His report specifies brewers' <yeast>; it does not identify the preparation here as a pure <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> strain.

Heat treatment asks a different question: does destroying activity in the extract stop <fermentation>? Buchner's preliminary report described weak or absent activity after heating and filtration, rather than one clean universal threshold. Harden's 1914 account of the subsequent work summarized loss of activity on heating the juice to $50\,{}^\circ\mathrm{C}$. Harden also described requirements for both <enzymes> and <coenzymes>. Cell-free therefore means neither chemical-free nor a preparation containing one purified <enzyme>. See https://www.gutenberg.org/files/44985/44985-h/44985-h.htm[Arthur Harden, Alcoholic Fermentation (1914), chapters II and VI: cell-free activity and the fermenting complex].

The inference is that extracted cellular machinery can sustain these <chemical reactions> without an intact living <biological cell>. This does not show that the extract reproduces, grows or maintains itself like a <yeast>. Moreover, gas evolution alone does not identify <ethanol>: <cellular respiration> can also release <carbon dioxide>. Product identification is a separate measurement; Harden's account describes both <ethanol> and <carbon dioxide> as products of cell-free <alcoholic fermentation>. The beautiful separation is between the <organism> that produces and maintains the machinery and the machinery that catalyzes a particular transformation.

= Schizosaccharomyces pombe
{c}
{parent=Yeast}
{wiki}

= S. pombe
{c}
{synonym}

<Schizosaccharomyces pombe> is a <yeast> <species> used as a <model organism>. It supplies the candidate <genes> in the <yeast cross-species complementation experiment>; the recipient remains <Saccharomyces cerevisiae>.

= Saccharomyces cerevisiae
{c}
{parent=Yeast}
{wiki}

= S. cerevisiae
{c}
{synonym}

<Saccharomyces cerevisiae> is a particular <species> of <yeast>, used in baking, brewing and laboratory research. During ordinary vegetative growth it reproduces by <budding>: a smaller daughter grows from the mother before separation. Laboratory strains can be maintained as either <haploid> or <diploid> <biological cells>. This makes it possible to investigate <genes> in a single <eukaryote>, then use controlled crosses and <meiosis> to connect a <genotype> to a <phenotype>. See https://smburgess.faculty.ucdavis.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/33/2019/08/Burgess-Powers-Mell-eLS.pdf[Burgess, Powers and Mell, Budding Yeast Saccharomyces cerevisiae as a Model Genetic Organism (2017), pp. 1–2].

The practical value of <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> as a <model organism> is the combination of easy laboratory cultivation, accessible <genetics>, and cellular functions shared with other <eukaryotes>. A <haploid> strain has one set of <chromosomes>, so a changed <allele> is not accompanied by a second copy at that <genetic locus>. But a <mutation> need not produce an observable <phenotype> under every condition. The <yeast> is also a whole <organism>, not a miniature human: transferring a result requires evidence that the relevant function is shared in the target system. See https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4012490/[Duina, Miller and Keeney, Budding Yeast for Budding Geneticists (2014): cultivation, genetic tools and conserved functions].

For a concrete example, Hartwell, Culotti and Reid's 1970 work followed temperature-sensitive <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> strains with successive microscope photographs. Failure to complete a particular part of the <cell cycle> helped identify required <gene> functions. Their distinction between when a function is required and where a cell finally stops is especially instructive: the visible endpoint need not be the time of the underlying defect. See https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/5271168/[Hartwell, Culotti and Reid, Genetic control of the cell-division cycle in yeast. I. Detection of mutants (1970): primary abstract]. This is a useful way to turn an observable change in a <biological cell> into a question about <genes> and causal order.

In <alcoholic fermentation>, <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> converts <sugars> into <ethanol> and <carbon dioxide>. The <carbon dioxide> helps dough rise; the <ethanol> is useful in alcoholic drinks. The <metabolism> also supports production of <ATP>, a usable cellular <energy> carrier. This is one metabolic ability of one <species>, not the definition of every <yeast>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-5-metabolism-without-oxygen[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 7.5: alcohol fermentation] and the Burgess, Powers and Mell account above.

For <glucose>, the ideal net <stoichiometry> is
$$
\mathrm{C}_6\mathrm{H}_{12}\mathrm{O}_6\longrightarrow 2\mathrm{C}_2\mathrm{H}_5\mathrm{OH}+2\mathrm{CO}_2.
$$
One <molecule> of <glucose> supplies six <carbon> <chemical atoms>: four end up in the two <ethanol> <molecules> and two in the two <carbon dioxide> <molecules>. The twelve <hydrogen> and six <oxygen> <chemical atoms> also balance. This is a net material balance across several <enzyme>-catalyzed steps, not a single <chemical reaction> step or the complete bookkeeping for cellular <energy> carriers. The standard pathway accounting yields a net two <ATP> per <glucose>: the initial breakdown produces the <ATP>, while the later conversion to <ethanol> regenerates a <coenzyme> needed to keep the breakdown going. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-2-glycolysis[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 7.2: net ATP accounting] and the section 7.5 cited above.

\Image[saccharomyces-fermentation-carbon-balance.png]
{title=Ideal <alcoholic fermentation>: <glucose> <carbon> divides between <ethanol> and <carbon dioxide>}
{description=Original schematic and theoretical toy calculation, not measured culture data. The dots count <carbon> <chemical atoms>; they do not depict molecular bonds or traced individual atoms. Starting with $10\,\mathrm{mmol}$ of <glucose>, conversion of $x\,\mathrm{mmol}$ leaves $10-x$ and forms $2x$ each of <ethanol> and <carbon dioxide>. The coincident product lines count <molecules>, not equal masses.}

At complete conversion, this toy gives $20\,\mathrm{mmol}$ of <ethanol> and $20\,\mathrm{mmol}$ of <carbon dioxide>. The <carbon> balance is $6\times10=2\times20+1\times20=60\,\mathrm{mmol}$ of <carbon> <chemical atoms>. A real culture also builds cellular material, can use other metabolic routes, and may retain dissolved <carbon dioxide>; the toy is a reference balance rather than a prediction of collected gas or beverage yield.

Although <alcoholic fermentation> does not require <molecular oxygen> as a reactant, the presence of <molecular oxygen> does not force <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> to stop fermenting. De Deken's 1966 experiments reported predominantly fermentative <glucose> use during exponential growth in air. Consequently, bubbles do not establish either absence of <molecular oxygen> or an exclusively fermentative <metabolism>. See https://www.microbiologyresearch.org/content/journal/micro/10.1099/00221287-44-2-149[De Deken, The Crabtree Effect: A Regulatory System in Yeast (1966): primary abstract].

= Plant
{parent=Eukaryote}
{wiki}

<Plants> are <eukaryotes>. A typical photosynthetic leaf cell has a <cell nucleus>, <mitochondria>, <chloroplasts>, and a <cell wall> outside its <cell membrane>. A root cell need not have <chloroplasts>; the <plant>'s parts specialize for different functions. <Plants> perform <cellular respiration> as well as <photosynthesis>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells[OpenStax Biology 2e, 4.3: plant cell organization].

= Pea
{parent=Plant}

= Flower
{parent=Plant}

= Stamen
{parent=Flower}

= Stigma
{disambiguate=botany}
{parent=Flower}

= Seed
{parent=Plant}

= Algae
{parent=Eukaryote}
{wiki}

<Algae> is a broad name for several photosynthetic <eukaryotic> groups, rather than one exclusive <clade>. The older name blue-green algae for <cyanobacteria> is misleading in a comparison of cellular organization: <cyanobacteria> are <bacteria>, with neither <chloroplasts> nor a <cell nucleus>.

= Reclinomonas americana
{c}
{parent=Eukaryote}

<Reclinomonas americana> is a microbial <eukaryote> whose mitochondrial <genome> retains an unusually rich set of bacterial features. The 1997 sequence report provides a concrete observation under <evidence for bacterial ancestry of organelles>.

= Animal
{parent=Eukaryote}
{wiki}

<Animals> are <multicellular organisms> with <eukaryotic> cellular organization. A representative <animal> cell has a <cell membrane>, <cell nucleus> and <mitochondria>, but no <cell wall> or <chloroplasts>. A <neuron> illustrates how this common plan can acquire a specialized shape and signaling function. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells[OpenStax Biology 2e, 4.3: animal cell organization].

= Insect
{parent=Animal}

= Drosophila pseudoobscura
{c}
{parent=Insect}
{wiki}

<Drosophila pseudoobscura> is the <species> of <insect> used in the <dietary divergence and mating isolation in Drosophila pseudoobscura> experiment. The independently maintained experimental <biological populations> were not already different <species>.

= Amphibian
{parent=Animal}
{wiki}

= Frog
{parent=Amphibian}
{wiki}

= Xenopus laevis
{c}
{parent=Frog}
{wiki}

<Xenopus laevis> is a <frog> used in <developmental biology>, including the <intestinal nucleus transfer experiment> and <Xenopus laevis egg extract> preparations.

= Fish
{parent=Animal}
{wiki}

<Fish> are aquatic <animals>; their feeding roles depend on their diet and life stage. Small and large <fish> in the illustrative <food web> are generic groups chosen to distinguish two feeding positions, rather than a measured relationship between named <species>.

= Taxonomy
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= Taxonomy
{synonym}

<Taxonomy> names and classifies <organisms>. Classification can organize known diversity and make comparisons useful, but ranks and names are not themselves evidence of ancestry. <Phylogenetics> infers that ancestry; a <clade> includes an ancestor and all its descendants. A shared feature such as lacking a <cell nucleus> need not identify an exclusive <clade>. The <organism> branch here organizes cell types for reading and is not a proposed <phylogenetic tree>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/20-1-organizing-life-on-earth[OpenStax Biology 2e, 20.1: classification and evolutionary history].

= Domain
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Taxonomy (biology)}
{wiki}

A biological <domain (biology)> is a high-level rank used to classify <organisms> in the <three-domain system>. Distinguish a taxonomic rank from a primary branch in an inferred <phylogenetic tree>: retaining familiar names does not settle the branching relationships.

= Three-domain system
{parent=Domain (biology)}
{wiki}

The <three-domain system> recognizes <bacteria>, <archaea> and <eukaryotes> as three domains. In 1990, Woese, Kandler and Wheelis proposed the domain rank and the names Bacteria, Archaea and Eucarya, using molecular comparisons to argue that the older <prokaryote>/<eukaryote> division hid important differences. See https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2112744/[Woese, Kandler and Wheelis, 1990: Towards a natural system of organisms].

The associated three-domain ancestry model places the nuclear lineage of <eukaryotes> alongside <archaea>, rather than within them. This historically influential interpretation should be distinguished from the <two-domain system>; listing three familiar groups does not establish three independent primary branches.

= Two-domain system
{parent=Domain (biology)}
{wiki}

The <two-domain system> recognizes <bacteria> and <archaea> as the two primary lineages, placing the nuclear ancestry of <eukaryotes> within <archaea>. In this model, <archaea> as a complete <clade> includes that <eukaryotic> lineage; restricting the name to <prokaryotic> members describes a different grouping. Williams and colleagues' study, published online in 2019 and in a 2020 journal issue, found support for this model and a close relationship to <Asgard archaea>. See https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-019-1040-x[Williams et al., 2020: Phylogenomics provides robust support for a two-domains tree of life].

This updates the interpretation of the 1977 and 1990 classifications rather than undoing their discovery of deep differences between <bacteria> and <archaea>. Deep <phylogenetic trees> depend on the sampled <genes>, <organisms> and inference model; the precise closest archaeal relatives and early sequence of events require further evidence. Bacterial ancestry of <mitochondria> through <endosymbiosis> is a separate part of <eukaryotic> history.

= Species
{parent=Taxonomy (biology)}
{wiki}

A <species> is a named unit of biological classification. The useful criteria vary among groups: reproductive compatibility can inform classification in many <animals>, while microbial comparisons also use <genomes> and other characters. <Escherichia coli> is a <species>; K-12 MG1655 identifies a particular strain within it. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/18-2-formation-of-new-species[OpenStax Biology 2e, 18.2: species concepts].

= Species concept
{parent=Species}

A <species concept> states what should make <organisms> members of the same <species>; an operational criterion states what observations will test that claim. <Reproductive isolation>, distinguishable <morphology> and ancestry inferred through <phylogenetics> answer different questions. In a lineage-based interpretation, they are evidence for separation of evolving <biological populations>, and need not appear together. <Species Concepts and Species Delimitation by Kevin de Queiroz>[pp. 880–883, Table 1, Figure 1 and “Conceptualization versus Delimitation”: distinguish defining properties from operational evidence]{ref}

= Biological species concept
{parent=Species concept}

The <biological species concept> groups naturally interbreeding <biological populations> with viable, fertile offspring, distinguished from other such groups by <reproductive isolation>. An operational test examines mating, <fertilization>, offspring survival and offspring <reproduction>, rather than only whether two adults can be paired. <Sexual reproduction> is its scope: <fossils> and <organisms> studied only through <asexual reproduction> cannot supply the same mating evidence. <Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others>[§18.2, “Species and the Ability to Reproduce”, “Speciation” and “Reproductive Isolation”]{ref}

For an original hypothetical example, imagine two separated <biological populations> that reproduce successfully after contact. The separation alone did not demonstrate <reproductive isolation>. Conversely, failure to mate in one unfamiliar chamber could reflect the assay conditions. Field observations of <gene flow> and tests under suitable conditions address the biological question more directly. A successful laboratory pairing does not establish frequent interbreeding in nature.

= Morphological species concept
{parent=Species concept}

A <morphological species concept> distinguishes <species> using <morphology>: measurable differences in form or structure. It can be applied to preserved specimens and <fossils> when mating cannot be observed. Similar appearance need not imply the same <species>, and different appearance need not imply different <species>. <Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others>[§18.2, “Species and the Ability to Reproduce”, paragraphs accompanying Figures 18.9–18.10: appearance can mislead]{ref}

An original operational example is to compare a chosen structure in specimens of comparable age, sex and rearing conditions. Suppose two apparent groups differ in size, but one group contains only juveniles. The observed difference does not yet diagnose two <species>. Specify the measured <phenotype>, variation within each sample, and whether the distinction persists across comparable specimens. A single striking specimen is a poor substitute for that comparison.

= Phylogenetic species concept
{parent=Species concept}

<Phylogenetic species concepts> use ancestry and diagnostic inherited differences; versions differ over requiring a <clade>, an exclusive ancestry pattern at <genetic loci>, or a distinguishable character combination. State which criterion is intended. <Species Concepts and Species Delimitation by Kevin de Queiroz>[p. 880, Table 1: distinct monophyletic, genealogical and diagnosable versions of phylogenetic criteria]{ref}

For an original operational example, suppose a <phylogenetic tree> inferred from one <genetic locus> separates two sampled <biological populations>. Compare further <genetic loci> and additional local samples before interpreting that branch as a <species> boundary. Otherwise the test may merely identify the particular ancestry of the sampled <alleles>. Adding more sites from the same <genetic locus> improves that locus's evidence without creating an independent ancestry test.

= Species delimitation
{parent=Species}

<Species delimitation> infers the boundaries and number of <species> from evidence. No single arbitrary percentage of <nucleotide sequence> difference is a universal boundary. Divergence within an old, widespread <species> can exceed divergence between recently separated <species>; geography and multiple kinds of evidence matter. <Species Concepts and Species Delimitation by Kevin de Queiroz>[pp. 884–885, “Alternatives to the Traditional Properties”: divergence cutoffs and geographic differentiation]{ref}

An original counterexample to a universal percentage rule: an assay could examine a strongly conserved <gene> in one comparison and a rapidly changing <nucleotide sequence> in another. Equal percentages then answer different historical questions. Even the denominator needs specifying: all sites, aligned sites, or only a filtered region? A threshold is an operational rule for a stated assay and group of <organisms>, whose errors need evaluating. It is not a definition that automatically transfers to every <species>.

\Image[species-evidence-criteria.png]
{title=Contrasting operational evidence for <species delimitation>}
{description=Original hypothetical comparison of <morphology>, mating and ancestry evidence. Each observation tests a different claim; the combinations are thought experiments, not a dataset or verdicts about named <species>. “Not tested” does not mean absent.}

The diagram's hypothetical pair B has similar <morphology> but reduced mating and distinct ancestry evidence. A <morphological species concept> could miss that distinction. Pair C instead has different <morphology> but compatible mating and ancestry that fails to separate the samples. That combination calls for checking variation in <phenotype> within a <species>. Pair A has only a structural difference observed: the other criteria remain untested. These examples show why missing evidence, disagreeing evidence and agreeing evidence require different interpretations.

= Species, populations and strains
{parent=Species}

A <species> can encompass many <biological populations>. A <biological population> specifies a set of <organisms> in a place and time; a <biological strain> identifies a maintained variant or lineage, often in a laboratory. A <species> name, a local census and a <biological strain> designation therefore describe different things. Multiple independently maintained cultures can also share the same <biological strain> designation.

For example, a culture identified as <Escherichia coli> K-12 MG1655 specifies more than the <species> alone. Two flasks inoculated from that culture are separate experimental <biological populations>; the separate containers do not establish two <species> or two different <biological strains>. This is an original experimental bookkeeping example, not a claim that every <biological strain> name encodes a complete <genotype>.

= Morphology
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= Morphology
{synonym}

<Morphology> concerns biological form and structure; a structural difference is one aspect of <phenotype>, not automatically a <species> boundary.

= Developmental biology
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= Biological development
{parent=Developmental biology}

= Blastula
{parent=Biological development}
{wiki}

A <blastula> is an early multicellular stage of <animal> <biological development>. The <intestinal nucleus transfer experiment> distinguishes development beyond this stage from the earliest divisions.

= Paleontology
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= Fossil
{parent=Paleontology}
{wiki}

= Ecology
{parent=Biology}

The goal of <ecology> is to explain where <organisms> live, how many there are, and how their interactions with one another and their surroundings change those patterns. Living partners include <prey>, <predators> and <parasites>; nonliving conditions include <light>, <temperature> and <water>. An <ecosystem> brings these interacting parts into one study system. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/44-1-the-scope-of-ecology[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 44.1: the scope of ecology].

Start with an invented aquatic <food web>: photosynthetic <algae> feed <zooplankton>, which feed small <fish>, which feed large <fish>. That <food chain> has four feeding positions and three feeding arrows. Dead material also reaches <decomposers> through <detritus>, so the <food web> branches rather than forming a single ladder.

\Image[illustrative-food-web.png]
{title=An illustrative aquatic <food web>: feeding arrows point from resource to <consumer>}
{description=Original schematic, not a measured <ecosystem>. Solid arrows mean RESOURCE → CONSUMER; dashed arrows mean transfer of dead material to <detritus>. Box sizes and arrow widths do not represent abundance or transfer rates. <Algae>, <zooplankton>, small <fish> and large <fish> are generic groups, not identified <species>. Selected dead-material transfers are shown; further feeding and recycling routes are omitted.}

The appeal of <ecology> is that an <organism>'s surroundings include other <organisms> that change those surroundings. <Photosynthesis> links <light> to living material; <predation> transfers that material between <organisms>; <decomposers> return its constituent <chemical elements> to forms that can be used again. These connections help explain why changing one part of an <ecosystem> can affect distant parts of its <food web>, although an arrow alone does not predict the size or even the direction of an indirect change. <Population dynamics> supplies quantitative tools when the question concerns changing counts.

In 1866, Ernst Haeckel's \i[Generelle Morphologie der Organismen] included a section titled \i[Oecologie und Chorologie] in volume 2, beginning on page 286. This places the terminology of <ecology> in an early systematic treatment of <organisms> and their relationships. See https://books.google.com/books?id=tKFbIVgJS8wC[Haeckel, Generelle Morphologie der Organismen, volume 2 (1866), contents and p. 286]. A later quantitative landmark is Raymond L. Lindeman's 1942 treatment of <ecosystems> through feeding relationships and <energy flow>. See https://doi.org/10.2307/1930126[Lindeman, The Trophic-Dynamic Aspect of Ecology, Ecology 23 (1942), 399–417].

= Population
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Ecology}

= Biological population
{synonym}

A <biological population> is a set of <organisms> of a <species> occupying a specified area or experimental setting at a specified time. State the boundary for the question being studied: a flask, a breeding locality, or a wider region connected by <gene flow>. A <biological population> need not be perfectly mixed or isolated.

= Ecosystem
{parent=Ecology}
{wiki}

An <ecosystem> is a system of interacting <organisms> and their nonliving surroundings. A pond study might include <algae>, <fish>, <bacteria>, <water>, dissolved <chemical compounds> and incoming <light>. The useful step is to state the boundary and time interval: the pond surface, bottom and shoreline might enclose the study region, but <water>, <organisms> and <energy> can cross them. The chosen boundary specifies what counts as internal storage, input or output; it does not make the <ecosystem> sealed. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-1-ecology-of-ecosystems[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 46.1: ecosystems and their components].

For a toy pond <water> budget over one day, begin with $1000\,\mathrm{m^3}$, add $120\,\mathrm{m^3}$ crossing inward, and remove $90\,\mathrm{m^3}$ crossing outward. With all other transfers omitted by assumption, the final amount is $1000+120-90=1030\,\mathrm{m^3}$. The same bookkeeping asks whether a change inside an <ecosystem> came from internal processes or exchanges with its surroundings. Changing the boundary to include an upstream reservoir changes which transfers are internal, while preserving the accounting.

= Primary producer
{parent=Ecosystem}

A <primary producer> builds organic <chemical compounds> from inorganic sources. Photosynthetic <algae>, <plants> and <cyanobacteria> use <light> <energy> for this role through <photosynthesis> and <carbon fixation>. The role supplies new living material to a <food web>; it does not mean that the <organism> creates <energy>.

= Consumer
{disambiguate=food chain}
{parent=Ecosystem}
{wiki}

= Consumer
{synonym}

A <consumer> obtains organic material by feeding on other <organisms> or their products. A <consumer> feeding on <primary producers> is a primary <consumer>; one feeding on primary <consumers> is a secondary <consumer>. These names describe positions in a <food chain>, rather than permanent labels for a <species>.

= Decomposer
{parent=Consumer (food chain)}
{wiki}

A <decomposer> breaks down dead organic material and absorbs products of that breakdown; many <bacteria> and <fungi> perform this role. <Decomposers> connect <detritus> from different <trophic levels> to material recycling, so placing all <decomposers> at one final step of every <food chain> is misleading. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-1-ecology-of-ecosystems[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 46.1: grazing and detrital food webs].

= Omnivore
{parent=Consumer (food chain)}
{wiki}

An <omnivore> feeds on both <primary producers> and <consumers>. Its mixed diet explains why a <trophic level> need not be a whole number or a fixed label throughout an <organism>'s life.

= Detritus
{parent=Ecosystem}
{wiki}

<Detritus> is nonliving organic material, including dead remains and organic wastes. It is a resource for <decomposers>, not itself an <organism> or a <predator>. In the illustrative <food web>, the dashed arrow from <algae> to <detritus> denotes a material transfer; the solid arrow from <detritus> to <decomposers> denotes resource use.

= Trophic level
{parent=Ecosystem}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{TL}$}

A <trophic level> describes feeding position relative to <primary producers>. Assign <primary producers> level 1; in the illustrated <food chain>, <zooplankton> occupy level 2, small <fish> level 3, and large <fish> level 4. Following a single feeding path adds one level at each step. The labels make dependence on the initial resource visible, but a branching <food web> can give an <organism> several feeding paths. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-1-ecology-of-ecosystems[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 46.1: feeding positions and branching food webs].

An <omnivore> can obtain food from more than one <trophic level>. As an explicitly assumed diet-weighted example, suppose an <omnivore> gets half its assimilated food <energy> from level-1 <primary producers> and half from level-2 <consumers>. Define its <trophic level> as one plus the weighted <mean> level of its food: $1+(1/2)1+(1/2)2=2.5$. This computed position depends on the specified diet and weighting convention; it is not an immutable property of the <species>. A different life stage or diet can change it.

= Energy flow
{disambiguate=ecology}
{parent=Ecosystem}
{wiki}

= Energy flow
{synonym}

<Energy flow> tracks <energy> entering an <ecosystem>, passing through <organisms> and leaving its boundary. To see its quantitative effect, assume net production at one <trophic level> is $P_1=1000\,\mathrm{kJ\,m^{-2}\,day^{-1}}$ and assume a transfer efficiency $\varepsilon=0.10$ at each of two successive steps. Here $P_i$ measures the <energy> incorporated into new living material per unit <area> per day, after that level's own <cellular respiration>; it is not a count of <organisms> or a standing stock. The <joule> supplies the <energy> unit, with $1\,\mathrm{kJ}=1000\,\mathrm J$.
$$
P_{i+1}=\varepsilon P_i,\qquad P_2=100,\qquad P_3=10\quad\mathrm{kJ\,m^{-2}\,day^{-1}}.
$$

\Image[trophic-energy-budget.png]
{title=An assumed <energy flow> budget: 1000 → 100 → 10 kJ per square metre per day}
{description=Original toy calculation with 10 percent transfer efficiency assumed at both steps. Bars use one linear scale; the labels retain visibility of the smallest bar. The differences 900 and 90 are production not transferred along the selected <food chain>, not measurements of <heat> alone. The example is separate from the four-position illustrative <food web>.}

Only $10/1000=0.01$, or one percent, of the initial production reaches level 3 in this toy <food chain>. The first step leaves $1000-100=900$ and the second $100-10=90$ outside the next level's new production. These differences can include uneaten material, wastes, and <energy> released as <heat> during <cellular respiration>; their separate amounts have not been assigned. <Detritus> can still carry <energy> to <decomposers>. The 10 percent efficiency is an assumption, not a universal law: transfer efficiencies vary with the <organisms>, food and conditions. The production ratio $P_{i+1}/P_i$ also differs from the fraction of an individual meal converted into growth. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-2-energy-flow-through-ecosystems[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 46.2: production and transfer efficiency].

The shrinking budget explains why supporting another <trophic level> requires enough production below it. It does not prove an exact maximum length for every <food chain>, particularly when a <food web> imports resources or has branching diets. Lindeman's 1942 paper grouped feeding relationships into <trophic levels>, compared transfers and <cellular respiration>, and emphasized the <ecosystem> as a unit connecting living and nonliving components. That viewpoint made different <organisms> comparable through a common <energy> budget. See https://doi.org/10.2307/1930126[Lindeman, The Trophic-Dynamic Aspect of Ecology (1942)].

Material recycling and <energy flow> describe different bookkeeping. <Carbon>, <nitrogen> and <phosphorus> can be reused through <chemical reactions> and transfers between <organisms> and their surroundings. <Energy> transferred as <heat> is conserved, but is no longer the same store of chemical <energy> available to a <consumer>; photosynthetic <primary producers> need continuing <light> input. A <decomposer> helps recycle material and also releases <heat> through <metabolism>. Thus loss from one feeding budget means transfer into other forms or routes, never destruction of <energy>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/46-3-biogeochemical-cycles[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 46.3: material cycles]. <Conservation of energy> applies to the whole accounting.

= Zooplankton
{parent=Ecosystem}
{wiki}

<Zooplankton> are drifting aquatic <consumers>, including small <animals>. In the illustrative <food web>, the chosen <zooplankton> feed on <algae> and serve as <prey> for small <fish>; this assumed diet is not a claim about every kind of <zooplankton>.

= Endosymbiosis
{parent=Ecology}

<Endosymbiosis> is an ecological association in which one <organism>, the <endosymbiont>, lives inside another. The definition specifies location, not that both partners benefit, nor that the internal partner has become an <organelle>. An intracellular association can remain an association between distinct <organisms>. <Endosymbiotic theory> addresses a different question: how particular ancestral <bacteria> became inherited, functionally integrated <organelles> during <evolution>.

The striking consequence is that a <eukaryotic> <biological cell> can combine histories: its nuclear lineage and its <mitochondria> need not have the same ancestry. <Mitochondria> have bacterial ancestry related to <Alphaproteobacteria>; the <plastids> of <plants> and many <algae>, including <chloroplasts>, have ancestry within <cyanobacteria>. These are ancestral lineages, not a claim that a named living bacterial <species> is the literal ancestor. Present-day <bacteria> and <organelles> have both evolved since their shared ancestors. <Origin and Evolution of Plastids and Photosynthesis in Eukaryotes by Geoffrey I. McFadden>[Opening paragraphs and “The endosymbiont: when and who?”: bacterial origins and uncertainty about closest relatives]{ref}

\Image[endosymbiosis-organelle-integration.png]
{title=From an internal partner to an integrated <organelle>}
{description=Original conceptual schematic, not an observed historical sequence. Persistence, metabolic exchange, <endosymbiotic gene transfer>, <organelle protein import> and coordination of division illustrate integration; they are not dated stages in a compulsory order. The illustrated partner is generic, and the host outline does not specify the ancestral host of <mitochondria>. The protein-delivery arrow abbreviates nuclear <gene expression>, <biological translation> in the <cytosol> and <organelle protein import>; it does not place <protein> synthesis inside the <cell nucleus>.}

= Endosymbiont
{parent=Endosymbiosis}

An <endosymbiont> is the internal partner in an <endosymbiosis>. Calling an <organism> an <endosymbiont> does not by itself classify it as an <organelle> or imply an irreversible evolutionary transition.

= Endosymbiotic theory
{parent=Endosymbiosis}

<Endosymbiotic theory> explains the bacterial origins of <mitochondria> and <plastids> through enduring internal associations and subsequent integration. Survival inside a <biological cell> is only part of the problem: exchange through <membrane transport>, inherited division, <gene expression> and delivery of required <proteins> must work together. The elegance is evolutionary reuse of an existing cellular capability, followed by changes that make the partners operate as one system. <Origin and Evolution of Plastids and Photosynthesis in Eukaryotes by Geoffrey I. McFadden>[Abstract and “How to tame your symbiont”: metabolic, genetic and division integration]{ref}

The historical <On the origin of mitosing cells by Lynn Sagan> (1967, abstract and Introduction, pp. 225–226) synthesized a symbiotic account of <eukaryotic> origins and proposed tests using molecular evidence. Its Introduction explicitly acknowledged earlier proposals. It also hypothesized origins for other structures; the modern evidence for <mitochondria> and <plastids> should be assessed separately from the rest of that historical scheme.

= Evidence for bacterial ancestry of organelles
{parent=Endosymbiotic theory}

Resemblance is a clue, not sufficient evidence of <common descent>. A small size, two surrounding membranes, division, retained <DNA> or bacterial-looking <ribosomes> does not uniquely identify an ancestor. Internal membranes exist outside these <organelles>, and a similar function can favor similar structures. The stronger question is whether independently examined molecular characters support the same historical relationship.

A concrete genomic observation is the mitochondrial sequence reported in 1997 for <Reclinomonas americana>: $69{,}034$ <base pairs> containing 97 identified <genes>, including four for a bacterial-type multisubunit <RNA polymerase>. This is a reported <genome> inventory, not a calculation from the pictured shape of a <mitochondrion>. Retention of such bacterial features strengthens the ancestry argument; a living <Reclinomonas americana> is not itself an ancestral <eukaryote>. The inspected abstract supplies these observations but not the detailed sequencing protocol or contamination controls, so those details are not attributed to this report here. <An ancestral mitochondrial DNA resembling a eubacterial genome in miniature by B. Franz Lang and others>[Abstract, Nature 387 (1997), pp. 493–497: genome length, gene inventory and bacterial-type RNA polymerase]{ref}

A sequence-based investigation obtains <nucleotide sequences> by <DNA sequencing>, identifies comparable <genes>, makes a <sequence alignment> and uses <phylogenetics> to infer relationships. The readout is a proposed relationship with support and uncertainty, not a photograph of an ancient encounter. Comparisons of <plastid> sequences with bacterial sequences support cyanobacterial ancestry, while the exact closest sampled cyanobacterial group has depended on the chosen data and inference. A bacterial affinity in one <gene> is weaker than coherent evidence across several <genes> and cellular systems. <Origin and Evolution of Plastids and Photosynthesis in Eukaryotes by Geoffrey I. McFadden>[“The endosymbiont: when and who?”: alternative phylogenetic placements and lateral gene transfer]{ref}

For such an analysis, useful checks include distinguishing <organelle> <DNA> from bacterial contamination, verifying sequence quality, comparing corresponding rather than unrelated <genes>, sampling several bacterial groups and testing sensitivity to the <sequence alignment> and evolutionary <mathematical model>. These are methodological requirements, not a claim that this article has rerun a particular published analysis. <Horizontal gene transfer>, <gene> loss and unequal evolutionary change can make an individual <gene>'s history differ from a whole <organism>'s history. A supported bacterial origin therefore need not identify an extant donor <species>, an exact date or the original mode of entry. <Origin and Evolution of Plastids and Photosynthesis in Eukaryotes by Geoffrey I. McFadden>[“The endosymbiont: when and who?”: ancient origin and confounding lateral transfer]{ref}

= Endosymbiotic gene transfer
{parent=Endosymbiotic theory}

<Endosymbiotic gene transfer> moves genetic material from an <endosymbiont> or its descendant <organelle> into the host <genome>, notably the nuclear <genome>. A transferred <DNA> segment can remain silent. For a transferred <gene> to replace an <organelle>-encoded function, nuclear <gene expression> must work and its <protein> must reach the right compartment through <organelle protein import>. Transfer of <DNA> and transport of <proteins> are distinct processes in opposite directions. <Experimental Reconstruction of Functional Gene Transfer from the Tobacco Plastid Genome to the Nucleus by Sandra Stegemann and Ralph Bock>[Introduction, p. 2869, and Discussion, p. 2875: transferred sequences and the additional targeting problem]{ref}

<Gene> loss and <endosymbiotic gene transfer> are different: absence from an <organelle> <genome> does not by itself show that a functional nuclear copy exists. Conversely, an imported <protein> may derive from a host <gene> rather than a transferred <gene>. The surviving <organelle> <genome> is therefore a partial record, not a complete inventory of either ancestral <bacteria> or the modern <organelle>'s machinery. <Origin and Evolution of Plastids and Photosynthesis in Eukaryotes by Geoffrey I. McFadden>[“How to tame your symbiont”: losses, transfers, host recruitment and repatriation of proteins]{ref}

= Functional plastid-to-nucleus gene transfer experiment
{parent=Endosymbiotic gene transfer}

<Experimental Reconstruction of Functional Gene Transfer from the Tobacco Plastid Genome to the Nucleus by Sandra Stegemann and Ralph Bock> (2006, Results, pp. 2870–2874) separated relocation of <DNA> from activation of a relocated <gene>. Engineered tobacco <chloroplasts> carried an nptII <gene> with nuclear expression signals beside an aadA <gene> with plastid expression signals. Selection on kanamycin recovered plants with the segment in the nuclear <genome>. Crosses then replaced the engineered <chloroplasts> with ordinary <chloroplasts>: the nuclear segment remained, but the plants were initially sensitive to spectinomycin and streptomycin. The transferred aadA <gene> was not yet functional in its new setting.

Leaf pieces were cultured on regeneration medium containing both drugs. Resistant regenerated plants supplied the functional readout. Using both drugs reduced selection of spontaneous plastid <mutations> conferring resistance mainly to one drug. Reciprocal crosses and selfing distinguished maternal plastid inheritance from nuclear <Mendelian segregation>; eight of sixteen initial candidates had the latter pattern. <Experimental Reconstruction of Functional Gene Transfer from the Tobacco Plastid Genome to the Nucleus by Sandra Stegemann and Ralph Bock>[Figure 1, Tables 1–2 and Results, pp. 2870–2871: selection, eliminated candidates and inheritance controls]{ref}

The molecular readout came from <RNA> hybridization and <DNA sequencing>. A <Northern blot> detected aadA <messenger RNA> in activated lines, with different transcript sizes. Thermal asymmetric interlaced <polymerase chain reaction> recovered upstream <DNA>; products present in activated lines but absent in original-line and ordinary-plant controls were sequenced. The mapped rearrangements connected aadA to the upstream nuclear <gene promoter>. Thus resistance, inheritance, <RNA> detection and changed <nucleotide sequences> supported activation of the transferred nuclear <gene>, rather than resistance alone. <Experimental Reconstruction of Functional Gene Transfer from the Tobacco Plastid Genome to the Nucleus by Sandra Stegemann and Ralph Bock>[Figures 3–4, Results, pp. 2872–2874, and “PCR and DNA sequencing”/“TAIL-PCR”, pp. 2876–2877]{ref}

\Image[endosymbiosis-evidence-gene-transfer.png]
{title=Separate evidence for ancestry, <DNA> transfer and functional activation}
{description=Original evidence map and simplified experimental logic. Boxes summarize questions, readouts and controls; no measured <phylogenetic tree>, <nucleotide sequence>, deletion map or quantitative response is copied. <Organelle protein import> remains a separate question beyond this tobacco experiment.}

This engineered test demonstrates one route to functional nuclear <gene expression> after <plastid> <DNA> transfer. It does not establish a natural transfer rate, replay ancient <endosymbiosis> or demonstrate that the resulting <protein> is imported into a <chloroplast>. The authors explicitly identify acquisition of targeting information as an additional step not reproduced by this experiment. <Experimental Reconstruction of Functional Gene Transfer from the Tobacco Plastid Genome to the Nucleus by Sandra Stegemann and Ralph Bock>[Discussion, p. 2875: antibiotic selection and missing transit-peptide acquisition]{ref}

= Parasitism
{parent=Ecology}

<Parasitism> is an interaction in which a <parasite> obtains resources from a <host>, reducing some component of the <host>'s performance. Its demographic effects depend on exposure, within-host processes and transmission opportunities.

= Parasitoid
{parent=Parasitism}

A <parasitoid> develops on or in a <host> and eventually kills it. Host-parasitoid transmission therefore has different offspring accounting from ordinary <predation> or a nonlethal parasite-burden model.

= Host
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Parasitism}

= Host
{synonym}

A biological <host> provides the environment or resources used by a <parasite> or other associated organism.

= Parasite
{parent=Parasitism}

A <parasite> is an organism benefiting from <parasitism>. <Microparasite> and <macroparasite> distinguish useful transmission-model descriptions of within-host multiplication and distributed <parasite> burden.

= Macroparasite
{parent=Parasite}

A <macroparasite> is typically modelled using the number of <parasites> carried by each <host>. Adult parasitic worms illustrate why <within-host parasite burden>, developmental stages, mating and <host> exposure heterogeneity matter beyond an infected/not-infected classification.

= Within-host parasite burden
{parent=Macroparasite}
{title2=$j$}

The <within-host parasite burden> is the number of <parasites>, or a specified parasite-load measure, carried by one <host>. A population's <mean> burden and its infected-host <prevalence> are different summaries of the burden distribution.

= Microparasite
{parent=Parasite}

A <microparasite> is typically modelled as multiplying rapidly within a <host>, so infection status rather than the exact organism count is the first between-host state variable. <SIS model>, <SIR model> and <SEIR model> descriptions are examples. Additional within-host structure can still be required.

= Predation
{parent=Ecology}

<Predation> is the consumption of <prey> by a <predator>, affecting the demographic rates of both populations.

In the usual animal-feeding sense, a <predator> kills and eats its <prey>. This distinguishes the interaction from feeding on already-dead material and from the prolonged exploitation of a living <host> in <parasitism>. In the illustrative <food web>, a small <fish> eating <zooplankton> is a <predator>; a large <fish> eating that small <fish> makes the same small <fish> its <prey>. These roles belong to a particular interaction. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/45-6-community-ecology[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 45.6: predation and community interactions].

For a toy count, suppose 50 small <fish> begin a day and 8 are eaten by large <fish>. With births, other deaths and movement explicitly omitted, 42 small <fish> remain, and the fraction removed by <predation> is $8/50=0.16$. This measures removal of <prey>, not production of new <predators>. Food intake can support survival and reproduction, but eight meals do not imply eight new <predators>; <energy flow> and life-history details intervene. <Lotka-Volterra equations> provide one idealized account of such <population dynamics>, rather than a guarantee that every interacting pair must cycle.

= Prey
{parent=Predation}

<Prey> is an <organism> consumed by a <predator> in <predation>. The name describes the receiving end of that interaction: a small <fish> can be <prey> for a large <fish> while being a <predator> of <zooplankton>. In a <food web> drawn with resource-to-<consumer> arrows, a feeding arrow goes outward from the <prey> toward its <predator>. This convention makes the route of food and <energy> easy to follow.

= Predator
{parent=Predation}

A <predator> is an <organism> that consumes <prey> through <predation>. A <predator> need not occupy the highest <trophic level> of an <ecosystem>, and the label does not specify its size or guarantee that it has no <predators> of its own. In the illustrative <food chain>, the small <fish> is a <predator> at level 3 and also <prey> for the large <fish> at level 4. Separating these relational labels from <species> names prevents a misleading division of the world into permanent <predator> and <prey> categories.

= Protein
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

A <protein> is a biological <molecule> made of one or more <polypeptide chains>: ordered <amino acid residues> joined by <peptide bonds>. <Biological translation> assembles a chain in the order specified by <messenger RNA> <codons>. <Protein folding> then brings parts of that chain into spatial relationships that support its function. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/[Cooper, The Cell: protein composition and structure]. These processes can overlap in a <biological cell>; the sequence-to-shape explanation does not require waiting until <biological translation> finishes before any folding begins. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9843/[Cooper, The Cell: protein folding and processing].

The useful connection is from information to physical work. A <protein>'s <protein primary structure> records an order; its <protein tertiary structure> can bring distant <amino acid residues> together at an <active site>. An <enzyme> uses such an arrangement to recognize a <substrate> and accelerate a <chemical reaction>. Other <proteins> serve as <receptors>, <membrane transport proteins> or structural material. For example, <actin> assembles into filaments whose mechanical properties can be described by <biopolymer mechanics>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26911/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: protein function and binding].

Order matters beyond composition. Let A stand for <alanine> and M for <methionine>, and read from the <N-terminus> to the <C-terminus>. The illustrative strings AMAMAMAMAMAM and AAAAAAMMMMMM each contain exactly six A and six M <amino acid residues>. Each has $12-1=11$ <peptide bonds> when made into one unbranched <polypeptide chain>. In the first string all eleven neighboring pairs have different letters; in the second only the pair at positions 6 and 7 does. A composition <histogram> therefore loses information about which <amino acid residues> are neighbors. Their arrangements can affect <protein folding> and function; these short invented strings neither specify a known functional <protein> nor establish what shape either would adopt.

\Image[protein-sequence-to-fold.png]
{title=From <amino acid> order to an imagined fold and binding site}
{description=Original schematic using two twelve-residue strings with equal composition. The imagined fold uses the first string: colors and numbers preserve its <protein primary structure>, while positions 4 and 9 approach one another. The binding-site cartoon explains spatial cooperation, not an actual <enzyme> or a measured <protein structure>. No actual coordinates, physical scale or exact <protein folding> predictor are implied.}

<Protein structure> is a moving arrangement, not a permanently rigid outline. <Substrate> binding can change an <enzyme>'s shape through <induced fit>, and a functional <protein> can include flexible regions. Sequence, surroundings and interacting <molecules> must therefore be considered together. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26911/[Alberts et al.: binding, conformational changes and protein function].

= Antibody
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

An <antibody> is a <protein> that recognizes particular molecular targets through binding. <Antibodies> participate in <immunity>; laboratory assays such as <Western blots> exploit their selective binding to detect chosen <proteins>.

An <IgG> gives a concrete architecture: two <immunoglobulin heavy chains> and two <immunoglobulin light chains>, joined by <disulfide bonds>. Each arm ends in a <paratope> assembled from an <antibody variable region> of each kind of chain. The two <paratopes> recognize the same <epitope> in this conventional <IgG>; the flexible hinge allows different arm separations. The <antibody constant regions> form the remaining portions. This is an illustrative <IgG>, rather than the architecture of every <antibody> format. <Immunobiology: The Immune System in Health and Disease, 5th edition by Charles A. Janeway, Jr. and others/The structure of a typical antibody molecule>[Immunobiology, 5th edition, §§3-1, 3-2 and 3-4, Figures 3.2 and 3.4: four chains, paired sites and hinge flexibility]{ref}

\Image[antibody-igg-chains-and-bivalent-binding.png]
{title=<IgG> chains, <paratopes> and two-arm binding}
{description=Original schematic of a conventional <IgG>. Color distinguishes <immunoglobulin heavy chains> from <immunoglobulin light chains>; darker tips mark <antibody variable regions>. Each outlined <paratope> includes both chain tips. The second panel contrasts one contact with two contacts to appropriately spaced <epitopes> on an imagined surface. Shapes, spacing and chain paths are illustrative, with no structural coordinates or physical scale.}

Three questions must be kept separate. <Antibody specificity> asks which <antigens> are recognized. <Antibody affinity> describes one <paratope>–<epitope> interaction. <Avidity> describes the combined attachment when several contacts can cooperate. A selective <antibody> can have modest <antibody affinity>, and a <monoclonal antibody> can still show <cross-reactivity>. For an assay, the practical question is whether the observed readout identifies the intended <antigen> under the tested conditions; <Western blot antibody specificity controls> make that question concrete.

= Primary antibody
{parent=Antibody}

In an indirect <Western blot>, the <primary antibody> binds an <epitope> on the proposed target <protein>. Its binding must be tested in the actual sample and assay conditions through <Western blot antibody specificity controls>.

= Secondary antibody
{parent=Antibody}

A <secondary antibody> recognizes another <antibody>, rather than the proposed target <protein> directly. In <Western blot antibody detection>, its attached label makes retained <primary antibodies> visible. A <secondary antibody> must recognize the kind of <primary antibody> used.

= Epitope
{parent=Antibody}
{wiki}

An <epitope> is the molecular feature recognized by an <antibody>. <Protein denaturation> can expose an <epitope> or destroy the spatial arrangement required for its recognition; an <antibody> that works on a folded <protein> need not work in <SDS-PAGE>-based <Western blot antibody detection>.

= Immunoglobulin G
{c}
{parent=Antibody}
{wiki}

= IgG
{c}
{synonym}

<Immunoglobulin G> is an <antibody> class whose conventional four-chain architecture supplies the example above.

= Immunoglobulin heavy chain
{parent=Antibody}
{wiki}

An <immunoglobulin heavy chain> extends through an arm and the stem of the illustrated <IgG>.

= Immunoglobulin light chain
{parent=Antibody}
{wiki}

An <immunoglobulin light chain> accompanies an arm of the illustrated <IgG>, contributing to its <paratope>.

= Antibody variable region
{parent=Antibody}
{title2=$V_H,V_L$}

The paired <antibody variable regions> of an <immunoglobulin heavy chain> and an <immunoglobulin light chain> contribute to an <antibody binding site>. Variation refers to differences between <antibody> sequences, not to a tip continually changing identity while binding.

= Antibody constant region
{parent=Antibody}
{title2=$C_H,C_L$}

An <antibody constant region> is a chain portion outside the <antibody variable region>. Constant does not mean identical across every <antibody> class.

= Fragment antigen-binding
{parent=Antibody}
{wiki}

= Fab fragment
{c}
{synonym}

A <Fab fragment> contains one <antibody binding site>, allowing comparison with the two-site intact <IgG> in the <antibody surface plasmon resonance experiment>.

= Paratope
{parent=Antibody}
{wiki}

= Antibody binding site
{synonym}

A <paratope> is the recognizing surface on an <antibody>; the <epitope> is the recognized feature on its <antigen>. Contacts depend on spatial arrangement and chemistry, including <hydrogen bonds> and <van der Waals forces>. Thus a <paratope> is not simply a slot for a <protein primary structure> string. A folded <protein> can bring distant <amino acid residues> into one <epitope>. <Immunobiology: The Immune System in Health and Disease, 5th edition by Charles A. Janeway, Jr. and others/The interaction of the antibody molecule with specific antigen>[Immunobiology, 5th edition, §§3-6, 3-8 and 3-9: paired variable regions, discontinuous epitopes and reversible noncovalent contacts]{ref}

= Antigen
{parent=Antibody}
{wiki}

In <antibody> binding, an <antigen> is material bearing a recognized <epitope>. An <antigen> need not be a <protein>; <polysaccharides> can also carry <epitopes>. One <antigen> may present several different <epitopes>, so recognizing one feature does not imply recognizing every part of the target. <Immunobiology: The Immune System in Health and Disease, 5th edition by Charles A. Janeway, Jr. and others/The interaction of the antibody molecule with specific antigen>[Immunobiology, 5th edition, §3-8: small recognized regions of protein and polysaccharide antigens]{ref}

= Antibody specificity
{parent=Antibody}

<Antibody specificity> describes preferential recognition of selected <epitopes>. It is established relative to tested alternatives and assay conditions, rather than inferred from a reagent's name. <Western blot antibody specificity controls> distinguish recognition of the proposed <protein> from retained label caused by other material.

= Cross-reactivity
{parent=Antibody specificity}
{wiki}

<Cross-reactivity> occurs when an <antibody> also recognizes another <antigen> beyond the one against which it was raised. This may involve related features, but obvious overall similarity is not required. <Immunobiology: The Immune System in Health and Disease, 5th edition by Charles A. Janeway, Jr. and others/Appendix I. Immunologists' Toolbox>[Immunobiology, 5th edition, Appendix I, antiserum generation: cross-reactive antigens and absorption]{ref}

For an imagined example, a <paratope> contacts a small surface patch shared by two otherwise different <proteins>. Both can bind even though their remaining surfaces differ. Increasing <antibody> <concentration> can reveal weak binding that was previously undetectable. Conversely, an extra <Western blot> band need not prove <cross-reactivity>: the intended <protein> may occur in multiple forms, or detection reagents may contribute background. The target-deleted and no-<primary antibody> comparisons under <Western blot antibody specificity controls> test different explanations.

= Monoclonal antibody
{parent=Antibody}
{wiki}

A <monoclonal antibody> preparation derives from one selected antibody-producing <biological cell> and its descendants, or reproduces their specified <antibody> sequences. Monoclonal describes the origin and composition of the preparation; it does not guarantee absence of <cross-reactivity> or establish <antibody affinity>. <Immunobiology: The Immune System in Health and Disease, 5th edition by Charles A. Janeway, Jr. and others/Glossary>[Immunobiology, 5th edition, Glossary: Monoclonal antibodies]{ref}

= Avidity
{parent=Antibody}
{wiki}

<Avidity> is the combined strength of multivalent attachment. An intact <IgG> can make two contacts when accessible <epitopes> have suitable spacing. <Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others>[Chapter 24, B Cells and Antibodies, A Typical Antibody Has Two Identical Antigen-Binding Sites; The Strength of an Antibody-Antigen Interaction Depends on Both the Number and the Affinity of the Antigen-Binding Sites: avidity]{ref} If one contact breaks while the other remains, the detached arm stays nearby and can bind again before the whole <antibody> escapes. That geometric advantage does not require changing either <paratope>'s intrinsic <antibody affinity>. Two arms binding two separate freely moving <antigens> do not automatically provide the same tethering advantage. <Epitope> spacing, accessibility and surface density matter; <avidity> is not obtained by adding two <dissociation constants>.

= Antibody affinity
{parent=Antibody}
{title2=$K_d$}

<Antibody affinity> concerns one <paratope> binding one <epitope>. A <Fab fragment> supplies a single-site comparison. <Immunobiology: The Immune System in Health and Disease, 5th edition by Charles A. Janeway, Jr. and others/Glossary>[Immunobiology, 5th edition, Glossary: affinity, single-site binding]{ref} In the dilute <chemical equilibrium> model below, smaller <dissociation constant> $K_d$ means larger <antibody equilibrium occupancy> at the same free <antigen> <concentration>. State the <antigen>, <temperature> and <buffer solution>: a <dissociation constant> is not a context-free score. It does not alone specify how fast attachment or detachment occurs.

= Antibody equilibrium occupancy
{parent=Antibody affinity}
{title2=$\theta$}

<Antibody equilibrium occupancy> counts occupied <antibody binding sites>, rather than retained assay label. For an original 1:1 model, let $A$ denote an unoccupied site, $E$ free <antigen> bearing one accessible <epitope>, and $AE$ their complex. At <chemical equilibrium>, write
$$
A+E\rightleftharpoons AE,\qquad c=[E],\qquad K_d=\frac{[A]c}{[AE]},\qquad A_T=[A]+[AE],\qquad \theta=\frac{[AE]}{A_T}=\frac{c}{K_d+c}.
$$
Square brackets mean <concentrations>. The <dissociation constant> $K_d$ here has <concentration> units. This is the same independent-site calculation as <receptor binding occupancy>, now applied to an <antibody binding site>. The last equality follows by substituting $[AE]=[A]c/K_d$ into the occupied fraction and cancelling $[A]$.

Assume identical independent sites, reversible 1:1 binding, dilute <chemical solution> behavior, fixed <temperature> and <buffer solution>, and enough time for <chemical equilibrium>. Exclude two-arm bridging, <cross-reactivity> and inaccessible sites from this model. Crucially, $c$ is free <antigen> <concentration>; substituting the amount supplied per volume requires negligible depletion by binding. Independent <IgG> arms could obey this model site by site, but it does not describe <avidity> from bridging a surface.

Stipulate $K_d=20\,\mathrm{nM}$, with $1\,\mathrm{nM}=10^{-9}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$. Free <antigen> <concentrations> $2$, $20$ and $200\,\mathrm{nM}$ give $\theta=1/11$, $1/2$ and $10/11$. A second stipulated <dissociation constant> of $200\,\mathrm{nM}$ instead gives $\theta=1/11$ at $c=20\,\mathrm{nM}$. Both curves approach one at large $c$: a nearly saturated readout can conceal an <antibody affinity> difference. These are invented values, with no universal threshold for a useful <antibody>.

\Image[antibody-equilibrium-occupancy-and-assay-readout.png]
{title=<Antibody equilibrium occupancy> and the additional steps in an assay readout}
{description=Original analytic curves for stipulated <dissociation constants> of $20$ and $200\,\mathrm{nM}$; marked examples belong to the first curve. The adjacent schematic separates <epitope> accessibility, binding, washing and detection. It contains no measured sensorgrams, fitted assay parameters or experimental signals. A <logarithmic scale> changes the display, not the independent-site assumptions.}

A <Western blot> readout additionally depends on <Western blot transfer>, accessible <epitopes>, loss during washing and <Western blot antibody detection>. <Western blot response saturation> can flatten the final signal even when binding itself remains responsive. An <ELISA> also reports retained label through an <enzyme> readout, rather than automatically reporting $\theta$. Thus half of an assay's maximum signal need not occur at $c=K_d$. Varying free <antigen> through <concentrations> near the candidate <dissociation constant>, while checking depletion and the readout's response, is more informative than comparing a single saturated point.

= Antibody surface plasmon resonance experiment
{parent=Antibody affinity}

A concrete comparison used a Biacore T100 with a CM5 chip bearing the target <receptor>; unused reactive surface groups were blocked. At $25\,{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a <pH> 7.4 <buffer solution>, intact <IgG> and its <Fab fragment> were injected for $180\,\mathrm s$ at $5\,\mu\mathrm L/\mathrm{min}$; dissociation was monitored for $3600\,\mathrm s$. The optical response tracks changes in <refractive index> near the surface, rather than counting occupied <paratopes> directly. A reference surface and blank injections corrected background; association and dissociation responses were fitted with a 1:1 model. The reported intact-<IgG> detachment was slower than that of the <Fab fragment>. <Antibody-Unfolding and Metastable-State Binding in Force Spectroscopy and Recognition Imaging by Parminder Kaur and others>[Kaur and others, Materials and Methods, Surface plasmon resonance; Table 1, whole IgG and Fab rows]{ref}

This comparison supports format-dependent attachment, not an intrinsic single-site <antibody affinity> for intact <IgG>. The reference and blanks address background, not two-arm <avidity>, surface accessibility or delivery by <diffusion>. The paper's fitted 1:1 constants alone cannot distinguish those mechanisms. Its <Fab fragment> comparison therefore motivates specifying the binding format before interpreting a <dissociation constant>; a good fit does not establish a unique molecular model.

= Western blot
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

= Immunoblot
{synonym}

A <Western blot> detects selected <proteins> after their separation and transfer to a membrane, using <antibodies>. In the <yeast cross-species complementation experiment>, detection helps distinguish failed <protein> production from failure of an expressed <protein> to restore growth.

The useful idea is to preserve where <proteins> migrated in <gel electrophoresis>, then ask which positions retain an <antibody>. <Western blot separation>, <Western blot transfer> and <Western blot antibody detection> answer different questions. A band is evidence of retained label at a position; <Western blot band interpretation> requires <Western blot controls> before assigning identity or comparing abundance.

\Image[western-blot-transfer-antibody-workflow.png]
{title=<Western blot separation>, <Western blot transfer> and <Western blot antibody detection>}
{description=Original schematic of an <SDS-PAGE>-based workflow, showing transfer towards the <anode> and an indirect <antibody> readout. The drawn bands and binding shapes are explanatory symbols, not measured data or a reconstruction of the original apparatus. The acidic <urea>-gel experiment discussed under <Western blot transfer> used the opposite polarity.}

= Western blot separation
{parent=Western blot}

In the common <SDS-PAGE> version, <protein denaturation> and binding of an anionic <surfactant> reduce differences due to folded shape and intrinsic <electric charge>. An <electric field> drives the resulting <protein> complexes through the gel; smaller chains generally migrate farther. A lane of reference <proteins> supplies positions for estimating apparent <mass>. This estimate depends on the separation conditions: it is not a direct weighing of a <molecule>, and different <proteins> can migrate together.

= Western blot transfer
{parent=Western blot}

<Western blot transfer> moves separated <proteins> out of the gel onto a <Western blot membrane>, using an <electric field> across the gel thickness. Maintaining close contact preserves the lane pattern. Contact gaps, incomplete removal from the gel and passage through the receiving sheet can change how much <protein> is retained.

The original 1979 experiment pressed a wetted, $0.45\,\mu\mathrm m$-pore <nitrocellulose Western blot membrane> against the gel between scouring pads and plastic pipette-tray grids, held by elastic bands. The <urea>-gel assembly faced the <cathode> in acidic solution, with $6\,\mathrm{V/cm}$ applied for one hour; the <SDS-PAGE> variant reversed polarity and used a <glycine>/<methanol> <buffer solution>. Stained gel/blot pairs checked pattern preservation. Corresponding labelled <ribosome> <protein> spots from two gels were compared through <radioactive decay> readouts after combustion. The stained gel remaining after transfer supplied a removal check; a second receiving sheet revealed breakthrough on overloading. These tests supported recovery for the examined <urea>-gel <proteins>, within experimental variation, while the <SDS-PAGE> results showed unequal staining recovery. Preserved band positions therefore did not establish universal quantitative transfer. <Electrophoretic transfer of proteins from polyacrylamide gels to nitrocellulose sheets: procedure and some applications by Harry Towbin and others>[Towbin, Staehelin and Gordon, pp. 4350–4352: Electrophoretic Blotting Procedures, Figure 1, Figures 2–3 and Table 1]{ref}

= Western blot membrane
{parent=Western blot transfer}

A <Western blot membrane> is a thin <protein>-binding support that receives the separated pattern. It is a laboratory support, distinct from a <cell membrane>. Retaining <proteins> on its surface makes them accessible for <Western blot antibody detection>.

= Nitrocellulose Western blot membrane
{parent=Western blot membrane}

A <nitrocellulose Western blot membrane> is a <Western blot membrane> made from nitrated <cellulose>. Its retention capacity is finite; the original <Western blot transfer> experiment tested breakthrough with a second receiving sheet.

= Western blot antibody detection
{parent=Western blot}

Blocking occupies unused binding sites on the <Western blot membrane>. The <primary antibody> is applied, unbound material is washed away, and a labelled <secondary antibody> detects retained <primary antibody>. A <fluorescence> label emits detectable <light> after excitation; an <enzyme>-linked label can instead generate a colored product or <light>. Washing reduces background but does not prove that remaining binding is selective.

In the original experiment, blocking used excess <protein>. An anti-<ribosome>-<protein> serum followed by labelled <secondary antibody> produced colored or <fluorescence> bands; preimmune serum supplied comparison strips. The color readout was an <enzyme> reaction product, rather than a measurement of target <enzyme> activity. <Electrophoretic transfer of proteins from polyacrylamide gels to nitrocellulose sheets: procedure and some applications by Harry Towbin and others>[Towbin, Staehelin and Gordon, pp. 4351–4353: Immunological Detection of Proteins on Nitrocellulose and Figure 4, including preimmune-serum controls]{ref}

= Western blot band interpretation
{parent=Western blot}

Band position gives an apparent <mass>, not unique <protein> identity. Intensity depends on retention, <epitope> recognition and detection response; it does not establish folded state, <enzyme> activity or successful <protein targeting>. Missing bands can reflect missing targets or failed <Western blot transfer> or <Western blot antibody detection>. <Considerations when quantitating protein abundance by immunoblot by Alicia A. McDonough and others>[McDonough and others, “Consider the Specificity of the Antibody for the Target” and “Consider the Linearity of the Detection System”]{ref}

The <yeast cross-species complementation experiment> consequently combines two logically different readouts: <Western blot> evidence about production of an <antibody>-recognized <protein>, and growth evidence about <functional complementation>. A detectable <protein> need not perform the biological task required for growth.

= Western blot controls
{parent=Western blot}

<Western blot loading controls> ask how much sample entered each lane; <Western blot transfer controls> ask what reached the receiving sheet; <Western blot antibody specificity controls> ask what the <antibodies> recognized. Each closes a different gap in <Western blot band interpretation>.

\Image[western-blot-lane-control-interpretation.png]
{title=Conceptual lanes for <Western blot controls>}
{description=Original schematic, with invented qualitative marks only. Candidate bands disappear in a target-deleted sample while a lower band persists; a separate panel shows how a loading or transfer difference can accompany a target-band difference. No lane, darkness, band position or relative amount represents a measurement, a specific real <protein> or confirmed identity. The no-primary comparison denotes a matched strip processed without <primary antibody>.}

= Western blot loading control
{parent=Western blot controls}

A <Western blot loading control> estimates lane-to-lane sample differences. A reference <protein>, such as <actin>, must have stable abundance and detection responsive to amount. Equal saturated bands fail to establish equal loading. <Considerations when quantitating protein abundance by immunoblot by Alicia A. McDonough and others>[McDonough and others, “Consider the Loading Controls”, Figure 4: sample doubling changes claudin detection but not the actin signal]{ref}

= Western blot transfer control
{parent=Western blot controls}

A <Western blot transfer control> tests retention after <Western blot transfer>. Total-<protein> staining of the <Western blot membrane> can reveal uneven lanes or blank patches; staining the gel after transfer tests what remained behind. Transferred reference bands check selected positions, not recovery of every target. A pretransfer loading image alone cannot establish the amount retained on the receiving sheet.

= Western blot antibody specificity control
{parent=Western blot controls}

A known target-containing sample is a positive comparison for <Western blot antibody detection>. A matched sample with <gene deletion> supplies a negative comparison: disappearance of the proposed band supports the assignment, while a persisting band indicates binding unrelated to the deleted target. Deletion must actually remove the recognized <protein>; changes in other <proteins> can complicate the comparison. Omitting the <primary antibody> checks binding or background from the <secondary antibody> and detection reagents, but cannot exclude binding of the <primary antibody> to an unintended <protein>. Independent <antibodies> recognizing different <epitopes> add evidence. <Considerations when quantitating protein abundance by immunoblot by Alicia A. McDonough and others>[McDonough and others, “Consider the Specificity of the Antibody for the Target”, Figure 2: positive tissue, knockout tissue and antibodies to different termini]{ref}

= Western blot normalization
{parent=Western blot}

Total-<protein> <Western blot normalization> compares a target-band signal with a signal representing many transferred <proteins> in that lane. It is distinct from measuring one reference <protein>, and from measuring the target itself. Its denominator is a loading proxy: normalization estimates target abundance relative to detected total <protein>, rather than target amount per <biological cell>. Different sample compositions, selective <Western blot transfer> losses or unequal stain responses can still matter.

For an original calculation, suppose background-corrected target signals $I_A=12$, $I_B=24$ and total-<protein> signals $P_A=30$, $P_B=60$ are in their respective proportional response ranges. Then
$$
\frac{I_B/P_B}{I_A/P_A}=\frac{24/60}{12/30}=1.
$$
The invented signals double together, so this calculation supplies no evidence for a higher target fraction. With $I_B=24$ but $P_B=30$, the same calculation gives $2$. These examples assume a common target response and a common total-<protein> response between lanes, negligible remaining background, and comparable target recovery; they do not equate total-<protein> staining with target abundance. Multiplying both signals in a lane by the same loading factor cancels in their <ratio>; a selective target loss does not cancel.

= Western blot response saturation
{parent=Western blot}

<Western blot response saturation> means that increasing target amount no longer increases the recorded signal proportionally. It can arise before imaging, through limited binding or reagent availability, as well as through detector limits. Shortening exposure can address a detector limit without repairing an already unresponsive assay. Test the target and normalization readouts with a dilution series under the actual assay conditions; merely seeing a gray band does not establish a proportional response.

In a 2013 methods experiment, a <biological cell> extract containing added purified <yeast> <protein> was separated on gels, transferred with a Trans-Blot Turbo to <polymer> membranes, and read with an <enzyme>-linked <secondary antibody>. A ChemiDoc MP imager and film recorded the same dilution-series blots. The target and an abundant reference <protein> had different usable response ranges; transferred total-<protein> imaging and the reference bands did not track loading equally well. This demonstrates the need for assay-specific response checks, rather than a universal conversion from darkness to amount. <A Defined Methodology for Reliable Quantification of Western Blot Data by Sean C. Taylor and others>[Taylor, Berkelman, Yadav and Hammond, Materials and Methods; Figures 2–4 and 5–7: transfer apparatus, film/imager comparison and loading normalization]{ref}

An illustrative response model separates background $b$, target amount $T$, retained fraction $q$, response scale $S$ and characteristic amount $K$:
$$
I(T)=b+S\frac{qT}{K+qT},\qquad S,K>0,\quad 0<q\leq1.
$$
This is an assumed model, not a fitted <Western blot> calibration. When $qT\ll K$, $I-b\approx(Sq/K)T$. When $qT\gg K$, $I-b$ approaches $S$. For two otherwise identical lanes, doubling $T$ gives the background-corrected <ratio>
$$
\frac{I(2T)-b}{I(T)-b}=\frac{2(K+qT)}{K+2qT}.
$$
At $qT=K$ the <ratio> is $4/3$, despite a true twofold amount change. Thus <Western blot normalization> cannot recover a lost amount difference merely by dividing two saturated readouts.

= Protein targeting
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

<Protein targeting> directs a <protein> to its cellular destination. Synthesis in the <cytosol> does not imply that the <protein> works there.

= Organelle protein import
{parent=Protein targeting}

<Organelle protein import> brings externally synthesized <proteins> into an <organelle>. Many <plastid> <proteins> made from nuclear <genes> carry targeting information recognized by machinery that transports them across the envelope membranes. This delivery enables nuclear <genes> to supply functions inside a <plastid>, but the presence of targeting information is not itself evidence that a <gene> was acquired through <endosymbiotic gene transfer>. <Origin and Evolution of Plastids and Photosynthesis in Eukaryotes by Geoffrey I. McFadden>[“How to tame your symbiont”: targeting, membrane passage and host-derived proteins]{ref}

= Polypeptide
{parent=Protein}

= Polypeptide chain
{synonym}

A <polypeptide> is a chain of <amino acid residues> linked by <peptide bonds>. The term describes chain chemistry; calling a chain a <polypeptide> alone does not establish that it is folded or functional. The <gene expression> example N–<methionine>–<alanine>–C contains two <amino acid residues> and one <peptide bond>; the twelve-residue example under <protein> contains eleven <peptide bonds>. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/AminoAcid/A1416.html[IUPAC-IUB: amino-acid symbols and representation of residues in chains].

= Amino acid residue
{parent=Polypeptide}

An <amino acid residue> is the portion of an <amino acid> incorporated into a <polypeptide chain>. For example, the A in the illustrative <protein primary structure> is an <alanine> residue, rather than a free <alanine> <molecule> floating beside the chain. Counting <amino acid residues> counts chain positions; repeated letters are separate positions. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/AminoAcid/A1416.html[IUPAC-IUB, section 3AA-16: symbols for amino-acid residues].

= Tyrosine residue
{parent=Amino acid residue}
{title2=$Y$}

A <tyrosine residue> is an <amino acid residue> whose <amino acid side chain> can accept a <phosphate> group during <receptor tyrosine kinase> signaling. The conventional one-letter sequence symbol is $Y$.

= Phosphotyrosine residue
{parent=Tyrosine residue}
{title2=$\mathrm{pY}$}

A <phosphotyrosine residue> is a <tyrosine residue> modified by <phosphorylation>. Its location and surrounding <protein primary structure> help determine which <SH2 domains> can recognize it.

= Amino acid side chain
{parent=Amino acid residue}
{title2=$R$}

An <amino acid side chain> is the variable group attached to an <amino acid alpha carbon>, conventionally denoted $R$. For <alanine> it is $\mathrm{CH_3}$: a <carbon> bonded to three <hydrogen> <chemical atoms>. In a <beta strand>, successive <amino acid side chains> alternate between the two faces in the idealized arrangement. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-4-proteins[Clark, Douglas and Choi: amino-acid R groups].

= Amino acid alpha carbon
{parent=Amino acid residue}
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_{\alpha}$}

The <amino acid alpha carbon> is the backbone <carbon> carrying the <amino acid side chain> in the usual <protein> <amino acid residue>. Marking successive <amino acid alpha carbons> is a useful way to trace a <polypeptide chain>, but omits intervening backbone <chemical atoms>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-4-proteins[Clark, Douglas and Choi: the central alpha carbon].

= Glutamic acid residue
{parent=Amino acid residue}
{title2=$\mathrm{Glu},\ \mathrm E$}

A <glutamic acid residue> is an <amino acid residue> represented by Glu or E in <polypeptide> sequence notation. GAA and GAG specify <glutamic acid residues> in the standard <genetic code>; the <ribosome> example uses GAA as its third <codon>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/Taxonomy/Utils/wprintgc.cgi#SG1[Elzanowski and Ostell, NCBI: standard genetic code, table 1]. The name denotes an incorporated chain position, as distinguished from a free <amino acid> <molecule>.

= Peptide bond
{parent=Polypeptide}
{wiki}
{title2=$-\mathrm{C}(=\mathrm{O})-\mathrm{NH}-$}

A <peptide bond> is the <covalent bond> between the carboxyl <carbon> of one <amino acid residue> and the amino <nitrogen> of the next. The displayed group shows the usual linkage, with the central carbon–nitrogen connection being the <peptide bond>. In an intact unbranched chain of $n$ <amino acid residues>, adjacent positions supply $n-1$ <peptide bonds>. Cutting one internal <peptide bond> produces two fragments; merely changing the chain's shape need not cut it. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-4-proteins[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: peptide bonds and protein structure].

= Protein backbone
{parent=Polypeptide}

The <protein backbone> is the repeating chain of <chemical atoms> that connects successive <amino acid residues>, including their <peptide bonds>. The attached groups that distinguish <amino acids> project from this shared framework. <Protein folding> changes the framework's spatial arrangement while ordinarily preserving its sequence connectivity. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/[Cooper: polypeptide backbone and attached groups].

= Protein structure
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

<Protein structure> describes both the chain's chemical organization and its spatial arrangement. Four levels answer different questions: <protein primary structure> gives the order; <protein secondary structure> gives recurring local patterns; <protein tertiary structure> gives one chain's overall arrangement; <protein quaternary structure> gives the assembly of multiple chains. These are levels of description, not four mandatory stages that every <protein> must pass through. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-4-proteins[Clark, Douglas and Choi: four levels of protein structure].

= Protein domain
{parent=Protein structure}
{wiki}

A <protein domain> is a distinguishable structural and functional region of a <protein>. A catalytic <protein domain> and a binding <protein domain> can carry out different tasks within one chain.

= SH2 domain
{c}
{parent=Protein domain}
{wiki}

An <SH2 domain> is a <protein domain> that recognizes a <phosphotyrosine residue> together with surrounding sequence features. This selective binding recruits a <protein> to a modified <receptor> or docking <protein>; it does not itself imply <catalysis>.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/Signaling through Enzyme-Linked Cell-Surface Receptors>[“Phosphorylated Tyrosines Serve as Docking Sites For Proteins With SH2 Domains”, Figure 15-53]{ref}

= Protein primary structure
{parent=Protein structure}
{wiki}

The <protein primary structure> specifies the ordered <amino acid residues> of each <polypeptide chain>, joined through <peptide bonds>. A chain is conventionally written from its <N-terminus> to its <C-terminus>. In the broader chemical sense, a complete <protein primary structure> also specifies <covalent bond> crosslinks between chain positions, including <protein disulfide bonds>; a bare sequence string leaves that information unstated. This describes chemical connectivity rather than the spatial arrangement of <protein tertiary structure> or <protein quaternary structure>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/P04843[IUPAC Gold Book: primary structure, sequence and chain crosslinking] and https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/AminoAcid/A1416.html[IUPAC-IUB, 3AA-16: writing residue sequences from the amino end]. Some conventions reserve <protein primary structure> for sequence alone and call the complete chemical connectivity the covalent structure; the distinction should be stated when comparing descriptions. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/misc/noGreek/ppep4.html[IUPAC-IUB polypeptide-conformation recommendations, section 6.1: the narrower sequence-only convention].

\Image[protein-primary-sequence-composition.png]
{title=Equal <amino acid composition> and equal termini do not determine <protein primary structure>}
{description=Original toy <polypeptide chains> MAGAMG and MMAAGG, read from the <N-terminus> to the <C-terminus>. Each contains two <methionine>, two <alanine> and two <glycine> <amino acid residues>, but their internal order differs. Letters denote chain positions rather than free <amino acids>; lines denote <peptide bonds>. Neither string is identified as a natural <protein> or a measured sequence.}

In this toy example M denotes <methionine>, A <alanine> and G <glycine>. Both <polypeptide chains> have six <amino acid residues>, five <peptide bonds>, an M at the <N-terminus> and a G at the <C-terminus>. Their <amino acid composition> is identical, yet position 2 is A in MAGAMG and M in MMAAGG. There are $6!/(2!2!2!)=90$ distinct orders with these counts, a <multinomial coefficient>. Even fixing those two end residues leaves $4!/(1!2!1!)=12$ orders. The counts constrain the answer without determining it.

For multiple <polypeptide chains>, record each sequence separately and specify any <protein disulfide bonds> between <cysteine> positions. Mature <insulin>, for example, has an A chain of 21 <amino acid residues> and a B chain of 30 <amino acid residues>. Their combined 51 residues give $(21-1)+(30-1)=49$ <peptide bonds>, rather than the 50 of one unbroken 51-residue chain. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/[Cooper, The Cell: insulin's two chains and cysteine crosslinks, figure 2.16]. A <protein disulfide bond> joins side groups; it does not concatenate the <C-terminus> of one chain with the <N-terminus> of another. The three <insulin> <protein disulfide bonds> connect A6 to A11 within the A chain, and A7 to B7 and A20 to B19 between chains. Positions are numbered from each chain's <N-terminus>. See https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15653430/[Replacement of the interchain disulfide bridge-forming amino acids A7 and B7 by glutamate impairs the structure and activity of insulin: native disulfide positions].

Why keep this information? <Protein primary structure> lets us distinguish changes of chemical identity from changes of shape, and compare corresponding positions between <proteins>. <Protein folding> can bring distant <amino acid residues> together at a <protein binding site> while preserving their sequence. The attractive idea is that a readable linear record can constrain a complicated three-dimensional object; it is not, by itself, a guarantee of a unique fold in every environment. See <protein folding> and the <ribonuclease A refolding experiment>. Merely bending an intact chain preserves its <protein primary structure>; <hydrolysis> of a <peptide bond>, or changing which positions a <protein disulfide bond> connects, changes its chemical connectivity.

= Amino acid composition
{parent=Protein primary structure}

The <amino acid composition> lists amounts or proportions of the different <amino acid residues> in a <protein> or specified <polypeptide chain>, without giving their order. The toy MAGAMG and MMAAGG each have counts M:2, A:2, G:2, or a proportion $1/3$ of each. Knowing composition therefore does not supply <protein primary structure>. Sanger's account begins by distinguishing the already available composition measurements from the largely unknown residue order. See https://garfield.library.upenn.edu/classics1985/A1985ADE5900002.pdf[Sanger's authored 1985 account of the move from composition to terminal-residue analysis].

= Glycine
{parent=Amino acid composition}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Gly},\ \mathrm G$}

<Glycine> is an <amino acid>, represented by Gly or G for an incorporated <amino acid residue>. Its <amino acid side chain> is a <hydrogen> <chemical atom>. It was identified at one <insulin> <N-terminus> in <Sanger's insulin sequencing experiment>. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/BlueBook/P10.html[IUPAC Blue Book, P-103.1: amino-acid names, symbols and structures].

= Phenylalanine
{parent=Amino acid composition}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Phe},\ \mathrm F$}

<Phenylalanine> is an <amino acid>, represented by Phe or F for an incorporated <amino acid residue>. It was identified at the other <insulin> <N-terminus> in <Sanger's insulin sequencing experiment>. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/BlueBook/P10.html[IUPAC Blue Book, P-103.1: amino-acid names and symbols].

= Cysteine
{parent=Amino acid composition}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Cys},\ \mathrm C$}

<Cysteine> is an <amino acid> whose <amino acid side chain> contains <sulfur>, represented by Cys or C for an incorporated <amino acid residue>. Two <cysteine> positions can be joined through a <protein disulfide bond>. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/BlueBook/P10.html[IUPAC Blue Book, P-103.1: cysteine's structure and symbols] and https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/[Cooper: cysteine crosslinks in proteins]. The C in a sequence means <cysteine>, whereas the C-end label in the toy diagram means <C-terminus>.

= Sanger's insulin sequencing experiment
{c}
{parent=Protein primary structure}

= Insulin sequencing experiment
{synonym}

The motivating question was experimental: how could one recover the order in a <protein> when breaking all its <peptide bonds> destroys that order? Frederick Sanger and colleagues used complementary tests on portions of <insulin>. The following is an explanation of their evidence, not a laboratory recipe.

First, mark ends before destroying the chains. Sanger used the colored reagent fluorodinitrobenzene, abbreviated FDNB, to label free amino groups. After acid <hydrolysis>, the attached label remained on the end-residue products. Separating these derivatives by their different movement through solvents and comparing them with reference derivatives made the labels readable. The <N-terminus> products identified <glycine> and <phenylalanine>. Sanger emphasized that finding a stable label and a way to separate its products were both essential. See https://garfield.library.upenn.edu/classics1985/A1985ADE5900002.pdf[Sanger, Citation Classic (1985), discussing The free amino groups of insulin (1945)]. FDNB also labels suitable free amino groups on <amino acid side chains>, so a labeled product is not automatically an <N-terminus> product. See https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/sanger-lecture.pdf[Sanger, The Chemistry of Insulin, Nobel lecture (1958), pp. 546–547].

Next, separate chains and preserve local order. <Oxidation> with performic acid broke the <protein disulfide bonds>, allowing A and B chains to be isolated. Partial <hydrolysis> and <enzyme> treatments produced fragments; these were separated and their composition and local sequences characterized. Overlaps between different fragment sets linked smaller sequences into longer ones. Many more fragments than minimally necessary were checked for consistency. See https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/sanger-lecture.pdf[Sanger's authored Nobel lecture, pp. 547–552: chain separation, fragment analysis and sequence reconstruction].

\Image[insulin-sequencing-evidence-workflow.png]
{title=Complementary evidence in <Sanger's insulin sequencing experiment>, with a toy overlap puzzle}
{description=Original schematic of tests on separate portions of <insulin>, followed by invented fragments from MAGAMG. The lower rows are aligned after deduction; the isolated fragments do not arrive with position labels. The cut patterns are chosen for teaching and assert no real <enzyme> specificity. This is neither a copied historical illustration nor measured experimental data.}

The toy fragments make the logic visible. Suppose one cut pattern yields MAG and AMG, and another yields MA, GA and MG. The first set alone permits MAGAMG or AMGMAG. The fragment GA in the second set crosses the MAG–AMG boundary in MAGAMG; it does not occur in AMGMAG. Among concatenations of the first set, therefore, the second set selects MAGAMG. This example assumes intact, correctly identified fragments from one homogeneous chain and known fragment membership in each cut pattern. Repeated stretches, missing fragments or sample mixtures can leave ambiguities; the picture illustrates inference from overlaps, not a universal guarantee of reconstruction.

Finally, determine the <protein disulfide bonds> separately. <Oxidation> used to separate whole chains erases their original connections. Sanger and colleagues instead studied fragments still joined by <protein disulfide bonds>, then oxidized the isolated joined fragments to identify their component pieces and locate the connected chain regions. An important trap was rearrangement of <disulfide bonds> during treatment: products can acquire connections absent from the starting <protein>. Their work therefore had to establish conditions that prevented rearrangement before interpreting a linked fragment as evidence. See https://www.nature.com/articles/1711025a0[Ryle and Sanger, A Disulphide Interchange Reaction, Nature 171, 1025–1026 (1953)] and https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/sanger-lecture.pdf[Sanger's Nobel lecture, pp. 553–554: preserving and identifying disulfide connections]. The result was an experimentally constrained chemical structure rather than a composition list or a photograph of a fold.

= Insulin
{parent=Protein primary structure}
{wiki}

<Insulin> is a <protein> <hormone>. Its mature form has two <polypeptide chains>; identifying their sequences and <protein disulfide bonds> provided the motivating example in <Sanger's insulin sequencing experiment>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/[Cooper: insulin as a two-chain hormone, figure 2.16].

A meal illustrates the <insulin> side of <blood glucose> <homeostasis>: rising <glucose> stimulates <beta cells> in the <pancreatic islets>, and released <insulin> favors uptake and storage of fuel. <Alpha cells> produce <glucagon>, while <delta cells> produce <somatostatin>, which inhibits release of both <insulin> and <glucagon>. The <pancreas> therefore contains interacting sensor and secretory <biological cells>, rather than one undifferentiated source of <hormones>. <Anatomy and Physiology 2e by J. Gordon Betts and others/17.9 The Endocrine Pancreas>{ref}

<Insulin> and <glucagon> are <peptide hormones>, built from <amino acid residues> joined by <peptide bonds>; <glucose> is a small <carbohydrate> <molecule>. A <hormone> changes <signal transduction>, not the identity of the fuel it regulates. Human mature <insulin> has 51 <amino acid residues> in two <polypeptide chains>. <Beta cells> process <proinsulin> to <insulin> and <C-peptide>, storing the products in <secretory granules> for <exocytosis>. Increased <glucose> <metabolism> raises the <ATP>-to-<ADP> <ratio>, tending to close <ATP-sensitive potassium channels>; <depolarization> and entry of <calcium> <ions> then trigger <exocytosis>. This is an important triggering pathway, with additional amplification and modulatory inputs.<Endotext/Insulin Biosynthesis, Secretion, Structure, and Structure-Activity Relationships>[pp. 1, 4–8, 10–11]{ref}

\Image[insulin-glucagon-tissue-control-circuit.png]
{title=<Insulin> and <glucagon> couple <pancreatic islets> to different tissue responses}
{description=Original qualitative <physiology> circuit. Each outer return closes a <negative feedback> loop for <blood glucose>; the arrows summarize direction, not measured gain or timing. <Insulin receptor> signaling changes <GLUT4> distribution in <skeletal muscle> and <adipose tissue>, while the <glucagon receptor> pathway principally supports <liver> <glucose> output. The local interactions within <pancreatic islets> explain why the two <hormones> are not independent switches.}

Binding to the <insulin receptor>, a <receptor tyrosine kinase>, starts <phosphorylation> of the <receptor> and <insulin receptor substrates>. Recruitment of <phosphoinositide 3-kinase> and activation of <Akt> transmit a major metabolic branch. In <skeletal muscle> and <adipose tissue>, that branch helps move <GLUT4> to the <cell membrane>, increasing <glucose> uptake by <facilitated diffusion>. <GLUT4> is a <membrane transport protein>, not an <insulin receptor>. <Insulin> also favors <glycogenesis> in <skeletal muscle> and the <liver>, and inhibits <lipolysis> in <adipose tissue>. In the <liver>, suppression of <glucose> output involves <enzyme> regulation, <gene expression>, and indirect changes in <substrate> supply. Its relative direct and indirect contributions are more complex than a single <Akt> arrow. <Insulin signaling in health and disease by Alan R. Saltiel>{ref}

<Insulin> is not a universal permission slip for <glucose> entry: <red blood cells> and much of the nervous system use uptake mechanisms that are not acutely dependent on <insulin>. Nor does the <liver> use the same <GLUT4> switch as <skeletal muscle>. This is why reduced <insulin> action can change <blood glucose> dramatically without stopping all <glucose> uptake. <The Genetic Landscape of Diabetes/Introduction to Diabetes>{ref}

<Diabetes> can disrupt production, response, and counterregulation. <Type 1 diabetes> usually involves destruction of <beta cells> and severe loss of endogenous <insulin>; <type 2 diabetes> combines <insulin resistance> with insufficient <beta cell> compensation. High circulating <insulin> can therefore coexist with impaired <insulin> action. Inappropriate <glucagon> release can add to excessive <liver> <glucose> output, and the response to low <blood glucose> can also be impaired: "more <glucagon>" is not a complete description of <diabetes>. These distinctions explain mechanisms, rather than prescribe treatment. <The Genetic Landscape of Diabetes/Introduction to Diabetes>{ref} <Endotext/Glucagon Physiology>{ref}

= Proinsulin
{parent=Insulin}
{wiki}

A single <polypeptide> precursor whose processing yields mature <insulin> and <C-peptide>; it is not the two-chain mature <hormone>. The biosynthetic distinction is described under <insulin>.

= C-peptide
{c}
{parent=Proinsulin}
{wiki}

= Glucose clamp technique
{parent=Insulin}
{wiki}

<Glucose clamp technique: a method for quantifying insulin secretion and resistance. by R. A. DeFronzo and others> separates two questions. In a hyperglycemic clamp, variable <glucose> infusion holds <blood glucose> above baseline while sampled <insulin> reports the response. The 1979 article's abstract reports an early release burst followed by a slower rise. In a euglycemic <insulin> clamp, infused <insulin> is held elevated and variable <glucose> infusion holds <blood glucose> at baseline. Frequent blood sampling, a <glucose> analyzer, and controlled infusion apparatus make the imposed <concentration> a maintained condition rather than an uncontrolled consequence. <Glucose clamp technique: a method for quantifying insulin secretion and resistance. by R. A. DeFronzo and others>{ref}<Endotext/Assessing Insulin Sensitivity and Resistance in Humans>[pp. 4–6]{ref}

The maintained <glucose> level controls an important <confounder>: a change in <blood glucose> would itself change uptake and <hormone> release. At a <steady state>, <conservation of mass> gives uptake equal to infused supply plus endogenous production, after accounting for losses. Consequently, infusion alone estimates uptake only if endogenous production is negligible and the other balance assumptions hold. Labelled <glucose> measurements can estimate residual endogenous production; matching infused <insulin> across comparisons matters. Whole-body uptake cannot by itself identify the responding tissue, and plasma <insulin> reflects both release and removal. This interpretation uses the accessible 1979 abstract and the later authored methods chapter; the original article's full methods were not available for inspection.<Endotext/Assessing Insulin Sensitivity and Resistance in Humans>[pp. 4–6]{ref}

= Protein secondary structure
{parent=Protein structure}
{wiki}

<Protein secondary structure> consists of recurring local arrangements of the <protein backbone>, notably the <alpha helix> and <beta sheet>. <Backbone hydrogen bonds> help stabilize these patterns. A <beta sheet> can bring together stretches far apart in the <protein primary structure>; local refers to the pattern of backbone geometry, not necessarily neighboring sequence positions. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/[Alberts et al.: alpha helices, beta sheets and backbone interactions].

= Alpha helix
{parent=Protein secondary structure}
{wiki}
{title2=$\alpha$}

An <alpha helix> is a coiled <protein secondary structure> with a repeated pattern of <backbone hydrogen bonds>. In the standard pattern, the backbone group of <amino acid residue> $i$ bonds to that of residue $i+4$: for example, positions 2 and 6. This is an additional spatial interaction, not a replacement <peptide bond> between those positions. Pauling, Corey and Branson proposed the helical arrangement in their 1951 paper by reasoning from the geometry of small <molecules>. See https://williams.chemistry.gatech.edu/course_Information/6572/papers/pauling_1951_helix.pdf[Pauling, Corey and Branson, The Structure of Proteins: Two Hydrogen-Bonded Helical Configurations of the Polypeptide Chain, PNAS 37, 205–211 (1951)].

= Beta sheet
{parent=Protein secondary structure}
{wiki}
{title2=$\beta$}

A <beta sheet> consists of extended stretches of <protein backbone> lying alongside one another, joined by <backbone hydrogen bonds>. The stretches can belong to different portions of the same <polypeptide chain> or to different chains.

Each extended stretch is a <beta strand>. Neighboring <beta strands> pack side by side, with repeated <backbone hydrogen bonds> between their backbone $\mathrm{C=O}$ and $\mathrm{N-H}$ groups: the <oxygen> is a <hydrogen bond acceptor>, and the backbone <nitrogen> with its attached <hydrogen> provides a <hydrogen bond donor>. These are interactions of the shared <protein backbone>, not merely contacts between <amino acid side chains>. Unlike an <alpha helix>, a <beta sheet> joins adjacent extended stretches rather than coiling one stretch around an axis. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-4-proteins[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 3.4: backbone groups in secondary structure].

Read each <beta strand> in the sequence direction from the <N-terminus> toward the <C-terminus>, even when it is an internal part of a longer <polypeptide chain>. In a <parallel beta sheet>, neighboring <beta strands> run in the same N→C direction; in an <antiparallel beta sheet>, they run in opposite N→C directions. A three-strand example can therefore have arrows right–right–right or right–left–right. A <beta sheet> can also mix the two arrangements. Direction does not identify chain membership: a single <polypeptide chain> can fold back to supply several <beta strands>, and different <polypeptide chains> can contribute to one <beta sheet>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: beta-sheet strand arrangements].

\Image[beta-sheet-strand-directions.png]
{title=<Parallel beta sheet> and <antiparallel beta sheet>: sequence directions}
{description=Three <beta strands> in each panel, with arrows showing N→C sequence direction. N-side and C-side labels indicate direction toward the <N-terminus> and <C-terminus>, not necessarily free chain ends. Dashed links symbolize interstrand <backbone hydrogen bonds>; they locate no <chemical atoms> and specify neither actual bond angles nor an exact pairing of <amino acid residues>. Chain connections are omitted.}

The pleated appearance comes from the geometry of the <protein backbone>. Successive <amino acid residues> carry <amino acid side chains> on alternating faces of an ideal <beta strand>, conventionally drawn above and below the sheet. The attachment positions are the <amino acid alpha carbons>. Thus a six-position cartoon has groups 1, 3 and 5 on one face and 2, 4 and 6 on the other. This alternation is a spatial pattern, not a rule that alternating positions must contain identical <amino acids>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-4-proteins[Clark, Douglas and Choi: side chains above and below the pleats].

\Image[beta-strand-pleating-and-side-chains.png]
{title=Pleating and alternating <amino acid side chains> along a <beta strand>}
{description=An original two-dimensional side-view cartoon of six consecutive <amino acid alpha carbons>, numbered in N→C sequence order, with generic R groups alternating above and below. Segments between marked positions summarize the intervening <protein backbone>, not every individual <covalent bond> or literal atomic coordinates. No <backbone hydrogen bonds> are drawn: their <hydrogen bond donor> and <hydrogen bond acceptor> groups would have to be shown separately from the marked <carbon> positions.}

Real <beta sheets> commonly twist and curve; pleating does not make them perfectly flat plates. Which <beta strands> are neighbors, how connecting regions join them, and how their faces pack are features of the three-dimensional <protein structure>. An alternating pattern in the <protein primary structure> alone neither establishes a <beta sheet> nor specifies a unique fold independently of surroundings and interacting <molecules>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK6824/[Breda et al., Protein Structure, Modelling and Applications: extended strands, sheet twist and three-dimensional organization] and <protein folding>.

Why this arrangement is useful: repeated <backbone hydrogen bonds> organize extended chain regions into a framework that can contribute to the interior of a folded <protein>. <Amino acid side chains> and connecting loops give that framework chemical variety and can shape a <protein binding site> for a <ligand>. Its attractive feature is the combination of a reusable backbone pattern with variable exposed groups: similar <beta sheets> can support different recognition surfaces. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/[Alberts et al.: beta-sheet cores and loops that form binding sites].

Historically, Linus Pauling and Robert B. Corey published \i[The Pleated Sheet, a New Layer Configuration of Polypeptide Chains] in PNAS 37, 251–256 (1951). They reasoned from <peptide bond> geometry and possible <backbone hydrogen bonds>, proposing an idealized layer rather than measuring every modern <beta sheet>. Their initial model should not be identified with all present-day <beta sheet> geometries; they published further pleated-sheet configurations later in 1951. See https://authors.library.caltech.edu/records/esvfg-ahe09[Pauling and Corey: original 1951 paper and scan], https://williams.chemistry.gatech.edu/course_Information/6572/papers/Pauling_PNAS-1951_sheet.pdf[accessible scan of the original paper]{ref}, and https://authors.library.caltech.edu/records/hq2fj-hqw64[Pauling and Corey, Configurations of Polypeptide Chains With Favored Orientations Around Single Bonds: Two New Pleated Sheets, PNAS 37, 729–740 (1951)].

= Beta strand
{parent=Beta sheet}
{title2=$\beta$}

A <beta strand> is an extended segment of <protein backbone> in the geometry characteristic of a <beta sheet>. Several <beta strands> can associate through <backbone hydrogen bonds>. A <beta strand> need not be an entire <polypeptide chain>.

= Parallel beta sheet
{parent=Beta sheet}

A <parallel beta sheet> has neighboring <beta strands> running in the same direction along their <amino acid residue> sequences, from the <N-terminus> toward the <C-terminus>.

= Antiparallel beta sheet
{parent=Beta sheet}

An <antiparallel beta sheet> has neighboring <beta strands> running in opposite N→C sequence directions. This specifies strand orientation rather than whether the <beta strands> belong to one <polypeptide chain> or several.

= Backbone hydrogen bond
{parent=Protein secondary structure}

A <backbone hydrogen bond> is a <hydrogen bond> involving groups of the <protein backbone>, typically a backbone <oxygen> <chemical atom> and a <hydrogen> <chemical atom> already joined by a <covalent bond> to backbone <nitrogen>. It can connect positions that are not adjacent along the <polypeptide chain>. The <peptide bonds> still determine which <amino acid residues> are consecutive. See the original structural argument cited under <alpha helix>.

= Protein tertiary structure
{parent=Protein structure}
{wiki}

The <protein tertiary structure> is the overall spatial arrangement of one <polypeptide chain>. It includes how its <protein secondary structures> and intervening regions fit together. For example, the schematic under <protein> places positions 4 and 9 near each other despite their separation in <protein primary structure>. Such cooperation can help construct an <active site>; sequence distance and spatial separation answer different questions. <Hydrophobicity>, <backbone hydrogen bonds> and other interactions help stabilize folds, and some <proteins> additionally contain <protein disulfide bonds>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/[Cooper: tertiary structure and interactions between chain regions].

= Protein binding site
{parent=Protein tertiary structure}

A <protein binding site> is a region of a <protein structure> that interacts with a <ligand>. Its arrangement of <amino acid residues> helps determine recognition; connecting loops supported by a <beta sheet> can contribute to it. An <enzyme>'s <active site> binds a <substrate> and accelerates a <chemical reaction>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26911/[Alberts et al.: protein binding sites and active sites].

= Protein disulfide bond
{parent=Protein tertiary structure}
{title2=$\mathrm{S-S}$}

A <protein disulfide bond> is a <disulfide bond> linking two <sulfur> <chemical atoms> carried by suitable <amino acid residues>. It can connect distant portions of a <polypeptide chain> or different chains. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/[Cooper: sulfur-containing residues and disulfide crosslinks]. The four internal <protein disulfide bonds> of <ribonuclease A> are central to the <ribonuclease A refolding experiment>; breaking these crosslinks need not break the <peptide bonds> along its <protein backbone>.

= Protein quaternary structure
{parent=Protein structure}
{wiki}

The <protein quaternary structure> is the arrangement of multiple <polypeptide chains> in a functional assembly. A single-chain <protein> has <protein tertiary structure> without requiring <protein quaternary structure>. For an exact bookkeeping example, two intact model chains of twelve <amino acid residues> each contain $2\times12=24$ residues and $2\times11=22$ <peptide bonds>. Assembly alone adds no <peptide bond> joining their ends. Stable functional assembly should also be distinguished from <protein aggregation>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26830/[Alberts et al.: proteins as assemblies of polypeptide subunits].

= Protein folding
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

<Protein folding> is the process by which a <polypeptide chain> acquires an organized spatial arrangement. The interactions of its <amino acid residues> with one another and the surroundings favor some arrangements over others. <Protein folding> can bring chemically useful groups together at an <active site> without changing their order along the <protein backbone>. Its importance is practical: knowing a sequence alone does not tell us whether a sample has the structure needed for function. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9843/[Cooper: acquiring functional protein structure].

The <ribonuclease A refolding experiment> showed that an isolated <protein> could recover its native properties without a pre-existing folded template under suitable laboratory conditions. Anfinsen interpreted this through a thermodynamic hypothesis: the native arrangement minimizes the whole system's <Gibbs free energy> in the appropriate environment. His Nobel lecture was delivered on 11 December 1972 and published in Science in 1973. The specified environment is part of the claim, not a disposable qualification. See https://web.iitd.ac.in/~amittal/1973_Anfinsen_Science.pdf[Anfinsen, Principles that Govern the Folding of Protein Chains, Science 181, 223–230 (1973): Nobel lecture, especially p. 223].

This does not imply that every <protein> rapidly folds unaided. <Molecular chaperones> can assist <protein folding> and reduce <protein aggregation>; required partners, surroundings and time still matter. Goloubinoff and colleagues' 1989 reconstitution experiments demonstrated assistance from two <molecular chaperones> and <ATP> in recovering an active <enzyme> from unfolded chains. This is experimental evidence that assistance can matter even when the chains already have their specified sequences. See https://www.nature.com/articles/342884a0[Goloubinoff, Christeller, Gatenby and Lorimer, 1989: chaperone-assisted reconstitution of an active enzyme].

= Chaperone
{disambiguate=protein}
{parent=Protein folding}
{wiki}

= Molecular chaperone
{synonym}

A <molecular chaperone> is a <protein> that assists other <proteins> in reaching or maintaining useful arrangements, for example by limiting unwanted association during <protein folding>. Assistance does not rewrite the client's <protein primary structure>. In a crowded <cytoplasm>, reducing <protein aggregation> can make successful <protein folding> more reliable. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9843/[Cooper: molecular chaperones and folding assistance].

= Denaturation
{disambiguate=biochemistry}
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

= Protein denaturation
{synonym}

<Protein denaturation> is the loss of a <protein>'s native spatial organization, often with loss of biological function. Changes in <temperature>, <pH> or surroundings can disrupt <protein secondary structure>, <protein tertiary structure> or <protein quaternary structure>. <Protein denaturation> ordinarily preserves the <peptide bonds> and <protein primary structure>; it is different from cutting the chain. Recovery by <protein folding> is possible in some cases, but removing the denaturing conditions does not guarantee that every <protein> recovers. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-4-proteins[Clark, Douglas and Choi: denaturation, environmental conditions and loss of function].

\Image[protein-folding-and-denaturation.png]
{title=<Protein folding>, <protein denaturation>, chain cleavage and <protein aggregation> are distinct events}
{description=Original toy schematic. Folded and unfolded drawings both preserve all twelve <amino acid residues> in the same order and all eleven <peptide bonds>. Cleavage removes one internal <peptide bond>, leaving two fragments with ten remaining <peptide bonds> in total. The separate <protein aggregation> drawing contains two associated chains with eleven <peptide bonds> each. Refolding is a qualified possibility, not a universal outcome. The drawings are not actual <protein> coordinates.}

In the diagram, unfolding preserves all eleven <peptide bonds>. Cleaving the connection between positions 6 and 7 leaves two six-residue fragments: each contains five <peptide bonds>, so there are $5+5=10$ in total. Simply cooling or otherwise restoring suitable conditions cannot recover the original chain by <protein folding> alone; the missing <covalent bond> would have to be remade. Conversely, <protein aggregation> can impede recovery without breaking any <peptide bond>. These distinctions explain why loss of function does not by itself reveal whether a <protein> unfolded, associated with other chains, or suffered chemical damage.

= Protein aggregation
{parent=Denaturation (biochemistry)}
{wiki}

<Protein aggregation> is the association of <proteins> into clusters, often involving exposed regions of partly folded or unfolded <polypeptide chains>. It can prevent recovery of normal function while leaving the <protein primary structures> intact. It is distinct from both unfolding one chain and the functional assembly described by <protein quaternary structure>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9843/[Cooper: partly folded chains, aggregation and chaperone protection].

= Ribonuclease A
{parent=Protein}

<Ribonuclease A> is an <enzyme> that catalyzes cleavage of <RNA>. The bovine pancreatic <ribonuclease A> studied by Anfinsen and colleagues has 124 <amino acid residues> and four internal <protein disulfide bonds>. It supplied a particularly informative test of the relationship between <protein primary structure>, <protein folding> and biological function: recovery could be measured through restored <enzyme> activity. See the primary sources under <ribonuclease A refolding experiment>.

= Ribonuclease A refolding experiment
{parent=Ribonuclease A}

Anfinsen, Haber, Sela and White's 1961 experiments began by treating bovine pancreatic <ribonuclease A> with <mercaptoethanol> in $8\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ <urea>. <Urea> promotes unfolding; <mercaptoethanol> breaks <protein disulfide bonds>. The <peptide bonds> along the chain remain intact. After separating the chain from those reagents, the authors allowed its reactive <sulfur> groups to form <protein disulfide bonds> in the presence of <molecular oxygen>, at <pH> $8.2$ and <temperature> $23$–$24\,{}^{\circ}\mathrm C$. They examined <protein> <concentrations> of $0.1$ and $2.0\,\mathrm{mg\,mL^{-1}}$. Measurements showed recovery of native properties under these conditions; the isolated major component had normal activity toward both <RNA> and a synthetic <substrate>. See https://williams.chemistry.gatech.edu/course_Information/6572_fall_2021/papers/ANFINSEN_1961.pdf[Anfinsen, Haber, Sela and White, The Kinetics of Formation of Native Ribonuclease during Oxidation of the Reduced Polypeptide Chain, PNAS 47, 1309–1314 (1961), pp. 1309–1311].

The observation supports the sufficiency of that intact sequence to recover native <protein structure> in the tested surroundings without a folded template. It does not establish that every <protein> refolds, that the original living <biological cell> needed no assistance, or that activity always returns immediately. <Protein folding>, forming <protein disulfide bonds> and recovering an <active site> are related processes rather than interchangeable measurements.

Anfinsen's lecture also discusses forming <protein disulfide bonds> while the chain remains in concentrated <urea>. This gave mostly inactive material; after removing <urea>, a small amount of <mercaptoethanol> permitted rearrangement toward the native form. As an exact combinatorial check, eight labeled reactive <sulfur> groups have $7\times5\times3\times1=105$ ways to be paired into four <protein disulfide bonds>: choose the partner of one group, then repeat with the unpaired groups. Only one pairing is the native one. Equal counting does not mean that all pairings are equally likely experimentally, nor that actual <protein folding> searches them uniformly. See https://web.iitd.ac.in/~amittal/1973_Anfinsen_Science.pdf[Anfinsen's Nobel lecture, 1973 publication: scrambled ribonuclease and the 105 pairings, p. 223].

= Lysozyme
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

<Lysozyme> is a <protein> <enzyme> that attacks the <peptidoglycan> of bacterial <cell walls>. Its <active site> is a cleft in the folded <protein structure>, where a suitable <substrate> can interact with positioned chemical groups. This illustrates why possessing the right <amino acid residues> is insufficient if their spatial arrangement is lost. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26911/[Alberts et al.: lysozyme as a worked example of protein binding and enzyme function].

A concrete structural example is Cheetham, Artymiuk and Phillips's 1992 study of hen egg-white <lysozyme> bound to a short <carbohydrate> that inhibits its action. The bound <carbohydrate> occupies part of the <active site>; it is not a reacting <substrate> in this complex. The study describes specific interactions and changes of shape accompanying binding. Thus a real binding site has chemical interactions and flexibility as well as a geometric outline. See https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/1569548/[Cheetham, Artymiuk and Phillips, 1992: structure of a lysozyme complex with bound inhibitor] and https://www.rcsb.org/structure/1HEW[RCSB PDB entry 1HEW: the authors' structure and bound three-unit carbohydrate]. The diagrams above illustrate the idea; their twelve-residue strings are not <lysozyme> sequences or coordinates.

= N-terminus
{c}
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

The N-terminus is the amino end of a <protein> or <amino acid> chain. Sequence notation lists residues from the <N-terminus> toward the <C-terminus>, the same direction in which <biological translation> builds a chain. The two-residue example under <gene expression> places <methionine> at the N-terminal end before any subsequent processing. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-4-proteins[OpenStax Biology 2e, 3.4: amino acids and polypeptide direction]{ref}

= C-terminus
{c}
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

The C-terminus is the carboxyl end of a <protein> or <amino acid> chain. During <biological translation>, each new residue is added at this end. The example under <gene expression> gives N–<methionine>–<alanine>–C, placing <alanine> at the <C-terminus>; its <stop codon> supplies no further residue. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-4-proteins[OpenStax Biology 2e, 3.4: polypeptide termini]{ref}

= Actin
{parent=Protein}

<Actin> is a <protein> whose monomers assemble into filaments. An idealized actin filament has a bending modulus and persistence length; inferring its effective elastic modulus from those measurements additionally requires a cross-sectional model.

= Epidermal growth factor
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

= EGF
{c}
{synonym}

<Epidermal growth factor> is a <protein> <ligand> for the <epidermal growth factor receptor>.

= Receptor
{disambiguate=biochemistry}
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

= Receptor
{synonym}

A biochemical <receptor> is a <protein> that recognizes a signal and couples its detection to a biological effect. Its binding partner is a <ligand>; recognition depends on compatible shape and chemical interactions at a binding site within the <protein structure>. Specificity is selective recognition, not a guarantee that only one <ligand> can bind. Binding also need not activate the <receptor>: a competing <ligand> may occupy it while preventing activation by another <ligand>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26911/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition: protein binding and specificity].

Compare a <cell-surface receptor>, accessible to an extracellular <ligand>, with an <intracellular receptor>, whose <ligand> must reach the interior. A <cell-surface receptor> can detect a <hydrophilic> <ligand> without transporting it through the <cell membrane>. In contrast, some membrane-permeable signals reach <intracellular receptors> that regulate <gene expression>. Location and downstream coupling are separate distinctions: an <ionotropic receptor> directly gates an <ion channel>, whereas a <metabotropic receptor> works through intracellular <signal transduction>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/9-1-signaling-molecules-and-cellular-receptors[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 9.1: receptor locations and mechanisms].

<Receptor binding occupancy> quantifies recognition separately from response. A <dissociation constant> measures binding in a specified equilibrium model; it does not by itself predict the cellular effect. This separation is useful when comparing signals that bind equally well but produce different responses, or when interpreting a binding experiment alongside a functional measurement.

= Ionotropic receptor
{parent=Receptor (biochemistry)}

An <ionotropic receptor> is a <receptor> in which binding of a <ligand> directly gates an <ion channel> within the same <protein> complex. The binding site detects the chemical signal; the pore supplies a path across the <cell membrane>. At a <chemical synapse>, a <neurotransmitter> therefore changes <synaptic conductance> without an obligatory intervening <G protein> or <second messenger>. <Neuroscience, 2nd edition edited by D. Purves and others/Two Families of Postsynaptic Receptors>{ref}

A familiar example is the nicotinic <acetylcholine> <receptor> of skeletal muscle: <acetylcholine> binding opens a pore passing <cations>, including <sodium> and <potassium>. The muscarinic <acetylcholine> <receptor> instead uses intracellular <signal transduction>; the same <neurotransmitter> can engage different mechanisms. Direct gating does not mean that an <ionotropic receptor> pumps <ions>: <electrochemical gradients> drive passage through the open <ion channel>. Nor does “ionotropic” specify excitation. The <reversal potential>, initial <membrane potential> and other conductances determine whether the effect favors an <action potential>, causes <hyperpolarization>, or supplies <shunting inhibition>. <Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/Ion Channels and the Electrical Properties of Membranes>{ref}

A concrete electrical approximation separates opening from drive. Let $N$ identical <ion channels> each have open-channel <electrical conductance> $\gamma$ and opening <probability> $p(t)$. At fixed <membrane potential> $V$, with common <reversal potential> $E_{\mathrm{rev}}$, the <expected value> of inward-positive <electric current> is
$$
I_{\mathrm{in}}(t)=N\gamma p(t)(E_{\mathrm{rev}}-V).
$$
For stipulated values $N=100$, $\gamma=20\,\mathrm{pS}$, $p=0.2$, $V=-60\,\mathrm{mV}$ and $E_{\mathrm{rev}}=0$, the mean open count is $20$, the population <electrical conductance> is $0.4\,\mathrm{nS}$ and $I_{\mathrm{in}}=24\,\mathrm{pA}$. One open <ion channel> carries $1.2\,\mathrm{pA}$. These are illustrative values, not measurements. At $V=E_{\mathrm{rev}}$, the net <electric current> vanishes despite open <ion channels>; neither binding nor opening alone determines the readout. The approximation omits changes in <ion> <concentrations> and voltage-dependent open-channel <electrical conductance>.

\Image[ionotropic-metabotropic-receptors-mechanisms.png]
{title=Direct <ionotropic receptor> gating and two routes of <metabotropic receptor> action}
{description=Original mechanism cartoons, not molecular structures or measured pathways. Solid blue arrows show one possible net movement of positive <ions> when the <electrochemical gradient> favors entry; dashed orange arrows relay a signal. In the first panel binding and pore functions belong to one <protein> complex. In the other panels a separate <ion channel> is regulated through a <G protein>, either locally or through an <enzyme>, <inositol trisphosphate> and stored <calcium>. Positions, shapes and arrow lengths encode no measured distances or times.}

A motivating experiment made the elementary electrical events visible. As Bert Sakmann recounts, chronically denervated frog muscle fibers had <acetylcholine>-sensitive <receptors> spread beyond the original nerve contact. Mild <enzyme> treatment exposed the <cell membrane>; a polished glass pipette sealed over a small patch to reduce background noise. Two intracellular electrodes held the local <membrane potential> through <voltage clamp>, while the patch pipette recorded tiny <electric currents>. Applying suberyldicholine, a <ligand> that activates the <acetylcholine> <receptor> and prolongs openings, revealed abrupt, approximately rectangular current events. Their amplitudes agreed reasonably with estimates from whole-membrane current fluctuations. This established discrete opening and closing events; it did not make every <ligand> response instantaneous. Background noise and finite recording bandwidth concealed sufficiently small or brief events.<Elementary Steps in Synaptic Transmission Revealed by Currents through Single Ion Channels by Bert Sakmann>[pp. 33–35]{ref}

Recording an intact membrane patch alone does not exclude intracellular relays. Tank, Huganir, Greengard and Webb supplied a complementary isolation test in 1983: they purified nicotinic <acetylcholine> <receptors> from an electric ray's electric organ, incorporated them into artificial <lipid> membranes, and fused small vesicles into larger vesicles by freezing and thawing. Sealed glass-pipette recordings from isolated membrane patches resolved functional <ion channels> with properties resembling those in muscle. Removing the intact <cytoplasm> and its signaling machinery supports the sufficiency of the <receptor> complex for channel function; an artificial <lipid> environment still cannot reproduce every native regulatory interaction.<Patch-recorded single-channel currents of the purified and reconstituted Torpedo acetylcholine receptor by D. W. Tank and others>[abstract]{ref}

Direct gating supports millisecond communication at many <chemical synapses>. Response duration also depends on <neurotransmitter clearance>, binding and channel-state transitions. Continued <ligand> exposure can prolong activity. Classify an <ionotropic receptor> by its direct connection between recognition and pore gating, rather than a universal duration cutoff. <Neuroscience, 2nd edition edited by D. Purves and others/Two Families of Postsynaptic Receptors>{ref}

= Metabotropic receptor
{parent=Receptor (biochemistry)}
{wiki}

A <metabotropic receptor> couples <ligand> recognition to intracellular <signal transduction>, which can regulate a separate <ion channel>, alter <enzyme> activity or change <gene expression>. In the usual <neurotransmitter> context, <metabotropic receptors> are <G protein-coupled receptors>: receptor activation is relayed by a <G protein>. There need not be a change in <membrane potential> at all. <Neuroscience, 2nd edition edited by D. Purves and others/Two Families of Postsynaptic Receptors>{ref}

Two routes should be distinguished. An activated <G protein> can regulate an <ion channel> locally at the <cell membrane>, without an obligatory diffusible <second messenger>. Alternatively, it can regulate an <enzyme> that changes the availability of a <second messenger>, such as <cAMP> or <inositol trisphosphate>. Downstream <proteins> then change channel opening, <phosphorylation> or other cellular activities. The <receptor>, <G protein>, <second messenger> and final target are distinct components; an activated <G protein> is a relay <protein>, rather than itself a small-molecule <second messenger>.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/General Principles of Cell Communication>[Figure 15-15]{ref}

Do not turn this useful <chemical synapse> classification into the claim that every <receptor> is a <GPCR>, or that every indirect response must pass through a <G protein>. For example, the <insulin> <receptor> is an <enzyme>-linked <cell-surface receptor> whose intracellular catalytic activity initiates <protein> <phosphorylation>. <Intracellular receptors> supply another category. “Metabotropic” commonly names the <GPCR> branch in <neuroscience>; the broader idea of biochemical coupling does not make all non-channel <receptors> structurally identical. <Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/Signaling through Enzyme-Linked Cell-Surface Receptors>{ref}

Separating recognition from execution allows <signal amplification> and branching. A persistently activated <receptor> can activate several relay <proteins>; a regulated <enzyme> can produce many <second messenger> <molecules>. As an explicitly invented count example, suppose one activated <receptor> activates four relay <proteins> during one interval, and each relay activates a distinct <enzyme> that produces fifteen <second messenger> <molecules> during the next interval. With sufficient substrates and no loss, this gives $4\times15=60$ <molecules>. Those counts do not specify a physiological gain or response duration. A directly gated <ion channel> also passes many <ions> while open, so amplification is not an exclusive property of <metabotropic receptors>: always name the input and output being compared. <The Cell: A Molecular Approach, 2nd edition by Geoffrey M. Cooper/Pathways of Intracellular Signal Transduction>{ref}

A particularly instructive electrophysiology experiment separated a chemical trigger from the <ion channel> carrying its eventual <electric current>. In 1987, Hirono, Ito and Sugiyama injected <messenger RNA> from rat brain into frog egg cells, then tested acquired responses to neurotensin and muscarinic <acetylcholine> stimulation. Under <voltage clamp> at $-60\,\mathrm{mV}$, neurotensin elicited transient, oscillatory inward <electric currents> after a delay of several tens of seconds. This delay refers to that response and preparation. <Reversal potential> measurements implicated increased permeability to chloride <anions>, rather than a pore specific to the applied <ligand>. Both neurotensin and <acetylcholine> responses were inhibited by pertussis toxin, which interferes with susceptible <G proteins>, and by intracellular EGTA, which binds free <calcium>. Injecting <inositol trisphosphate> mimicked the responses; a large prior dose of <inositol trisphosphate> reduced subsequent responses to either extracellular signal. These interventions implicate a shared intracellular relay and <calcium>-dependent channel opening.<Neurotensin and acetylcholine evoke common responses in frog oocytes injected with rat brain messenger ribonucleic acid by C. Hirono and others>[abstract]{ref}

The informative comparison with the nicotinic <acetylcholine> <receptor> experiment is mechanistic: an extracellular <ligand> can produce an inward <electric current> either by directly gating the pore or by mobilizing an intracellular <second messenger> and stored <calcium> that activate another pore. Intracellular <inositol trisphosphate> bypasses reception, whereas EGTA tests the need for free <calcium>. However, neither inhibition nor mimicry alone identifies every molecular connection: a perturbation can affect several targets, and an injected mediator can activate a pathway without proving that it is the only natural route. The egg cell's endogenous machinery and the mixed <messenger RNA> preparation also limit extrapolation to an intact <neuron>. The experiment establishes functional coupling in its preparation, rather than the exact molecular composition or timing of every <metabotropic receptor>.

Even <G protein>-dependent channel regulation need not involve a lengthy <second messenger> cascade. Pfaffinger and colleagues reported in 1985 that <acetylcholine>-evoked <potassium> channel activation in embryonic chick atrial cells required intracellular GTP, a <guanine>-containing <nucleotide>, and was abolished by pertussis-toxin pretreatment. Changes in cyclic <nucleotides>, including <cAMP>, did not account for the channel response. The report discusses intrinsic activation delays of roughly $30$–$100\,\mathrm{ms}$, illustrating relatively rapid <metabotropic receptor> action. These observations support a <G protein> coupling step without cyclic-nucleotide <second messengers>; they do not by themselves exclude every possible intermediary or prove the specific subunit-channel interaction.<GTP-binding proteins couple cardiac muscarinic receptors to a K channel by P. J. Pfaffinger and others>[abstract]{ref}

A small timing model makes the extra steps visible without inventing an experimental trace. Let $u(t)$ jump from zero to one at $t=0$ and remain on. For a direct-gating model output $d$, one relay variable $x$, and its downstream output $y$, take zero <initial conditions> and
$$
\tau_d\frac{dd}{dt}=u-d,\qquad \tau_x\frac{dx}{dt}=u-x,\qquad \tau_y\frac{dy}{dt}=x-y.
$$
All outputs are normalized <dimensionless variables>; they are neither recorded <electric currents> nor fitted <concentrations>. With illustrative <time constants> $\tau_d=2\,\mathrm{ms}$, $\tau_x=20\,\mathrm{ms}$ and $\tau_y=100\,\mathrm{ms}$, their <exponential function> solutions for $t\geq0$ are
$$
d(t)=1-e^{-t/\tau_d},\qquad x(t)=1-e^{-t/\tau_x},\qquad y(t)=1-\frac{\tau_y e^{-t/\tau_y}-\tau_x e^{-t/\tau_x}}{\tau_y-\tau_x}.
$$
Initially $d'(0)=1/\tau_d$, while $y'(0)=0$ because its relay must first build up. Both outputs eventually approach one. Equal normalized final values do not imply equal biological amplitudes or absence of <signal amplification>; normalization deliberately removes that question.

\Image[ionotropic-metabotropic-receptors-timing.png]
{title=One-step and two-step responses to a sustained input: an illustrative timing model}
{description=Original analytic model curves with stipulated <time constants> of $2$, $20$ and $100\,\mathrm{ms}$ and zero <initial conditions>. Both panels show the same functions on different time windows; the dashed curve is the intermediate relay. Values are normalized and do not represent measured <electric currents>, physiological receptor-specific timings, or universal boundaries between <ionotropic receptors> and <metabotropic receptors>. The model omits <signaling feedback>, saturation and input removal.}

The model's useful feature is the delayed buildup created by sequential relaxation, not its arbitrary numbers. Actual kinetics also depend on <ligand> delivery, relay deactivation, <neurotransmitter clearance> and the final target. A local <G protein> route can act promptly, while an <ionotropic receptor> can remain active during continued exposure or feed a longer-lasting intracellular response. The beauty of <metabotropic receptor> coupling is its flexibility: the same recognition event can adjust electrical responsiveness and other cellular tasks through different targets. To classify an observed response, combine timing with molecular identity and controlled perturbations.

= G protein-coupled receptor
{c}
{parent=Metabotropic receptor}
{wiki}

= GPCR
{c}
{synonym}

A <G protein-coupled receptor> is a <cell-surface receptor> that relays activation through a <G protein> on the intracellular side of the <cell membrane>. The activated <G protein> can regulate an <enzyme> or <ion channel>, so a <GPCR> supplies a concrete <metabotropic receptor> example: <ligand> recognition and the eventual response are connected through distinct components. Different <G proteins> couple to different targets. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26912/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition: signaling through G-protein-linked cell-surface receptors].

= Glucagon receptor
{parent=G protein-coupled receptor}
{wiki}

= Glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor
{parent=G protein-coupled receptor}
{wiki}

= GLP-1 receptor
{c}
{synonym}

= Cell-surface receptor
{parent=Receptor (biochemistry)}
{wiki}

A <cell-surface receptor> detects an extracellular signal at the <cell membrane>. Its changed state affects the intracellular side, coupling reception to <signal transduction> or directly to an <ion channel>'s opening.

= Receptor tyrosine kinase
{parent=Cell-surface receptor}
{wiki}

= RTK
{c}
{synonym}

A <receptor tyrosine kinase> combines extracellular <ligand> recognition with an intracellular <protein kinase> <protein domain>. A membrane-spanning segment connects these parts across the <cell membrane>: the signal changes the intracellular machinery without requiring the <ligand> to enter the <cytosol>. The <protein kinase> transfers a <phosphate> group from <ATP> to selected <tyrosine residues> on <proteins>, including the <receptor> itself.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/Signaling through Enzyme-Linked Cell-Surface Receptors>[“Activated Receptor Tyrosine Kinases Phosphorylate Themselves”: extracellular binding, intracellular catalytic domains and ATP-dependent phosphate transfer]{ref}

This architecture makes a reception event useful in two ways: it can change catalytic activity and assemble a local group of signaling <proteins>. <Trans-autophosphorylation> can create <phosphotyrosine residues> recognized by <SH2 domains>. Recruitment positions a downstream <enzyme> or relay <protein> near its partners. Neither every modified site nor every recruited <protein> has the same effect; association is a specific molecular connection, rather than a universal command to activate everything nearby.<Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/Signaling through Enzyme-Linked Cell-Surface Receptors>[“Phosphorylated Tyrosines Serve as Docking Sites For Proteins With SH2 Domains”, Figure 15-53: signaling enzymes, adaptors and negative regulators]{ref}

Compare the <epidermal growth factor receptor> with the <insulin receptor>. Their common topology does not impose one activation geometry or make assembly equivalent to activity. The <receptor phosphorylation assay> consequently needs more than evidence that two <protein> chains associate.

\Image[receptor-tyrosine-kinase-activation-architectures.png]
{title=<Receptor tyrosine kinase> architecture and distinct activation mechanisms}
{description=Original topology cartoons contrasting <EGFR> with the preassembled <insulin receptor>. Orange dots mark <phosphotyrosine residues>; S–S marks <protein disulfide bonds>. The <EGFR> panel separates asymmetric catalytic contact from partner-tail <phosphorylation>. Shapes, distances and drawn <ligand> counts are explanatory, rather than atomic structures or binding stoichiometry.}

= Epidermal growth factor receptor
{parent=Receptor tyrosine kinase}
{wiki}

= EGFR
{c}
{synonym}

In an illustrative <EGFR> activation route, <EGF> stabilizes an associated pair of <receptor> chains. Asymmetric contact between their <protein kinase> <protein domains> activates one catalytic partner without requiring its activation-loop <phosphorylation>. Subsequent <phosphorylation> of cytoplasmic tails creates recruitment sites. Preassociated inactive <EGFR> populations can also occur: the cartoon's separated starting chains are not a universal initial condition.<Cell signaling by receptor-tyrosine kinases by Mark A Lemmon and Joseph Schlessinger>[Lemmon and Schlessinger, “Mechanisms of Receptor Activation” and “Allosteric activation of TKDs”, Figure 2F]{ref}

= Insulin receptor
{parent=Receptor tyrosine kinase}
{wiki}

The mature <insulin receptor> already contains two extracellular $\alpha$ chains and two membrane-spanning $\beta$ chains, joined through <protein disulfide bonds>, before <insulin> binds. <Insulin> induces rearrangement of this $(\alpha\beta)_2$ assembly. Partner-mediated <phosphorylation> of the inhibitory activation loop permits increased <protein kinase> activity. Ligand-induced creation of a previously absent dimer is therefore an unsuitable universal explanation.<Cell signaling by receptor-tyrosine kinases by Mark A Lemmon and Joseph Schlessinger>[“Mechanisms of Receptor Activation” and “TKD autoinhibition by the activation loop: insulin and FGF receptors”, Figure 2E]{ref}

= Insulin receptor substrate
{parent=Insulin receptor}
{wiki}

An <insulin receptor substrate> is a docking <protein> phosphorylated by the activated <insulin receptor>. Its <phosphotyrosine residues> recruit downstream signaling <proteins>, including <SH2 domain>-containing components of <phosphoinositide 3-kinase>. This connects reception to the pathway described under <insulin>, rather than making the <insulin receptor substrate> another extracellular <ligand>.<Cell signaling by receptor-tyrosine kinases by Mark A Lemmon and Joseph Schlessinger>[“Linking RTK activation to cell signaling”: IRS docking proteins and Figure 3A]{ref}

= Receptor phosphorylation assay
{parent=Receptor tyrosine kinase}

A <receptor phosphorylation assay> asks whether, and how much, a specified <receptor> is modified by <phosphorylation> under defined conditions. It is distinct from a binding assay for <receptor binding occupancy>, a test of <protein kinase> activity on a supplied <substrate>, and a test of downstream <signal transduction>. Those measurements answer related but different questions.

For a <Western blot> implementation, compare matched samples with <ligand> omitted or added, detect a specified phosphorylated <epitope>, and separately detect total <receptor> abundance. Preserve the sampled state by stopping further <enzyme> reactions during sample preparation; otherwise <protein kinase> or <protein phosphatase> activity after sampling can change the result. A general anti-<phosphotyrosine residue> <antibody> recognizes many modified <proteins>; a site-selective <antibody> gives a narrower readout, but still needs <Western blot antibody specificity controls>.

Reason through the controls before interpreting the difference. Loss of the proposed signal after receptor-specific <gene deletion> supports <protein> identity. Treatment with a <protein phosphatase>, alongside preservation of the total-<receptor> signal, tests phosphate-dependent recognition. <Western blot loading controls> and <Western blot transfer controls> address sample recovery; a usable detection range addresses <Western blot response saturation>. Lower signal after perturbing <protein kinase> activity supports dependence on that perturbation, but alternative targets and altered <receptor> abundance can complicate the inference. These are proposed assay comparisons, not outcomes attributed to the historical membrane experiment.

\Image[receptor-phosphorylation-assay-control-logic.png]
{title=Controls connecting a <receptor phosphorylation assay> to a defensible inference}
{description=Original reasoning diagram for a <Western blot> implementation. Separate comparisons address <protein> identity, phosphate-dependent <epitope> recognition, recovery and detection response. Boxes prescribe questions; they contain no measured bands, observed experimental outcomes or synthetic time course.}

An original idealized measurement model explains the normalization. Let $N$ be the retained number of <receptor> <molecules>, $f$ the fraction carrying the selected modified <epitope>, and $c_p,c_t>0$ fixed detection gains. After subtracting their respective backgrounds, suppose the phosphorylated and total-<receptor> signals lie in a linear response range:
$$
A=c_pNf,\qquad B=c_tN,\qquad \frac{A}{B}=\frac{c_p}{c_t}f.
$$
Dividing by total <receptor> cancels $N$ under these assumptions. It differs from total-<protein> <Western blot normalization>, whose denominator includes many other <proteins>. For invented values $c_p=c_t=1$, samples with $(N,f)=(100,0.2)$ and $(200,0.2)$ give $A=20$ and $40$, but both give $A/B=0.2$. Twice the phospho signal here means twice the retained <receptor> amount, with no change in fractional modification. Unequal recovery, changed <epitope> accessibility, nonzero uncorrected background or <Western blot response saturation> invalidates the cancellation. Unknown relative gains prevent reading an absolute modified fraction directly from the <ratio>.

= Ligand-dependent membrane phosphorylation experiment
{parent=Receptor phosphorylation assay}

A motivating experiment used isolated membranes from A-431 <biological cells>, which supplied abundant <EGFR>. Separate membrane aliquots were incubated at $0\,{}^{\circ}\mathrm C$ with <ATP> carrying radioactive <phosphorus> in its terminal <phosphate> group, plus <magnesium> or <manganese> <ions>. Radioactivity in material recovered by <chemical acid>-induced <precipitation (chemistry)> supplied the readout. Adding <EGF> beforehand enhanced incorporation relative to the unstimulated preparation; this placed a ligand-dependent biochemical response in a cell-free membrane system.<Epidermal Growth Factor by Stanley Cohen>[Cohen, Epidermal Growth Factor, printed p. 339: A-431 membrane aliquots, terminally labeled ATP, divalent ions and trichloroacetic acid-insoluble incorporation]{ref}

The follow-up comparisons separated increased addition from suppressed removal: <phosphate> removal was rapid but unaffected by <EGF>, and anti-<EGF> <antibody> reversed activation by removing the <ligand>. These observations support reversible regulation of membrane-associated <protein kinase> activity.<Epidermal Growth Factor by Stanley Cohen>[printed p. 341, numbered observations 1–4: rapid activation, dephosphorylation, lack of soluble kinase release and anti-EGF reversal]{ref} Total incorporation alone cannot identify which <protein> or <amino acid residue> received the label, prove intrinsic receptor <catalysis>, or predict an intact <biological cell>'s response.

The same account records an instructive identification error: receptor <phosphorylation> was initially assigned to the wrong <amino acid residue> because released modified <amino acids> migrated together in the electrophoretic <electric field>. Reanalysis identified <phosphotyrosine residues>. Retained radioactivity is evidence about the assay's separation and detection, not unique chemical identity.<Epidermal Growth Factor by Stanley Cohen>[printed p. 341: co-migration in the electrophoretic system and subsequent identification of tyrosine phosphorylation]{ref}

= Reversible receptor phosphorylation model
{parent=Receptor tyrosine kinase}

<Protein kinase> activity adds modification, while <protein tyrosine phosphatase> activity can remove it. An original one-site model shows why a <receptor phosphorylation assay> cannot determine either process from a single modified fraction. Let $f(t)\in[0,1]$ be the fraction of a fixed <receptor> population carrying one selected <phosphotyrosine residue>. Stipulate constant effective first-order <rate constants> $k_+,k_->0$, with <ATP> availability absorbed into $k_+$, and no <receptor> synthesis, degradation or trafficking:
$$
\frac{df}{dt}=k_+(1-f)-k_-f,\qquad
f_* = \frac{k_+}{k_++k_-},\qquad
\tau=\frac{1}{k_++k_-}.
$$
Here $f_*$ is the <steady state> fraction and $\tau$ the <time constant>. The <ordinary differential equation> has the <exponential function> solution
$$
f(t)=f_*+(f(0)-f_*)e^{-t/\tau}.
$$
The balance equation and a single relaxation step give this result; it is an illustrative model, rather than a fit or a claim about every <RTK>.

With stipulated <rate constants> $(k_+,k_-)=(2,1)\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}$, $f_*=2/3$ and $\tau=1/3\,\mathrm{min}$. Doubling both <rate constants> leaves $f_*=2/3$ but changes $\tau$ to $1/6\,\mathrm{min}$. The balanced <phosphate> turnover per site,
$$
J_*=k_+(1-f_*)=k_-f_*=\frac{k_+k_-}{k_++k_-},
$$
doubles from $2/3$ to $4/3\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}$. Identical <steady state> modification therefore permits different catalytic fluxes. Removing the addition term instead gives $f(t)=f(0)e^{-k_-t}$; this illustrates why a perturbation that stops <protein kinase> activity can expose removal dynamics, subject to the model's unchanged-removal assumption. A maintained <steady state> with ongoing <ATP> consumption is not <chemical equilibrium>.

= Intracellular receptor
{parent=Receptor (biochemistry)}
{wiki}

An <intracellular receptor> recognizes a <ligand> inside a <biological cell>, including in the <cytoplasm> or <cell nucleus>. Some <intracellular receptors> regulate <transcription> after binding a membrane-permeable signal; an <intracellular receptor> does not universally imply direct control of <DNA>. See the receptor-location reference under <receptor>.

= Receptor binding occupancy
{parent=Receptor (biochemistry)}
{title2=$\theta$}

<Receptor binding occupancy> is the fraction of binding sites occupied by a <ligand>. Consider reversible, noncooperative binding at <chemical equilibrium>, with homogeneous independent sites: every site has the same binding properties, and binding at one site does not change binding at another. Let $L$ denote free <ligand>, $R$ an unoccupied <receptor> site and $RL$ the bound complex. In this dilute <concentration> model,
$$
L+R\rightleftharpoons RL,\qquad K_d=\frac{[L][R]}{[RL]},\qquad R_T=[R]+[RL],\qquad \theta=\frac{[RL]}{R_T}=\frac{[L]}{K_d+[L]}.
$$
Square brackets denote <concentrations>; $R_T$ is the total site <concentration>. Here the <dissociation constant> $K_d$ has <concentration> units. Use free <ligand> in excess so binding negligibly depletes it; only under that approximation may the supplied total <ligand> <concentration> substitute for $[L]$. See https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5521016/[Analysis of Receptor–Ligand Interactions: equilibrium binding and saturation].

For an illustrative <dissociation constant> $K_d=10\,\mathrm{nM}$, where $1\,\mathrm{nM}=10^{-9}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, free <ligand> <concentrations> $1$, $10$ and $100\,\mathrm{nM}$ give $\theta=1/11\approx0.091$, $\theta=1/2=0.5$ and $\theta=10/11\approx0.909$, respectively. At $[L]=K_d$, exactly half the sites are occupied in this model. Raising $[L]$ tenfold from $10$ to $100\,\mathrm{nM}$ raises occupancy from $50\%$ to about $91\%$, rather than tenfold: the finite pool of sites causes saturation.

\Image[receptor-binding-occupancy.png]
{title=<Receptor binding occupancy> for a toy <dissociation constant> of $10\,\mathrm{nM}$}
{description=The same independent-site <chemical equilibrium> model on linear and logarithmic <ligand> <concentration> axes. Marked examples are $1/11$, $1/2$ and $10/11$. The logarithmic axis changes the apparent curve shape without introducing cooperative binding. The vertical axis is fraction occupied, not cellular response.}

A short derivation uses the <law of mass action>: at <chemical equilibrium>, association balances dissociation, giving $[RL]=[L][R]/K_d$. Substitute this into $\theta=[RL]/([R]+[RL])$ and cancel $[R]$ to obtain the displayed formula. The limiting values follow directly: zero free <ligand> gives zero occupancy, and increasing $[L]$ far above $K_d$ drives occupancy toward one.

<Receptor binding occupancy> is not automatically the fraction of maximal response. <Signal amplification>, differing effects of bound <ligands>, and downstream saturation can change the relationship. Consequently, $[L]=K_d$ specifies half occupancy here; it does not universally specify either half-maximal or maximal cellular response. These numbers describe a toy binding model, not measured responses of every <receptor>. See https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11267233/[Higham and Colquhoun, 2024: the affinity–efficacy problem].

= G protein
{c}
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

A <G protein> is a <protein> switch whose activity depends on the bound <guanine> <nucleotide>. The three-subunit <G proteins> coupled to <G protein-coupled receptors> relay activation to other <proteins>; they are distinct from the <receptors> themselves. See the Alberts reference under <G protein-coupled receptor>.

= Ligand
{disambiguate=biochemistry}
{parent=Protein}
{wiki}

= Ligand
{synonym}

A biochemical <ligand> is an <ion> or <molecule> that binds a <protein>. For a <receptor>, a <ligand> may activate signaling, prevent activation, or have another effect. This usage concerns <protein> binding; it is distinct from the coordination-chemistry use of the word. See the protein-binding reference under <receptor>.

= Morphogen
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

A <morphogen> is a signal whose spatial <concentration> helps specify different <biological cell> fates or behaviors during <biological development>. A <morphogen gradient> provides a positional cue: <biological cells> at different distances from a source experience different local <concentrations> and can follow different developmental pathways. A spatial <concentration> variation alone does not establish that a substance is a <morphogen>; its graded developmental effects matter. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26825/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition (2002): morphogens as graded developmental signals].

The beautiful idea is that one distributed signal can help organize several regions of a developing body. A continuous <morphogen gradient> can supply the input for distinct domains of <gene expression>, rather than requiring a separate signal for every position. A <morphogen response threshold> is a useful idealization of that interpretation: high, intermediate and low <concentrations> are assigned different outcomes. The <morphogen reaction-diffusion equation> describes one way to generate a <morphogen gradient>; the equation is a model of transport and reaction, while the <morphogen> is the signal being transported.

Detection and interpretation also depend on the receiving <biological cell>. For an extracellular <morphogen>, <receptors> and <signal transduction> connect the local <concentration> to a response; <receptor binding occupancy> is only one step in that connection. The same <morphogen gradient> can be interpreted differently in different tissues because their developmental context differs. Thus a <morphogen response threshold> summarizes a particular response in a particular context, rather than being a universal property of the signal. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9968/[Scott F. Gilbert, Developmental Biology, 6th edition (2000): positional information and context-dependent interpretation].

An important experimental example came in 1988. Wolfgang Driever and Christiane Nüsslein-Volhard used antibodies to examine the bicoid <protein> in early fruit-fly embryos. They reported a <concentration> profile approximately described by an <exponential function>, highest at the front, with the <protein> concentrated in <cell nuclei>. Their abstract proposed <diffusion> from localized <messenger RNA> and dispersed degradation as a likely origin of the <morphogen gradient>, rather than proving a unique transport mechanism. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3383244/[Driever and Nüsslein-Volhard, A gradient of bicoid protein in Drosophila embryos, Cell 54 (1988), 83–93: primary abstract]{ref}

In a companion study, Driever and Nüsslein-Volhard genetically changed the amount and distribution of bicoid <messenger RNA>, measured the resulting <protein> <morphogen gradient>, and compared developmental maps. Increased local bicoid <protein> levels shifted prospective anterior structures toward the rear; decreased levels shifted them toward the front. This linked a measured <concentration> profile to positional effects in <biological development>. The toy calculations below illustrate that logic with arbitrary numbers, not measured bicoid parameters. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3383245/[Driever and Nüsslein-Volhard, The bicoid protein determines position in the Drosophila embryo in a concentration-dependent manner, Cell 54 (1988), 95–104: primary abstract]{ref}

= Morphogen gradient
{parent=Morphogen}
{title2=$c(x)$}

A <morphogen gradient> is a spatially varying <concentration> profile of a <morphogen>. The profile $c(x)$ specifies the local amount; its mathematical <gradient>, $c'(x)$ in one dimension, specifies how rapidly that amount changes with position. In the simplest positional interpretation, a <biological cell> responds to its local <concentration>, without having to measure the <gradient> across itself. Production, transport and removal together determine a <morphogen gradient>.

For a worked example, take a stationary one-dimensional tissue occupying $x\geq0$, constant <diffusion coefficient> $D>0$, and uniform <first-order reaction> removal at rate $k>0$. At <steady state>, this specialization of the <morphogen reaction-diffusion equation> gives the <ordinary differential equation> and <boundary conditions>
$$
D\frac{d^2c}{dx^2}-kc=0,\qquad c(0)=c_0,\qquad \lim_{x\to\infty}c(x)=0.
$$
Here $D$ has units of length squared per time, $k$ has units of inverse time, and $c_0$ is a prescribed source <concentration>. The <Dirichlet boundary condition> fixes the <concentration> at $x=0$. It does not prescribe a source <flux>; any required <flux> follows from the solution. These assumptions define a toy <morphogen gradient>, not a universal account of transport during <biological development>.

Define the <diffusion length> $\ell=\sqrt{D/k}$. Substitution into the <ordinary differential equation> shows that its general solution is $A e^{x/\ell}+B e^{-x/\ell}$. The vanishing <boundary condition> at infinity forces $A=0$, and the source <Dirichlet boundary condition> forces $B=c_0$. Hence
$$
c(x)=c_0e^{-x/\ell},\qquad \ell=\sqrt{D/k}.
$$
Over every additional <diffusion length>, the <concentration> falls by the same factor $e^{-1}$. Faster <diffusion> increases $\ell$; faster removal decreases $\ell$. This makes the <diffusion length> a useful bridge between microscopic transport and the spatial reach of a <morphogen gradient>.

Choose $c_0=100$ arbitrary <concentration> units and $\ell=100\,\mu\mathrm m$, where $1\,\mu\mathrm m=10^{-6}\,\mathrm m$. Then $c(x)=100e^{-x/(100\,\mu\mathrm m)}$: at $x=0$, $100\,\mu\mathrm m$ and $200\,\mu\mathrm m$, the <concentrations> are $100$, approximately $36.79$ and approximately $13.53$. The following diagram combines this <morphogen gradient> with the idealized responses calculated under <morphogen response threshold>.

\Image[morphogen-gradient-and-thresholds.png]
{title=A toy <morphogen gradient> and three idealized developmental bands}
{description=The <steady state> profile uses $c_0=100$ arbitrary <concentration> units and <diffusion length> $100\,\mu\mathrm m$. <Morphogen response thresholds> at $50$ and $20$ place the boundaries at approximately $69.31$ and $160.94\,\mu\mathrm m$. The lower strip shows assigned fates A, B and C, not measured tissues. The plotted domain ends at $350\,\mu\mathrm m$; the mathematical domain continues to infinity.}

= Morphogen response threshold
{parent=Morphogen}
{title2=$\theta$}

A <morphogen response threshold> is a <concentration> level used to distinguish developmental responses to a <morphogen>. In an idealized threshold model, otherwise equivalent <biological cells> exposed to $c\geq\theta$ take one response and those exposed to $c<\theta$ take another. Two <morphogen response thresholds> can assign three bands: fate A for $c\geq50$, fate B for $20\leq c<50$, and fate C for $c<20$, all in the toy arbitrary <concentration> units. The A/B/C labels specify a response rule, not named cell types or a claim that every <morphogen> produces sharp boundaries. See the positional-information model in Gilbert's reference under <morphogen>.

For the decreasing <morphogen gradient> $c(x)=c_0e^{-x/\ell}$ and $0<\theta<c_0$, solve $c(x_\theta)=\theta$ using the <natural logarithm>:
$$
x_\theta=\ell\ln\!\left(\frac{c_0}{\theta}\right).
$$
With $c_0=100$ and <diffusion length> $\ell=100\,\mu\mathrm m$, the <morphogen response thresholds> $50$ and $20$ give
$$
x_{50}=100\ln2\,\mu\mathrm m\approx69.3147\,\mu\mathrm m,\qquad x_{20}=100\ln5\,\mu\mathrm m\approx160.9438\,\mu\mathrm m.
$$
Thus fate A occupies $0\leq x\leq x_{50}$, fate B occupies $x_{50}<x\leq x_{20}$, and fate C occupies $x>x_{20}$ in this idealization. The equality convention belongs to the response rule. If $\theta=c_0$, the crossing is at the source; if $\theta>c_0$, that <morphogen response threshold> is never reached anywhere in the tissue.

Now double the prescribed source <concentration> from $c_0$ to $2c_0$, keeping $D$, $k$, <diffusion length> $\ell$ and a fixed <morphogen response threshold> $0<\theta<c_0$ unchanged. The whole <morphogen gradient> doubles, but the boundary moves by an additive distance:
$$
\Delta x_\theta=\ell\ln\!\left(\frac{2c_0}{\theta}\right)-\ell\ln\!\left(\frac{c_0}{\theta}\right)=\ell\ln2.
$$
For $\theta=20$, the crossing moves from $160.9438\,\mu\mathrm m$ to $100\ln10\,\mu\mathrm m\approx230.2585\,\mu\mathrm m$, a shift of $69.3147\,\mu\mathrm m$. Every fixed <morphogen response threshold> below the original source <concentration> shifts by this same distance in the toy model. The example compares two <Dirichlet boundary conditions>, $c(0)=100$ and $c(0)=200$; doubling an experimentally supplied source <flux> is a different boundary specification.

\Image[morphogen-source-strength-boundary-shift.png]
{title=Doubling source <concentration> shifts a toy <morphogen response threshold> boundary}
{description=Profiles with source <concentrations> $100$ and $200$ share <diffusion length> $100\,\mu\mathrm m$. The same <morphogen response threshold> $20$ is crossed at approximately $160.94$ and $230.26\,\mu\mathrm m$. The arrow marks the predicted $69.31\,\mu\mathrm m$ shift. Source strength here means prescribed boundary <concentration>, with all numbers arbitrary and illustrative.}

These calculations are useful because they separate changes in source <concentration>, <diffusion length> and response rule. Their limits are equally concrete: growing tissues change the geometry, a <transient response> can precede <steady state>, molecular fluctuations can blur a boundary, and <signaling feedback> or altered transport can change the <morphogen gradient> or its interpretation. A local instantaneous <morphogen response threshold> need not capture exposure duration, <signaling adaptation> or the receiving <biological cell>'s state. Experiments on cultured human <biological cells> found different responses to the level and rate of change of developmental signals, illustrating why signal dynamics can matter. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6398983/[Heemskerk et al., Rapid changes in morphogen concentration control self-organized patterning in human embryonic stem cells, eLife 8 (2019), e40526: primary experiments]{ref} Quantitative measurements of bicoid <protein> and its downstream <gene expression> also make precision a question for experiment, rather than something guaranteed by drawing threshold lines. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2253670/[Gregor et al., Probing the limits to positional information, Cell 130 (2007), 153–164: primary measurements]{ref} <Morphogen gradients> provide one mechanism for spatial patterning in <biological development>, alongside other signals and interactions.

= Twin
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

Twins are two offspring from the same gestation. <Monozygotic twins> arise from one fertilized egg, while <dizygotic twins> arise from two. The distinction supplies the biological premise for <twin studies>.

= Biosignature
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

A <biosignature> is observable chemical, morphological or other evidence attributable to biological activity after assessing plausible abiotic explanations. It can concern present or past life on Earth or elsewhere. An <exoplanet biosignature> is the remote planetary specialization, requiring atmospheric and environmental context.

= Mortality
{parent=Biology}

Mortality describes deaths in a defined population during a specified observation window. A cumulative death <probability> is distinct from a death rate per person-time. Comparing treatments requires the same endpoint definition, time origin and ascertainment in both arms.

= Neuroscience
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

The study of nervous systems, from the properties of individual <neurons> to circuits, development, behavior and information processing.

= Voltage clamp
{parent=Neuroscience}
{wiki}

A <voltage clamp> uses electrical feedback to hold a preparation's <membrane potential> near a commanded value while measuring the <electric current> required to maintain it. Holding voltage separates changes in <electrical conductance> from changes in electrical driving force. Imperfect voltage control and finite recording bandwidth limit the inference. The muscle experiment under <ionotropic receptor> used separate intracellular electrodes for voltage control and a sealed patch pipette for recording local <electric current>.<Elementary Steps in Synaptic Transmission Revealed by Currents through Single Ion Channels by Bert Sakmann>[pp. 33–35; Figure 1]{ref}

= Extracellular single-unit recording
{parent=Neuroscience}

<Extracellular single-unit recording> uses an <electrode> outside a <neuron> to detect electrical events attributed to that unit's <action potentials>. It records a <spike train>, not the intracellular <membrane potential> or every input <synapse>. https://xbio-live.s3.amazonaws.com/narratives/57358/Hubel%20and%20Wiesel%201959_Guided%20paper.pdf[Hubel and Wiesel (1959), p. 575: extracellular recording methods]{ref}

= Primary visual cortex
{parent=Neuroscience}
{title2=V1}

The <primary visual cortex>, often abbreviated V1, is an early cortical stage of visual processing. Its <neurons> have <receptive fields>: changing a stimulus in a particular part of the visible scene can change their <spike trains>. <Orientation selectivity> adds a further distinction: at the same location, a properly aligned bar can evoke a strong response while a rotated bar evokes little. <Simple cells> make the dependence on local position and <visual contrast> especially clear. See the original experiments under <orientation selectivity> and <simple cell>.

In their 1962 study, David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel recorded from forty cats under thiopental anesthesia, maintained light anesthesia while monitoring cortical electrical activity, and used succinylcholine to stabilize the eyes. White-light patterns were projected onto a screen $1.5\,\mathrm m$ away. <Extracellular single-unit recording> with tungsten <electrodes> sampled individual units; <receptive fields> were mapped separately for each eye. This connected controlled stimulus geometry to individual <spike trains>. https://www.gatsby.ucl.ac.uk/teaching/courses/tn1-2020/additional/systems/JPhysiol-1962-Hubel-106-54.pdf[Hubel and Wiesel, Receptive fields, binocular interaction and functional architecture in the cat's visual cortex (1962), pp. 107–108: methods]{ref}

These acute, anesthetized-cat recordings do not directly measure human perception or responses during awake behavior. The useful idea is a local description of structure: a population can distinguish where a feature lies, which way it is aligned, and which side is brighter. The <signed spatial filter model of a simple cell> turns that idea into a computable example. One <neuron>'s preferred stimulus does not establish recognition of an entire object. Likewise, <orientation selectivity> in one <neuron> and an orderly map of preferences across <primary visual cortex> are different findings: rodents can have strong <orientation selectivity> without the orderly arrangement typical of some other <species>. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17720489/[Dario L. Ringach, Specificity and randomness in the visual cortex (2007): authored review of species differences]{ref} The existing <correlation-based development of visual cortical maps> and <orientation-map self-organization> sections describe hypotheses about how organization develops.

= Correlation-based development of visual cortical maps
{parent=Primary visual cortex}

Consider afferent activities $z_i$, cortical activity $y_a$ and a constrained <Hebbian learning> rule $\dot w_{ai}=\epsilon\langle y_az_i\rangle-\Lambda_a$. If $y_a=\sum_{b,j}K_{ab}w_{bj}z_j$, then $\langle y_az_i\rangle=\sum_{b,j}K_{ab}w_{bj}C_{ji}$, where $C$ is the input correlation matrix. Thus input correlations and lateral interactions select growing weight modes, while <synaptic normalization> and bounds stop unlimited growth. The model tests how organization can emerge without assigning every final cortical feature in advance.

= Complex cell
{parent=Primary visual cortex}
{wiki}

A <complex cell> typically responds selectively to an oriented edge or bar over a finite spatial region with less dependence on spatial phase and contrast polarity than a <simple cell>. It need not have the simple cell's separated linear ON and OFF subregions. Phase tolerance is local, not invariance to arbitrary displacement outside its <receptive field>.

= Quadrature energy model of a complex cell
{parent=Complex cell}
{title2=$E=(k_e*I)^2+(k_o*I)^2$}

Two oriented linear filters in quadrature have responses $A\cos\varphi$ and $A\sin\varphi$ to a suitably matched narrowband grating. Their squared sum is $A^2$, independent of phase $\varphi$, while orientation and spatial-frequency selectivity remain. A nonlinear output function or normalization can transform this energy into firing rate. Ideal quadrature gives an exact phase identity for the matched stimulus; finite approximate filter pairs need not have perfect invariance for arbitrary images.

= Pooling model of a complex cell
{parent=Complex cell}

Rectify outputs of <simple cells> with similar orientations and varying phases or positions, then sum or pool them. Opposite-polarity subunits allow response to both light and dark features. Pooling gives approximate phase and position tolerance over the subunit coverage; its extent and contrast dependence depend on the pooling rule.

= Ocular dominance column
{parent=Primary visual cortex}
{wiki}

A cortical region whose visual responses preferentially reflect input from one eye. Competitive activity-dependent models can explain segregation of correlated inputs, while anatomical constraints and species differences also matter.

= Activity-dependent ocular dominance segregation
{parent=Ocular dominance column}

Two eye-specific afferent populations compete under <Hebbian learning> and <synaptic normalization>. In a simplified symmetric two-eye model, the eye-difference mode $d_a=w_{aL}-w_{aR}$ evolves with a linear operator containing $K_{ab}(C_{\rm same}-C_{\rm cross})_{ab}$. Dominant spatial modes can produce alternating eye preference, while saturation sets finite contrast. The interaction width, arbor constraints and correlation structure influence the column scale. This is a mechanistic hypothesis whose predictions can be tested, not a unique inference from the existence of columns.

= Orientation selectivity
{parent=Primary visual cortex}
{wiki}

<Orientation selectivity> is a systematic dependence of a visual <neuron>'s response on the axis of a bar, edge or stripe pattern presented in its <receptive field>, under specified stimulus conditions. Here the <angle> $\theta$ measures the stripe axis from horizontal: $0^\circ$ is horizontal and $90^\circ$ vertical. An axis has no arrow, so $\theta$ and $\theta+180^\circ$ describe the same orientation. A preference for one direction of movement across that axis is an additional property; the 1962 recordings included both equal and unequal responses to opposite movements. https://www.gatsby.ucl.ac.uk/teaching/courses/tn1-2020/additional/systems/JPhysiol-1962-Hubel-106-54.pdf[Hubel and Wiesel (1962), p. 112: orientation versus opposite movements]{ref}

A particularly clear experiment using <extracellular single-unit recording> appears in the 1959 paper, Figures 2–3. A bright $1^\circ\times8^\circ$ slit was centered on the same <receptive field> and rotated through successive <angles>; each presentation lasted one second. The vertical slit strongly increased the extracellularly recorded <spike train>, whereas the horizontal slit was ineffective. Small-spot tests had identified <ON and OFF receptive-field subregions>, with a central ON region and opposing flanks: rotation illuminated less of the center and more of the flanks. Geometry and location therefore mattered together. These dimensions belong to that recorded unit, not to every <neuron>. https://xbio-live.s3.amazonaws.com/narratives/57358/Hubel%20and%20Wiesel%201959_Guided%20paper.pdf[Hubel and Wiesel, Receptive fields of single neurones in the cat's striate cortex (1959), pp. 578–579, Figures 2–3: primary recordings]{ref}

An <orientation tuning curve> summarizes a controlled <angle> sweep. Keep location, size and <visual contrast> fixed; for a stripe pattern also specify <spatial frequency> and <phase>. The same <signed spatial filter model of a simple cell> can produce strong <orientation selectivity> and strong dependence on <phase>, as the following original toy illustrates. Its smooth curves are calculated predictions, not digitized or invented biological measurements.

\Image[orientation-selectivity-angle-phase.png]
{title=<Orientation selectivity> and <phase> sensitivity in a toy <Gabor filter>}
{description=A chosen <Gaussian function> times a <cosine> supplies the signed filter. At fixed <phase> its normalized output peaks for vertical stripes; shifting the same stripes changes the signed drive and the rectified output. The <angle> labels refer to the stripe axis, perpendicular to the direction in which brightness varies. Spatial units and all parameters are arbitrary; curves use an infinite-domain <integral>, while the small image panels show only a crop. See <signed spatial filter model of a simple cell> for the calculation.}

= Orientation tuning curve
{parent=Orientation selectivity}
{title2=$r(\theta)$}

An <orientation tuning curve> plots a specified response against the stimulus-axis <angle> $\theta$, with the other stimulus variables controlled. A practical count summary for <extracellular single-unit recording> is
$$
\widehat r(\theta)=\frac{1}{mT}\sum_{k=1}^{m}N_k(\theta),
$$
where $N_k(\theta)$ counts <action potentials> in the same duration $T>0$ on trial $k$, and $m$ is the number of repeats at each <angle>. This estimates spikes per unit time in that window; the window must specify whether it covers onset, sustained stimulation, or both. Record a blank-background response separately, and report whether it has been subtracted. Randomizing <angle> order and repeating each condition helps separate <orientation selectivity> from changes over time, including <neural adaptation>. This is a proposed measurement procedure, not a claim that the original experiments used this estimator.

A <simple cell>'s <orientation tuning curve> can also change when the stimulus shifts. An <angle> sweep at fixed <phase>, a sweep averaging over <phase>, and a sweep of moving stripes ask different questions. Consequently, a single preferred <angle> does not specify the whole <receptive field>. The toy curve under <orientation selectivity> holds <phase> fixed and plots dimensionless output, rather than spikes per second.

= Orientation-map self-organization
{parent=Orientation selectivity}

Correlation-based learning among spatially arranged ON and OFF inputs, with <synaptic competition> and lateral interactions, can select elongated <simple cell> receptive fields. Neighbor interactions coordinate preferred orientations across cortex, while competition allows different orientations to persist. Selectivity within one cell and a spatial map across many cells are separate predictions, and neither follows from unbounded <Hebbian learning> alone.

= Simple cell
{parent=Primary visual cortex}
{wiki}

A <simple cell> is a visual cortical <neuron> whose <receptive field> has spatially separable <ON and OFF receptive-field subregions>. Elongated neighboring subregions give a preferred orientation and a sensitivity to the location and polarity of <visual contrast>. “Simple” describes the response organization; it does not describe a simpler <cell body> or a particular shape of <neuron>.

Hubel and Wiesel's 1962 classification emphasized four observations: distinguishable <ON and OFF receptive-field subregions>, summation within each region, antagonism between regions, and qualitative prediction of responses to differently shaped stimuli from those regions' arrangement. A slit fitting an elongated center was more effective than a smaller spot; a larger patch could invade opposing flanks and lose effectiveness. An edge separating bright and dark regions could be effective when its boundary matched the boundary between subregions. These were observations about <spike trains>, rather than an assertion of exact <linearity>. https://www.gatsby.ucl.ac.uk/teaching/courses/tn1-2020/additional/systems/JPhysiol-1962-Hubel-106-54.pdf[Hubel and Wiesel (1962), pp. 109–112: simple-field criteria and slit/edge experiments]{ref}

The following deliberately coarse <signed spatial filter model of a simple cell> makes the geometry easy to count. A centered bright bar contributes positively at five sites. Rotating it preserves its area but mixes positive and negative weights; shifting it preserves its orientation but changes which weights it samples. The model is useful because it predicts distinct effects of these three manipulations instead of treating any bright stimulus as equivalent.

\Image[orientation-selectivity-signed-bars.png]
{title=A signed toy <simple cell> filter separates orientation, location and <visual contrast> polarity}
{description=The $5\times5$ chosen weight map has a positive central column and negative flanks. Bright, unchanged and dark image values are $+1$, $0$ and $-1$. The <dot product> gives signed drive $D$; a <rectified linear unit> gives toy output $R$. Centered, rotated, uniform, shifted-bright and shifted-dark examples give $D=1,0,0,-1/4,1/4$. Colors and weights are illustrative, not a measured <receptive field> or a map of identified <synapses>.}

A <simple cell> is not an ideal linear sensor. Its <spike train> is nonnegative in event count, and <neural adaptation> can change responsiveness. The <signed spatial filter model of a simple cell> supplies a useful approximation to spatial drive and an explicit output rule. Comparing predictions with unseen stimuli is more informative than assuming the rule from the name “simple”. In contrast, the existing <complex cell> models explore tolerance to <phase> and local displacement.

= Signed spatial filter model of a simple cell
{parent=Simple cell}
{title2=$D=\sum_{i,j}K_{ij}C_{ij},\quad R=\max(0,D)$}

A <signed spatial filter model of a simple cell> assigns a weight $K_{ij}$ to each sampled location and takes its <dot product> with the <visual contrast> image $C_{ij}$. Positive weights favor bright features; negative weights favor dark features. The signed drive
$$
D=\sum_{i,j}K_{ij}C_{ij},\qquad R=\max(0,D)
$$
is a <linear function> of the image, while the <rectified linear unit> output $R$ is nonlinear. $D$ and $R$ below are arbitrary model units. To obtain a predicted firing rate one would need a calibrated gain, background response and possibly a different output function. The model does not generate a <spike train>; a <linear-nonlinear-Poisson cascade model> adds a stochastic event-generation assumption.

For the first diagram, every one of five rows has weights
$$
(K_{i1},K_{i2},K_{i3},K_{i4},K_{i5})=(-1/20,-1/20,1/5,-1/20,-1/20).
$$
A bright central column, with $C_{i3}=1$ and zero elsewhere, gives $D=5/5=1$. A bright middle row gives $D=-1/20-1/20+1/5-1/20-1/20=0$; a uniformly bright patch also gives $D=0$, since every row sums to zero. Move the bright column one site right: $D=5(-1/20)=-1/4$, so $R=0$. Make that shifted column dark instead: $D=1/4$ and $R=1/4$. Thus an OFF-like weight can increase output for darkness. A negative weight is a descriptive sensitivity, not evidence for a particular inhibitory <synapse>.

For a smooth example use the real, even <Gabor filter>
$$
K(x,y)=\exp\!\left(-\frac{x^2}{2\sigma_x^2}-\frac{y^2}{2\sigma_y^2}\right)\cos(qx),\qquad C_{\theta,\varphi}(x,y)=\cos\!\bigl(q(x\sin\theta-y\cos\theta)+\varphi\bigr).
$$
The <Gaussian function> localizes the weights; the <cosine> supplies alternating polarity. Choose $\sigma_x=1/2$, $\sigma_y=1$ and $q=\pi$ in arbitrary spatial units. The stripes' <spatial frequency> is $q/(2\pi)=1/2$ cycles per unit. Their axis makes <angle> $\theta$ with horizontal, while brightness changes across the perpendicular axis. $\varphi$ is their spatial <phase>. Normalize the full-plane <integral> of $KC_{\theta,\varphi}$ by its value at $\theta=\pi/2$, $\varphi=0$. The <Gaussian integral> and the product identity for <cosines> give
$$
D(\theta,\varphi)=A(\theta)\cos\varphi,\qquad R(\theta,\varphi)=\max(0,D(\theta,\varphi)),
$$
where
$$
A(\theta)=\frac{\exp\!\left[-\frac{q^2}{2}\left(\sigma_x^2(\sin\theta-1)^2+\sigma_y^2\cos^2\theta\right)\right]+\exp\!\left[-\frac{q^2}{2}\left(\sigma_x^2(\sin\theta+1)^2+\sigma_y^2\cos^2\theta\right)\right]}{1+\exp(-2\sigma_x^2q^2)}.
$$
At $\varphi=0$, horizontal stripes give $R\approx0.004159$, $60^\circ$ stripes give $R\approx0.286741$, and vertical stripes give $R=1$. At the same vertical orientation, $\varphi=\pi/2$ gives $D=R=0$, while reversing the polarity with $\varphi=\pi$ gives $D=-1$ and $R=0$. The preferred axis persists while the response changes dramatically with position. Shifting the vertical stripes by a quarter of their wavelength changes <phase> by $\pi/2$ up to sign, without rotating them.

These are two different toy filters, not discretizations of the same fitted <receptive field>. The discrete filter exactly rejects a uniform contrast patch; this <Gabor filter> need not reject uniform contrast because its weight <integral> need not vanish. Neither toy includes stimulus history, recurrent interactions, saturation or a measured response baseline. Their value is a transparent prediction: <orientation selectivity> can emerge from spatial summation, and it does not by itself imply tolerance to <phase>.

= Neuron
{parent=Neuroscience}
{wiki}

= Nerve cell
{synonym}

A <neuron> is a specialized <biological cell> that receives, integrates and transmits signals. Its shape separates places where inputs arrive from places where outputs reach other <biological cells>: this functional asymmetry is <neuronal polarity>. Understanding a <neuron> therefore means connecting its anatomy to changes in <membrane potential>, rather than treating it as a wire that merely forwards every input.

In a common <multipolar neuron>, branching <dendrites> receive inputs at <synapses>; the <soma> contains the <cell nucleus> and most major <organelles>; the <axon> carries signals to distant or nearby targets; and <axon terminals> form output contacts. All these regions belong to one <biological cell> enclosed by a continuous <cell membrane>. An <axon> can branch, allowing one <neuron> to influence several receiving <biological cells>.<Anatomy and Physiology 2e by J. Gordon Betts and others/12.2 Nervous Tissue>[§12.2, Parts of a Neuron: nucleus, organelles, processes, branching axon, initial segment and terminals]{ref}

At an input <chemical synapse>, a <neurotransmitter> acts on a <receptor>, changing an <electric current> directly or through <signal transduction>.<Neuronal Dynamics: From Single Neurons to Networks and Models of Cognition by Wulfram Gerstner and others/3.1 Synapses>[§3.1, opening paragraphs: ionotropic and metabotropic receptors and postsynaptic currents]{ref} A resulting <synaptic potential> contributes to <synaptic integration> in the <dendrites> and <soma>. In many spiking <neurons>, sufficiently strong <depolarization> recruits <voltage-gated ion channels> in the <axon initial segment>, initiating an <action potential> that propagates along the <axon>. At an output <chemical synapse>, <calcium> entry through <voltage-gated ion channels> promotes <synaptic vesicle> <exocytosis>, releasing <neurotransmitter>. An <action potential> is thus regenerated electrically along a <cell membrane>; it is not a packet of <neurotransmitter> travelling from the <dendrites> to the <axon terminals>.<Neuroscience, 2nd edition edited by D. Purves and others>[Chemical Synapses, Figure 5.3: voltage-gated calcium entry and synaptic vesicle exocytosis]{ref}

\Image[neuron-polarized-anatomy-and-diversity.png]
{title=Neuronal anatomy, usual signaling direction and alternative shapes}
{description=Original schematic, not microscopy and not to scale. The upper <multipolar neuron> identifies <dendrites>, the <soma>, the <cell nucleus>, the <axon initial segment>, the <axon> and <axon terminals>. Solid arrows show a common input-to-output route; a dashed arrow indicates possible propagation of an <action potential> back toward <dendrites>. The lower sketches compare a <bipolar neuron> and a <pseudounipolar neuron>, whose <soma> lies off the main transmission route. Shapes and relative dimensions are illustrative.}

The compartments are specializations, not exclusive jobs. Inputs can contact the <soma> or <axon> as well as <dendrites>; <dendrites> can support active <voltage-gated ion channels>. Paired recordings from a <pyramidal neuron>'s <soma> and <dendrite> or <axon> established that an <action potential> can begin in the <axon> and propagate back into the <dendrites>. The usual output direction therefore does not make every electrical signal strictly one-way.<Active propagation of somatic action potentials into neocortical pyramidal cell dendrites by Greg J. Stuart and others>{ref} An <electrical synapse>, which directly couples <cytoplasms> through <gap junctions>, also differs from the sending-and-receiving arrangement of a <chemical synapse>.<Neuronal Dynamics: From Single Neurons to Networks and Models of Cognition by Wulfram Gerstner and others/1.1 Elements of Neuronal Systems>[§1.1.3: electrical and chemical synapses]{ref}

Nor does every <neuron> have the upper diagram's shape. A <bipolar neuron> has two main processes, one <dendrite> and one <axon>; a <multipolar neuron> has several <dendrites>; and a <pseudounipolar neuron> has a single process from the <soma> that divides into peripheral and central branches. In the last arrangement, sensory signals can travel past the <soma> rather than obligatorily through it. These shape categories do not specify every <neuron>'s <receptor>, connectivity or firing behavior.<Anatomy and Physiology 2e by J. Gordon Betts and others/12.2 Nervous Tissue>[§12.2, Types of Neurons and Figure 12.9: multipolar, bipolar and human pseudo-unipolar cells; sensory projection past the cell body]{ref} Some <neurons> in the <retina> communicate through graded changes in <membrane potential> without generating <action potentials>.<Neuronal Dynamics: From Single Neurons to Networks and Models of Cognition by Wulfram Gerstner and others/1.1 Elements of Neuronal Systems>{ref}

\b[Why timing changes the computation.] <Synaptic integration> can combine inputs at different locations, called <spatial summation>, and inputs arriving at different times, called <temporal summation>. A minimal example uses the <leaky integrate-and-fire model> with the illustrative parameters already given there: resting and reset <membrane potential> $-65\,\mathrm{mV}$, firing threshold $-50\,\mathrm{mV}$, <electrical resistance> $R=100\,\mathrm{M\Omega}$, <capacitance> $C=200\,\mathrm{pF}$, <time constant> $\tau=RC=20\,\mathrm{ms}$ and <neuronal refractory period> $2\,\mathrm{ms}$. These are chosen model values, not measured properties of a particular <neuron>. Before firing, a constant inward <electric current> $I$ starting from rest gives
$$
V(t)=-65\,\mathrm{mV}+RI\left(1-e^{-t/\tau}\right).
$$
The <exponential function> expresses the competition between charging the <cell membrane>'s <capacitance> and leakage through its <electrical resistance>.<Neuronal Dynamics: From Single Neurons to Networks and Models of Cognition by Wulfram Gerstner and others/1.3 Integrate-And-Fire Models>[§1.3.1–§1.3.3, equations (1.5), (1.7), (1.12) and (1.13): passive integration, step response, threshold and reset]{ref}

Two $100\,\mathrm{pA}$ inputs, each lasting $40\,\mathrm{ms}$, produce different outputs depending on their timing. Either input alone reaches only $-56.3534\,\mathrm{mV}$. Applied together, they give $I=200\,\mathrm{pA}$ and reach threshold after $20\ln4\,\mathrm{ms}\approx27.7259\,\mathrm{ms}$, producing one <action potential> event during the pulse. If instead the second input begins $20\,\mathrm{ms}$ after the first ends, the earlier <depolarization> has partly decayed: the second pulse peaks at approximately $-55.9229\,\mathrm{mV}$ and neither pulse produces an <action potential>. The two-input protocols deliver the same total <electric charge>, $8\,\mathrm{pC}$, yet only the coincident protocol fires. Counting total input alone misses this timing dependence.

\Image[neuron-input-timing-and-spike-output.png]
{title=The same total input can yield different neuronal outputs}
{description=Synthetic exact <leaky integrate-and-fire model> responses, not empirical recordings. A single $100\,\mathrm{pA}$ pulse is below threshold; two coincident pulses produce one <action potential> event, shown as a tick rather than a biological waveform; two separated pulses deliver the same total <electric charge> as the coincident pair but produce no events. The lower traces show <membrane potential>, its reset and the brief <neuronal refractory period>. All inputs are prescribed <electric currents> in a single compartment, without a modeled <synaptic conductance> or spatial <dendrite>.}

This example makes a <neuron> a detector of sufficiently clustered input, not a perfect logical gate: different parameters change the result. Actual <dendrites> introduce location-dependent filtering and active responses, and an <inhibitory postsynaptic potential> or <shunting inhibition> can change whether another input succeeds. The single-compartment <leaky integrate-and-fire model> omits these mechanisms.<Neuronal Dynamics: From Single Neurons to Networks and Models of Cognition by Wulfram Gerstner and others/1.4 Limitations of the Leaky Integrate-and-Fire Model>[§1.4.2 and §1.4.4: shunting inhibition, spatial structure and active dendritic responses]{ref} Across connected <neurons>, branching <axons> distribute outputs while converging <synapses> combine inputs; <spike trains> can support <temporal coding> and <rate coding>, and <synaptic plasticity> changes the influence of an input. Connectivity and cellular dynamics together determine the computation.<Neuronal Dynamics: From Single Neurons to Networks and Models of Cognition by Wulfram Gerstner and others/1.1 Elements of Neuronal Systems>[§1.1.1–§1.1.2: axonal branching and spike trains]{ref}<Neuronal Dynamics: From Single Neurons to Networks and Models of Cognition by Wulfram Gerstner and others/3.1 Synapses>[§3.1.3: changes in synaptic efficacy with stimulation history]{ref}

\b[Testing the connection between structure and function.] <Whole-cell recording> connects anatomy to a measured <membrane potential>. In a published mouse protocol, a two-photon microscope guides solution-filled glass pipettes to <neurons>; an amplifier and bath reference electrode complete the electrical recording apparatus. A high-resistance seal forms against the <cell membrane>, then brief suction opens the membrane patch beneath the tip. In <current clamp>, the amplifier supplies a chosen <electric current> and records the resulting <membrane potential>, unlike <voltage clamp>, which controls voltage and measures <electric current>. The investigators used $500\,\mathrm{ms}$ current steps of several amplitudes to characterize excitability and firing, and negative current pulses to estimate input <electrical resistance>.<Multiple Two-Photon Targeted Whole-Cell Patch-Clamp Recordings From Monosynaptically Connected Neurons in vivo by Jean-Sébastien Jouhanneau and others>{ref}

The observable is a voltage trace, including graded responses and <action potentials>. Comparing injected <electric current> with that trace tests how input becomes output; it does not identify every underlying <ion channel>. The protocol checks access <electrical resistance>, which can distort voltage and <action potential> measurements, and includes intervals without stimulation to record spontaneous activity. In a teaching replication, repeated zero-input trials and monitoring baseline drift would provide further controls; these are recommended comparisons, not additional results attributed to the paper. A recording at the <soma> also cannot establish the local voltage everywhere in a branching <dendrite>, and this anesthetized preparation does not establish behavior in every awake <neuron>.<Multiple Two-Photon Targeted Whole-Cell Patch-Clamp Recordings From Monosynaptically Connected Neurons in vivo by Jean-Sébastien Jouhanneau and others>{ref}

A complementary experiment used simultaneous <whole-cell recordings> from the <soma> and an apical <dendrite> or <axon> of the same <pyramidal neuron> in a brain slice. The relative onset times of <action potentials>, together with <sodium> currents in excised <dendrite> membrane patches, supported axonal initiation followed by active propagation into <dendrites>. Pairing sites within one <neuron> is a useful comparison because it avoids confusing differences between cells with differences between compartments. Instrument timing and comparable recording quality would be essential controls when reproducing the inference. Only the published abstract was accessible here, so no unverified timing values, drug controls or channel distributions are supplied.<Active propagation of somatic action potentials into neocortical pyramidal cell dendrites by Greg J. Stuart and others>{ref}

= Neuronal polarity
{parent=Neuron}

= Synaptic integration
{parent=Neuron}

<Synaptic integration> is the conversion of inputs at a <neuron>'s <synapses> into a changing <membrane potential> and, in a spiking <neuron>, the timing or absence of <action potentials>. <Spatial summation> concerns inputs at different locations; <temporal summation> concerns inputs at different times. A useful question is whether individually ineffective inputs can act together, and whether an <inhibitory postsynaptic potential> changes that cooperation. The answer depends on location, timing and the receiving <neuron>'s state.

A <synaptic potential> is a voltage response, whereas <synaptic conductance> describes activated <ion channels>. With inward-positive <electric current>, the contribution of input $j$ is
$$
I_j(t)=g_j(t)(E_j-V).
$$
Here $g_j$ is its <synaptic conductance>, $E_j$ its <reversal potential> and $V$ the local <membrane potential>. The driving force $E_j-V$ is an <electric potential difference>. An <EPSP> generally brings the <membrane potential> toward a more positive <reversal potential>, but its driving force shrinks during <depolarization>. An <IPSP> need not produce <hyperpolarization>: a <reversal potential> above rest but below the <neuronal firing threshold> can produce <depolarization> while opposing a larger excursion. At $E_j=V$, the added <synaptic conductance> initially carries zero <electric current> yet can cause <shunting inhibition> of another input.<Neuronal Dynamics: From Single Neurons to Networks and Models of Cognition by Wulfram Gerstner and others/3.1 Synapses>[§3.1, equation (3.1), and §3.1.1: conductance, reversal and voltage-dependent sign; our current convention is inward-positive]{ref}

Two distinct nonlinearities matter. Even a passive <cell membrane> can show sublinear <synaptic integration> when added <synaptic conductances> reduce driving force and effective <electrical resistance>. Active <voltage-gated ion channels> and <NMDA receptors> can instead amplify sufficiently concentrated inputs and produce a <dendritic spike>. Neither summing prescribed <electric currents> nor adding separate measured <EPSPs> is an exact universal rule for a branching <neuron>.<Neuronal Dynamics: From Single Neurons to Networks and Models of Cognition by Wulfram Gerstner and others/1.4 Limitations of the Leaky Integrate-and-Fire Model>[§1.4.2 and §1.4.4: shunting, saturation and active dendritic interactions]{ref}

= Spatial summation
{parent=Synaptic integration}

<Spatial summation> combines inputs arriving at different <synapses>. Location matters because a <dendrite> has axial <electrical resistance> and leaky <membrane capacitance>: a local <synaptic potential> need not arrive at the <soma> with its original amplitude or time course. A <passive dendritic cable> makes this attenuation explicit. The <superposition principle> applies to fixed <electric current> inputs in a linear passive model, but adding <synaptic conductances> changes the model's coefficients. Compare voltage changes relative to the same baseline, rather than adding absolute <membrane potentials>.

\Image[synaptic-spatial-conductance-passive-integration.png]
{title=<Spatial summation>, reduced driving force and <shunting inhibition> in passive models}
{description=Original calculations, not biological recordings. A: equal imposed <electric current> steps at the <soma> and a <dendrite> in two coupled passive compartments yield different somatic voltage traces; applying both gives their exact sum relative to rest. B: activating two excitatory <synaptic conductances> together gives less <depolarization> than adding the separate voltage responses. C: a <synaptic conductance> whose <reversal potential> equals rest suppresses the same excitation. All steps last from $10$ to $60\,\mathrm{ms}$. There are no active <ion channels>, <action potentials> or resets. The equations and parameters are under <passive spatial and conductance integration>.}

\b[A motivating experiment.] A 2004 study used $300$–$350\,\mathrm{\mu m}$ cortical slices from $18$–$28$-day-old rats at $35$–$36\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. Somatic <whole-cell recordings> measured <EPSPs> in layer-5 <pyramidal neurons>. Two fine stimulating <electrodes> were positioned beside imaged <dendrites>; <calcium>-sensitive dye localized activation. Each site was stimulated alone, then both together, usually with paired pulses $20\,\mathrm{ms}$ apart. Comparing the combined voltage trace with the sum of individual changes tested the summation rule. Nearby inputs on one branch showed strong amplification at intermediate strengths; inputs on different branches were approximately linear.<Computational subunits in thin dendrites of pyramidal cells by Alon Polsky and others>[pp. 622–623, Figures 1–2; p. 626, Methods]{ref}

Blocking both excitatory <receptor> classes eliminated voltage and <calcium> responses, checking against direct electrical activation. <NMDA receptor> blockers eliminated or markedly reduced amplification; comparisons with inhibition intact and blocked addressed inhibitory contributions. These controls support local active integration. Passing <axons> could still recruit unintended <synapses>; somatic voltage does not measure every local <membrane potential>. The study tested subthreshold slice responses and pairs of branches, not arbitrary activity in an intact brain.<Computational subunits in thin dendrites of pyramidal cells by Alon Polsky and others>[pp. 622–624: controls; pp. 625–626: limitations]{ref}

= Passive dendritic cable
{parent=Spatial summation}
{title2=$\lambda=\sqrt{r_m/r_a}$}

A <passive dendritic cable> models a uniform <dendrite> with axial <electrical resistance>, membrane leak and <membrane capacitance>, excluding active <ion channels>. Let $r_a$ be axial <electrical resistance> per length, $r_m$ membrane <electrical resistance> times length, and $c_m$ <membrane capacitance> per length. For voltage deviation $v=V-E_L$ from the leak <reversal potential>, <electric charge conservation> gives the <partial differential equation>
$$
c_m\frac{\partial v}{\partial t}=\frac{1}{r_a}\frac{\partial^2v}{\partial x^2}-\frac{v}{r_m}+i(x,t),\qquad \tau_m=r_mc_m,\qquad \lambda=\sqrt{\frac{r_m}{r_a}}.
$$
$i$ is imposed inward <electric current> per length. Units are $r_a:\Omega/\mathrm m$, $r_m:\Omega\,\mathrm m$ and $c_m:\mathrm F/\mathrm m$, so $\tau_m$ is a time and $\lambda$ a length. On a source-free semi-infinite cable with imposed $v(0)=v_0$ and $v(x)\to0$ as $x\to\infty$, the <steady state> is
$$
v(x)=v_0e^{-x/\lambda}.
$$
Differentiate twice to verify the <ordinary differential equation> $\lambda^2v''-v=0$. At $x=\lambda$, the amplitude is $e^{-1}\approx0.368$ of the boundary amplitude. This is a stationary attenuation law, not the complete waveform of a brief <EPSP>. Transient passive spread also broadens and delays responses. Finite ends, branch points and the <soma>'s load alter the solution; distance alone cannot specify every real <synaptic potential>.<Neuronal Dynamics: From Single Neurons to Networks and Models of Cognition by Wulfram Gerstner and others/3.2 Spatial Structure: The Dendritic Tree>[§3.2.1, equations (3.17)–(3.20): passive cable and length scale; §3.2.2, Figure 3.7: transient attenuation and delay]{ref}

= Passive spatial and conductance integration
{parent=Spatial summation}

The spatial panel uses two compartments with <membrane potentials> $V_s,V_d$, one representing the <soma> and one a <dendrite>. Set $v_s=V_s-E_L$, $v_d=V_d-E_L$, with leak <reversal potential> $E_L=-65\,\mathrm{mV}$, each <membrane capacitance> $C=100\,\mathrm{pF}$, each leak <electrical conductance> $g_L=5\,\mathrm{nS}$ and axial <electrical conductance> $g_c=10\,\mathrm{nS}$. <Electric charge conservation> gives
$$
C\dot v_s=-(g_L+g_c)v_s+g_cv_d+I_s,\qquad C\dot v_d=g_cv_s-(g_L+g_c)v_d+I_d.
$$
This chosen passive model has two decay <time constants>, $C/g_L=20\,\mathrm{ms}$ for the equal-voltage mode and $C/(g_L+2g_c)=4\,\mathrm{ms}$ for the voltage-difference mode. Starting from rest, a $100\,\mathrm{pA}$ step into the <soma> gives, with $u$ the time since onset,
$$
v_s(u)=10(1-e^{-u/20})+2(1-e^{-u/4})\quad\mathrm{mV},
$$
while the same step into the <dendrite> gives
$$
v_s(u)=10(1-e^{-u/20})-2(1-e^{-u/4})\quad\mathrm{mV}.
$$
$u$ is expressed in $\mathrm{ms}$. Their long-time rises are $12$ and $8\,\mathrm{mV}$ respectively. Applying both inputs gives $20(1-e^{-u/20})\,\mathrm{mV}$ exactly: spatial attenuation coexists with linear summation of prescribed <electric currents>. After the steps end, both modes decay with their respective <time constants>. This deliberately coarse model is not a reconstructed <dendrite> or a fit to the experiment above.

The conductance panels use the existing one-compartment <synaptic potential> equation, with $C=200\,\mathrm{pF}$, $g_L=10\,\mathrm{nS}$, $E_L=-65\,\mathrm{mV}$ and excitatory <reversal potential> $E_e=0\,\mathrm{mV}$. For a constant excitatory <synaptic conductance> $g_e$, the <steady state> voltage rise and <membrane time constant> are
$$
\Delta V_\infty=\frac{g_e(E_e-E_L)}{g_L+g_e},\qquad \tau_{\mathrm{eff}}=\frac{C}{g_L+g_e}.
$$
Each isolated $2\,\mathrm{nS}$ input has a limiting rise $130/12\approx10.833\,\mathrm{mV}$. Adding those isolated rises predicts $21.667\,\mathrm{mV}$, but opening both together gives $260/14\approx18.571\,\mathrm{mV}$. The two conductances share a changing driving force. This is sublinear input summation even though the voltage <ordinary differential equation> is linear for each prescribed conductance waveform.

Add $g_i=20\,\mathrm{nS}$ with <reversal potential> $E_i=E_L$ to the joint $4\,\mathrm{nS}$ excitation. The limiting rise becomes $260/34\approx7.647\,\mathrm{mV}$ and the <membrane time constant> becomes $200/34\approx5.882\,\mathrm{ms}$. The shunt alone leaves rest unchanged. At the joint-excitation-only <steady state> $V\approx-46.429\,\mathrm{mV}$, however, its inward <electric current> would be $20(-65+46.429)\approx-371.429\,\mathrm{pA}$: it opposes that excursion. The finite $50\,\mathrm{ms}$ traces in the diagram approach, but need not reach, these limiting voltages. See <shunting inhibition> for the constant-<electric current> comparison.

= Temporal summation
{parent=Synaptic integration}

<Temporal summation> is overlap of responses to inputs arriving at different times. It can occur at one repeatedly activated <synapse> or across several <synapses>; it is not defined by reuse of a particular contact. For prescribed <electric current> inputs in a passive compartment, residual <depolarization> from earlier pulses changes the voltage reached by later pulses. The <membrane time constant> sets how rapidly that residual decays, while the duration of <synaptic conductance> and spatial filtering by a <passive dendritic cable> can add other timescales.

\Image[synaptic-temporal-spacing-passive-rc.png]
{title=Equal-charge inputs with different spacing in a passive model of <temporal summation>}
{description=Original passive <current clamp> calculations with $C=200\,\mathrm{pF}$, $g_L=10\,\mathrm{nS}$ and rest $-65\,\mathrm{mV}$. Three equal $300\,\mathrm{pA}$ pulses, each $5\,\mathrm{ms}$ long, start at $10,15,20\,\mathrm{ms}$ or at $10,40,70\,\mathrm{ms}$. The first train consists of adjacent pulses and the second allows decay between pulses. The dashed $-50\,\mathrm{mV}$ line is an illustrative <neuronal firing threshold>; trajectories continue passively through it, with no <action potential> or reset. The same total injected <electric charge> produces different peak voltages.}

For the diagram, write $v=V-E_L$, choose inward-positive imposed <electric current> $I$, and use the <ordinary differential equation>
$$
C\dot v=-g_Lv+I(t),\qquad \tau_m=\frac{C}{g_L}=20\,\mathrm{ms}.
$$
A rectangular pulse of amplitude $I_0=300\,\mathrm{pA}$ and duration $d=5\,\mathrm{ms}$, starting from rest, ends with
$$
A=\frac{I_0}{g_L}(1-e^{-d/\tau_m})=30(1-e^{-1/4})\,\mathrm{mV}\approx6.636\,\mathrm{mV}.
$$
Afterwards its contribution decays as $Ae^{-(t-d)/\tau_m}$. The <superposition principle> therefore gives, at the end of the third pulse with onset spacing $\Delta\geq d$,
$$
v_{\mathrm{peak}}=A\left(1+e^{-\Delta/\tau_m}+e^{-2\Delta/\tau_m}\right).
$$
For $\Delta=5\,\mathrm{ms}$ this is $15.829\,\mathrm{mV}$, so the peak <membrane potential> is $-49.171\,\mathrm{mV}$. For $\Delta=30\,\mathrm{ms}$ it is $8.447\,\mathrm{mV}$, giving $-56.553\,\mathrm{mV}$. Both protocols inject $3I_0d=4.5\,\mathrm{pC}$ of <electric charge>; equal charge does not imply equal peak voltage because the leak drains charge while it is delivered.

In the adjacent-pulse case, the illustrative <neuronal firing threshold> $-50\,\mathrm{mV}$ is crossed at $10+20\log 2\approx23.863\,\mathrm{ms}$. A <leaky integrate-and-fire model> would emit its first event there and reset, so its later trajectory would differ from the passive diagram. The widely spaced train never reaches that threshold. Thus changing input timing alone can change whether an event occurs. A real <neuron>'s <neuronal firing threshold> is also state-dependent, and <synaptic conductance> pulses are not prescribed <electric current> pulses.<Neuronal Dynamics: From Single Neurons to Networks and Models of Cognition by Wulfram Gerstner and others/1.4 Limitations of the Leaky Integrate-and-Fire Model>[Opening equations (1.31)–(1.32) and §1.4.3: ideal threshold/reset and dependence on preceding activity]{ref}

Measured responses to repeated presynaptic <action potentials> may also change through <synaptic plasticity>. A larger second response can therefore reflect a stronger second input as well as residual postsynaptic voltage. To isolate passive <temporal summation>, compare known injected <electric current> waveforms with their single-pulse responses under stable recording conditions; to test actual <synapses>, measure whether input efficacy changes. These are proposed discriminating comparisons, not additional controls claimed for the slice experiment.<Neuronal Dynamics: From Single Neurons to Networks and Models of Cognition by Wulfram Gerstner and others/3.1 Synapses>[§3.1.3: rapid synaptic dynamics, facilitation and depression]{ref}

= Membrane time constant
{parent=Temporal summation}
{title2=$\tau_m=C/G$}

The <membrane time constant> of a passive uniform compartment is its <membrane capacitance> $C$ divided by its total fixed <electrical conductance> $G$. After input removal, voltage deviation from the relevant equilibrium undergoes <exponential decay>; in one <membrane time constant> it falls to $e^{-1}$ of its initial value. For leak alone, $G=g_L$; while fixed <synaptic conductances> are open, $G=g_L+\sum_jg_j$. Thus <shunting inhibition> can both reduce voltage responses and shorten the integration window. This is a local passive timescale: a branching <dendrite> can have several decay modes, and a time-varying <synaptic conductance> need not admit one constant $\tau_m$.

= Neuronal firing threshold
{parent=Synaptic integration}
{title2=$\vartheta$}

A <neuronal firing threshold> specifies the condition for initiating an <action potential>. The <leaky integrate-and-fire model> uses a prescribed <membrane potential> $\vartheta$; real initiation involves active <voltage-gated ion channels> and depends on recent activity and recording site. A threshold drawn on a passive voltage trace is a comparison criterion, not a simulated <action potential>.

= Dendritic spike
{parent=Synaptic integration}

A <dendritic spike> is a regenerative local voltage event in a <dendrite>. Its effect at the <soma> depends on propagation and attenuation; local regeneration does not by itself establish a somatic <action potential>. It is an active mechanism beyond the <passive dendritic cable>.

= NMDA receptor
{c}
{parent=Synaptic integration}

An <NMDA receptor> is an <ionotropic receptor> gating an <ion channel> with voltage-dependent <magnesium> block. This voltage dependence permits cooperative amplification when inputs produce sufficient local <depolarization>; <calcium> can also pass through the open channel.<Neuronal Dynamics: From Single Neurons to Networks and Models of Cognition by Wulfram Gerstner and others/3.1 Synapses>[§3.1.2, equation (3.6): voltage-dependent magnesium block and calcium permeability]{ref}

