A <reaction substrate> on which an <enzyme> acts in a <chemical reaction>.

= Flow cytometry
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

<Flow cytometry> records signals from suspended particles passing an illuminated interrogation region. For a <flow cytometric DNA-content assay>, a <laser> excites a <DNA>-binding dye and a detector records its <fluorescence>. An event is a recorded passage, not a guarantee that exactly one intact <biological cell> was measured.

= Flow cytometric DNA-content assay
{parent=Flow cytometry}

Under controlled staining and detector settings, integrated <fluorescence> can be approximately proportional to nuclear <DNA> amount. A <histogram> of accepted single-cell events then separates a $2C$ region, an intermediate region and a $4C$ region. These are ordinarily assigned to <G0 phase>/<G1 phase>, <S phase>, and <G2 phase>/<M phase> respectively in the stated <diploid> model.

\Image[dna-content-assay-illustrative-peaks.png]
{title=Illustrative <DNA>-content assay peaks: equal amount need not mean equal <cell cycle> stage}
{description=Original synthetic curves, not measured data or a fit to an experiment. The horizontal axis is a proportional <fluorescence> scale expressed in $C$ units for a reference <diploid> population. Component shapes and weights are arbitrary. The $2C$ peak combines <G0 phase> and <G1 phase>; the $4C$ peak combines <G2 phase> and <M phase>. The plotted curve cannot itself distinguish these paired states.}

A Cornell protocol fixes a single-cell suspension by adding cold 70% <ethanol> dropwise while mixing, reducing clumping. After washing, its staining solution contains propidium iodide at $50\,\mathrm{\mu g/ml}$; <ribonuclease A> removes interfering <RNA>. It specifies excitation at $488\,\mathrm{nm}$ and emission around $600\,\mathrm{nm}$. The resulting readout is <fluorescence> per event, not a direct count of <chromosomes>. <DNA Cell Cycle Analysis with PI>[DNA Cell Cycle Analysis with PI, p. 1: dye properties, staining steps 1–7 and reagent recipe]{ref}

Useful controls include an unstained sample for background and a reference population with known nuclear <DNA> amount, processed with identical settings. Two attached $2C$ <biological cells> can mimic one $4C$ event. Comparing signal integral with pulse height or width helps exclude such doublets; a gate selecting intact single particles also limits debris. These are proposed assay checks, not measurements underlying the illustration.

A $4C$ peak cannot distinguish <G2 phase> from <mitosis>; a $2C$ peak cannot distinguish <G1 phase> from <G0 phase>. Mixed <ploidy>, uneven staining and aggregates can invalidate phase assignments. Peak areas describe the accepted sample at collection, and do not by themselves establish phase durations, division rates or a particular arrest mechanism. A treatment comparison needs matched handling and another readout appropriate to the claimed mechanism.

= Biological cell
{parent=Biology}
{wiki=Cell_(biology)}

A <biological cell> is a membrane-bounded basic unit of living organization. In a cell-level experiment it can be an <experimental unit>, provided that treatment assignment and relevant interactions are handled at that level.

The shared architecture of cellular life combines a selective <cell membrane>, internal <cytoplasm>, inherited information in <DNA>, and <ribosomes> that assemble <proteins>. <Metabolism> supplies transformations of matter and <energy> needed for maintenance and growth. These components form a coupled system: a boundary retains useful <molecules>, <enzymes> organize <chemical reactions>, and inherited information supports rebuilding the machinery. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: prokaryotic cells].

A worked structural comparison: in a typical <prokaryote>, <DNA> occupies a region without a surrounding <cell nucleus>; in a typical nucleated <eukaryote>, much of the <DNA> lies inside a membrane-bounded <cell nucleus>. Both still have a <cell membrane>, <cytoplasm> and <ribosomes>. The extra boundary compartmentalizes processes; it does not create a different basic principle of living organization. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: eukaryotic cells].

\Image[cell-compartments.png]
{title=Shared cellular architecture with different DNA compartmentalization}
{description=Original simplified schematics of a <prokaryote> and a nucleated <eukaryote>. Colors identify corresponding components; sizes and component counts are illustrative, and additional structures are omitted.}

Shared architecture describes cellular life and its developmental machinery, rather than requiring every specialized <biological cell> to retain every component. A mature mammalian <red blood cell>, for example, lacks a <cell nucleus>, although it develops from nucleated precursors. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/40-2-components-of-the-blood[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: components of blood]. This distinction makes cellular unity compatible with striking specialization.

= Cell state
{parent=Biological cell}

A <cell state> describes current properties of a <biological cell>, including its <gene expression>, structure and activity. A snapshot of <cell state> does not alone establish its future <cell fate>.

= Stem cell
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <stem cell> can produce both renewed <stem cells> and differentiated descendants. Its developmental range depends on the type and setting; self-renewal does not imply unrestricted <cell fate>.

= Progenitor cell
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <progenitor cell> is a precursor with relatively restricted differentiation potential and, generally, more limited self-renewal than a <stem cell>. Usage depends on the lineage and assay; an expression marker alone does not establish renewal capacity.

= Secretory granule
{parent=Biological cell}

A storage compartment that participates in regulated <exocytosis>; the <insulin> example distinguishes storage of a <hormone> from storage of <glycogen> fuel.

= Cell cycle
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

The <cell cycle> is the sequence of events by which a <biological cell> prepares for and completes division, including <DNA replication> and distribution of <chromosomes>. In <Saccharomyces cerevisiae>, changing bud appearance provides an accessible readout; see the Burgess, Powers and Mell account and Hartwell, Culotti and Reid's primary study cited there.

For a typical cycling <diploid> <eukaryotic> <biological cell>, the order is <G1 phase>, <S phase>, <G2 phase>, then <M phase>. <DNA replication> doubles nuclear <DNA> amount during <S phase>; <mitosis> distributes the copies, and <cytokinesis> separates the daughters. The useful distinction is between copying a library and allocating its copies: <DNA replication> changes how much material exists, whereas division changes which <biological cell> contains it.

\Image[cell-cycle-phases-and-dna-content.png]
{title=The <cell cycle>: copying nuclear <DNA> once, then distributing it to two daughters}
{description=Original schematic for a typical cycling <diploid> <eukaryotic> <biological cell>. Each box states nuclear <DNA> content and <chromosome> count; <M phase> is summarized by its completed daughters. Arrow lengths do not represent phase durations. The <G0 phase> branch shows reversible withdrawal where re-entry is possible, rather than claiming that all nondividing <biological cells> can return.}

<Gene expression> continues to supply <RNA> and <proteins> as the <biological cell> grows; it is different from duplicating the <genome>. In the <yeast cross-species complementation experiment>, restored growth requires successful repeated <cell cycles>, but growth alone does not locate a defect within a phase. Bud appearance in <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> provides another readout, while a <flow cytometric DNA-content assay> asks how much nuclear <DNA> is present and <DNA pulse labeling> asks whether new <DNA> was synthesized during an exposure.

= Interphase
{parent=Cell cycle}
{wiki}

<Interphase> comprises <G1 phase>, <S phase> and <G2 phase>, including growth and <DNA replication>.

= G1 phase
{c}
{parent=Interphase}
{wiki}
{title2=$G_1$}

During <G1 phase>, growth and preparation precede <DNA replication>. The ordinary <diploid> nuclear state is $2n,2C$.

= S phase
{c}
{parent=Interphase}
{wiki}
{title2=$S$}

During <S phase>, nuclear <DNA replication> produces paired <sister chromatids>. <DNA> content rises from $2C$ to $4C$, while the <chromosome> count remains $2n$.

= G2 phase
{c}
{parent=Interphase}
{wiki}
{title2=$G_2$}

<G2 phase> follows completed nuclear <DNA replication> and precedes <mitosis>. The ordinary <diploid> state is $2n,4C$. <Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/The Cell Cycle>[Biology 2e, §10.2, Interphase and its G1, S and G2 subsections; Figure 10.5]{ref}

= M phase
{c}
{parent=Cell cycle}
{title2=$M$}

<M phase> includes <mitosis> and ordinarily <cytokinesis>, which can overlap in time.

= Mitosis
{parent=M phase}
{wiki}

<Mitosis> allocates copied <chromosomes> to daughter <cell nuclei>. The <spindle apparatus> aligns <sister chromatids> before separation in <anaphase>.

= Anaphase
{parent=Mitosis}
{wiki}

In mitotic <anaphase>, <sister chromatids> separate into daughter <chromosomes> moving toward opposite poles.

= Cytokinesis
{parent=M phase}
{wiki}

<Cytokinesis> partitions the <cytoplasm>. Without <cytokinesis>, two <cell nuclei> can share one <biological cell>. <Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/The Cell Cycle>[Biology 2e, §10.2, The Mitotic Phase, Figure 10.6 and Cytokinesis]{ref}

= G0 phase
{c}
{parent=Cell cycle}
{wiki}
{title2=$G_0$}

<G0 phase> describes withdrawal from the division cycle. Some <biological cells> re-enter <G1 phase>; others remain nondividing. <Metabolism> and <gene expression> can continue. <Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/The Cell Cycle>[Biology 2e, §10.2, G0 Phase]{ref} In the ordinary <diploid> case, $2C$ alone cannot distinguish <G0 phase> from <G1 phase>.

= Cell-cycle DNA content
{parent=Cell cycle}
{title2=$(2n,2C)\to(2n,4C)\to2\,(2n,2C)$}

Let $n$ count <chromosomes> in one complete set, and let $C$ denote the nuclear <DNA> amount in one unreplicated <haploid> set of this <organism>. <Ploidy> counts sets, whereas $C$ units count material; $C$ is not a universal mass shared by all <species>. Here we count a paired replicated <chromosome> as one until its <sister chromatids> separate. A copied <diploid> <genome> is therefore still <diploid> through <S phase>: $2n$ persists even as $2C$ becomes $4C$.

An original bookkeeping example uses a hypothetical <diploid> <biological cell> with $n=3$. Name its three <homologous chromosome> pairs $A,a$, $B,b$ and $D,d$. Assume ordinary nuclear <DNA replication>, no <chromosome> loss, and one continuous double-stranded <DNA> <molecule> per unreplicated <chromosome> or replicated <chromatid>. Count nuclear material only, excluding <mitochondrion> <DNA>.

\Table
[
\Tr[\Th[Stage and counting compartment]\Th[<Chromosomes>]\Th[Double-stranded <DNA> <molecules>]\Th[Nuclear <DNA> amount]]
\Tr[\Td[One <G1 phase> <biological cell>]\Td[6 unreplicated]\Td[6]\Td[$2C$]]
\Tr[\Td[One <G2 phase> <biological cell>]\Td[6 replicated]\Td[12 in 6 pairs of <sister chromatids>]\Td[$4C$]]
\Tr[\Td[Whole undivided <anaphase> <biological cell>]\Td[12 separated daughter <chromosomes>]\Td[12]\Td[$4C$ total]]
\Tr[\Td[Each completed daughter <cell nucleus>]\Td[6 unreplicated]\Td[6]\Td[$2C$ each]]
]

For example, $A$ becomes the paired copies $A_1,A_2$ during <S phase>. They remain one replicated <chromosome> until separation. Each completed daughter receives one $A$ copy and one $a$ copy, and similarly for the other two pairs. Across both daughters, $2\times6=12$ double-stranded <DNA> <molecules> and $2(2C)=4C$: division allocates the copied material without another round of <DNA replication>.

The whole undivided <anaphase> <biological cell> transiently contains $4n$ separately counted daughter <chromosomes>. This counting change does not establish a four-set <ploidy> in a completed daughter <genome>: each pole receives $2n$, and each completed daughter <cell nucleus> is <diploid>. Always state whether a number refers to one <cell nucleus>, one pole, or the entire undivided <biological cell>. Compare <meiotic DNA content>: after <meiosis I>, a <haploid> <cell nucleus> can contain $2C$, so amount alone cannot determine <ploidy>.

= Cell cycle analysis
{parent=Cell cycle}
{wiki}

<Cell cycle analysis> assigns evidence to stages of the <cell cycle>. A <flow cytometric DNA-content assay> measures accumulated nuclear <DNA>, whereas <DNA pulse labeling> detects synthesis over an exposure window. Agreement between these readouts is useful because an amount and a synthesis event answer different questions.

= DNA pulse labeling
{c}
{parent=Cell cycle analysis}

<DNA pulse labeling> exposes <biological cells> to a detectable precursor for a bounded interval. After removal, later sampling can follow the labeled material and descendants. A short pulse asks about synthesis during that interval; overnight labeling instead accumulates evidence across many hours. Detectable synthesis does not by itself prove completion of a <cell cycle>, and <DNA repair> can also incorporate precursors.

= DNA synthesis labeling with EdU
{c}
{parent=DNA pulse labeling}

= EdU labeling
{c}
{synonym}

<EdU labeling> uses a modified <nucleoside>, 5-ethynyl-2'-deoxyuridine, incorporated into newly synthesized <DNA>, then chemically couples it to a fluorescent reporter.

A 2008 experiment injected adult mice with $100\,\mathrm{\mu g}$ of this label and inspected intestinal sections 96 hours later. Fixed sections on slides received $10\,\mathrm{\mu M}$ Alexa568-azide for 10 minutes. Imaging with a <fluorescence microscope> combined the reporter image with a Hoechst <DNA> counterstain and a transmitted-light view. Labeled <cell nuclei> marked descendants of <biological cells> synthesizing <DNA> during exposure, rather than identifying current <S phase> at collection. <A chemical method for fast and sensitive detection of DNA synthesis in vivo by Adrian Salic and Timothy J. Mitchison>[A chemical method for fast and sensitive detection of DNA synthesis in vivo, Figure 4 legend: mouse pulse, section preparation and imaging]{ref}

The same study's cultured-cell controls compared overnight $10\,\mathrm{\mu M}$ label, no label, and label with $10\,\mathrm{mM}$ <hydroxyurea>. No-label samples had little reporter background; <hydroxyurea> strongly suppressed incorporation. The camera detected residual signal with a 3-second exposure versus 40 milliseconds under otherwise identical settings, demonstrating why acquisition settings matter. These controls support synthesis-dependent labeling, without proving that every labeled <biological cell> completes division. <A chemical method for fast and sensitive detection of DNA synthesis in vivo by Adrian Salic and Timothy J. Mitchison>[Figure 2 legend, A–M: NIH 3T3 controls, fluorescence and differential interference contrast imaging, exposure-time comparison]{ref}

= Spindle apparatus
{parent=Cell cycle}

The <spindle apparatus> distributes <chromosomes> during division of a <cell nucleus>.

= Cell envelope
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

The <cell envelope> comprises a <biological cell>'s surrounding boundary layers, including its <cell membrane> and a <cell wall> or additional outer structures when present. The term is especially useful when discussing <bacteria>, where the layers must be distinguished.

= Bacterial outer membrane
{parent=Cell envelope}
{wiki}

A <bacterial outer membrane> is an additional membrane outside the <peptidoglycan> in typical <Gram-negative bacteria>. It adds a barrier while the inner <cell membrane> remains the boundary of the <cytoplasm>.

= Cell wall
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <cell wall> is a supporting structure outside a <cell membrane>. It helps preserve shape and bears the load when <osmosis> raises internal <pressure>. The <cell membrane> supplies the <selective permeability> described in its own article; the <cell wall> supplies mechanical reinforcement. These functions cooperate: a porous supporting layer can restrain expansion without being the boundary that retains dissolved <solutes>. <Turgor pressure> makes this cooperation visible in a firm leaf; <plasmolysis> and a <lysozyme wall-removal experiment> separate the two functions experimentally.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/5.2 Passive Transport>[Biology 2e, §5.2, Tonicity in Living Systems, Figure 5.13: wall support and plasmolysis]{ref}

The material is not universal. Most <bacteria> use <peptidoglycan>, whose linked <sugar> chains and <amino acid> cross-links are explained below. A <plant cell wall> uses <cellulose>; a <fungal cell wall> contains <chitin> and <glucans>. <Animals> lack a <cell wall>, some <bacteria> are naturally wall-free, and <archaea> have different wall chemistry. Thus a <cell wall> is neither a universal cellular component nor one chemical recipe.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/22.2 Structure of Prokaryotes: Bacteria and Archaea>[Biology 2e, §22.2, Figure 22.13 and The Cell Wall of Prokaryotes: wall-free bacteria and archaeal differences]{ref}<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/4.3 Eukaryotic Cells>[Biology 2e, §4.3, Animal Cells versus Plant Cells and The Cell Wall: cellulose and animal comparison]{ref}<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/24.1 Characteristics of Fungi>[Biology 2e, §24.1, Cell Structure and Function: chitin and glucans]{ref}

\Image[cell-envelope-comparison.png]
{title=Representative <cell envelopes>: different supporting layers outside the <cell membrane>}
{description=Original layer-order schematic comparing <Gram-positive bacteria>, <Gram-negative bacteria>, a <plant cell wall>, a <fungal cell wall>, a member of <archaea> with a protein wall and an <animal> cell. Colors distinguish a <cell wall> from a <cell membrane> and a <bacterial outer membrane>. Thicknesses and spacing are illustrative, not measurements. These examples do not exhaust variation within any group.}

The <bacterial outer membrane> in typical <Gram-negative bacteria> is an additional membrane outside the <peptidoglycan>, not another name for that supporting mesh. The <cell envelope> includes the boundary layers together. An extra barrier can change access to the <peptidoglycan>, so response to <lysozyme> cannot be inferred from the mere presence of a <cell wall>.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/22.2 Structure of Prokaryotes: Bacteria and Archaea>[Biology 2e, §22.2, Figure 22.16: inner membrane, peptidoglycan and outer membrane]{ref}

= Plant cell wall
{parent=Cell wall}

A <plant cell wall> provides reinforcement outside the <cell membrane>, with <cellulose> as a major structural component. It is a composite rather than a solid sheet of pure <cellulose>. Wall construction varies with the kind and developmental state of the <biological cell>.

= Fungal cell wall
{parent=Cell wall}

A <fungal cell wall> contains structural <polysaccharides> including <chitin> and <glucans>. Its composition and organization vary among <fungi>; the diagram illustrates a composite rather than a pure <chitin> shell. A <fungus> sharing a <cell wall> with a <plant> does not imply shared wall chemistry.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/24.1 Characteristics of Fungi>[Biology 2e, §24.1, Cell Structure and Function: structural wall polysaccharides]{ref}

= Chitin
{parent=Fungal cell wall}
{tag=Polysaccharide}
{wiki}

<Chitin> is a structural <polysaccharide> built from modified <sugar> units. It contributes reinforcement to a <fungal cell wall>.

= Glucan
{parent=Fungal cell wall}
{tag=Polysaccharide}
{wiki}

A <glucan> is a <polysaccharide> built from <glucose> units. Different linkages produce different structures; some <glucans> contribute to a <fungal cell wall>.

= Turgor pressure
{parent=Cell wall}
{wiki}
{title2=$P_{\mathrm{in}}-P_{\mathrm{out}}$}

<Turgor pressure> is the internal <hydrostatic pressure> above the external <pressure> in a <biological cell>. When <water> enters a walled <biological cell> by <osmosis>, expansion loads the <cell wall>. Rising <pressure> can oppose further net entry while the <cell membrane> retains its <selective permeability>. Mechanical support therefore does not require the <cell wall> to block <water>.

As an original mechanical check, idealize a small flat patch of <cell wall> of <surface area> $A$ under uniform <turgor pressure> $\Delta P=P_{\mathrm{in}}-P_{\mathrm{out}}$. Its outward <force> is $F=\Delta P A$. Doubling $A$ at fixed <pressure> doubles the load; equal internal and external <pressures> give zero net pressure load. This is a force balance, not a measured wall strength or a complete model of a curved, growing <biological cell>. It says nothing about which <solutes> cross the <cell membrane>.

= Plasmolysis
{parent=Cell wall}
{wiki}

<Plasmolysis> is withdrawal of the membrane-bounded contents from parts of a <cell wall> after <water> loss. A sufficiently concentrated external <hypertonic solution>, with <solutes> effectively excluded by the <cell membrane> over the observation time, can cause this. The <cell wall> remains; the enclosed material contracts. <Plasmolysis> is therefore distinct from <osmotic lysis>, which breaks the cellular boundary during excessive swelling. A return to a less concentrated surrounding <chemical solution> can reverse <plasmolysis> if the <biological cell> remains functional.<Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/5.2 Passive Transport>[Biology 2e, §5.2, Tonicity in Living Systems and Figure 5.13: membrane withdrawal under hypertonic conditions]{ref}

\Image[cell-wall-osmotic-experiment-logic.png]
{title=<Plasmolysis> with a retained <cell wall>, and osmotic challenge after wall removal}
{description=Original qualitative experiment logic. The upper pair compares a turgid <plant> cell with <plasmolysis> in an external <hypertonic solution>. The lower pair compares a supported <protoplast> with <osmotic lysis> after dilution for a susceptible preparation. Arrows indicate interventions or possible net <water> movement, not measured rates. The dilution comparison concerns <lysozyme>-susceptible <bacteria>; the upper comparison concerns <plant> cells. No measured dimensions, times or survival fractions are implied.}

= Protoplast
{parent=Cell wall}
{wiki}

A <protoplast> is the living contents of a <biological cell> bounded by its <cell membrane> after removal of the <cell wall>. Removing the <cell wall> need not immediately destroy the <cell membrane>; stability depends on the preparation and surroundings. <Lysozyme> acts on susceptible bacterial <peptidoglycan>, not on <cellulose> or <chitin>, so it is not a universal wall-removal <enzyme>.

= Lysozyme wall-removal experiment
{parent=Protoplast}

The 1953 paper <The isolation of protoplasts from Bacillus megaterium by controlled treatment with lysozyme by Claes Weibull> (pp. 688–690, Figures 2–3) tested <Bacillus megaterium> strain KM. Apparatus included a Klett-Summerson colorimeter with a red filter and a Spencer no. 8 <phase-contrast microscope>. <Lysozyme> in phosphate buffer produced empty residual bodies; adding $0.2\,\mathrm{M}$ <sucrose> preserved granular spheres. Diluting their surroundings caused rapid <osmotic lysis>.

The same study compared untreated, boiled, <lysozyme>-exposed and <sucrose>-protected samples. Filled, stoppered tubes reduced damage from air exposure. After <centrifugation>, a Beckman DU spectrophotometer measured ultraviolet <wave absorption> of the separated liquid. Protected samples resembled untreated controls in releasing little ultraviolet-absorbing material; boiled and unprotected samples released much more. Later-growth cultures reduced spontaneous breakdown as a confounder.<The isolation of protoplasts from Bacillus megaterium by controlled treatment with lysozyme by Claes Weibull>[pp. 688–689, Materials and Methods; pp. 692–694, release comparison and Figure 6]{ref}

Together, the shape and release readouts support removal of mechanical protection while a permeability barrier persists. They do not establish unchanged <selective permeability> to every <solute>, long-term viability, or universal susceptibility.

An instructive extension of this experimental logic is a matched comparison: <lysozyme> present or absent, crossed with osmotic support present or absent. Keep starting material, buffer, observation interval and handling comparable; include a blank to distinguish optical effects of reagents from changes in the <biological cells>. Then dilute already formed <protoplasts> as a separate intervention. This separates access and wall removal from failure after removal. These are proposed controls for interpreting the mechanism, not additional reported results from the historical paper. A fall in optical signal alone cannot establish rupture: shape changes also alter <scattering of light>.

= Peptidoglycan
{parent=Cell wall}
{wiki}

<Peptidoglycan> is a mesh of linked <sugar> chains cross-linked by short chains of <amino acids> in the <cell walls> of most <bacteria>. Its absence from the <cell walls> of <archaea> helps distinguish envelope chemistry without confusing either <cell wall> with a <cell membrane>. See https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-3-unique-characteristics-of-prokaryotic-cells[OpenStax Microbiology, 3.3: cell walls and peptidoglycan].

= Cell membrane
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

= Plasma membrane
{synonym}

The <cell membrane>, also called the <plasma membrane>, is the selective boundary separating a <biological cell>'s <cytoplasm> from its surroundings. Both <prokaryotes> and <eukaryotes> have a <cell membrane>. Where a <cell wall> is present, it is a distinct structure outside the <cell membrane>; the two names are not synonyms. This separation lets a <biological cell> maintain internal conditions while exchanging material through <membrane transport>. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Cell-Membrane-Plasma-Membrane[NHGRI: cell membrane].

The familiar architecture is a <lipid bilayer> containing <membrane proteins>. In a <phospholipid>-based <lipid bilayer>, two <membrane leaflets> face opposite ways: <hydrophilic> heads face the <water> on each side, while <hydrophobic> tails face one another in the interior. The heads are therefore exposed both outside and inside the <biological cell>. This arrangement combines a water-facing surface with a barrier to many water-soluble <solutes>; the schematic represents organization rather than an atomistic structure. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26871/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the lipid bilayer].

\Image[cell-membrane-selective-barrier.png]
{title=Two <membrane leaflets> and selective routes through a <cell membrane>}
{description=Original <cell membrane> schematic showing <phospholipids>, <hydrophilic> heads, <hydrophobic> tails, an <ion channel>, and a <carrier protein>. Arrows illustrate possible passage, not measured rates. Shapes, sizes, spacing and numbers of <molecules> are illustrative.}

<Selective permeability> means that different substances cross at different rates under comparable conditions. Small <nonpolar> <molecules> can pass through a <lipid bilayer> by <diffusion>, while <ions> encounter a strong barrier without a suitable route. An <ion channel> supplies a selective water-filled pathway when open; a <carrier protein> binds a <solute> and changes shape to expose it to the other side. These <membrane proteins> therefore add specific routes to the barrier. An open <ion channel> permits <passive transport>, whereas a <carrier protein> can mediate either <passive transport> or <active transport>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26815/[Alberts et al.: principles of membrane transport].

The useful combination is retention plus controlled exchange: a completely impermeable boundary would prevent uptake, whereas unrestricted mixing would erase differences in <concentration> and <membrane potential>. The <fluid mosaic model> connects these functions to an organized but mobile <lipid bilayer> containing <proteins>.

= Selective permeability
{parent=Cell membrane}

<Selective permeability> means that a <cell membrane> permits different substances to pass at different rates under comparable conditions. Both the <lipid bilayer> and the available <membrane proteins> contribute to this selection.

= Membrane protein
{parent=Cell membrane}
{wiki}

= Membrane transport protein
{parent=Membrane protein}
{wiki}

= GLUT4
{c}
{parent=Membrane transport protein}
{wiki}

= Carrier protein
{parent=Membrane transport protein}

A membrane <carrier protein> binds a <solute> and changes its exposed binding site from one side of a <cell membrane> to the other. Depending on its mechanism and coupling, it can mediate <passive transport> or <active transport>.

= Gap junction
{parent=Cell membrane}
{wiki}

= Ion channel
{parent=Cell membrane}
{wiki}

An <ion channel> is a <membrane protein> that provides a selective pathway for <ions> across a <cell membrane>. An open pore permits <passive transport> down the relevant <electrochemical gradient>; it does not supply the <free energy> for uphill transport as a pump involved in <active transport> does. Two things are needed for a net <electric current>: an available pathway and an <electrochemical potential> difference. Opening a pore alone need not produce a net <electric current>. See <Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/Ion Channels and the Electrical Properties of Membranes>.

The <selective permeability> of an <ion channel> depends on interactions between its pore and the passing <ions>, not simply on a hole's diameter. Some <ion channels> strongly favor <potassium> over <sodium>; others admit several kinds of positively charged <ions>. Thus selective does not necessarily mean exclusive to one <ion> species. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK11123/[Purves et al., Neuroscience, 2nd edition (2001): molecular structure of ion channels] and the mixed-channel example in https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK11013/[Purves et al.: principles derived from studies of the neuromuscular junction].

\Image[ion-channel-selective-passage.png]
{title=An open <ion channel> supplies a selective passive pathway}
{description=Original model schematic: selected positive <ions> can traverse a pore in a <membrane protein>, while the differently shaped particles are excluded from this modeled pore. The arrow shows one possible net direction when the corresponding <electrochemical gradient> favors entry; the pore does not enforce that direction. Shapes, particle counts and dimensions are illustrative, not physical microscopic data or a universal selectivity rule.}

Gating is the opening and closing of an <ion channel>'s pathway. A <voltage-gated ion channel> responds to <membrane potential>; an <ionotropic receptor> can respond to a bound <ligand>. Gating changes the available <electrical conductance>, while the <electrochemical gradient> supplies the drive for <passive transport>. For a fixed <reversal potential> in the simple model below, opening more identical <ion channels> increases the magnitude of the <electric current> at a fixed nonzero drive, without choosing its sign independently of <membrane potential>. The <two-state ion-channel gating> model describes how the open fraction can change. See the Alberts reference above.

For a worked approximation, hold an open population's <electrical conductance> at $g=10\,\mathrm{nS}$ and its <reversal potential> at $E_{\mathrm{rev}}=-80\,\mathrm{mV}$. Use $V=V_{\mathrm{inside}}-V_{\mathrm{outside}}$ for <membrane potential> and \b[inward-positive <conventional current>]:
$$
I_{\mathrm{in}}=g(E_{\mathrm{rev}}-V),\qquad g\geq0.
$$
At $V=-100,-80,-60\,\mathrm{mV}$, respectively, the <electric currents> are $+200,0,-200\,\mathrm{pA}$, since $1\,\mathrm{nS}\times1\,\mathrm{mV}=1\,\mathrm{pA}$. Positive <electric current> means net positive <electric charge> entering, or net negative <electric charge> leaving; it does not mean that every <ion> moves inward. For a pore passing only positive <ions>, the three results mean net inward passage, no net passage, and net outward passage. These stipulated values describe an illustrative Ohmic approximation, not measured properties of a particular <ion channel>; see <reversal potential> for the current plot and limitations.

The useful connection is between a molecular gate and a controllable <electric current>. A <ligand> can change <electrical conductance> through an <ionotropic receptor>, or <membrane potential> can change it through a <voltage-gated ion channel>, connecting recognition and electrical signaling. In a historical landmark, Erwin Neher and Bert Sakmann reported currents from individual <ion channels> in frog muscle membranes in 1976. See https://doi.org/10.1038/260799a0[Neher and Sakmann, Single-channel currents recorded from membrane of denervated frog muscle fibres, Nature 260, 799–802 (1976)].

= ATP-sensitive potassium channel
{c}
{parent=Ion channel}
{wiki}

= Membrane transport
{parent=Cell membrane}
{wiki}

<Membrane transport> is the passage of substances across a <cell membrane>. For the small <solutes> considered here, distinguish the available route from its driving force: a <lipid bilayer>, <ion channel>, or <carrier protein> determines how passage can occur, while differences in <chemical potential> and <membrane potential> determine which direction is energetically downhill. A large driving force cannot make an unavailable route conduct. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26815/[Alberts et al.: principles of membrane transport].

For an ideal dilute <chemical solution> at uniform <temperature> and <pressure>, with the same reference molar <chemical potential> on both sides, the molar <electrochemical potential> change for transferring a <solute> from compartment A to B is
$$
\Delta\widetilde\mu_{\mathrm{A}\to\mathrm{B}}=RT\ln\!\left(\frac{c_{\mathrm B}}{c_{\mathrm A}}\right)+zF(V_{\mathrm B}-V_{\mathrm A}).
$$
Here $c_{\mathrm A},c_{\mathrm B}$ are <concentrations>, $T$ is absolute <temperature>, $R$ is the <molar gas constant>, $z$ is signed <ion> charge number, $F$ is the <Faraday constant>, and $V_{\mathrm B}-V_{\mathrm A}$ is the <electric potential> difference. The <natural logarithm> contains a dimensionless ratio. The electrical term vanishes for an uncharged <solute>. More generally, use <thermodynamic activities> in place of the ideal <concentration> ratio. This combines the ideal-solution <chemical potential> difference with electrical <energy> per mole; see https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/E01945[IUPAC Gold Book: electrochemical potential]. For a <biological cell>, taking B inside and A outside makes the voltage term use the existing <membrane potential> convention.

\Image[membrane-transport-gradients.png]
{title=<Passive transport> and <active transport> for a stipulated <concentration> difference}
{description=Ten illustrative neutral <solute> tracers are split 8:2 between equal fixed-<volume> compartments. The lower sketches compare conceptual <free energy> profiles for <passive transport> and the <solute> part of <active transport>. Heights are unscaled and are not experimental barriers; the lower-right curve excludes the driving process, so it is not a full coupled-cycle <free energy> profile.}

= Passive transport
{parent=Membrane transport}
{wiki}

<Passive transport> is net movement down the transported substance's <electrochemical gradient> without coupling that movement to another source of <free energy>. <Facilitated diffusion> is <passive transport> through an <ion channel> or a passive <carrier protein>. Providing such a route can speed passage, but does not turn an uphill transfer into a downhill one. For an uncharged <solute> under the ideal conditions above, net <passive transport> runs from higher to lower <concentration>; for an <ion>, the <membrane potential> also matters. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9847/[Cooper, The Cell: transport of small molecules].

In the figure, stipulate eight neutral <solute> tracers in A and two in B, with equal fixed <volumes>, identical surroundings, and no imposed <electric potential> difference. The <concentration> ratio is $c_{\mathrm A}/c_{\mathrm B}=8/2=4$, so A-to-B transfer has $\Delta\widetilde\mu=-RT\ln4\simeq-1.38629RT$, or $-3.458\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ at the assumed <temperature> $T=300\,\mathrm K$. If this <solute> can cross, <thermodynamic equilibrium> has equal expected <concentrations>: five tracers per side on average. Individual crossings occur in both directions; an observed ten-particle snapshot need not contain exactly five on each side. These stipulated counts illustrate a driving force, not a measured flow rate or a prediction of the time to equilibrate.

For a separate illustrative <ion> calculation, keep $c_{\mathrm A}/c_{\mathrm B}=4$, but assign $z=+1$ and $V_{\mathrm A}-V_{\mathrm B}=-60\,\mathrm{mV}$ at $300\,\mathrm K$. Using $R\simeq8.31446\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$ and $F\simeq96485.33\,\mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$, B-to-A transfer gives $RT\ln4-0.060F\simeq3.458-5.789=-2.331\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. Thus an appropriate open <ion channel> can permit <passive transport> toward the higher <concentration>: the electrical contribution outweighs the opposing <chemical potential> contribution. This stipulated <membrane potential> is not inferred from the tracer counts.

= Facilitated diffusion
{parent=Passive transport}
{wiki}

<Facilitated diffusion> is <passive transport> through a <membrane transport protein>, such as an <ion channel> or <carrier protein>, down the relevant <electrochemical gradient>. The <protein> supplies a route; it does not by itself provide <free energy> for uphill transfer.

= Osmosis
{parent=Passive transport}
{wiki}

<Osmosis> is net movement of <water> across a boundary whose permeability differs for <water> and relevant <solutes>. The direction depends on the <chemical potential> of <water>, including both dissolved material and <pressure>. A <cell wall> can let <water> reach the <cell membrane>, yet oppose expansion through <turgor pressure>. Zero net movement does not mean individual <water> <molecules> have stopped crossing.

= Tonicity
{parent=Osmosis}
{wiki}

<Tonicity> describes how a surrounding <chemical solution> changes cellular <volume> through <osmosis>. It depends on which <solutes> remain effectively excluded on the timescale of interest, not simply on the amount of dissolved material. The following comparisons take a functional <cell membrane>, an initially unpressurized <biological cell>, and effectively excluded <solutes> as the reference; rising <turgor pressure> can oppose entry in a walled <biological cell>.

= Hypotonic solution
{parent=Tonicity}

A <hypotonic solution> favors initial net <water> entry under the reference conditions above. A susceptible <biological cell> may undergo <osmotic lysis>; a sufficiently strong <cell wall> can instead support <turgor pressure>.

= Hypertonic solution
{parent=Tonicity}

A <hypertonic solution> favors initial net <water> loss under the reference conditions above. In a walled <plant> cell, enough loss can produce <plasmolysis>.

= Isotonic solution
{parent=Tonicity}

An <isotonic solution> produces no sustained change in cellular <volume> under the stated comparison conditions. <Water> can still exchange in both directions.

= Cytolysis
{parent=Osmosis}
{wiki}

= Osmotic lysis
{synonym}

<Osmotic lysis> is rupture after excessive <water> entry and swelling. It is an outcome for susceptible <biological cells>, not an inevitable response of every <biological cell> to a <hypotonic solution>. <Cell walls> can bear the resulting load, and cellular mechanisms can regulate <water> balance. A <protoplast> challenge tests a particular preparation under particular conditions.

= Active transport
{parent=Membrane transport}
{wiki}

<Active transport> moves a substance uphill in its <electrochemical gradient> by coupling the transfer to a downhill process. Some <carrier proteins> use <ATP> <hydrolysis>; others couple uphill transfer of one <solute> to downhill transfer of another <solute>, often an <ion>. The latter mechanism uses a stored <electrochemical gradient> and need not consume <ATP> directly at that <carrier protein>. Maintaining the driving <electrochemical gradient> still requires a source of <energy>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26896/[Alberts et al.: carrier proteins and active membrane transport].

For the neutral 8:2 example, B-to-A transfer costs $RT\ln4\simeq1.38629RT$ per mole. As an explicitly assumed bookkeeping example, suppose a tightly coupled cycle supplies a downhill <free energy> change of $-2RT$ per mole of transferred <solute>. The total is then $\Delta G_{\mathrm{cycle}}=RT\ln4-2RT\simeq-0.61371RT$. This makes forward cycling energetically possible under those assumptions; it specifies neither a molecular mechanism nor a rate. Merely adding a <carrier protein> cannot supply the missing <free energy>. Conversely, as the transported <concentration> ratio rises, the required uphill cost rises, so a fixed driving process has a finite thermodynamic limit.

= Exocytosis
{parent=Membrane transport}
{wiki}

<Exocytosis> releases the contents of a membrane-bounded compartment to the extracellular space by fusion with the <cell membrane>. <Synaptic vesicle> fusion is an example. Its contents are released, while its membrane becomes connected to the <cell membrane>; see <synaptic vesicle> for recovery and reuse.

= Endocytosis
{parent=Membrane transport}
{wiki}

<Endocytosis> takes material into a <biological cell> by inward budding and separation of part of its <cell membrane>. At a <chemical synapse>, it helps recover membrane and <proteins> after <synaptic vesicle> <exocytosis>. This recovery is distinct from uptake of individual <neurotransmitter> <molecules> through <membrane transport proteins>.

= Fluid mosaic model
{parent=Cell membrane}
{wiki}

The <fluid mosaic model> describes a <cell membrane> as a fluid <lipid bilayer> with embedded <membrane proteins>. It connects a continuous barrier to a mosaic of functional components: <diffusion> within the membrane can coexist with selective passage across it. S. J. Singer and G. L. Nicolson presented the <fluid mosaic model> in Science in 1972; see https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/4333397/[Singer and Nicolson, The fluid mosaic model of the structure of cell membranes]. The useful picture does not require every <protein> to move freely: attachments and interactions can restrict movement, as discussed in https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9898/[Cooper, The Cell: structure of the plasma membrane].

= Cytoplasm
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

The <cytoplasm> is the material inside a <cell membrane> but outside the <cell nucleus>, when one is present. It includes the <cytosol>, the <organelles> suspended in it, and other structures; it is not just a name for the liquid. In a <prokaryote>, which has no <cell nucleus>, the <nucleoid> and <ribosomes> are within the <cytoplasm>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 4.3: cytoplasm and cytosol] and https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 4.2: prokaryotic organization].

\Image[eukaryotic-cell-compartments.png]
{title=<Cell nucleus>, <cytosol> and <organelles> in a nucleated <eukaryotic> <biological cell>}
{description=Original schematic: the pale blue region represents <cytosol>, while the surrounding <cytoplasm> also includes the depicted <mitochondrion> and <ribosomes>. The <cell nucleus> and <cell nucleolus> are excluded from the <cytoplasm>. A <nuclear pore complex> provides a route across the double <nuclear envelope>. Dimensions and component counts are illustrative; many structures are omitted.}

Try classifying four positions in the diagram. A point in the pale blue region belongs to both <cytosol> and <cytoplasm>. A point inside the <mitochondrion> belongs to an <organelle> included in the <cytoplasm>, but not to the surrounding <cytosol>. A point on a free <ribosome> belongs to the <cytoplasm>, but the <ribosome> itself is not <cytosol>. A point inside the <cell nucleus> belongs to neither <cytoplasm> nor <cytosol>. These distinctions prevent the misleading inference that everything outside the <cell nucleus> shares one undivided liquid environment.

The usefulness of the <cytoplasm> is this combination of shared surroundings and specialized structures: <enzymes> in the <cytosol> carry out many steps of <metabolism>, while <organelles> maintain their own internal conditions. A soluble <protein> made by a free <ribosome> may work in the <cytosol> or be transported elsewhere, including into the <cell nucleus>. Cellular organization therefore combines separation with exchange, rather than assigning each <protein> permanently to its place of synthesis. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26932/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell (2002): traffic between nucleus and cytosol].

= Cytosol
{parent=Cytoplasm}
{wiki}

The <cytosol> is the aqueous part of the <cytoplasm> surrounding its structures, including <organelles>. It contains <water>, <ions> and dissolved <molecules>, including many <proteins>; it is a crowded working environment rather than pure <water>. The liquid inside a <cell nucleus> or a membrane-bounded <organelle> is not the surrounding <cytosol>.

= Nucleoid
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <nucleoid> is the region occupied by <chromosome> <DNA> in a <prokaryotic> <biological cell>. It has no surrounding <nuclear envelope>, but its <DNA> is organized rather than freely spread at random. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-2-prokaryotic-cells[OpenStax Biology 2e, 4.2: the nucleoid].

= Organelle
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

An <organelle> is a specialized subcellular structure. Usage varies over whether non-membrane-bounded structures such as <ribosomes> count as <organelles>, so explicitly say membrane-bounded <organelles> when comparing <eukaryotic> compartmentalization with <prokaryotic> organization. <Mitochondria> and <chloroplasts> are membrane-bounded examples. See https://openstax.org/books/microbiology/pages/3-4-unique-characteristics-of-eukaryotic-cells[OpenStax Microbiology, 3.4: cellular compartments].

= Plastid
{parent=Organelle}
{wiki}

A <plastid> is an <organelle> of the lineage that includes <chloroplasts>. A <chloroplast> is a photosynthetic <plastid>; the terms are not interchangeable because some <plastids> are nonphotosynthetic. <Endosymbiotic theory> connects <plastid> origins to <cyanobacteria>, while <endosymbiotic gene transfer> and <organelle protein import> explain genetic and functional integration with the host.

= Mitochondria
{parent=Organelle}
{wiki}

= Mitochondrion
{synonym}

A <mitochondrion> is an <organelle> whose inner membrane houses machinery for much of aerobic <cellular respiration> and <ATP> production in typical <eukaryotic> cells. <Mitochondria> descend from a bacterial <endosymbiosis> and retain a <genome> and <ribosomes>, although many required <proteins> are encoded by nuclear <genes>. This combination connects compartmentalized function to <evolution>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/23-1-eukaryotic-origins[OpenStax Biology 2e, 23.1: mitochondrial ancestry].

= Chloroplast
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <chloroplast> is a compartment in photosynthetic <plant> and <algae> <biological cells>. Its internal <thylakoids> carry out <light-dependent reactions>; its surrounding <chloroplast stroma> contains the <enzymes> of the <Calvin cycle>. <Cyanobacteria> perform <oxygenic photosynthesis> without <chloroplasts>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26819/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: chloroplasts and photosynthesis].

= Chloroplast stroma
{parent=Chloroplast}

The <chloroplast stroma> is the fluid compartment inside the <chloroplast> envelope but outside its <thylakoids>. The <Calvin cycle> operates here; the interior of a <thylakoid> is a separate compartment.

= Thylakoid
{parent=Chloroplast}
{wiki}

A <thylakoid> is a flattened membrane-bounded compartment. In a <chloroplast>, its membrane contains the machinery for <light-dependent reactions>, including <chlorophyll>. Stacks of <thylakoids> pack membrane area into a small space within the <chloroplast>; the <light-dependent reactions> schematic simplifies this architecture.

= Cell nucleus
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

The <cell nucleus> is a membrane-bounded compartment characteristic of <eukaryotic> <biological cells>, containing most of the cellular <DNA> in a typical nucleated <biological cell>. Its double <nuclear envelope> separates this <DNA> from the surrounding <cytoplasm>. Most does not mean all: <mitochondria> and <chloroplasts> retain their own <DNA>. Nor does every specialized <eukaryotic> <biological cell> retain a <cell nucleus>: mature mammalian <red blood cells> have lost theirs. <Prokaryotes> organize their <DNA> in a <nucleoid> without a <nuclear envelope>, and still have <ribosomes>. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Nucleus[NHGRI: nucleus], https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/23-1-eukaryotic-origins[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 23.1: organelle DNA]{ref} and https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/40-2-components-of-the-blood[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 40.2: mature mammalian red blood cells].

Follow one nuclear <protein>-coding <gene>: its <DNA> is used in <transcription> to make <RNA>; after the appropriate processing, <messenger RNA> is exported through a <nuclear pore complex>; a <ribosome> in the <cytoplasm> then uses it in <biological translation> to build an <amino acid> chain. The <DNA> remains in the <cell nucleus>. The <RNA> is a template, not the material converted into a <protein>. The <ribosome> diagram below follows this route; <gene expression> gives a complementary <nucleotide sequence> example. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26932/[Alberts et al.: selective RNA export and protein import].

This architecture makes <gene expression> interesting in a spatial way: nuclear <transcription> and cytoplasmic <biological translation> are separated by a selective boundary. A <messenger RNA> can be processed before export and use, while <proteins> needed for nuclear functions travel inward. Inside the <cell nucleus>, the <cell nucleolus> helps assemble <ribosome> subunits for export. Thus the compartment housing most <DNA> also helps build the machinery that interprets its messages elsewhere.

Robert Brown described and named the <cell nucleus> in his account of <plant> <biological cells>, read to the Linnean Society in 1831 and published in its Transactions in 1833. This historical observation identified a recurring structure, without establishing its later-understood role in <DNA> storage or <gene expression>. See https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1095-8339.1829.tb00158.x[Brown, On the Organs and Mode of Fecundation in Orchideæ and Asclepiadeæ, Transactions 16, 685–738 (1833)]. This is a documented contribution rather than a claim that no one had observed the structure earlier.

= Nuclear envelope
{parent=Cell nucleus}
{wiki}

The <nuclear envelope> is the double-membrane boundary around a <cell nucleus>. Regulated passage connects nuclear processes to the surrounding <cytoplasm>: <transcription> produces <RNA> in the <cell nucleus>, while exported <messenger RNA> can undergo <biological translation> on <ribosomes> outside it. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Nuclear-Membrane[NHGRI: nuclear membrane].

= Nuclear pore complex
{parent=Nuclear envelope}
{wiki}

= Nuclear pore
{synonym}

A <nuclear pore complex> is an assembly of <proteins> spanning the <nuclear envelope> and regulating passage between the <cell nucleus> and <cytosol>. It is a selective gateway, not simply an unregulated hole: <messenger RNA> export and import of nuclear <proteins> require appropriate transport machinery. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26932/[Alberts et al.: nuclear pore complexes].

= Nucleolus
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Cell nucleus}

= Cell nucleolus
{synonym}

The <cell nucleolus> is a region inside a <cell nucleus> without a surrounding membrane, where much <ribosomal RNA> is made and processed and <ribosome> subunits begin to assemble with <proteins>. The subunits are exported to the <cytoplasm>; the <cell nucleolus> is not itself a translating <ribosome>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 4.3: nucleolus and ribosomal subunits].

= Ribosome
{parent=Biological cell}
{wiki}

A <ribosome> is the molecular machinery for <biological translation>: it reads <messenger RNA> and joins <amino acids> into a <polypeptide> that can become a <protein>. Both <prokaryotes> and <eukaryotes> have <ribosomes>. A <ribosome> consists of <ribosomal RNA> and <proteins> organized into small and large subunits, with no enclosing membrane. <Ribosomal RNA> contributes to both structure and the <active site> that forms <peptide bonds>; a <ribosome> is therefore not merely a collection of <proteins>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 4.3: ribosome composition] and https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10937990/[Nissen et al., The structural basis of ribosome activity in peptide bond synthesis, Science 289, 920–930 (2000)].

\Image[ribosome-translation-schematic.png]
{title=Nuclear <transcription>, <RNA> export and <ribosome>-mediated <biological translation>}
{description=Original schematic of a nuclear <protein>-coding <gene> in a nucleated <eukaryotic> <biological cell>. The upper route tracks <DNA> to <RNA> by <transcription>, <RNA> export, then <biological translation>. The lower example reads three <messenger RNA> <codons> from $5'$ to $3'$ and shows a growing <polypeptide> segment from its <N-terminus> toward its <C-terminus>. Shapes and distances are illustrative; processing, flanking signals, additional machinery and termination are omitted.}

For a computed decoding example, choose the <reading frame> starting at the first displayed <nucleotide> of $5'\text{-}\mathrm{AUG\ GCU\ GAA}\text{-}3'$. Under the standard <genetic code>, AUG specifies <methionine> (Met), GCU specifies <alanine> (Ala), and GAA specifies a <glutamic acid residue> (Glu) in the growing <polypeptide>. Nine <nucleotides> divide into three <codons>, giving the ordered three-<amino acid residue> segment Met–Ala–Glu. The <ribosome> reads the <messenger RNA> $5'\to3'$, and the chain grows from its <N-terminus> toward its <C-terminus>. If synthesis begins at this AUG in an appropriate <eukaryotic> initiation context, these are the first three <amino acid residues> before any subsequent processing. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 15.1: standard codon assignments].

For this three-<amino acid residue> segment, joining the three delivered <amino acids> requires two <peptide bonds>, one between Met and Ala and another between Ala and Glu. There is no <stop codon> in the displayed <nucleotide sequence>, so this is a growing segment rather than a complete released <protein>. An isolated nine-<nucleotide> <RNA> is not claimed to supply all signals needed for initiation. This small calculation separates three jobs: the <messenger RNA> specifies order, <transfer RNA> delivers the <amino acids>, and the <ribosome> catalyzes their joining. Each new <amino acid residue> is added at the <C-terminus>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-5-ribosomes-and-protein-synthesis[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 15.5: initiation and chain elongation].

The beauty of a <ribosome> is its reusable chemistry: the same machinery can assemble many different <proteins> by following different <messenger RNA> templates. The <genetic code> connects an alphabet of <nucleotides> to an alphabet of <amino acids>, while <transfer RNA> links recognition of the message to delivery of building material. Changing the message can therefore change the product without building a different <ribosome> for every <protein>.

In 1955 George E. Palade described small particles in the <cytoplasm>, both free and associated with membranes, in https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2223592/[A small particulate component of the cytoplasm, Journal of Biophysical and Biochemical Cytology 1, 59–68]. The paper documents the structural observations, rather than the full modern decoding mechanism. Complementary biochemical work by Hoagland, Stephenson, Scott, Hecht and Zamecnik described a soluble <RNA> intermediate in <protein> synthesis in 1958, helping establish the role now assigned to <transfer RNA>; see https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/13538965/[A soluble ribonucleic acid intermediate in protein synthesis, Journal of Biological Chemistry 231, 241–257]. Together, structural observations and biochemical experiments connected visible cellular machinery with its working parts.

= Gliding bacterium
{parent=Biological cell}

A <gliding bacterium> moves along a surface without swimming through the bulk fluid. In a thin-fluid-film model, its relative motion dissipates energy through <Couette flow>, although the actuation mechanism can involve additional dissipation.

= Neutrophil
{parent=Biological cell}

A <neutrophil> is an immune <biological cell>. Its adhesive rolling on a surface involves contact mechanics, fluid stresses, and binding and unbinding of adhesive bonds.

= Red blood cell
{parent=Biological cell}

A red blood cell is a specialized <biological cell> that transports <oxygen> in blood. Its deformability lets it pass through vessels narrower than its unstressed size. A compliant-body <lubrication> model isolates the coupling between its deformation, the surrounding viscous film and a driving <pressure> difference; it is an idealization rather than a complete membrane or cytoplasm model.

= Membrane potential
{parent=Biological cell}
{title2=$V=V_{\mathrm{inside}}-V_{\mathrm{outside}}$}
{wiki}

The voltage difference across a cell membrane. Ion concentration gradients and selective membrane conductances determine it; changing conductance can change the voltage.

= Depolarization
{parent=Membrane potential}
{wiki}

For a negative neuronal <membrane potential>, <depolarization> raises it toward zero or beyond, making the interior less negative relative to the exterior. This describes a voltage change, not necessarily increased <action potential> generation; see the depolarizing inhibition example under <synaptic potential>.

= Hyperpolarization
{parent=Membrane potential}

<Hyperpolarization> makes a neuronal <membrane potential> more negative relative to the exterior. It is a voltage description; inhibition need not produce <hyperpolarization>, as <shunting inhibition> illustrates.

= Reversal potential
{parent=Membrane potential}
{wiki}
{title2=$I=g(E_{\mathrm{rev}}-V)$}

A <reversal potential> $E_{\mathrm{rev}}$ is a <membrane potential> at which a specified open <ion channel>, or specified combination of <ion channels>, carries zero net <electric current>, with the surrounding conditions fixed; the <electric current> changes sign across that voltage. Specifying which current matters: an individual <ion> contribution and a sum of several contributions can reverse at different voltages. Zero net <electric current> does not mean that all <ions> have stopped moving. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK11013/[Purves et al., Neuroscience, 2nd edition (2001): reversal of a mixed-ion channel current].

Throughout these examples, <membrane potential> is $V=V_{\mathrm{inside}}-V_{\mathrm{outside}}$, and \b[<conventional current> is inward-positive]. Positive <electric current> corresponds to net positive <electric charge> entering or net negative <electric charge> leaving. Negative <electric current> corresponds to the reverse charge transfer. A convenient Ohmic approximation is
$$
I_{\mathrm{in}}=g(E_{\mathrm{rev}}-V),\qquad g\geq0,
$$
where $g$ is the available <electrical conductance>. For fixed $g>0$, $V<E_{\mathrm{rev}}$ gives positive <electric current>, $V=E_{\mathrm{rev}}$ gives zero, and $V>E_{\mathrm{rev}}$ gives negative <electric current>; the line has slope $-g$. If $g=0$, the modeled pathway carries zero <electric current> at every voltage: closing it does not locate its <reversal potential>. <Neuronal Dynamics: From Single Neurons to Networks and Models of Cognition by Wulfram Gerstner and others/3.1 Synapses> (§3.1: conductance-based synaptic current) uses outward-positive $g(V-E)$; the formula here reverses that sign to match this wiki's inward-positive convention.

For the stipulated single-pathway example from <ion channel>, $E_{\mathrm{rev}}=-80\,\mathrm{mV}$ and $g=10\,\mathrm{nS}$ give
$$
\begin{array}{c|rrr}
V\ (\mathrm{mV})&-100&-80&-60\\
E_{\mathrm{rev}}-V\ (\mathrm{mV})&20&0&-20\\
I_{\mathrm{in}}\ (\mathrm{pA})&200&0&-200
\end{array}
$$
The open <ion channel> carries zero net <electric current> at $-80\,\mathrm{mV}$ even though $g$ remains positive. Near that point a change of <membrane potential> changes the <electric current>, which is why zero current at one voltage does not make an open pathway electrically irrelevant; see <shunting inhibition>.

\Image[ion-channel-current-and-reversal.png]
{title=Inward-positive <electric current>: one reversal and two-channel cancellation}
{description=Original computed curves for stipulated constant <electrical conductances> and fixed <reversal potentials>. Left: $g=10\,\mathrm{nS}$, $E_{\mathrm{rev}}=-80\,\mathrm{mV}$. Right: a <potassium> pathway with $g_{\mathrm K}=10\,\mathrm{nS}$, $E_{\mathrm K}=-80\,\mathrm{mV}$ and a <sodium> pathway with $g_{\mathrm{Na}}=2\,\mathrm{nS}$, $E_{\mathrm{Na}}=+50\,\mathrm{mV}$. Their net <electric current> vanishes at $-58.3333\,\mathrm{mV}$ while both individual <electric currents> remain nonzero. These are model values, not empirical measurements.}

For an <ion channel> passing only one <ion> species by <passive transport>, its <reversal potential> can equal that species' <Nernst equilibrium potential>, where the chemical and electrical contributions to <electrochemical potential> balance. At that <thermodynamic equilibrium>, passage in both directions can continue with no net transport. For an <ion channel> admitting several <ion> species, opposing contributions can cancel while each species continues to have net transport. Such a <reversal potential> is not generally any one species' <Nernst equilibrium potential>. See the Purves reference above.

To work out that cancellation explicitly, put two Ohmic <ion channel> populations across the same <cell membrane>. Stipulate constant <electrical conductances> and fixed <reversal potentials>:
$$
g_{\mathrm K}=10\,\mathrm{nS},\quad E_{\mathrm K}=-80\,\mathrm{mV},\qquad g_{\mathrm{Na}}=2\,\mathrm{nS},\quad E_{\mathrm{Na}}=+50\,\mathrm{mV}.
$$
The <potassium> and <sodium> contributions add because both pathways experience the same <membrane potential>:
$$
\begin{aligned}
I_{\mathrm{total}}&=g_{\mathrm K}(E_{\mathrm K}-V)+g_{\mathrm{Na}}(E_{\mathrm{Na}}-V)\\
&=(g_{\mathrm K}+g_{\mathrm{Na}})(E_{\mathrm{eff}}-V),\\
E_{\mathrm{eff}}&=\frac{g_{\mathrm K}E_{\mathrm K}+g_{\mathrm{Na}}E_{\mathrm{Na}}}{g_{\mathrm K}+g_{\mathrm{Na}}}
=\frac{-700}{12}\,\mathrm{mV}\approx-58.3333\,\mathrm{mV}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus $I_{\mathrm{total}}=12\,\mathrm{nS}(E_{\mathrm{eff}}-V)$. This <weighted mean> follows simply by expanding and regrouping the two current terms; it assumes additive Ohmic contributions and a positive total <electrical conductance>. At $V=E_{\mathrm{eff}}$, the inward-positive individual <electric currents> are
$$
I_{\mathrm K}=-\frac{650}{3}\,\mathrm{pA}\approx-216.6667\,\mathrm{pA},\qquad
I_{\mathrm{Na}}=+\frac{650}{3}\,\mathrm{pA}\approx+216.6667\,\mathrm{pA}.
$$
The <potassium> contribution is outward and the <sodium> contribution inward. Equal and opposite <electric currents> give $I_{\mathrm{total}}=0$; neither pathway has stopped transporting its <ions>. The calculation treats two distinct pathways. Applying it to one mixed-species <ion channel> would additionally require a justified decomposition into independent Ohmic contributions; a general mixed-channel <reversal potential> cannot be obtained by assuming those hypotheses silently.

This is an instantaneous balance of the specified passive <electric currents>. It would be the stationary voltage of a passive <capacitance> model containing only these constant pathways and no applied <electric current>, but it is not a complete prediction of a living cell's resting <membrane potential>. Other <electric currents>, voltage-dependent gating, and pumps involved in <active transport> can matter. Pumps that transfer net <electric charge> are electrogenic and contribute their own <electric current>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK10857/[Purves et al.: functional properties of the sodium/potassium pump].

The toy model holds <ion> <concentrations> and therefore the stipulated <reversal potentials> fixed. Continuing inward <sodium> and outward <potassium> transport would change finite reservoirs unless <active transport> or external control maintained them. Transport can be a small fraction of the reservoir's <ions> over a short interval, making fixed <concentrations> a useful approximation; it is not a claim that the reservoirs never change. See https://neuronaldynamics.epfl.ch/online/Ch2.S1.html[Gerstner et al., Neuronal Dynamics (2014), §2.1: equilibrium potentials, channel currents and maintained gradients].

A <reversal potential> makes the effect of opening an <ion channel> predictable from the present <membrane potential>. The same open pathway can carry inward or outward <electric current> as $V$ crosses its <reversal potential>. The two-pathway example reveals a particularly useful distinction: zero net charge transfer can coexist with continuing exchange of different substances. That distinction connects <membrane transport> to <electric current> measurements without mistaking electrical balance for <thermodynamic equilibrium>.

= Nernst equilibrium potential
{c}
{parent=Reversal potential}
{title2=$E_{\mathrm{ion}}=\frac{RT}{zF}\ln\!\left(\frac{a_{\mathrm{outside}}}{a_{\mathrm{inside}}}\right)$}

The <Nernst equilibrium potential> is the inside-minus-outside <membrane potential> at which one <ion> species has equal <electrochemical potential> on both sides of a <cell membrane>. At uniform <temperature> $T$, using the same reference <chemical potential> on both sides, $z\neq0$ is the signed <ion> charge number, $R$ the <molar gas constant>, $F$ the <Faraday constant>, and $a$ the dimensionless <thermodynamic activity> of that <ion>. For an ideal dilute <chemical solution>, the activity ratio becomes the <concentration> ratio. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK11102/[Purves et al.: the forces that create membrane potentials and the Nernst equation].

The short derivation is to set the outside-to-inside <electrochemical potential> change from <membrane transport> to zero: $RT\ln(a_{\mathrm{inside}}/a_{\mathrm{outside}})+zFV=0$, and solve for $V$. An open pathway carrying only this species by <passive transport> then has this <reversal potential>. A balance of several nonzero <ion> <electric currents> does not establish this equilibrium condition for each species.

= Voltage-gated ion channel
{parent=Membrane potential}
{wiki}

A membrane channel whose opening depends on <membrane potential>. Regenerative activation and subsequent inactivation or opposing currents help produce an <action potential>.

= Two-state ion-channel gating
{parent=Voltage-gated ion channel}
{title2=$\dot X=\alpha_X(V)(1-X)-\beta_X(V)X$}

<Two-state ion-channel gating> models an individual gate as a random switch between closed and open states. Write $p(t)$ for its open <probability>; this is the $X$ in the header equation. An individual gate's state is always closed or open, rather than partly open. A population of identical gates can have a fraction open near $p(t)$, and averaging repeated identically prepared trials estimates the same <probability>. A finite population's observed fraction fluctuates. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK10841/[Purves and colleagues, Neuroscience, 2nd edition (2001): Ion Channels Underlying Action Potentials] for single-channel steps and their population averages.

At fixed <membrane potential> $V$, let constant rates $\alpha,\beta>0$ govern closed-to-open and open-to-closed transitions respectively, in $\mathrm{s}^{-1}$. This is a two-state <continuous-time Markov chain>. Over a short interval $\Delta t$, an initially closed gate opens with <probability> $\alpha\Delta t+o(\Delta t)$, and an initially open gate closes with <probability> $\beta\Delta t+o(\Delta t)$. Gain minus loss gives the deterministic <ordinary differential equation> for the <probability>:
$$
\frac{dp}{dt}=\alpha(1-p)-\beta p=\frac{p_\infty-p}{\tau},\qquad
p_\infty=\frac{\alpha}{\alpha+\beta},\qquad \tau=\frac{1}{\alpha+\beta}.
$$
Here $p_\infty$ is the fixed-voltage limiting open <probability>, and $\tau$ is a <time constant>. The ratio of the rates fixes the limit, while their sum fixes the speed. With initial <probability> $p(0)=p_0\in[0,1]$,
$$
p(t)=p_\infty+(p_0-p_\infty)e^{-t/\tau}.
$$
For a short derivation, subtract $p_\infty$: the deviation obeys $d(p-p_\infty)/dt=-(p-p_\infty)/\tau$, yielding the <exponential function>. After one <time constant>, $1/e$ of the initial deviation remains. The solution stays between $p_0$ and $p_\infty$, so it remains a valid <probability>. The rate and <time constant> formulations are described in https://neuronaldynamics.epfl.ch/online/Ch2.S2.html[Gerstner and colleagues, Neuronal Dynamics, §2.2.2: transition rates and channel kinetics].

For a worked toy condition A, choose $\alpha=\beta=50\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and $p_0=0.1$. Then $p_\infty=0.5$, $\tau=0.01\,\mathrm{s}=10\,\mathrm{ms}$, and
$$
p(10\,\mathrm{ms})=0.5-0.4e^{-1}\approx0.352848.
$$
In a separately prepared condition B, choose $\alpha=200\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, $\beta=50\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and the same $p_0=0.1$. Now $p_\infty=0.8$, $\tau=4\,\mathrm{ms}$, and $p(10\,\mathrm{ms})=0.8-0.7e^{-2.5}\approx0.742540$. Condition B approaches a higher limit more quickly. These are two stipulated constant-rate conditions with $V$ held fixed in each experiment, not an empirical function relating rates to voltage or a sequential switch between conditions. The initial $p_0=0.1$ need not be the limiting <probability> at the newly imposed voltage.

\Image[ion-channel-gating-relaxation.png]
{title=<Two-state ion-channel gating>: two constant-rate relaxation conditions}
{description=Original computed <probability> curves, both starting at $p_0=0.1$. Dashed lines show the limiting open <probabilities> $0.5$ and $0.8$; dotted lines mark <time constants> $10$ and $4\,\mathrm{ms}$. The dot marks condition A at $10\,\mathrm{ms}$, where $p\approx0.352848$. Smooth curves represent open <probability> or population averages, not an individual gate's random state trace or an ionic <electric current>.}

In the <Hodgkin-Huxley model>, the rates depend on <membrane potential>: $\alpha_X(V)$ and $\beta_X(V)$. Its $X_\infty(V)=\alpha_X(V)/(\alpha_X(V)+\beta_X(V))$ is the limit for voltage held at that $V$. During an <action potential>, changing $V$ changes the rates and gates feed back onto $V$ through ionic <electric currents>; substituting the present voltage into the constant-rate solution does not solve that coupled system. Gating describes availability of a conducting pathway. Even an open <ion channel> carries zero net ionic <electric current> at its <reversal potential>.

The useful bridge is from abrupt random molecular events to a smooth population prediction. A channel requiring several <independent permissive gates in an ion channel> has an open <probability> equal to their product; this can make channel availability much smaller than each gate's availability. The <Hodgkin-Huxley model> uses $\bar g_{\mathrm{Na}}m^3h$ and $\bar g_{\mathrm K}n^4$ for <sodium> and <potassium> <electrical conductances>, respectively.

= Independent permissive gates in an ion channel
{parent=Two-state ion-channel gating}
{title2=$P_{\mathrm{open}}=p^q$}

A permissive gate allows conduction when it is open. Suppose an <ion channel> conducts only when all $q$ gates are open, where $q$ is a positive <integer>. If the gate-open <events> are mutually <independent events> and each has the same open <probability> $p$ at the time considered, then
$$
P_{\mathrm{open}}=\prod_{j=1}^q p=p^q.
$$
This follows from the multiplication rule for <independent events>. Identical transition rates alone do not guarantee independence: the initial gate states must also be independently prepared, and the model must not couple their transitions. Gates sharing a fluctuating <membrane potential> can be correlated; the simple product requires an appropriate independence assumption, for example independent gates at an externally fixed voltage.

At $p=0.5$, channels requiring $q=1,2,4$ permissive gates have open <probabilities> $0.5$, $0.25$ and $0.0625$. For four independent gates there are $2^4=16$ possible open/closed configurations, equally probable at $p=0.5$; only the all-open configuration conducts. Thus $0.5$ is the gate-open <probability>, while $0.0625$ is the whole-channel open <probability>. Neither is a fractional state of one observed channel. Among $160$ independently prepared identical four-gate channels, the <expected value> of the open count is $160/16=10$, although an actual count fluctuates.

\Image[independent-ion-channel-gates.png]
{title=<Independent permissive gates in an ion channel>: an all-open requirement}
{description=Left: whole-channel open <probability> $p^q$ for one, two and four identical independent permissive gates. Right: all sixteen configurations of four gates at $p=0.5$, each with <probability> $1/16$; filled circles are open gates and empty circles closed gates. Only the highlighted all-open configuration permits conduction. This is an illustrative model, not a molecular structure or an ionic <electric current> measurement.}

For unequal independent gate-open <probabilities> $p_j$, use $P_{\mathrm{open}}=\prod_j p_j$ rather than $p^q$. This is the distinction behind the <Hodgkin-Huxley model>'s $m^3h$: $m$ and $h$ are different activation and inactivation-availability variables, rather than one common $p$. Its $n^4$ uses four identical activation factors. Independence and the all-open requirement are model assumptions; correlated gates or alternative conducting configurations require a different calculation. The simple product is useful because a modest gate-open <probability> can produce sharply suppressed whole-channel availability without any additional gate interaction.

= Metabolism
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

The network of <chemical reactions> that maintains a living system, supplies energy and builds or processes its material. Gas emissions can arise as <primary metabolic byproducts> or <secondary metabolic byproducts>, with different implications for an <atmospheric biosignature gas>.

<Enzymes> make this network operate on biologically useful timescales. Following material through successive <substrates> and <reaction products> explains why a single fast <enzyme> need not make the entire network fast: another step may constrain the flow. Changing <enzyme> amounts or activity therefore changes which routes can carry material, connecting molecular <chemical kinetics> to the maintenance and growth of a <biological cell>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26911/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: multienzyme complexes and cell metabolism].

= Ethanol
{parent=Metabolism}
{tag=Chemical compound}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_2\mathrm{H}_5\mathrm{OH}$}

<Ethanol> is the <chemical compound> produced by the <alcoholic fermentation> described under <Saccharomyces cerevisiae>. Its two <carbon> <chemical atoms> explain the product bookkeeping there. See https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C64175&Mask=4[NIST Chemistry WebBook: ethanol formula and identity]; the Biology 2e section cited under <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> identifies <ethanol> as the alcohol product.

= Fermentation
{parent=Metabolism}
{wiki}

In its biochemical sense, <fermentation> is <metabolism> that obtains <ATP> from breakdown of organic <substrates> without a respiratory electron transport chain. The alcoholic route is one example; not every <fermentation> produces <ethanol>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/7-5-metabolism-without-oxygen[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 7.5: fermentation versus anaerobic respiration].

= Ethanol fermentation
{parent=Fermentation}
{wiki}

= Alcoholic fermentation
{synonym}

<Alcoholic fermentation> produces <ethanol> and <carbon dioxide> from <sugars> through an <enzyme>-catalyzed pathway. See <Saccharomyces cerevisiae> for its ideal <glucose> balance and <yeast> for the distinction between living cells and active extracts.

= Glycogen
{parent=Metabolism}
{wiki}

<Glycogen> is a highly branched <polysaccharide> of <glucose> units, serving as a storage form of <glucose> in animals. Liver and muscle provide familiar examples. The classical <second messenger> experiments measured regulation of an <enzyme> involved in <glycogen> breakdown, <glycogen phosphorylase>. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 3.2: glycogen]{ref}

= Glycogenesis
{parent=Metabolism}
{wiki}

<Glycogenesis> builds <glycogen>; <glycogenolysis> breaks it down. Their opposing directions matter in the <insulin> and <glucagon> circuit.

= Glycogenolysis
{parent=Metabolism}
{wiki}

= Gluconeogenesis
{parent=Metabolism}
{wiki}

<Gluconeogenesis> synthesizes <glucose> from non-<carbohydrate> precursors. It is distinct from <glycogenolysis>, which mobilizes an existing <glycogen> store.

= Lipolysis
{parent=Metabolism}
{wiki}

= Photosynthesis
{parent=Metabolism}
{wiki}

<Photosynthesis> captures <light> <energy> and transforms it into chemical forms that a <biological cell> can use. In <plants>, <algae> and <cyanobacteria>, this supports <carbon fixation>: incorporation of <carbon> from <carbon dioxide> into <chemical compounds> used to build living material. Capturing <light> and incorporating <carbon> are distinct processes coupled through <metabolism>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-1-overview-of-photosynthesis[OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.1: overview of photosynthesis].

For <oxygenic photosynthesis>, a simplified net equation writes a six-<carbon> <sugar>, <glucose>, as the representative product:
$$
6\mathrm{CO}_2+6\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}+\text{light}\longrightarrow\mathrm{C}_6\mathrm{H}_{12}\mathrm{O}_6+6\mathrm{O}_2.
$$
This is net <stoichiometry>, not a one-step mechanism. A worked bookkeeping check counts six <carbon> <chemical atoms>, twelve <hydrogen> <chemical atoms>, and eighteen <oxygen> <chemical atoms> on each side: the product side has six <oxygen> <chemical atoms> in the <sugar> and twelve in <molecular oxygen>. The <light> label supplies <energy>, not additional <chemical atoms>.

Crucially, the released <molecular oxygen> comes from <water>, not <carbon dioxide>. Cancelling <water> made and consumed in different steps hides this distinction in the net equation. The <Calvin cycle> supplies a net three-<carbon> <triose phosphate>, such as <glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate>, for subsequent <sugar> synthesis; it does not directly export <glucose>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis[OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.2: light-dependent reactions] and https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules[OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: using light energy to make organic molecules].

\Image[photosynthesis-energy-and-matter.png]
{title=<Photosynthesis>: <water> supplies released <molecular oxygen>; <carbon dioxide> supplies <sugar> <carbon>}
{description=Original schematic of <oxygenic photosynthesis>. Solid blue arrows trace selected material transfers; dashed orange arrows show <energy> transfer and the coupling through <ATP> and <NADPH>. These carriers are also <molecules>, not pure <energy>. Arrows omit intermediate <chemical reactions> and do not specify rates or yields.}

The useful achievement is connecting incoming <light> to both usable <energy> and new biological material. <ATP> is an immediate <energy> currency; <NADPH> supplies <electrons> for synthesis; <carbon dioxide> supplies the <carbon> incorporated into <carbohydrates>. These roles are complementary. <Conservation of energy> still holds: <photosynthesis> transforms <energy>, never creates it, and some absorbed <energy> is dissipated as <heat>. A <plant> also carries out <cellular respiration>, including in <biological cells> that perform <photosynthesis>. Gas exchange reflects both processes. <Oxygenic photosynthesis> remains central to interpreting terrestrial <molecular oxygen> as an <atmospheric biosignature gas>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules[OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: photosynthesis and respiration].

= Oxygenic photosynthesis
{parent=Photosynthesis}

<Oxygenic photosynthesis> uses <water> as an <electron> source and releases <molecular oxygen>. <Plants>, <algae> and <cyanobacteria> provide examples. The defining distinction from <anoxygenic photosynthesis> is <molecular oxygen> release, not merely capture of <light>.

= Anoxygenic photosynthesis
{parent=Photosynthesis}
{wiki}

<Anoxygenic photosynthesis> captures <light> without producing <molecular oxygen>. It occurs in some <bacteria> and uses <electron> sources other than <water>. Whether <carbon fixation> accompanies this <light> capture depends on the <organism> and conditions; <photosynthesis> does not imply one universal <carbon>-assimilation pathway. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26819/[Alberts et al.: chloroplasts and photosynthetic bacteria].

= Light-dependent reactions
{parent=Photosynthesis}
{wiki}

In a <chloroplast>, <light-dependent reactions> take place at <thylakoid> membranes. <Chlorophyll> captures <light>; <electron> transfer helps establish an <electrochemical gradient> across the <thylakoid> membrane, enabling formation of <ATP>. The <oxygenic photosynthesis> pathway also forms <NADPH> and releases <molecular oxygen> from <water>. <ATP> and <NADPH> become available in the <chloroplast stroma> for the <Calvin cycle>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26819/[Alberts et al.: chloroplast light reactions].

\Image[chloroplast-photosynthesis-compartments.png]
{title=<Thylakoid> membranes and <chloroplast stroma> couple two stages of <photosynthesis>}
{description=Original <chloroplast> schematic, not an experimental photograph or a scale drawing. <Light-dependent reactions> supply <ATP> and <NADPH> to the stromal <Calvin cycle>; depleted carriers return for reuse. The <molecular oxygen> arrow leaves the <water>-using stage, while <carbon dioxide> enters <carbon fixation>.}

= Chlorophyll
{parent=Light-dependent reactions}
{wiki}

<Chlorophyll> is a family of <molecules> that absorb <light> for <photosynthesis>. In <chloroplasts>, <chlorophyll> works with <proteins> in <thylakoid> membranes; absorbing <light> initiates <energy> capture rather than directly constructing a <sugar>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-2-the-light-dependent-reactions-of-photosynthesis[OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.2: photosynthetic pigments].

= Carbon fixation
{parent=Metabolism}
{wiki}

<Carbon fixation> incorporates <carbon> from <carbon dioxide> into organic <chemical compounds>. It identifies a material transformation, rather than a particular source of <energy>: <carbon fixation> can be supported by <photosynthesis> or by other <metabolism>. The <Calvin cycle> is one pathway, not the definition of all <carbon fixation>.

= Calvin cycle
{c}
{parent=Carbon fixation}
{wiki}

= Calvin-Benson cycle
{c}
{synonym}

The <Calvin cycle> couples <carbon fixation> to use of <ATP> and <NADPH>. In a <chloroplast> it takes place in the <chloroplast stroma>. Its <chemical reactions> incorporate <carbon dioxide>, use <NADPH>-supplied <electrons>, and regenerate the <molecules> that accept further <carbon dioxide>.

In the standard <Calvin cycle> bookkeeping, three <carbon dioxide> <molecules> require nine <ATP> and six <NADPH> for one net three-<carbon> <triose phosphate>. A six-<carbon> <sugar> equivalent therefore corresponds to six <carbon dioxide>, eighteen <ATP> and twelve <NADPH>; subsequent <sugar> synthesis is separate. These are pathway requirements, not a universal measured <photosynthesis> yield. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules[OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: Calvin cycle bookkeeping].

Calling the <Calvin cycle> light-independent means its individual <chemical reactions> do not directly capture <light>; it does not mean the <Calvin cycle> runs only at night or independently of the supplies from <light-dependent reactions>.

= Cellular respiration
{parent=Metabolism}
{wiki}

<Cellular respiration> transfers <energy> from chemical fuels into usable forms including <ATP>. <Plants> carry out <cellular respiration> as well as <photosynthesis>; in its aerobic form, <cellular respiration> consumes <molecular oxygen> and releases <carbon dioxide>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules[OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: energy flow].

= Secondary metabolic byproduct
{parent=Metabolism}

A compound released through specialized functions such as signaling, defense or stress adaptation rather than the core reactions needed for energy and biomass. <Dimethyl sulfide> and <chloromethane> are examples discussed as atmospheric biosignatures. Their specialized origins may improve specificity while lowering the global emission flux.

= Primary metabolic byproduct
{parent=Metabolism}

A product released by reactions needed for energy acquisition, growth or biomass construction. <Methane> from energy-yielding microbial reactions and <molecular oxygen> from oxygenic <photosynthesis> are examples. Abiotic chemistry can make some of the same molecules, so production mechanism and environmental abundance matter.

= Evolution
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

<Evolution> is descent with inherited modification. Within a population, changes in <allele frequencies> provide a precise genetic description; over longer histories, branching descent connects populations through <common descent>. An individual growing larger is <biological development>, whereas an inherited variant becoming more frequent across generations is <evolution>. See https://evolution.berkeley.edu/evolution-101/an-introduction-to-evolution/[UC Museum of Paleontology: the definition of evolution].

<Mutation> supplies new <alleles>, <genetic recombination> reshuffles inherited material, <natural selection> favors variants with greater reproductive contribution in a specified environment, and <genetic drift> changes frequencies through chance. <Gene flow> moves variants between populations. Consequently, an increasing <allele frequency> alone does not identify <natural selection>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/19-2-population-genetics[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: population genetics].

Evidence includes ordered <fossils>, shared inherited structures and similarities in <DNA>; <phylogenetics> uses such observations to infer relationships. A <phylogenetic tree> is an inferred historical account, with uncertainty about particular branches. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/18-1-understanding-evolution[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: evidence of evolution]. Evidence also comes from experiments: Blount, Borland and Lenski reported an <Escherichia coli> lineage able to use <citrate> with <oxygen> present by 31,500 generations, and tested historical dependence by replaying <evolution> from stored ancestors. See https://www.blount-lab.org/uploads/2/3/0/3/23037222/2008_pnas_blount_et_al.pdf[Blount, Borland and Lenski, 2008: original experimental report]. Such observations constrain explanations; no single frequency equation captures every evolutionary process.

= Speciation
{parent=Evolution}
{wiki}

<Speciation> is the formation of distinct <species> through <evolution>. Detecting a difference between <biological populations> is a starting observation; its interpretation depends on the stated <species concept> and the evidence for lasting separation. <Reproductive isolation> is one important route to limiting <gene flow> in <sexual reproduction>.

= Reproductive isolation
{parent=Speciation}
{wiki}

<Reproductive isolation> consists of barriers that reduce <gene flow> through <sexual reproduction>. Distinguish <premating isolation> from barriers after mating. Barriers before <fertilization> also include incompatible <gametes>; barriers after <fertilization> can reduce offspring survival or fertility. <Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others>[§18.2, “Reproductive Isolation”, opening paragraphs, gametic barriers and hybrid inviability/sterility]{ref}

An assay should state which step it measures. Observing fewer cross-group matings does not measure offspring fertility; observing <fertilization> does not demonstrate that the offspring will reproduce. These distinctions make a partial barrier informative without declaring completed <speciation>.

= Premating isolation
{parent=Reproductive isolation}

<Premating isolation> reduces mating between <biological populations>. The <dietary divergence and mating isolation in Drosophila pseudoobscura> assay measures this stage using observed pairings.

= Postmating reproductive isolation
{parent=Reproductive isolation}

<Postmating reproductive isolation> acts after mating. Specify whether the test concerns <fertilization>, offspring survival, or the offspring's own <reproduction>; those outcomes are not interchangeable.

= Dietary divergence and mating isolation in Drosophila pseudoobscura
{parent=Reproductive isolation}

The 1989 experiment used eight <Drosophila pseudoobscura> <biological populations> from one Bryce Canyon population: four maintained on starch medium, four on maltose, in cages at $25\,{}^\circ\mathrm{C}$. Testing began about a year after establishment. <Reproductive Isolation as a Consequence of Adaptive Divergence in Drosophila pseudoobscura by Diane M. B. Dodd>[p. 1308, population origin, media and cage conditions]{ref}

Before testing, flies spent one generation on cornmeal–molasses–agar. Virgins aged 3–6 days entered multiple-choice chambers: twelve females from each population, followed by males from both populations introduced nearly simultaneously without anesthesia. Observers scored matings for 60–90 minutes under bright indirect lighting. One group's right wing tips were clipped for identification, with clipping reversed between replicates. <Reproductive Isolation as a Consequence of Adaptive Divergence in Drosophila pseudoobscura by Diane M. B. Dodd>[p. 1308, mating-preference procedure and wing-marking control]{ref}

All sixteen cross-diet comparisons had positive <mating assortment contrasts>; eleven departed significantly from <random mating>. Same-diet comparisons did not show significant departures. Common-medium rearing addressed immediate diet conditioning; same-diet controls addressed separation and establishment <population bottlenecks>. Mate choice was not the selection target; the <biological populations> were maintained separately. The result supports partial <premating isolation> accompanying dietary divergence, not completed <speciation>. <Reproductive Isolation as a Consequence of Adaptive Divergence in Drosophila pseudoobscura by Diane M. B. Dodd>[pp. 1309–1310, Tables 1–2 and discussion of conditioning and physical isolation]{ref}

\Image[dietary-divergence-mating-assay.png]
{title=Design of the <dietary divergence and mating isolation in Drosophila pseudoobscura> assay}
{description=Original design schematic. Arrows indicate procedure, not a measured <reproductive isolation> rate. The four mating categories are possible outcomes; no invented counts are shown.}

The <causal inference> remains limited. A negative same-diet <hypothesis test> does not prove zero <reproductive isolation>. One generation under common conditions does not exclude every persistent environmental influence. The chamber readout alone cannot identify a particular <gene>, establish offspring fertility, or predict <gene flow> after sustained contact in nature. Further experiments would need to target those questions separately.

= Mating assortment contrast
{parent=Reproductive isolation}
{title2=$I=(N_{\mathrm{same}}-N_{\mathrm{cross}})/N$}

For observed pairings between two labels $A,B$, let $N_{\mathrm{same}}=N_{AA}+N_{BB}$ and $N_{\mathrm{cross}}=N_{AB}+N_{BA}$, where the first label denotes the female's group and the second the male's. A <mating assortment contrast> is
$$
I=\frac{N_{\mathrm{same}}-N_{\mathrm{cross}}}{N},\qquad N=N_{\mathrm{same}}+N_{\mathrm{cross}}>0.
$$
It lies between $-1$ and $1$. Positive $I$ describes an excess of same-label pairings over cross-label pairings; whether that is evidence of <assortative mating> depends on the null <mathematical model> and the assay. The four counts form a <contingency table>.

Here is an original scope check. Suppose independently paired females and males have <probabilities> $p_f,p_m$ of carrying label $A$. Then
$$
q_{\mathrm{same}}=p_fp_m+(1-p_f)(1-p_m),\qquad \mathbb E[I]=2q_{\mathrm{same}}-1.
$$
With $p_f=p_m=1/2$, the <expected value> is zero. With $p_f=p_m=4/5$, it is $9/25=0.36$, despite pairing being independent of label. This counterexample concerns unequal pool composition; it does not replace the group introductions and <contingency table> tests in the <dietary divergence and mating isolation in Drosophila pseudoobscura> experiment. Even with balanced pools, finite observed samples fluctuate: a positive $I$ alone is not a <hypothesis test>, much less a universal <species> boundary.

= Common descent
{parent=Evolution}
{wiki}

= Horizontal gene transfer
{parent=Evolution}
{wiki}

<Horizontal gene transfer> moves genetic material between lineages through routes other than ordinary inheritance from parent to offspring. <Endosymbiotic gene transfer> is a specialized case involving an internal partner or its descendant <organelle>.

= Gene flow
{parent=Evolution}
{wiki}

= Natural selection
{parent=Evolution}
{wiki}

<Natural selection> involves systematic differences in expected <reproductive success> associated with variation in <phenotype>. For a continuing evolutionary response, the relevant variation must be inherited: offspring must tend to preserve the differences being favored. Actual offspring counts also fluctuate by chance, producing <genetic drift>. Survival matters insofar as it affects contribution to future generations. <Genotype fitness> is therefore contextual, depending on environment and the life cycle being counted, rather than simply measuring strength or longevity. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/19-1-population-evolution[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: population evolution].

In chapter IV of his 1859 book, Darwin explained <natural selection> through preservation of favorable variations and greater chances of survival and reproduction. See https://darwin-online.org.uk/converted/published/1859_Origin_F373/1859_Origin_F373.html[Darwin, On the Origin of Species, first edition, chapter IV]. The beautiful consequence is cumulative change without foresight: inherited differences are filtered repeatedly by present conditions. <Natural selection> neither generates a needed <mutation> on demand nor guarantees progress toward a predetermined goal.

= Haploid selection recurrence
{parent=Natural selection}
{title2=$p'=\frac{wp}{wp+(1-p)}$}

Consider an explicitly idealized <haploid> population with two inherited types $A,B$, one <allele> per individual at the tracked <genetic locus>, and discrete, nonoverlapping generations. Assume faithful type transmission, no <mutation> or <gene flow>, a constant environment, and reproductive contributions independent of type frequency. Let the <expected values> of offspring surviving to the next census be $m_A,m_B>0$, with relative <genotype fitness> $w=m_A/m_B$.

For $A$ frequency $p$, expected offspring contributions are proportional to $pm_A$ and $(1-p)m_B$. Normalizing gives the deterministic large-population <mathematical model>
$$
p'=\frac{pm_A}{pm_A+(1-p)m_B}=\frac{wp}{wp+(1-p)}.
$$
The denominator is mean relative <genotype fitness>. For $w=1.2$ and $p_0=0.2$, $p_1=0.24/1.04=3/13\approx0.23077$. Equivalently, 200 $A$ and 800 $B$ parents with $m_A=1.2,m_B=1$ contribute expected counts 240 and 800. Fractional per-parent <expected values> are averages, not literal fractional offspring. The ratio of these expectations is a deterministic approximation, not generally the expectation of a random ratio.

The odds multiply particularly simply:
$$
\frac{p_{t+1}}{1-p_{t+1}}=w\frac{p_t}{1-p_t},\qquad p_t=\frac{w^tp_0}{1-p_0+w^tp_0}.
$$
Thus $p_{10}\approx0.60753$: repeated modest advantage can substantially change composition. For $w>1$ and $0<p<1$, $p'-p=(w-1)p(1-p)/(1+(w-1)p)>0$. The deterministic boundary $p=0$ remains unchanged: <natural selection> needs existing variation.

\Image[natural-selection-frequency.png]
{title=Deterministic selection and stochastic sampling in a finite population}
{description=Both panels start at $p_0=0.2$. The left compares the deterministic <haploid selection recurrence> with eight simulated finite-population paths at $w=1.2$; the right shows eight neutral <genetic drift> paths at $w=1$. Each simulation samples $N=50$ offspring independently per generation. These are illustrative <mathematical models>, not experimental measurements.}

A distinct stochastic extension fixes $N$ offspring and samples their types independently using a <binomial distribution>: $K\sim\operatorname{Binomial}(N,q)$, where $q=wp/(wp+1-p)$, then $p'=K/N$. Its conditional <expected value> is $q$ and conditional <variance> is $q(1-q)/N$. With $w=1$, the conditional <expected value> remains $p$, yet realized frequencies wander through <genetic drift>. With $w>1$, <genetic drift> can still lower $p$ or lose $A$ entirely. Over several generations, the deterministic curve need not equal the mean stochastic trajectory, because the recurrence is nonlinear.

= Reproductive success
{parent=Natural selection}
{wiki}

= Phylogenetics
{parent=Evolution}
{wiki}

= Clade
{parent=Phylogenetics}
{wiki}

A <clade> comprises an ancestor and all its descendants. Membership is about <common descent>, not a taxonomic rank or a degree of cellular complexity. In a <two-domain system>, the nuclear lineage of <eukaryotes> nests within <archaea>; a group containing only the <prokaryotic> members excludes descendants and is therefore not that complete <clade>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/20-2-determining-evolutionary-relationships[OpenStax Biology 2e, 20.2: clades and shared ancestry].

= Phylogenetic tree
{parent=Phylogenetics}
{wiki}

A <phylogenetic tree> describes ancestral relationships. In a <probabilistic graphical model> on a rooted <tree>, <latent variables> at ancestral vertices are inferred from observations at leaves using <belief propagation>.

= Felsenstein pruning algorithm
{c}
{parent=Phylogenetic tree}

The <Felsenstein pruning algorithm> computes a site <likelihood function> by <sum-product belief propagation> from leaves to root. For a finite-state <Markov kernel> $K$, the subtree <likelihood function> obeys $L_v(a)=\prod_{w\text{ child of }v}\sum_bK(a,b)L_w(b)$, with observed-state indicators at leaves. Summing against the root <probability distribution> gives the site <likelihood function>. An outward pass gives edge <posterior probabilities> and expected transition counts for the <expectation-maximization algorithm>.

= Calcium in biology
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

Calcium has roles in cell signalling and skeletal structure. Dietary <calcium intake> and <serum calcium> are distinct quantities: physiological regulation means a change in one need not correspond to an equivalent change in the other.

= Serum calcium
{parent=Calcium in biology}

Serum calcium is the concentration of calcium in blood serum. It can be an <exposure> in an <observational study> or <Mendelian randomization>, but genetically changing it need not reproduce the effect of changing dietary <calcium intake>.

= Hypercalcaemia
{parent=Serum calcium}
{wiki}

= Hypercalcemia
{synonym}

Hypercalcaemia is an abnormally elevated <serum calcium> concentration. A diagnosis in medical records depends on testing and diagnostic practice, which can introduce <selection bias> into an <observational study>.

= Calcium intake
{parent=Calcium in biology}

Calcium intake is the amount of calcium consumed, including food and supplements. It is an <exposure> that can be changed by an intervention, and should not be identified automatically with <serum calcium> in a <causal inference> analysis.

= Reproduction
{parent=Biology}

= Asexual reproduction
{parent=Reproduction}

<Asexual reproduction> produces offspring without fusion of <gametes>. An operational <species concept> for such <organisms> needs evidence other than interbreeding.

= Budding
{parent=Reproduction}
{wiki}

<Budding> produces a new individual from an outgrowth of its parent. In <Saccharomyces cerevisiae>, the daughter <biological cell> grows while attached to the mother and later separates; see the Burgess, Powers and Mell account cited there. <Budding> describes <reproduction>, not <fermentation>.

= Sexual reproduction
{parent=Reproduction}

= Gamete
{parent=Sexual reproduction}

A gamete is a reproductive <biological cell> that can fuse with another <gamete> during <fertilization>. In the ordinary <diploid> inheritance model, a <haploid> <gamete> carries one copy at each <genetic locus>.

= Ovum
{parent=Gamete}

An <ovum> is a female <gamete>.

= Fertilization
{parent=Sexual reproduction}

<Fertilization> is fusion of <gametes>. Fusion of two <haploid> <gametes> restores the <diploid> state in the inheritance model used for <Mendelian segregation>.

= Pollination
{parent=Sexual reproduction}

= Pollen
{parent=Pollination}

= Oogenesis
{parent=Sexual reproduction}

<Oogenesis> is development of female <gametes>; the unequal divisions of human <meiosis> produce <polar bodies> alongside the developing <ovum>.

= Polar body
{parent=Oogenesis}

A <polar body> is a small product of an unequal division during <oogenesis>.

= Genetics
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

<Genetics> studies how <genes> are inherited, how their <alleles> vary, and how that variation contributes to <phenotypes>. Its central distinction is between an inherited description, the <genotype>, and what an <organism> actually expresses, the <phenotype>. Following <alleles> through a <pedigree> answers a family-level question; following <allele frequencies> through generations leads to <population genetics> and <evolution>.

The beauty of <Mendelian segregation> is that discrete inherited copies can generate predictable statistical patterns without making every offspring identical. A single <genetic locus> already connects <meiosis>, <probability> and observable variation. The <Hardy-Weinberg principle> then supplies a population baseline: it separates reshuffling inherited copies from processes such as <natural selection> that change their frequencies. These simple models are useful precisely because their assumptions can be stated and checked.

= Strain
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Genetics}

= Biological strain
{synonym}

A <biological strain> is a maintained variant or lineage within a <species>. Naming the <species> alone does not specify a laboratory <genotype> or its <genetic background>.

= Genetic background
{parent=Genetics}

The <genetic background> is the rest of the <genotype> against which a focal <allele> or <gene deletion> is studied. Other <genes> can influence the resulting <phenotype>, so comparisons should specify which background is held fixed.

= Functional complementation
{parent=Genetics}

<Functional complementation> is restoration of a specified function or <phenotype> by introducing a <gene> into a deficient recipient. In the <yeast cross-species complementation experiment>, the restored property is growth without supplied <methionine>, under the assay conditions. Rescue need not restore every property of the original <genotype>.

= Molecular genetics
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

= Plasmid
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

A <plasmid> is a <DNA> element capable of replication separately from the main <chromosomes> of its host. Engineered <plasmids> can carry <genes> for <genetic transformation> and <functional complementation>.

= Genetic transformation
{parent=Molecular genetics}

<Genetic transformation> introduces <DNA> into recipient <biological cells>. It can change their <genotype> and sometimes their <phenotype>; the <yeast cross-species complementation experiment> separates detecting introduction of a <plasmid> from detecting its functional effect.

= Replica plating
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

<Replica plating> transfers samples from corresponding <biological colonies> or patches to several growth conditions while preserving their spatial arrangement. A master plate makes it possible to relate growth or failure at one location to the same source population on another plate.

= Genome
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

A <genome> is the complete set of genetic information under discussion. For a cellular <organism> it is encoded in <DNA>, including <genes> and other sequences. Specify whether a comparison concerns a nuclear <genome>, a <mitochondrion>'s <genome> or all cellular genetic material: the <cell nucleus> does not contain every <DNA> sequence in a typical <eukaryote>. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Genome[NHGRI: genome].

= DNA sequencing
{c}
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

<DNA sequencing> determines the order of <nucleotide> bases in <DNA>. Sequence data can test molecular rearrangements, as in the <functional plastid-to-nucleus gene transfer experiment>, or support <phylogenetics> after a suitable <sequence alignment>.

= Sequence alignment
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

A <sequence alignment> places <nucleotide sequences> or <protein> sequences in correspondence for comparison. The choice of corresponding positions affects evolutionary inference; matching a short stretch alone does not establish <common descent> of entire <organisms>.

= Polymerase chain reaction
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

The <polymerase chain reaction> amplifies a selected <DNA> region using repeated copying steps. Amplified material can be examined by <DNA sequencing>; appropriate template controls help distinguish a new rearrangement from material already present in the starting preparation.

= Northern blot
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

A <Northern blot> separates <RNA> by size and detects selected sequences through hybridization with a probe. Detection of a <messenger RNA> supports <transcription> of a <gene>, but does not alone establish a functional <protein> or its destination.

= DNA replication
{c}
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

<DNA replication> copies a double-stranded <DNA> <molecule> into two daughter double-stranded <DNA> <molecules>. In <semiconservative replication>, each daughter retains one parental strand and receives one newly synthesized complementary strand. <DNA polymerases> build the new strands from incoming <nucleotides>; they do not make <RNA> or <proteins> in this copying step. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26850/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition (2002): DNA replication mechanisms].

For a five-<base pair> example, separate $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ATGCC}\text{-}3'$ from its partner $3'\text{-}\mathrm{TACGG}\text{-}5'$. <Base pairing> specifies a new TACGG partner for the old ATGCC strand and a new ATGCC partner for the old TACGG strand. With error-free copying, both daughter <DNA> <molecules> have the original <nucleotide sequence>, but different physical strand histories. One initial duplex contains ten <nucleotides>; one complete round produces two duplexes containing twenty <nucleotides>, ten retained and ten newly incorporated.

\Image[dna-replication-semi-conservative.png]
{title=<Semiconservative replication>: follow two original <DNA> strands through two complete copying rounds}
{description=Blue O1 and purple O2 are the original strands; green strands were made in round 1 and orange strands in round 2. Each arrow connects a parent duplex to its daughters. After round 2, two of four duplexes retain an original strand, and the other two retain a round-1 strand. All eight strands have the appropriate complementary <nucleotide sequences>; colors identify strand age, not different <nucleobases>. The flattened duplexes show ancestry and orientation, not a replication fork or molecular shape.}

This is useful for inheritance: both daughter <biological cells> can receive a copy of the <DNA> record. The elegant feature is that <base pairing> lets each strand specify its missing partner. Direction matters too: a replicating <DNA polymerase> reads its template $3'\to5'$ and extends the new strand at its $3'$ end, so synthesis proceeds $5'\to3'$. These are opposite directions on <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>, not two alternative directions of synthesis. The enzyme needs a pre-existing paired strand end to extend; the diagram omits the machinery that starts and coordinates copying. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26850/[Alberts et al.: template-directed synthesis and the requirement for a primer].

The strand accounting predicts observable differences between copying mechanisms. In 1958, Meselson and Stahl grew <Escherichia coli> with the heavier <nitrogen> <isotope> $^{15}\mathrm{N}$, then supplied $^{14}\mathrm{N}$ and measured extracted <DNA> by density-gradient centrifugation. After one generation their samples contained intermediate-density <DNA>; after two, intermediate-density and light <DNA> occurred in equal amounts. This supported <semiconservative replication> in the tested <Escherichia coli> culture. It was an <isotope> assay of sampled <DNA>, not simultaneous testing of every kind of <DNA> or direct colored-strand photography. See <The replication of DNA in Escherichia coli by Matthew Meselson and Franklin W. Stahl>.

= Semiconservative replication
{parent=DNA replication}
{wiki}

In <semiconservative replication>, each daughter duplex retains one strand of its immediate parent. Under complete copying without strand loss or exchange, one starting duplex gives $2^n$ duplexes after $n$ rounds, but still only two original strands. For $n\geq1$, the fraction of duplexes containing an original strand is $2/2^n=2^{1-n}$: all after one round, half after two, a quarter after three. The fraction of all strands that are original is instead $2^{-n}$. A strand made in round 1 is parental when round 2 begins.

= DNA polymerase
{c}
{parent=DNA replication}
{wiki}

A replicating <DNA polymerase> is an <enzyme> that extends a paired strand end with <nucleotides> selected against a <DNA> template. New <DNA> grows $5'\to3'$ while the template is read $3'\to5'$. <Base pairing> specifies the partner; the <DNA polymerase> catalyzes its incorporation. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26850/[Alberts et al.: DNA polymerase].

= DNA replication inhibitor
{c}
{parent=DNA replication}

A <DNA replication inhibitor> interferes with <DNA replication>; the observed effect depends on exposure and assay conditions.

= Hydroxyurea
{parent=DNA replication inhibitor}
{wiki}

<Hydroxyurea> supplies the synthesis-blocking comparison in the experiment under <EdU labeling>.

= DNA repair
{c}
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

<DNA repair> addresses damage to <DNA>. Incorporation of a labeled precursor during <DNA repair> need not indicate duplication of the whole <genome> in <S phase>.

= Gene expression
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

<Gene expression> uses a <gene>'s information to produce functional <RNA> or a <protein>. <Transcription> makes <RNA> from a <DNA> template; for a <protein>-coding <gene>, <biological translation> then uses <messenger RNA> to specify an <amino acid> chain. Many <genes> instead produce functional <RNA>, including <transfer RNA> and <ribosomal RNA>, without a translated <protein> product. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26887/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition (2002): from DNA to RNA].

Follow the chosen <coding strand> $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ATGGCTTAA}\text{-}3'$: <transcription> of its complementary template gives $5'\text{-}\mathrm{AUGGCUUAA}\text{-}3'$ in <messenger RNA>. In the stated <reading frame>, its three <codons> are AUG, GCU and UAA: <methionine>, <alanine> and a <stop codon>. Thus nine <nucleotides> specify two <amino acid> residues followed by termination, not three residues. This is an illustrative coding segment embedded in suitable expression and initiation context, not a claim that an isolated nine-<nucleotide> <RNA> is sufficient to initiate <biological translation>.

\Image[gene-expression-transcription-translation.png]
{title=<Gene expression>: oriented <DNA> template, <messenger RNA> <codons> and the resulting <amino acid> order}
{description=The <coding strand> matches the displayed <messenger RNA> after T is replaced by U; <RNA polymerase> uses the opposite, complementary <DNA> strand. The <ribosome> reads AUG, GCU and UAA from $5'$ to $3'$. AUG and GCU specify <methionine> and <alanine>; UAA signals termination without supplying an <amino acid>. The chain is shown from its <N-terminus> to its <C-terminus>. The labels summarize successive operations, not simultaneous binding of all depicted components; flanking signals and machinery are omitted.}

The distinction between copying and use explains how <biological cells> can preserve the same <DNA> yet produce different products. <DNA replication> duplicates the record; <gene expression> selects and uses parts of it. <Transcription> does not use all <DNA> at once, and <gene expression> can be controlled at several steps, including <transcription>, <RNA> processing and <biological translation>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/16-1-regulation-of-gene-expression[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: regulation of gene expression]. The arrows describe template-directed synthesis: the <DNA> does not physically turn into a <protein>, and the <messenger RNA> does not supply the <amino acids> from which that <protein> is assembled.

= Galactose induction
{parent=Gene expression}

In the GAL1 <gene promoter> system used in the <yeast cross-species complementation experiment>, the inducing <sugar> galactose promotes <gene expression> from the engineered construct, whereas <glucose> provides a noninducing comparison. The comparison concerns an imposed regulatory system, not the donor <species>’ native regulation. <Pathways Over Time Plasmids Engage Students in Functional Genomics Research by Clare O’Connor>[O’Connor (2018), Plasmid features enable multiple student experiments and Complementation reveals conservation of function: GAL1 promoter]{ref}

= Promoter
{disambiguate=genetics}
{parent=Gene expression}
{wiki}

= Gene promoter
{synonym}

A <gene promoter> is a <DNA> region involved in initiating <transcription>. Moving a coding sequence into a nuclear <genome> does not guarantee that its original <gene promoter> can drive nuclear <gene expression>.

= Transcription factor
{parent=Gene expression}
{wiki}

A <transcription factor> is a <protein> that regulates <transcription>. Combinations of <transcription factors> help select <gene expression> patterns during <cell differentiation>. <Anatomy and Physiology 2e by J. Gordon Betts and others>[Anatomy and Physiology 2e, §3.6, Differentiation and Figure 3.35: transcription factors]{ref}

= Transcription
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Gene expression}
{wiki}

= Transcription
{synonym}

<Transcription> is synthesis of <RNA> using one <DNA> strand as a template by an <RNA polymerase>. During elongation the <RNA polymerase> reads that template $3'\to5'$ while extending <RNA> $5'\to3'$. Only the selected region is transcribed; other regions need not be active, and the template strand can differ between <genes>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26887/[Alberts et al.: RNA polymerase and selection of transcribed DNA regions].

For the displayed example, align these three <nucleotide sequences> in the same left-to-right positions:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{coding DNA:}\quad&5'\text{-}\mathrm{ATGGCTTAA}\text{-}3',\\
\text{template DNA:}\quad&3'\text{-}\mathrm{TACCGAATT}\text{-}5',\\
\text{RNA product:}\quad&5'\text{-}\mathrm{AUGGCUUAA}\text{-}3'.
\end{aligned}
$$
The first template triplet TAC selects AUG: T pairs with A, A with U, and C with G. The next CGA selects GCU, and ATT selects UAA. The <coding strand> therefore matches the <RNA> lettering except for T versus U, whereas the template is complementary. Reading that template in its own $5'\to3'$ direction gives $5'\text{-}\mathrm{TTAAGCCAT}\text{-}3'$; orientation cannot be discarded. The <gene expression> diagram keeps all three rows aligned. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-2-prokaryotic-transcription[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: template and coding strands in transcription].

This correspondence makes <nucleotide sequences> interpretable, but replacing T by U is valid only after identifying the <coding strand>. Moreover, <RNA> may be processed after <transcription>, so the initial product and mature <messenger RNA> need not have identical uninterrupted <nucleotide sequences>. <Transcription> also makes functional <RNA> that will not undergo <biological translation>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26887/[Alberts et al.: RNA processing and RNA as the final gene product].

= RNA polymerase
{c}
{parent=Transcription (biology)}
{wiki}

An <RNA polymerase> used in <transcription> is an <enzyme> that synthesizes <RNA> from a <DNA> template. Unlike a replicating <DNA polymerase>, it can start a new strand without a pre-existing paired strand end; initiation still requires the appropriate <DNA> signals and associated machinery. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26887/[Alberts et al.: RNA polymerase and transcription initiation].

= Coding strand
{parent=Transcription (biology)}
{wiki}

For a specified <transcription> unit, the <coding strand> is the <DNA> strand matching the initial <RNA> product in the $5'\to3'$ direction, with T in place of U. The opposite <DNA> strand is the template. The name does not imply that every transcribed <gene> encodes a <protein>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-2-prokaryotic-transcription[Clark, Douglas and Choi: coding and template strands].

= Translation
{disambiguate=biology}
{parent=Gene expression}
{wiki}

= Biological translation
{synonym}

<Biological translation> uses a <ribosome> to read <messenger RNA> <codons> $5'\to3'$ and assemble the corresponding <amino acid> chain. <Transfer RNA> adaptors match <codons> through <base pairing> and carry the <amino acids>; the <genetic code> supplies the correspondence. The chain grows from the <N-terminus> toward the <C-terminus>, by adding each new residue at the <C-terminus>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-5-ribosomes-and-protein-synthesis[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: ribosomes, transfer RNA and protein synthesis].

In the <gene expression> diagram, read $5'\text{-}\mathrm{AUG\ GCU\ UAA}\text{-}3'$ in the chosen <reading frame>. Under the standard <genetic code>, AUG specifies <methionine> and GCU specifies <alanine>; UAA is a <stop codon>. The result before any subsequent processing is the two-residue chain N–<methionine>–<alanine>–C, followed by termination. The <stop codon> contributes zero <amino acids>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: the standard genetic code]. This example assumes appropriate initiation at the displayed AUG in a suitable context in a <eukaryote>; the diagram displays the coding segment, not a complete independently translatable <messenger RNA>.

Initiation establishes the starting position and <reading frame>, using a <ribosome>, an initiating <transfer RNA> and additional factors. At a standard <stop codon>, a release factor promotes release of the chain rather than delivery of another <amino acid> by <transfer RNA>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-5-ribosomes-and-protein-synthesis[Clark, Douglas and Choi: initiation and termination of translation]. A short two-residue example illustrates decoding, not the size or folding of a typical functional <protein>.

The useful connection is between two different alphabets: <nucleotides> specify the order of <amino acids> through the <genetic code>. <Base pairing> helps select <transfer RNA>, while the <ribosome> catalyzes joining the delivered <amino acids>. The <messenger RNA> remains a template rather than being consumed as the chain's building material. Separating template, adaptor and product makes clear why <transcription> and <biological translation> are distinct mechanisms.

= Genetic code
{parent=Translation (biology)}
{wiki}

The <genetic code> assigns <RNA> <codons> to <amino acids> or termination signals. Four possible <nucleobases> at each of three positions give $4^3=64$ <codons>. In the standard <genetic code>, 61 specify <amino acids> and three are <stop codons>; several <codons> can specify the same <amino acid>. Variants exist, so state which <genetic code> is being used. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code[Clark, Douglas and Choi: genetic code assignments and variants].

= Codon
{parent=Genetic code}

A <codon> is a three-<nucleotide> unit read in a specified <reading frame> during <biological translation>. For example, GCU specifies <alanine> in the standard <genetic code>. Its letters are read $5'\to3'$.

= Stop codon
{parent=Codon}
{wiki}

In the standard <genetic code>, UAA, UAG and UGA are <stop codons>: they signal termination of <biological translation> rather than an <amino acid> assignment. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code[Clark, Douglas and Choi: stop codons].

= Reading frame
{parent=Genetic code}
{wiki}

A <reading frame> specifies how consecutive <nucleotides> are grouped into <codons>. Starting at position 1 of AUGGCUUAA gives AUG / GCU / UAA; starting at position 2 gives UGG / CUU with AA left over. The letters are unchanged but the groups differ. A possible grouping alone does not establish an actual <biological translation> initiation site.

= Non-coding DNA
{parent=Molecular genetics}
{wiki}

= Ploidy
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}
{title2=$n,2n,\ldots$}

= Haploid
{parent=Ploidy}
{title2=$n$}

= Diploid
{parent=Ploidy}
{title2=$2n$}

Diploid <biological cells> have two sets of <chromosomes>. In the ordinary one-locus inheritance model, a <diploid> <genotype> therefore contains two <allele> copies at each <genetic locus>.

= Panmixia
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

= Random mating
{synonym}

Panmixia is random mating within a breeding population, without systematic choice by genotype or phenotype. In a homogeneous genetic model it makes the two parental gamete draws independent, yielding the <Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium> genotype probabilities $p_i^2$ and $2p_ip_j$. It is a modelling assumption; <assortative mating> and <population stratification> are distinct ways a population can depart from it.

// Target: causal-inference.bigb

= Mutation
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

A mutation changes inherited genetic sequence. Under an <infinite sites mutation model>, every mutation occurs at a previously unmutated position, so mutations below the sample's common ancestor correspond to <segregating sites>.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Gene deletion
{parent=Mutation}

A <gene deletion> removes a chosen <gene> sequence from a <genome>. An experimental <gene deletion> tests the consequences of that removal in a specified <genetic background> and environment; lack of an observed <phenotype> does not prove that the <gene> has no function under other conditions.

= Mutation rate
{parent=Mutation}
{title2=$u$}
{wiki}

A mutation rate measures the frequency of <mutation> events per specified unit, such as per chromosome segment per generation. Its units and the population-size convention must be specified. If time is measured in $N$ generations and $\theta=2Nu$, each ancestral lineage has mutation intensity $Nu=\theta/2$ per unit of coalescent time.

= Chromosome
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

A chromosome carries <genes> and other genetic material. A <haplotype> records the <alleles> carried on one chromosome at specified <genetic loci>.

= Homologous chromosome
{parent=Chromosome}
{wiki}

Homologous chromosomes carry corresponding <genetic loci>, although their <alleles> may differ. In the <diploid> $AB/ab$ example, the two homologous chromosomes carry the parental <haplotypes> $AB$ and $ab$.

= Chromatid
{parent=Chromosome}
{wiki}

A chromatid is one of the two copies of a replicated <chromosome>. Each <homologous chromosome> contributes two sister chromatids before separation in <meiosis>; <chromosomal crossovers> can exchange segments between nonsister chromatids.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Sister chromatids
{parent=Chromatid}

<Sister chromatids> are the paired <chromatids> originating from <DNA replication> of the same <chromosome>.

= Gene
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

A <gene> is an inherited functional unit of genetic material. In cellular <organisms>, a <gene> is a region of <DNA> whose information produces a functional <RNA> or contributes to a <protein>. This connects inheritance to cellular chemistry: a change in a <gene> can alter the amount or activity of a <protein>, and thereby a <phenotype>. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Gene[NHGRI: gene].

Alternative inherited versions at a <genetic locus> are <alleles>. The distinction matters: two parents can carry the same <gene> but different <alleles>, and a <genetic locus> can have variation outside a <gene>. Neither the word <gene> nor a change in <DNA> alone specifies the resulting <phenotype>.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Genetic-study ascertainment
{parent=Genetics}

= Ascertainment in a genetic study
{synonym}

<Ascertainment in a genetic study> specifies how individuals or families enter a genetic study. If $A$ is the event of inclusion, then $P(X\mid A)=P(X)P(A\mid X)/P(A)$. Selection on disease <phenotype> can change an unconditional <pedigree likelihood>. When comparing <genetic marker> <probabilities> conditional on a specified disease pattern, selection depending only on that pattern cancels from the linkage <likelihood ratio>.

= Meiosis
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

= Meioses
{synonym}

<Meiosis> reduces two sets of <chromosomes> to one. In the ordinary <diploid> life cycle, this makes it possible for <fertilization> of two <haploid> <gametes> to restore the <diploid> state rather than double the number of sets in every generation. Its two divisions perform different tasks: <meiosis I> separates <homologous chromosomes>, whereas <meiosis II> separates <sister chromatids>. One round of <DNA replication> precedes both divisions; there is no second round between them. See <Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition>[Meiosis, “Duplicated Homologous Chromosomes Pair During Meiosis” and “Gametes Are Produced by Two Meiotic Cell Divisions”, Figures 20-6–20-7]{ref}.

Two <homologous chromosomes> carry corresponding <genetic loci>, potentially with different <alleles>; two <sister chromatids> originate by <DNA replication> of one <chromosome>. After <chromosomal crossover> between nonsister <chromatids>, the <sister chromatids> need not carry identical <alleles> everywhere. Separating the two relationships explains why a <haploid> product of <meiosis I> still contains replicated <chromosomes>.

\Image[meiosis-chromosome-segregation.png]
{title=One <DNA replication> followed by <homologous chromosome> separation and separation of <sister chromatids>}
{description=Original simplified model with two <homologous chromosome> pairs, no <chromosomal crossover>, and symmetric divisions. The four final compartments represent <haploid> products, not four functional <ova>. Human <oogenesis> uses unequal divisions, retaining most <cytoplasm> in one developing <ovum> and producing <polar bodies>.}

The two divisions explain <Mendelian segregation>; variable orientation of different <homologous chromosome> pairs connects it to <independent assortment>. <Genetic recombination> adds another source of <allele> combinations. These mechanisms reshuffle inherited material without requiring a new <mutation>.

The symmetric diagram is useful for counting, but human <oogenesis> produces one functional <ovum> rather than four. The <polar bodies> receive discarded <chromosomes> and little <cytoplasm>. See <Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/Human Reproductive Anatomy and Gametogenesis> (§43.3, Oogenesis and Figure 43.13: unequal divisions and polar bodies).

= Meiosis I
{parent=Meiosis}

The first division of <meiosis> separates replicated <homologous chromosomes>; their <sister chromatids> remain associated.

= Meiosis II
{parent=Meiosis}

The second division of <meiosis> separates <sister chromatids>, without an intervening round of <DNA replication>.

= Meiotic DNA content
{parent=Meiosis}
{title2=$(2n,2C)\to(2n,4C)\to(n,2C)\to(n,C)$}

<Ploidy> counts sets of <chromosomes>; <DNA> amount counts material. Let $n$ be the number of <chromosomes> in one complete set and $C$ the <DNA> amount of one unreplicated set. For the ordinary <diploid> model, read each row per completed <cell nucleus>, not as a total across all products:

\Table
[
\Tr[\Th[Stage]\Th[<Ploidy>]\Th[<DNA> amount]\Th[<Chromosome> organization]]
\Tr[\Td[Before <DNA replication>]\Td[$2n$]\Td[$2C$]\Td[Two unreplicated sets]]
\Tr[\Td[After <DNA replication>]\Td[$2n$]\Td[$4C$]\Td[Two sets; each <chromosome> has two <sister chromatids>]]
\Tr[\Td[After <meiosis I>]\Td[$n$]\Td[$2C$]\Td[One replicated set]]
\Tr[\Td[After <meiosis II>]\Td[$n$]\Td[$C$]\Td[One unreplicated set]]
]

In the illustrated $n=2$ example, the starting <biological cell> has four <chromosomes>. <DNA replication> gives eight <chromatids>, organized into four replicated <chromosomes>. Each product of <meiosis I> has two replicated <chromosomes>, hence four <chromatids>; each final product has two unreplicated <chromosomes>. Across completed products, $2(2C)=4C$ after <meiosis I> and $4(C)=4C$ after <meiosis II>: division redistributes the replicated <DNA>. A <haploid> <cell nucleus> can therefore have $2C$ without becoming <diploid>.

= Mendelian segregation
{parent=Genetics}
{c}
{title2=$Aa\times Aa:\quad\tfrac14AA,\ \tfrac12Aa,\ \tfrac14aa$}

In ordinary <diploid> <meiosis>, the two copies at a <genetic locus> separate: each <gamete> receives one copy. With fair transmission, a <heterozygote> $Aa$ produces <gametes> carrying $A$ or $a$ with <probability> $1/2$ each. This is a transmission mechanism conditional on a parental <genotype>, rather than a statement about how common either <allele> is in a population.

For the one-locus cross $Aa\times Aa$, assume independent parental transmissions, no new <mutation>, and no differences in <fertilization> or survival between the resulting <genotypes>. Multiplying the two <gamete> <probabilities> gives four equally likely ordered outcomes: $AA,Aa,aA,aa$, each with <probability> $1/4$. Combining the two orders of the <heterozygote> gives
$$
P(AA)=0.25,\qquad P(Aa)=0.50,\qquad P(aa)=0.25.
$$
Only under the additional ideal model of <complete dominance> of $A$, complete <penetrance>, and no environmental changes to the scored characteristic do $AA$ and $Aa$ share the dominant <phenotype>. Its <probability> is then $0.25+0.50=0.75$. The inheritance calculation alone does not determine a <phenotype> or predict a generic <disease> risk.

\Image[mendelian-segregation.png]
{title=Fair Mendelian transmission in an Aa × Aa cross, with phenotype grouping under complete dominance and penetrance}

These are <probabilities>, not a requirement that every four offspring contain one $AA$, two $Aa$ and one $aa$. For 100 independent offspring under the ideal model, the <expected values> of the three counts are $25,50,25$; the $aa$ count has a <binomial distribution> with <standard deviation> $\sqrt{100(0.25)(0.75)}\approx4.33$. Each new transmission keeps the same <conditional probabilities> given the parental <genotypes>.

In his paper published in 1866, Mendel reported 5,474 round and 1,850 wrinkled pea seeds: $5474/7324\approx0.7474$ round, close to the ideal $0.75$. Following descendants also distinguished two inherited classes within the round <phenotype>, illustrating why <genotype> cannot always be read from appearance.<Experiments in Plant Hybridization by Gregor Mendel>[sections 5–6]{ref} At several <genetic loci>, transmission patterns additionally depend on <genetic linkage> and <genetic recombination>.

= Independent assortment
{parent=Mendelian segregation}
{title2=$\theta=\tfrac12$}

<Independent assortment> means that transmission at different <genetic loci> has <conditional independence> given the parental <genotype>. With fair <Mendelian segregation>, a parent <heterozygous> at two <genetic loci>, $AaBb$, produces $AB,Ab,aB,ab$ <gametes> with <probability> $1/4$ each. For example,
$$
P(A,B\mid AaBb)=P(A\mid AaBb)P(B\mid AaBb)=\frac12\frac12=\frac14.
$$
Here $AB$ labels an <allele> combination; it does not assert that the two <genetic loci> lie on the same <chromosome>.

The physical model is stochastic orientation of different <homologous chromosome> pairs toward the two poles of the <spindle apparatus> in <meiosis I>. One pair's orientation does not determine another's. For two pairs, one division can send $A,B$ together and $a,b$ together; another can send $A,b$ together and $a,B$ together. See <Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/The Process of Meiosis> (§11.1, Metaphase I and Figure 11.5: random orientation of chromosome pairs).

\Image[independent-assortment-chromosome-orientation.png]
{title=Two relative <homologous chromosome> orientations generate four <allele> combinations across <meioses>}
{description=Original $AaBb$ schematic for two separate <chromosome> pairs, fair independent orientations, no <chromosomal crossover>, and symmetric divisions. Fixing $A$ at the left pole removes the duplicate obtained by reversing both poles. Each displayed orientation has <probability> $1/2$. A single division gives two copies each of two complementary combinations; all four equally likely combinations appear across divisions.}

With $k$ distinguishable <homologous chromosome> pairs, there are $2^k$ choices of parental <homologous chromosome> origin for a product at a specified pole, each with <probability> $2^{-k}$ under this model. For $k=3$, enumerate $MMM,MMP,MPM,MPP,PMM,PMP,PPM,PPP$, where M and P mark parental origins. Whole-pole reversal pairs complementary choices, leaving $2^{k-1}$ unordered partitions for $k\geq1$. For $k=2$ there are four choices for a specified pole but only the two relative orientations drawn. This counts possible products across <meioses>, not eight different products of one division when $k=3$. Identical <alleles> at scored <genetic loci> can also make different parental-origin choices observationally indistinguishable.

<Independent assortment> of different <chromosome> pairs does not imply independence of every pair of <genes>. Nearby <genetic loci> on the same <chromosome> can show <genetic linkage>. <Chromosomal crossover> can create new <haplotypes>, but does not automatically erase <genetic linkage>. See <Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others/Laws of Inheritance> (§12.3, Law of Independent Assortment and Linked Genes Violate the Law of Independent Assortment). In the ordinary fair two-locus model with parental <haplotypes> $AB/ab$ and <recombination fraction> $r$,
$$
P(AB)=P(ab)=\frac{1-r}{2},\qquad P(Ab)=P(aB)=\frac r2.
$$
Thus $r=0$ gives only the two parental <haplotypes>, while $r=1/2$ gives four equal <probabilities>. A <recombination fraction> of $1/2$ alone does not establish that the <genetic loci> lie on different <chromosomes>.

A motivating experiment appears in <Experiments in Plant Hybridization by Gregor Mendel> (§9, “The Reproductive Cells of the Hybrids”). In <peas>, hybrids for round versus wrinkled <seeds> and yellow versus green <seeds> were crossed reciprocally with constant wrinkled-green <plants>. The readout was visible <seed> shape and color: hybrid seed parents gave 31 round-yellow, 26 round-green, 27 wrinkled-yellow and 26 wrinkled-green; the reciprocal cross gave 24, 25, 22 and 27.

The apparatus was controlled <pollination>: opening immature <flowers>, removing <stamens> with forceps, and applying selected <pollen> to the <stigma (botany)>. Two-year trials established constant parental characters; greenhouse pots checked interference from insects in outdoor beds. Reciprocal crosses tested both transmission directions. These procedures are described in <Experiments in Plant Hybridization by Gregor Mendel> (§§2–3).

With <complete dominance> at both <genetic loci>, this <test cross> makes each <phenotype> report one hybrid <gamete> combination. The ideal <expected values> are $110/4=27.5$ and $98/4=24.5$ per class. The observations support four transmitted combinations, approximately equally frequent. They do not directly image <chromosome> orientation or prove independence for all <genes>; failed development and <seed> germination also limit the inference, as §9 notes.

= Test cross
{parent=Mendelian segregation}

A <test cross> crosses an individual with a <homozygote> for recessive <alleles>, allowing suitable offspring <phenotypes> to reveal transmitted <gamete> combinations.

= Genetic marker
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

A <genetic marker> is an observed inherited genomic state used to track chromosomes, test <genetic association>, or perform <genetic linkage> analysis. <Genetic marker> <alleles> need not themselves cause the <phenotype>. Several <genetic markers> on one nonrecombining region form a single observed <haplotype>.

= Identity by descent
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

= IBD
{synonym}
{c}

= Identical by descent
{synonym}

Allele copies are identical by descent if they derive from the same specified ancestral copy. Identical observed states need not imply this ancestry. Two diploid individuals are 2-IBD when both of their allele copies can be paired as identical by descent; autozygous individuals carrying two copies of the same ancestral copy are mutually 2-IBD when that ancestor is shared.

= Sibling IBD ascertainment under multiplicative penetrance
{parent=Identity by descent}
{title2=$(z_0,z_1,z_2)=\dfrac{(\alpha^4,2\alpha^2\beta,\beta^2)}{(\alpha^2+\beta)^2}$}

For <full siblings> in an <outbred pedigree>, condition on both being affected under <multiplicative diallelic penetrance> and on residual disease outcomes being independent given their <genotypes>. The usual <Mendelian IBD sharing of full siblings> probabilities $(1/4,1/2,1/4)$ are weighted by $p^2\alpha^4,p^2\alpha^2\beta,p^2\beta^2$, respectively, giving the displayed conditional sharing probabilities. Since $\beta-\alpha^2=\phi(1-\phi)(\theta-1)^2\geq0$, $z_1\leq1/2$, strictly for a polymorphic locus with a nontrivial effect.

= Jacquard identity coefficients
{parent=Identity by descent}
{c}
{title2=$\Delta_1,\ldots,\Delta_9$}

For two individuals carrying labelled copies $(a,b)$ and $(c,d)$, condensed identity coefficients give the <probabilities> of nine <identity by descent> patterns, identifying states that differ only by exchanging the two homologues within either individual. They retain within-individual <autozygosity>, which three ordinary outbred sharing coefficients omit. One convention is
$$
\begin{array}{c|l}
1&abcd\\2&ab\mid cd\\3&abc\mid d\text{ or }abd\mid c\\4&ab\mid c\mid d\\5&acd\mid b\text{ or }bcd\mid a\\6&cd\mid a\mid b\\7&ac\mid bd\text{ or }ad\mid bc\\8&\text{one cross-individual pair, two singletons}\\9&a\mid b\mid c\mid d
\end{array}
$$
Each block consists of copies identical by descent, and different blocks have distinct ancestral origins. For siblings whose parents are otherwise outbred <first cousins>, these coefficients are $(1,0,2,1,2,1,15,30,12)/64$. This follows by conditioning on whether the parents share one ancestral copy and enumerating the four independent <Mendelian segregation> choices to their two children. The <kinship coefficient> is $\Delta_1+(\Delta_3+\Delta_5+\Delta_7)/2+\Delta_8/4$, here $9/32$. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2585748/[Weir's discussion of identity states] uses this convention.

= Identity-by-descent sharing of inbred full siblings
{parent=Jacquard identity coefficients}
{title2=$(P(J=0),P(J=1),P(J=2))=(7/32,17/32,1/4)$}

Let $J$ be the largest number of chromosome copies that can be paired across the two <full siblings> as <identical by descent>, counting repeated copies when a child is <autozygous>. For offspring of otherwise outbred <first cousins>, with probability $3/4$ the parental copies have no common ancestral origin, giving ordinary sibling sharing $(1/4,1/2,1/4)$. With probability $1/4$, write the parental copies as $(A,B)$ and $(A,C)$ with three distinct origins. Each child independently has one of $AA,AB,AC,BC$, equally likely. Among sixteen ordered child pairs, two share no copy, ten share one, and four share two. The mixture gives the displayed probabilities. These three numbers do not determine all <Jacquard identity coefficients>; the formula $\phi=\mathbb E[J]/4$ is restricted to noninbred individuals and fails here.

= First-cousin identity-by-descent probabilities
{parent=Identity by descent}
{title2=$(k_0,k_1,k_2)=(3/4,1/4,0)$}

For <first cousins> in an otherwise <outbred pedigree>, compare the copies transmitted from their related parents, who are <full siblings>. These transmissions trace to the same shared grandparent with <probability> $1/2$ and then to the same homologous copy of that grandparent with probability $1/2$, giving $k_1=1/4$. The unrelated parental contributions cannot match, so two shared copies have probability zero. Selecting one random copy from each cousin chooses those two potentially shared copies with probability $1/4$, giving <kinship coefficient> $k_1/4=1/16$.

= Identical-by-descent segment
{parent=Identity by descent}

= IBD segment
{synonym}
{c}

An identical-by-descent segment is a chromosomal tract inherited from the same ancestral chromosome without an intervening breakpoint in the tracked ancestry. With a specified total number $m$ of independent <meioses> and a uniform recombination map, the probability that a fixed tract of genetic length $d$ survives without <genetic recombination> is approximately $e^{-md}$. Recent common ancestry therefore tends to give longer shared tracts. Lengths of tracts selected by covering a random point are length-biased relative to tracts sampled by their boundaries.

= Kinship coefficient
{parent=Identity by descent}
{title2=$\phi_{XY}$}

The kinship coefficient $\phi_{XY}$ is the <probability> that one randomly selected <allele> copy from each of $X,Y$ is <identical by descent>. For a noninbred individual, $\phi_{XX}=1/2$, and more generally $\phi_{XX}=(1+F_X)/2$. For distinct individuals with parents $M_X,P_X$, <Mendelian segregation> gives $\phi_{XY}=[\phi_{M_XY}+\phi_{P_XY}]/2$. The offspring's <inbreeding coefficient> equals the parents' kinship coefficient.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Finite-population chromosomal coancestry
{parent=Kinship coefficient}
{title2=$\Phi_{G+1}=\Phi_G+\dfrac{1+F_G-2\Phi_{m,G}}{4(N-1)}$}

For $N$ individuals in monogamous families of exactly two offspring, let $\Phi_G$ be <mean> pairwise <kinship coefficient>, $F_G$ <mean> inbreeding and $\Phi_{m,G}$ <mean> kinship of mates. A random distinct offspring pair are siblings with <probability> $1/(N-1)$. Accounting separately for within-family and cross-family pairs gives the displayed recurrence, while $F_{G+1}=\Phi_{m,G}$. Random mating makes $\Phi_{m,G}=\Phi_G$. Sibling avoidance changes the mating-pair <mean>; it does not create unrelated founders or stop eventual drift to ancestral identity in a closed finite population.

= Sex-linked cousin kinship
{parent=Kinship coefficient}
{title2=$F_X\in\{0,1/8,3/16\}$}

For otherwise outbred opposite-sex <first cousins>, the <probability> that the mother's randomly transmitted X copy matches the father's X by descent depends on which of their parents are siblings. It is zero when the male cousin is related through his father, $1/8$ when the female cousin is related through her father and the male through his mother, and $3/16$ when their mothers are sisters. Daughters can be X-autozygous; sons have one X and a Y and are not homozygous for a paired X chromosome. Recombination and pseudoautosomal regions require a locus-based formulation instead of whole-chromosome blocks.

= Mendelian IBD sharing of full siblings
{parent=Identity by descent}
{c}
{title2=$J\sim\operatorname{Bin}(2,1/2)$}

In an <outbred pedigree>, the two <full siblings> receive the same paternal copy with probability $1/2$, and independently the same maternal copy with probability $1/2$. Thus the number $J$ of shared copies has a <binomial distribution> with probabilities $(1/4,1/2,1/4)$, and $E[J/2]=1/2$.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Autozygosity
{parent=Identity by descent}

= Autozygous
{synonym}

<Autozygosity> is <homozygosity> by descent: the maternal and paternal copies in one individual derive from the same ancestral copy. Unlike ordinary <homozygosity>, it concerns the ancestry of chromosome copies rather than only their observed states. In a <pedigree> with one ancestral disease-allele copy and fully penetrant <recessive inheritance>, every affected individual is <autozygous> for that copy.

= Identity by state
{parent=Identity by descent}

= IBS
{synonym}
{c}

Two allele copies are identical by state when their observed states agree, regardless of whether they derive from one recent ancestral copy. Equal marker <haplotypes> can therefore reflect either <identity by descent> or matching founder haplotypes.

= Genetic association
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

A genetic association is a statistical dependence between a <genetic variant> and a <phenotype>. An associated marker need not be causal: <linkage disequilibrium> can correlate it with a causal locus, and <population stratification> can produce an association without such a link.

= Genotype relative risk
{parent=Genetic association}
{title2=$R_g=P(D\mid g)/P(D\mid g_0)$}

= Genotypic relative risk
{synonym}

The genotype relative risk compares the <penetrance> for <genotype> $g$ with that of a chosen baseline $g_0$. The common baseline factor cancels when computing <conditional probabilities> of transmitted genotypes given an affected offspring. It is a ratio of disease probabilities, unlike an <odds ratio>.

= Disease-ascertained transmission probability
{parent=Genotype relative risk}
{title2=$P(g\mid g_f,g_m,D)=\frac{q_gR_g}{\sum_hq_hR_h}$}

Let $q_g=P(g\mid g_f,g_m)$ be the <Mendelian segregation> probability, and assume the child's disease status depends on the parental <genotypes> only through its own genotype, with <penetrance> proportional to $R_g$. <Bayes theorem> gives the displayed conditional law. For a diallelic parent pair there are four equally likely labelled transmission outcomes. If an unordered genotype occurs in $m_g$ of them, $q_g=m_g/4$, so its probability is $m_gR_g/\sum_hm_hR_h$. Dropping multiplicities would, for example, misweight a heterozygous child of two heterozygotes.

= Haplotype-relative-risk
{parent=Genetic association}
{wiki}
{title2=$\widehat{\mathrm{OR}}=\frac{wz}{xy}$}

The haplotype-relative-risk design compares transmitted <alleles> to the corresponding untransmitted parental copies, used as internal controls for an affected offspring. If allele-1/allele-2 transmitted counts are $w,x$ and untransmitted counts are $y,z$, the allele-1 <odds ratio> estimate is $wz/(xy)$. An unmatched <Pearson chi-squared test of independence> treats this as a two-by-two table. The <transmission disequilibrium test> instead keeps parent-specific pairing and uses only discordant transmissions. The sampling assumptions of the unmatched calculation and any missing-parent reconstruction must be justified separately.

= Association mapping
{parent=Genetic association}
{wiki}

Association mapping localizes genetic influences on a <phenotype> by testing population <genetic association> with markers. <Linkage disequilibrium> allows a typed marker to tag a nearby causal variant; historical <genetic recombination> can narrow the associated interval. <Population stratification> can produce association without a local causal relation.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Linkage-disequilibrium marker tagging
{parent=Association mapping}

An observed <genetic marker> tags an unobserved causal <genetic variant> when <linkage disequilibrium> correlates their allele indicators. Their squared <allelic correlation> $r^2$ measures prediction strength. Under standard local-alternative score-test approximations, using the marker rather than the causal allele reduces the noncentrality parameter by roughly $r^2$, suggesting a sample-size multiplier about $1/r^2$. This is an approximation requiring the same disease model and sampling design, not a universal power identity.

= Transmission disequilibrium test
{parent=Genetic association}
{wiki}
{title2=$\chi^2_{\mathrm{TDT}}=\frac{(b-c)^2}{b+c}$}

= TDT
{synonym}
{c}

For affected offspring and heterozygous parents at a diallelic locus, let $b$ count transmissions of allele 1 with allele 2 untransmitted and $c$ count the reverse. Under the fair-transmission null, conditional on $b+c$, $b$ is binomial with probability $1/2$. The asymptotic statistic is $(b-c)^2/(b+c)$, with a one-degree-of-freedom chi-squared null distribution. Homozygous-parent transmissions give no contrast. Conditioning within families protects this test from ordinary <population stratification>.

= Incomplete-trio transmission-selection bias
{parent=Transmission disequilibrium test}

If one parent is <heterozygous> and the other parent's <genotype> is missing, a homozygous child reveals the observed parent's transmitted <allele>, whereas a heterozygous child need not. Selecting only revealed transmissions can therefore bias the <transmission disequilibrium test>. For allele-1 frequency $p$ under random mating and fair transmission, the probabilities of an allele-1 or allele-2 homozygous child from an observed heterozygous parent are $p/2$ and $(1-p)/2$. Among selected homozygous children, the observed allele-1 transmission probability is $p$, not generally $1/2$. A complete-trio analysis avoids this reconstruction rule; methods retaining missing-parent families need an explicit <likelihood function> or a valid conditional adjustment. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3297829/[Family-based association tests using genotype data with uncertainty] discusses this bias.

= Family pseudo-control genotype
{parent=Transmission disequilibrium test}

Combine the untransmitted maternal and paternal alleles into a hypothetical control <genotype>, paired with the child's transmitted genotype. Even when two heterozygous parents have a heterozygous child and parent-specific phase is ambiguous, the combined untransmitted genotype is unambiguously heterozygous. Such pseudo-controls are matched family controls, not independent random individuals; <Hardy-Weinberg principle> is therefore not guaranteed merely by this construction.

= Transmitted and untransmitted alleles under multiplicative penetrance
{parent=Family pseudo-control genotype}

Under random mating and <Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium>, label each parental allele by whether it is transmitted. Let $(A,B)$ and $(C,D)$ be the transmitted/untransmitted pairs of two parents. Before ascertainment all four draws are independent with population frequencies $\pi$. If offspring disease weights the transmitted pair by $\psi_A\psi_C$, then
$$
\mathbb P(A=a,B=b,C=c,D=d\mid\text{offspring disease})=\pi_a^*\pi_c^*\pi_b\pi_d.
$$
The transmitted pair is an affected-case genotype and the untransmitted pair a population <family pseudo-control genotype> under this homogeneous model. For unordered genotypes, compatible labelled transmissions must be summed. Conditioning on parental genotypes instead removes nuisance population frequencies and gives the usual <transmission disequilibrium test>.

// Target: statistical-modelling.bigb

= Genetic linkage
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

Why can two individually fair <allele> transmissions fail to be independent? <Genetic loci> on the same <chromosome> can travel together through <meiosis>. This tendency is <genetic linkage>; in an ordinary two-locus model, detectable <genetic linkage> means a <recombination fraction> below $1/2$. A <genetic marker> can therefore help track a neighboring <genetic locus> through a <pedigree>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/13-1-chromosomal-theory-and-genetic-linkage[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: chromosomal theory and genetic linkage].

Take a <diploid> individual <heterozygous> at two <genetic loci>, with <phase-known genotype> $AB/ab$: one <homologous chromosome> carries <alleles> $A,B$, the other $a,b$. The parental <haplotypes> are $AB,ab$; the recombinant <haplotypes> are $Ab,aB$. Assume fair <Mendelian segregation>, equal <probabilities> within each reciprocal pair of <gametes>, and no <allele>-dependent differences in <gamete> recovery. If the <recombination fraction> is $r=0.2$, the <gamete> <probabilities> are $0.4,0.4,0.1,0.1$ in that order. Each <genetic locus> still transmits its two <alleles> equally, but $P(AB)=0.4$ exceeds $P(A)P(B)=0.25$: fairness at each <genetic locus> does not imply <independence> between <genetic loci>.

\Image[linked-locus-gamete-probabilities.png]
{title=<Gamete> <probabilities> from the <phase-known genotype> AB/ab: parental versus recombinant <haplotypes> at three <recombination fractions>}

At $r=0$, only the parental <haplotypes> occur, each with <probability> $0.5$: this is <complete genetic linkage>. At $r=0.5$, all four <haplotypes> have <probability> $0.25$, giving <independent assortment>. A <recombination fraction> near $0.5$ alone cannot establish whether the <genetic loci> lie on different <chromosomes> or far apart on one <chromosome>.

<Genetic linkage> concerns joint transmission through <meiosis>. <Linkage disequilibrium> concerns <haplotype> frequencies in a population, while <genetic association> compares <allele> or <genotype> frequencies with a <phenotype>. These questions require different observations; <pedigrees> directly reveal co-transmission.

= Genetic map
{parent=Genetic linkage}

A genetic map turns observations of inherited <haplotypes> into an ordered account of <genetic loci> along a <chromosome>. Its distances describe <genetic recombination>, which makes the map useful for locating a <genetic locus> relative to known <genetic markers>. A <genetic map> measures inheritance separation; separation measured in <base pairs> describes positions along <DNA>. <Recombination hotspots> help explain why equal numbers of <base pairs> need not give equal <genetic map> distances. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21116/[T. A. Brown, Genomes, 2nd edition (2002), chapter 5: Mapping Genomes].

Write $d$ for the <expected value> of the number of <chromosomal crossovers> encountered along one transmitted <chromatid> between the chosen <genetic loci>, measured in <morgans>; $100d$ is the distance in <centimorgans>. This counts exchanges along that transmitted <chromatid>, rather than all exchanges in the four-<chromatid> meiotic bundle. For a short interval with very few multiple exchanges, the <recombination fraction> satisfies $r\approx d$: about $1\%$ recombinant <gametes> then corresponds to about one <centimorgan>.

For a longer interval, hidden even numbers of <chromosomal crossovers> make that approximation inaccurate. Under the <Haldane mapping function>, $d=0.2$ <morgans> gives $r\approx0.164840$; conversely $r=0.2$ gives $d\approx0.255413$ <morgans>, or $25.5413$ <centimorgans>. Applying the inverse formula to an already specified <genetic map> distance would confuse a count with a <probability>.

<Genetic map> distances add along ordered adjacent intervals because the total exchange count is the sum of its interval counts. For <independent random variables> describing counts in disjoint intervals of the <Haldane mapping function> model, the endpoint <recombination fraction> instead obeys
$$
r_{12}=r_1(1-r_2)+(1-r_1)r_2=r_1+r_2-2r_1r_2.
$$
Exactly one interval must change parental origin. Thus $r_1=r_2=0.2$ gives $r_{12}=0.32$, although the two <genetic map> distances add to approximately $0.510826$ <morgans>. The simple composition formula requires the stated <independence>; <crossover interference> can invalidate it.

= Haldane mapping function
{parent=Genetic map}
{c}
{title2=$r(d)=\tfrac12(1-e^{-2d})$}

= Haldane's mapping function
{synonym}
{c}

The Haldane mapping function connects additive <genetic map> distance to endpoint <recombination fraction> in a toy model with no <crossover interference>. Specify the model directly along a transmitted <chromatid>: the exchange count $N$ in the interval has a <Poisson distribution> with <expected value> $d$, where $d$ is the distance in <morgans>. Counts in disjoint intervals are <independent random variables>. This is the transmitted-<chromatid> convention; $d$ is not the mean number of exchanges across all four <chromatids> in <meiosis>.

An odd exchange count changes parental phase. Summing the odd terms of the <Poisson distribution> gives
$$
r=P(N\text{ is odd})=e^{-d}\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{d^{2k+1}}{(2k+1)!}=\frac{1-e^{-2d}}{2}.
$$
For $0\le r<1/2$, the inverse and short-interval approximation are
$$
d=-\frac12\log(1-2r),\qquad r=d-d^2+O(d^3).
$$
Here $\log$ is the <natural logarithm>. At $d=0.2$, the <recombination fraction> is $0.16483997698$; at $r=0.2$, the distance is $0.2554128119$ <morgans>, or about $25.5413$ <centimorgans>. The linear approximation $r\approx d$ is useful only near zero. The <recombination fraction> approaches $1/2$ as $d$ grows; $r=1/2$ has no finite inverse in this model.

\Image[recombination-fraction-map-distance.png]
{title=<Haldane mapping function> versus the short-interval approximation: <genetic map> distance keeps growing while the <recombination fraction> approaches one half}

The model is a useful baseline, not a universal rule for every interval during <meiosis>. <Crossover interference> can change the relationship between distance and <recombination fraction>. For the count convention and assumptions, see https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2704907/[Weeks, Tang and Kwon (2009): genetic map functions and exchange-count conventions]. Haldane's original paper is bibliographically recorded as \i[The combination of linkage values, and the calculation of distances between the loci of linked factors], \i[Journal of Genetics] 8 (1919), 299–309, in https://jbshaldane.org/books/1990-Selected-Genetics-Papers/index.html[Selected Genetics Papers of J. B. S. Haldane, edited by K. Dronamraju (1990), contents].

= Morgan
{disambiguate=unit}
{parent=Genetic map}
{title2=$1\,\mathrm{M}=100\,\mathrm{cM}$}

= Morgan
{synonym}

A morgan is a unit of <genetic map> distance: an <expected value> of one exchange along the transmitted <chromatid> in the interval. One morgan equals 100 <centimorgans>; it does not mean a <recombination fraction> of one.

= Centimorgan
{parent=Morgan (unit)}
{wiki}
{title2=$1\,\mathrm{cM}=0.01\,\mathrm{M}$}

A centimorgan is one hundredth of a <morgan>. For a sufficiently short <genetic map> interval, one centimorgan corresponds approximately to a $1\%$ <recombination fraction>, rather than an exact identity for all intervals.

= Nonparametric linkage analysis
{parent=Genetic linkage}

Nonparametric linkage analysis tests excess <identity by descent> among relatives with a shared <phenotype>, without specifying its complete <penetrance> model or disease-allele frequency. Its null still uses <Mendelian segregation> and the specified <pedigree>.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Mean allele-sharing test for affected siblings
{parent=Nonparametric linkage analysis}
{title2=$Z=(\bar J-1)/\sqrt{1/(2n)}$}

For independent pairs of affected <full siblings> with unrelated, noninbred parents and a fully informative <genetic marker>, the no-linkage null gives shared <identity by descent> count $J\sim\operatorname{Bin}(2,1/2)$. Therefore $EJ=1$ and $\operatorname{Var}J=1/2$. An excess-sharing test uses the displayed approximately <standard normal> statistic and an upper-tail critical value. Pairs sharing family members are dependent, so their variance needs separate treatment. This targeted test differs from an omnibus test of all three sharing probabilities.

= Affected-sib-pair analysis
{parent=Nonparametric linkage analysis}

An affected-sib-pair analysis examines marker <identity by descent> between affected <full siblings>. Without <genetic linkage> to the disease locus, the probabilities of sharing zero, one and two copies are $1/4,1/2,1/4$. Excess sharing supplies evidence for linkage under disease models that preferentially transmit a common risk allele to both siblings.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Recessive affected-sib-pair likelihood
{parent=Affected-sib-pair analysis}
{title2=$L_0=u^2,\ L_1=uv,\ L_2=v^2$}

For unaffected <genetic carriers> of a disease with complete <penetrance> and <recessive inheritance>, suppose parental marker-disease <haplotype> phases are equally likely and the marker is fully informative. Write $u=\theta(1-\theta)$ and $v=1/2-u$, where $\theta$ is the <recombination fraction>. Conditional on sharing a parental marker copy, the probability that both siblings inherit that parent's disease allele is $[(1-\theta)^2+\theta^2]/2=v$; conditional on inheriting different marker copies it is $\theta(1-\theta)=u$. Independent paternal and maternal transmissions therefore give $L_0=u^2,L_1=uv,L_2=v^2$. Conditional instead on both siblings being affected, the IBD probabilities are $s^2,2s(1-s),(1-s)^2$, where $s=2u\in[0,1/2]$.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Allele-sharing sufficiency for recessive linkage
{parent=Recessive affected-sib-pair likelihood}

For independent affected sib pairs with IBD counts $n_0,n_1,n_2$, the <likelihood function> of the <recombination fraction> is proportional to $s^{2n_0+n_1}(1-s)^{2n_2+n_1}$, with $s=2\theta(1-\theta)$. At fixed sample size, the total shared-copy count $2n_2+n_1$ is a <sufficient statistic> by the <Fisher-Neyman factorization theorem>. Thus a one-sided <likelihood-ratio test> has the same ordering as a test for excess mean IBD sharing. This equivalence depends on the stated fully penetrant recessive model and does not extend to arbitrary disease models.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Parametric linkage analysis
{parent=Genetic linkage}

Parametric linkage analysis forms a <pedigree likelihood> from a specified disease model, including <penetrance>, <allele> frequencies and <recessive inheritance> or another transmission model. It estimates or tests the <recombination fraction> between a disease locus and a <genetic marker>.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Complete genetic linkage
{parent=Genetic linkage}
{title2=$\theta=0$}

= Complete linkage
{synonym}

<Complete linkage> in a two-locus model means <recombination fraction> zero: only parental <haplotype> combinations are transmitted. Multiple <genetic markers> in a nonrecombining block represent one <haplotype> segregation observation.

= Homozygosity mapping
{parent=Genetic linkage}

Homozygosity mapping searches for shared homozygous marker regions in individuals with a rare recessive condition, often in an <inbred> pedigree. Under a single ancestral disease copy, affected individuals inherit two copies identical by descent. Marker homozygosity alone gives <identity by state>, so founder haplotype frequencies matter. If a particular haplotype has founder frequency $p$ and $K$ distinct founder chromosome copies underlie all selected descendant copies, their marker-pattern probability is $E[p^K]$. For complete linkage and a single disease copy, the affected-only numerator is $p$, yielding a <LOD score> $\log_{10}(p/E[p^K])$ under linkage-equilibrium founder assumptions.

= LOD score
{parent=Genetic linkage}
{c}
{title2=$Z(\theta)=\log_{10}[L(\theta)/L(1/2)]$}

The LOD score compares the likelihood at recombination fraction $\theta$ with independent assortment:
$$
Z(\theta)=\log_{10}\frac{L(\theta)}{L(1/2)}.
$$
When phenotype likelihood is independent of $\theta$, it can be computed from marker probabilities conditional on phenotype and ascertainment. Positive values support linkage; the observed marker pattern must be specified precisely because affected-only and affected-plus-unaffected observations give different likelihoods.

= Genetic recombination
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

Genetic recombination rearranges inherited genetic material into new combinations. In the <meiosis> example considered here, <chromosomal crossover> exchanges segments between nonsister <chromatids> of <homologous chromosomes>. A transmitted <chromatid> can consequently contain <DNA> from both parental homologues. This produces new <haplotypes> without requiring a new <mutation> at either scored <genetic locus>. See <Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/Meiosis>.

For <phase-known genotype> $AB/ab$, a single <chromosomal crossover> between the selected <genetic loci> can yield $Ab$ and $aB$ on the participating <chromatids>; the other two <chromatids> can remain $AB$ and $ab$. Thus an exchange in the four-<chromatid> bundle does not mean every resulting <gamete> is recombinant. Only a <chromosomal crossover> encountered along the transmitted <chromatid> within the selected interval affects its two-locus score.

Follow parental origin along the transmitted <chromatid> from the first <genetic locus> to the second. Each exchange switches that origin. An odd number of such <chromosomal crossovers> changes the endpoint combination; an even number restores parental phase. Two exchanges can therefore leave the scored <haplotype> as $AB$ even though an internal segment came from the other parental <homologous chromosome>. Recombinant status is relative to the chosen <genetic loci>, not a complete record of exchanges along the <chromosome>.

This <integer parity> distinction connects the physical process of <genetic recombination> to the observed <recombination fraction>. It also explains why a long <genetic map> interval can accumulate many exchanges while its endpoint <recombination fraction> approaches $1/2$ in the <Haldane mapping function> model.

= Chromosomal crossover
{parent=Genetic recombination}
{wiki}

= Crossing over
{synonym}

A chromosomal crossover is a reciprocal exchange of segments between nonsister <chromatids> of <homologous chromosomes> during <meiosis>. It can change the <haplotypes> inherited at selected <genetic loci>; whether it does so depends on the transmitted <chromatid> and interval.

= Crossover interference
{parent=Chromosomal crossover}

Crossover interference means that an exchange affects the occurrence of other nearby <chromosomal crossovers>. The <Haldane mapping function> assumes this dependence is absent. See <Molecular Biology of the Cell, 4th edition by Bruce Alberts and others/Meiosis>.

= Four-gamete test
{parent=Genetic recombination}
{wiki}

Under an <infinite sites mutation model>, two variable sites on one unrecombined rooted tree have nested or disjoint derived-allele clades. At most three of the four biallelic <haplotypes> can then occur. Observation of all four haplotypes proves that the single-tree, single-mutation-per-site model cannot hold: some ancestral <genetic recombination> is necessary if recurrent mutation is excluded. The test gives no proof of absence of recombination when only three haplotypes occur.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Recombination hotspot
{parent=Genetic recombination}
{wiki}

A recombination hotspot is a short region with an elevated local rate of <genetic recombination>. Repeated ancestral breakpoints there can separate neighboring <haplotype blocks> of strong <linkage disequilibrium>.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Recombination fraction
{parent=Genetic recombination}
{title2=$0\le\theta\le\tfrac12$}

The recombination fraction is the <probability> that a transmitted <gamete> carries a recombinant <haplotype> between two specified <genetic loci>, relative to the parent's known <haplotype> phase. Write it as $r$ here; the symbol $\theta$ is also common in <genetic linkage> analysis. In the ordinary symmetric two-locus model, $0\le r\le1/2$, with $r=0$ for <complete genetic linkage> and $r=1/2$ for <independent assortment>.

For <phase-known genotype> $AB/ab$, fair <Mendelian segregation> and equal reciprocal <gamete> <probabilities> give
$$
P(AB)=P(ab)=\frac{1-r}{2},\qquad P(Ab)=P(aB)=\frac r2.
$$
For example, 1,000 independent informative transmissions with observed counts $400,400,100,100$ in this order have empirical <recombination fraction> $(100+100)/1000=0.2$. Those illustrative counts match the <expected values> in the toy model, but actual counts fluctuate. The <genetic linkage> diagram shows both the parental and recombinant classes, so their separate <probabilities> are not confused with the total $r$.

The <recombination fraction> measures the <probability> of an odd number of origin switches along the transmitted <chromatid>. It is neither the <expected value> of the exchange count nor the <probability> of at least one exchange: even positive counts can be invisible at the two endpoints. Converting an observed <recombination fraction> to a <genetic map> distance therefore needs an explicit model such as the <Haldane mapping function>. The distinction between transmitted-<chromatid> counts and four-<chromatid> exchange counts is discussed in https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2704907/[Weeks, Tang and Kwon (2009), Casares' map function: No need for a 'corrected' Haldane's map function, Genetica 135, 305–307].

= Pedigree
{parent=Genetics}

A pedigree records parent–offspring relationships. A <founder in a pedigree> has no recorded parents; all other individuals have a genotype conditional on their parental genotypes. Inbreeding can produce loops in the undirected diagram while the directed ancestry remains acyclic.

= Outbred pedigree
{parent=Pedigree}

For calculations of <identity by descent>, an outbred pedigree has unrelated, noninbred <founders in a pedigree>, and no additional ancestral relationships beyond those explicitly specified. Founder gene copies are treated as distinct ancestral copies even if they have <identity by state>.

// Target: biology.bigb

= First cousin
{parent=Pedigree}

First cousins are children of <full siblings>. An otherwise outbred first-cousin pair shares two noninbred grandparents and has <kinship coefficient> $1/16$.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Full sibling
{parent=Pedigree}

Full siblings share both parents. With unrelated, noninbred <founders in a pedigree>, the two parental contributions give independent opportunities for <identity by descent>.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Pedigree likelihood
{parent=Pedigree}

For latent <genotypes> $G_i$ and observed <phenotypes> $X_i$, assume conditional phenotype independence, independent founder genotypes, and conditionally independent Mendelian transmissions. The <likelihood function> is
$$
\sum_{(G_i)}\prod_i P(X_i\mid G_i)\prod_{i\in F}P(G_i)\prod_{i\notin F}P(G_i\mid G_{m(i)},G_{f(i)}).
$$
This follows by the chain rule applied to the acyclic ancestry graph, then the law of total probability over all latent genotypes. <Penetrance> supplies the phenotype factors and <genetic recombination> supplies multilocus transmission factors.

= Phase averaging in a linkage likelihood
{parent=Pedigree likelihood}
{title2=$L(\theta)=\sum_s\pi_sL_s(\theta)$}

An unphased multilocus <genotype> can have several pairs of <haplotypes>. Its <pedigree likelihood> must average over these possible phases with prior weights $\pi_s$, rather than maximize over phases after observing the offspring. Under <linkage disequilibrium>, the weights need not be equal. They can be obtained by conditioning population haplotype frequencies on the observed parental <genotype>; different choices of what is conditioned on change only parameter-independent factors when those frequencies are held fixed.

= Score equation for a phase-averaged linkage likelihood
{parent=Phase averaging in a linkage likelihood}

For a known-phase contribution $L_1(\theta)\propto\theta^{r_1}(1-\theta)^{m_1-r_1}$ and an independent phase-averaged contribution $L_2(\theta)$, an interior maximum satisfies
$$
[r_1-m_1\theta]L_2(\theta)+\theta(1-\theta)L_2'(\theta)=0.
$$
This follows by differentiating $\log L_1+\log L_2$ and multiplying by $\theta(1-\theta)L_2$. The constrained <maximum likelihood estimate> on $[0,1/2]$ must also be compared with the endpoints. An interior root cannot be asserted without the observed pedigree counts; complete nonrecombinant data, for example, have their maximum at zero.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Pedigree graphical model
{parent=Pedigree likelihood}

Assign one <genotype> <random variable> to each person in a <pedigree>. Independent <founders in a pedigree> carry population genotype laws, and each other genotype has its <conditional distribution> given its two parents from <Mendelian segregation>. The ancestry <directed acyclic graph> is a <Bayesian network> for the joint genotype law. Its <moral graph> contains each parent-child edge and each coparent edge. Each founder factor and each child-with-parents factor belongs to a <clique>, giving an undirected graphical representation. The graph is an independence representation; equality with the minimal <conditional independence graph> needs additional faithfulness assumptions, particularly because inheritance can produce zero probabilities.

= Elston-Stewart algorithm
{parent=Pedigree likelihood}
{c}

Pedigree peeling eliminates latent <genotypes> by moving sums inside products whenever the other factors do not depend on the eliminated genotype. For one parent genotype $g$ and one child genotype $h$, the likelihood becomes
$$
\sum_g P(g)P(X_p\mid g)\left[\sum_hP(h\mid g)P(X_c\mid h)\right].
$$
The inner bracket is the child's message to its parent. With two states each, it needs four multiplications and two additions; multiplying the two parent weights and messages needs four more multiplications and the final sum one addition. The unrearranged four terms instead use twelve multiplications. Larger pedigrees use the same variable-elimination identity, with additional handling of pedigree loops.

= Founder in a pedigree
{parent=Pedigree}

= Founders in a pedigree
{synonym}

= Pedigree founder
{synonym}

A founder is an individual whose parents are outside the recorded <pedigree>. The standard <pedigree likelihood> assigns unrelated founders independent genotype distributions based on population allele or haplotype frequencies. If founders are related, their joint prior must instead account for that relationship.

= Recessive inheritance
{parent=Genetics}

In a fully penetrant autosomal recessive model with normal allele $A$ and disease allele $a$, $aa$ is affected and $AA,Aa$ are unaffected. A carrier cross $Aa\times Aa$ has offspring probabilities $1/4,1/2,1/4$ for $AA,Aa,aa$, respectively, from the two independent parental meioses.

= Sibling recurrence risk
{parent=Recessive inheritance}

Sibling recurrence risk is the <conditional probability> that another child has the disease, given an affected sibling. For unaffected parents, complete <penetrance> and a single recessive disease allele, the affected child identifies both parents as <genetic carriers>. Independent <Mendelian segregation> then gives recurrence probability $1/4$, whether or not the parents are related. Incomplete penetrance, phenocopies, different causal loci or affected parents change this calculation.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Genetic carrier
{parent=Recessive inheritance}

A genetic <genetic carrier> has one disease-associated recessive <allele> and one normal <allele> at a <genetic locus>, and is unaffected under a fully penetrant recessive model. Two <genetic carriers> have affected-offspring <probability> $1/4$ under <Mendelian segregation>; a <genetic carrier> and an individual with no disease <allele> have affected-offspring <probability> zero under that same mutation-free model.

= Phenotype
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

A <phenotype> is an observable characteristic of an <organism>, arising from its <genotype>, environment and other influences. Seed shape is a <phenotype>; $Aa$ is a <genotype>. The distinction prevents appearance from being mistaken for a complete inherited description. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Phenotype[NHGRI: phenotype].

For a specified binary <phenotype> $T$, let its <penetrance> in <genotype> $g$ be $r_g=P(T\mid g)$. The <law of total probability> gives $P(T)=\sum_g r_gP(g)$. Thus the ideal $Aa\times Aa$ example has $P(T)=r_{AA}/4+r_{Aa}/2+r_{aa}/4$: the familiar $3/4$ follows only when these <penetrances> are $1,1,0$. A <pedigree likelihood> keeps inheritance and the conditional <phenotype> model as separate factors.

= Dominance
{disambiguate=genetics}
{parent=Phenotype}

= Complete dominance
{parent=Dominance (genetics)}

For a specified characteristic, complete dominance means that the <heterozygote> has the same <phenotype> as one of the two <homozygotes>. It does not mean that the dominant <allele> is more common, more advantageous, or transmitted more often. <Penetrance> specifies the <conditional probability> of an expressed <phenotype> given a <genotype> and is a separate modeling assumption.

= Penetrance
{parent=Phenotype}
{wiki}

Penetrance is the probability of an expressed <phenotype> conditional on a specified <genotype>. It can depend on age, sex and environment. A fully penetrant recessive model assigns disease probability one to the recessive homozygote and zero to other genotypes; incomplete penetrance or phenocopies replace these indicators by intermediate probabilities.

= Genetic variance of penetrance
{parent=Penetrance}
{title2=$\operatorname{Var}\{\mathbb E(Y\mid G)\}$}

For a binary disease trait $Y$ with <genotype> $G$, the genetic variance of <penetrance> is the between-genotype term in the <law of total variance>. The averaged within-genotype term is $\mathbb E[q_G(1-q_G)]$. Under <multiplicative diallelic penetrance>, the genetic term is $p^2(\beta^2-\alpha^4)$. This decomposition attributes variance to the modeled locus, not automatically to all hereditary causes.

= Multiplicative diallelic penetrance
{parent=Penetrance}
{title2=$q_G=p\theta^G,\quad G\sim\operatorname{Bin}(2,\phi)$}

With <Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium>, let $G$ count copies of the risk <allele>. The <penetrance> $p\theta^G$ must lie in $[0,1]$ for every possible <genotype>. Writing $\alpha=1-\phi+\phi\theta$ and $\beta=1-\phi+\phi\theta^2$ gives $\mathbb E(q_G)=p\alpha^2$ and $\mathbb E(q_G^2)=p^2\beta^2$.

= Genotype
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

A <genotype> records the inherited <alleles> carried by an individual at one or more <genetic loci>. At a two-copy <diploid> <genetic locus> with <alleles> $A,a$, the possible unordered <genotypes> are $AA,Aa,aa$. The first and last are <homozygotes>; the middle is a <heterozygote>. The notation records inherited states, without yet asserting their <phenotypes>. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/genotype[NHGRI: genotype].

An unordered $Aa$ combines the two parental orders $Aa$ and $aA$. This small bookkeeping point explains the factor two in <Hardy-Weinberg proportions> and in an $Aa\times Aa$ cross. If parental origin or arrangement across several <genetic loci> matters, a <phase-known genotype> retains extra information about the two <haplotypes>.

= Genotyping
{parent=Genotype}

<Genotyping> determines the <alleles> carried at chosen <genetic loci>. A <diploid> <genotype> call need not identify its two <haplotypes>; that additionally requires a <phase-known genotype> or <haplotype estimation>.

= Genotyping error
{parent=Genotype}

A <genotyping error> is an incorrect observed <allele> or <genotype> call. A probabilistic observation model replaces the deterministic <genetic marker> factor by $P(X=x\mid G=g)$, allowing nonzero <probability> of an observed call inconsistent with the latent <genotype>. Such error can bias family transmission tests and linkage <likelihoods>.

= Heterozygosity
{parent=Genotype}

= Heterozygous
{synonym}

<Heterozygosity> means that the two <allele> states at a diploid <genetic locus> differ; an individual with that property is a <heterozygote>. <Heterozygous> parental <genotypes> provide alternative <alleles> for a <transmission disequilibrium test>.

= Homozygosity
{parent=Genotype}

= Homozygous
{synonym}

<Homozygosity> means that the two <allele> states at a diploid <genetic locus> are the same; an individual with that property is a <homozygote>. Observed <homozygosity> does not by itself establish <identity by descent>.

= Heterozygote
{parent=Genotype}

A heterozygote carries different <alleles> at a diploid locus. Under ordinary Mendelian segregation each is transmitted to an offspring with probability $1/2$. Only heterozygous parents provide a comparison between alternative transmissions in the <transmission disequilibrium test>.

= Homozygote
{parent=Genotype}

A homozygote carries two identical <alleles> at a locus; a <heterozygote> carries different alleles. Homozygosity of an observed <haplotype> is identity by state, and need not be <identity by descent>.

= Haplotype
{parent=Genotype}
{wiki}

A haplotype is the set of <alleles> on one chromosome across specified <genetic loci>. Closely linked markers without intervening <genetic recombination> are transmitted as one haplotype; they are not independent observations of inheritance.

= Haplotype frequency
{parent=Haplotype}
{title2=$f_{AB}$}

A <haplotype frequency> is the proportion of chromosome copies carrying specified <alleles> together across chosen <genetic loci>. Its <marginal distributions> give <allele frequencies>; its departures from products of those frequencies are <linkage disequilibrium>.

= Haplotype estimation
{parent=Haplotype}
{wiki}

<Haplotype estimation> reconstructs which <alleles> share a <chromosome> from observed <genotypes> and other information, such as <pedigrees>. A doubly <heterozygous> <genotype> can be $AB/ab$ or $Ab/aB$; a <phase-known genotype> distinguishes them. Uncertainty in that reconstruction must propagate to estimated <haplotype frequencies> and <linkage disequilibrium>.

= Haplotype block
{parent=Haplotype}
{wiki}

A haplotype block is a chromosomal region with limited historical <genetic recombination>, strong local <linkage disequilibrium> and comparatively few common <haplotypes>. Its definition is operational and population-dependent; it does not mean that recombination never occurs within it.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Phase-known genotype
{parent=Genotype}

At several <genetic loci>, a phase-known genotype specifies which <alleles> occur together on each chromosome, hence its two <haplotypes>. An unphased multilocus <genotype> may be compatible with several haplotype pairs. For two heterozygous loci, $AB/ab$ and $Ab/aB$ have identical unphased genotypes but different transmission probabilities when <genetic linkage> is present.

= Genetic locus
{parent=Genetics}

= Locus (genetics)
{synonym}

A locus is a position or region of a genome. An <allele> specifies an inherited state there; a <haplotype> specifies states at several loci on one chromosome.

= Allele
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

An <allele> is one of the alternative inherited states at a <genetic locus>, often distinguished by a difference in <DNA> sequence. A population can contain many <alleles>, although a two-copy <diploid> individual carries at most two at that <genetic locus>, one inherited from each parent. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Allele[NHGRI: allele].

Count copies, not just individuals, when computing <allele frequency>: in 100 individuals with <genotype> counts $49\ AA$, $42\ Aa$, $9\ aa$, there are $2(49)+42=140$ copies of $A$ among 200, so $p_A=0.70$. The <heterozygotes> contribute to both <allele> counts. This converts observed <genotypes> into the population quantity needed by the <Hardy-Weinberg principle>.

= Allele frequency
{parent=Allele}
{title2=$p_A$}

The allele frequency is the proportion of gene copies at a specified <genetic locus> carrying an <allele>. Two unrelated copies can have the same observed allele without being <identical by descent>; under independent sampling their matching probability is the sum of squared allele frequencies. Pedigree <autozygosity> instead traces copies to the same ancestral copy.

= Allele fixation
{parent=Allele frequency}
{title2=$u=0\text{ or }u=1$}

An <allele> fixes when all copies at the relevant locus in the population are that <allele>. Fixation can occur through selection or <genetic drift>. Spatial polymorphism means that fixation within an individual habitat need not imply fixation across a <metapopulation>.

= Population genetics
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

Population genetics studies the frequencies of <alleles> and <genotypes> in populations, and their changes through inheritance, mutation, selection, migration and random sampling. <Kingman's coalescent> describes a neutral sample's ancestry backward in time.

= Heterozygote advantage
{parent=Population genetics}

A <heterozygote> has greater <genotype fitness> than either corresponding <homozygote>. In the deterministic random-mating two-allele viability-selection model this gives an attracting interior <allele frequency>, unlike the repelling interior frequency produced by <underdominance>.

= Underdominance
{parent=Population genetics}
{wiki}

= Heterozygote disadvantage
{synonym}

<Underdominance> means that the <heterozygote> has lower <genotype fitness> than both corresponding <homozygotes>. In a deterministic randomly mating biallelic population, it produces an unstable interior allele-frequency threshold separating the two fixation outcomes, unlike <heterozygote advantage>.

= Underdominant allele-frequency dynamics
{parent=Underdominance}
{title2=$\dot u=u(1-u)[(a+b)u-b]$}

With <homozygote> fitness advantages $a,b>0$ over the <heterozygote>, the <allele frequency> $u$ of the first <allele> has an unstable threshold $b/(a+b)$. Below it that <allele> decreases; above it it increases. In a discrete viability-selection generation the same numerator gives $u'-u$ after division by <mean> <genotype fitness>. Equal <homozygote> advantages give $\dot u=su(1-u)(2u-1)$, with stable endpoints and unstable midpoint.

= Genotype fitness
{parent=Population genetics}
{title2=$w$}

<Genotype fitness> measures the reproductive or viability success assigned to a <genotype> in a specified selection model. Relative fitnesses weight <Hardy-Weinberg proportions> after viability selection; continuous-time fitness differences instead act as selection-rate differences. The convention and time units must be specified before comparing selection with migration.

= Ancestral recombination graph
{parent=Population genetics}
{wiki}

= ARG
{synonym}
{c}

An ancestral recombination graph records ancestral coalescences and <genetic recombination> events for sampled chromosomal material. Backwards in time, each pair of ancestral lineages merges at rate one in standard diploid coalescent units, while a lineage splits at a recombination breakpoint at rate $\rho/2$ times the genetic span of the material it carries. The breakpoint splits that lineage's ancestral material into two parental contributions. Waiting times are exponential with the sum of the current event rates. At each genomic position, retaining the ancestry at that position gives a marginal <phylogenetic tree> with the neutral <Kingman's coalescent> distribution. Different positions have dependent trees because they share parts of the graph.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Population bottleneck
{parent=Population genetics}
{wiki}

A population bottleneck is a temporary large reduction in population size. It changes ancestral coalescence rates and can leave fewer ancestral <haplotypes> and increased <linkage disequilibrium> after the population expands.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Genetic drift
{parent=Population genetics}
{wiki}

Genetic drift is random variation of <allele> frequencies due to finite-population reproduction. In the neutral ancestry description, randomly sampled gene copies merge into common ancestors according to <Kingman's coalescent>.

// Target: probability-and-statistics.bigb

= Effective population size
{parent=Population genetics}
{wiki}
{title2=$N_e$}

Effective population size is the size of an idealized population giving the relevant rate of <genetic drift> or ancestral coalescence in the population being modeled. In the standard neutral diploid coalescent, time is measured in $2N_e$ generations and the population-scaled <mutation> parameter is $\theta=4N_e\mu$.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Segregating site
{parent=Population genetics}
{title2=$S$}

A segregating site is a position at which at least two states occur in the sampled chromosomes. Under an <infinite sites mutation model>, mutations below the sample's common ancestor correspond one-to-one to segregating sites. Their number is commonly denoted $S$.

= Watterson estimator
{parent=Segregating site}
{c}
{title2=$\widehat\theta_W=\frac{S}{\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}1/i}$}

For a neutral sample of size $n\ge2$ under the <infinite sites mutation model>, the Watterson estimator is $\widehat\theta_W=S/a_{n-1}$, where $a_{n-1}=\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}1/i$. Since the coalescent has $ES=\theta a_{n-1}$, this estimator is unbiased. It matches the observed segregating-site count to its expected value, but need not maximize the exact <segregating-site likelihood under the neutral coalescent>.

= Asymptotic normality of the Watterson estimator
{parent=Watterson estimator}
{title2=$\sqrt{\log n}(\widehat\theta_W-\theta)\xrightarrow{d}N(0,\theta)$}

For fixed $\theta>0$, a <geometric mutation count in a coalescent epoch> with mean $\mu_i=\theta/i$ has third <moment> $\mu_i+6\mu_i^2+6\mu_i^3$. The centered absolute third moment is at most $4\mathbb E[Y_i^3]+4\mu_i^3$, so its sum is $O(\log n)$. The total <variance> is asymptotic to $\theta\log n$, making the <Lyapunov condition> with $\delta=1$ hold. Rescaling by the <harmonic sum> gives the displayed limit. At $\theta=0$ the estimator is identically zero and the limit is degenerate. The <variance of the Watterson estimator> decreases only logarithmically with sample size at a fixed nonrecombining locus.

= Variance of the Watterson estimator
{parent=Watterson estimator}
{title2=$\operatorname{Var}(\widehat\theta_W)=\frac{\theta}{H_{n-1}}+\frac{\theta^2H_{n-1}^{(2)}}{H_{n-1}^2}$}

The <moments of the mutation count on a neutral coalescent tree> give this <variance> for $\widehat\theta_W=S/H_{n-1}$. Since $H_{n-1}\sim\log n$ and the sum of inverse squares is bounded, it tends to zero. Together with unbiasedness, this gives <mean-square convergence> to the mutation parameter, so it is a <consistent estimator>.

= Infinite sites mutation model
{parent=Population genetics}
{wiki}

= Infinitely many sites mutation model
{synonym}

The infinite-sites model assumes that every mutation occurs at a previously unmutated site, so each mutation on the sample genealogy produces a distinct segregating site. If mutation rate per lineage is $\theta/2$ in standard coalescent time units, a tree of total length $L$ has a <Poisson distribution> of mutation count with mean $\theta L/2$.

= Segregating-site likelihood under the neutral coalescent
{parent=Infinite sites mutation model}

For $n\ge2$, the <probability generating function> of the segregating-site count conditional on mutation parameter $\theta$ is
$$
E[z^S\mid\theta]=\prod_{i=1}^{n-1}\frac{i}{i+\theta(1-z)}.
$$
This follows by integrating the Poisson generating function over the independent exponential epoch lengths. With $W=L/2$, its density is $(n-1)e^{-w}(1-e^{-w})^{n-2}$. Expanding the binomial factor and integrating $w^k e^{-(\theta+r+1)w}$ gives
$$
P(S=k\mid\theta)=(n-1)\theta^k\sum_{r=0}^{n-2}\frac{(-1)^r\binom{n-2}{r}}{(\theta+r+1)^{k+1}}.
$$
For $k=0$ use $\theta^0=1$, including at $\theta=0$. A posterior density divided by a positive prior density is proportional to this likelihood, allowing posterior simulation output to estimate its maximizer.

= Inbreeding
{parent=Population genetics}
{wiki}

= Inbred
{synonym}

Inbreeding is reproduction between genetically related individuals. It increases the chance that an offspring's maternal and paternal copies at a locus are <identical by descent>. Such homozygosity by descent is autozygosity.

= Inbreeding coefficient
{parent=Inbreeding}
{title2=$F$}

The inbreeding coefficient is the <probability> that the two <allele> copies at a locus within an individual are <identical by descent>. It equals the <kinship coefficient> of the individual's parents. It concerns ancestral identity, rather than merely <homozygosity> of observed allelic states.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Inbreeding genotype frequencies
{parent=Inbreeding coefficient}

For a biallelic locus with frequencies $p=1-q$ and $q$, and <inbreeding coefficient> $F$, ancestral-copy identity with probability $F$ gives <genotype> probabilities $P(AA)=p^2+Fpq$, $P(Aa)=2pq(1-F)$ and $P(aa)=q^2+Fpq$. In the IBD component both copies equal a single ancestral draw; in the remaining component the two ancestral alleles are independent population draws. A fully penetrant recessive disease therefore has risk $q^2+Fq(1-q)$.

// Target: biology.bigb

= First-cousin inbreeding coefficient
{parent=Inbreeding coefficient}
{title2=$F=1/16$}

For an otherwise <outbred pedigree>, each of the two shared grandparents contributes $(1/2)^{2+2+1}=1/32$ to the <kinship coefficient> of first cousins. Thus their child has <inbreeding coefficient> $1/16$. Equivalently, cousin kinship is one quarter of the $1/4$ kinship of their sibling parents.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Linkage disequilibrium
{parent=Population genetics}
{wiki}
{title2=$D=P(AB)-P(A)P(B)$}

= LD
{c}
{synonym}

<Linkage disequilibrium> means that knowing an <allele> at one <genetic locus> changes the <probability> of an <allele> at another <genetic locus> in population <haplotypes>. It compares their <joint probability distribution> with the product of their <marginal distributions>. This is useful because a <genetic marker> can carry information about another <genetic locus>: <tag SNPs> exploit this redundancy, without establishing a causal <genetic association>.

Consider two <genetic loci> with <alleles> $A,a$ and $B,b$. Sample one <haplotype>, rather than an unphased <diploid> <genotype>. Write its four <haplotype frequencies> as $f_{AB},f_{Ab},f_{aB},f_{ab}$, summing to one, and set the marginal <allele frequencies> to $p=f_{AB}+f_{Ab}$ and $q=f_{AB}+f_{aB}$. The <linkage disequilibrium> coefficient is
$$
D=f_{AB}-pq=f_{AB}f_{ab}-f_{Ab}f_{aB}.
$$
The second expression follows by substituting $f_{ab}=1-f_{AB}-f_{Ab}-f_{aB}$. The entire two-locus <joint probability distribution> is then
$$
\begin{array}{c|cc|c}
 &B&b&\text{total}\\\hline
A&pq+D&p(1-q)-D&p\\
a&(1-p)q-D&(1-p)(1-q)+D&1-p\\\hline
\text{total}&q&1-q&1
\end{array}
$$
Every row and column sum is unchanged when $D$ varies. <Linkage equilibrium> means $D=0$: all four <haplotype frequencies> equal products of <allele frequencies>, so the two <allele> indicators are <independent random variables>. For $p>0$, the <conditional probability> $P(B\mid A)=q+D/p$ shows directly how positive $D$ enriches $B$ among copies carrying $A$, while negative $D$ depletes it. Positive $D$ also enriches $ab$; negative $D$ enriches $Ab$ and $aB$. Swapping the labels $A$ and $a$ reverses the sign of $D$, so neither sign is intrinsically favorable.

For example, fix the <allele frequencies> at $p=0.60,q=0.40$. At <linkage equilibrium>, the <haplotype frequencies> in the order $AB,Ab,aB,ab$ are $(0.24,0.36,0.16,0.24)$. Positive <linkage disequilibrium> $D=0.12$ instead gives $(0.36,0.24,0.04,0.36)$, with $P(B\mid A)=0.60$; negative <linkage disequilibrium> $D=-0.12$ gives $(0.12,0.48,0.28,0.12)$, with $P(B\mid A)=0.20$. Counting each <allele> separately would miss the difference.

\Image[haplotype-joint-frequencies-fixed-marginals.png]
{title=Three <haplotype> <joint probability distributions> with identical marginal <allele frequencies>, showing negative <linkage disequilibrium>, <linkage equilibrium> and positive <linkage disequilibrium>}

Nonnegative <haplotype frequencies> constrain $D$. Define the nonnegative bounds $L=\min(pq,(1-p)(1-q))$ and $U=\min(p(1-q),(1-p)q)$. The four entries above give
$$
-L\le D\le U,\qquad \max(0,p+q-1)\le f_{AB}\le\min(p,q).
$$
Thus $|D|\le1/4$ globally, but a particular pair of <allele frequencies> usually permits a smaller, asymmetric interval. The <standardized linkage disequilibrium> convention used here retains the sign:
$$
D'=\begin{cases}D/U&D>0,\\0&D=0,\\D/L&D<0,\end{cases}
\qquad -1\le D'\le1.
$$
This convention requires $0<p,q<1$; if either <genetic locus> has only one <allele> in the population, $D=0$ but normalized measures are undefined. Some presentations report only $|D'|$. At $D'=1$ or $-1$, at least one of the four <haplotypes> has zero frequency; this alone does not prove that <genetic recombination> has never occurred.

For the <indicator random variables> $X=\mathbf{1}_{A}$ and $Y=\mathbf{1}_{B}$, their <covariance> is $D$, and their <variances> are $p(1-p)$ and $q(1-q)$. The <allelic correlation> measure is their squared <correlation coefficient>,
$$
r_{\mathrm{LD}}^2=\frac{D^2}{p(1-p)q(1-q)},\qquad
0\le r_{\mathrm{LD}}^2\le\frac{\max(L^2,U^2)}{p(1-p)q(1-q)}\le1.
$$
The subscript distinguishes it from the <recombination fraction> $r$ in <linkage-disequilibrium decay under recombination>. Squaring loses the association's sign. In the illustrated positive and negative examples, $r_{\mathrm{LD}}^2=0.25$ in both, whereas $D'=0.75$ and $-0.50$, respectively. For another original example, take $p=0.10,q=0.50,f_{AB}=0.10$: then $D=0.05,D'=1$, yet $r_{\mathrm{LD}}^2=1/9$. Every $A$ carries $B$, but most copies carrying $B$ lack $A$. The frequency constraints and the distinction between these normalizations are analyzed in <Mathematical properties of the r² measure of linkage disequilibrium by Jenna M. VanLiere and others> (sections 2.1 and 2.5, pp. 131–132 and 135–136).

<Genetic linkage> describes transmission through <meiosis>; <linkage disequilibrium> describes a population's <haplotype frequencies>. Nearby <genetic loci> on a <chromosome> can be in <linkage equilibrium>. Conversely, mixing populations can create <linkage disequilibrium> even between <genetic loci> on different <chromosomes>. For example, mix equal numbers of independently sampled <haplotypes> from two populations, each internally at <linkage equilibrium>, with $p=q=0.80$ in one and $p=q=0.20$ in the other. The pooled <allele frequencies> are $p=q=0.50$, but $f_{AB}=(0.64+0.04)/2=0.34$, giving $D=0.09$. This is a consequence of <population stratification>, not a measurement of the <recombination fraction>.

Measured <haplotype frequencies> also depend on the sample. Finite <sample size>, related individuals, <genetic-study ascertainment>, <genotyping error> and uncertain <haplotype estimation> can change an estimate. A missing rare <haplotype> can give empirical $|D'|=1$ without demonstrating its absence from the population. Report the sample definition and <allele frequencies> alongside the association measure; a nonzero sample estimate alone does not establish nonzero population <linkage disequilibrium>.

A concrete dataset is the 2005 <A haplotype map of the human genome by The International HapMap Consortium>. The International HapMap Consortium used <genotyping> platforms including Illumina BeadArray to obtain <SNP> <genotype> calls from 269 <DNA> samples across four collections: 30 parent–offspring trios from Ibadan, 30 trios from Utah, 45 unrelated individuals from Beijing and 44 from Tokyo. Parent–offspring trios and the PHASE algorithm supplied <haplotype estimation>, rather than direct observation of every <chromosome>. In Figure 7, 36 <SNPs> resolve seven <haplotypes> in a <chromosome> 2 region in the Utah panel; seven <tag SNPs> capture the 36 <SNPs> at $r_{\mathrm{LD}}^2\ge0.8$. The readout demonstrates reusable <allelic correlation>, not causal effects.

Controls required over 80% complete calls, at most one discordance among five duplicate <genotypes>, at most one <Mendelian segregation> error per trio panel, and a <Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium> test with $P>0.001$; blind reassessment checked performance. Minor <allele frequencies> of at least 0.05 were targeted, the collections were not population-representative, and inferred <haplotypes> retain phase uncertainty. These limits restrict extrapolation of <linkage disequilibrium>.<A haplotype map of the human genome by The International HapMap Consortium>[pp. 1300–1302, Table 1; Figure 7, p. 1306; Methods, p. 1316]{ref}

= Linkage equilibrium
{parent=Linkage disequilibrium}
{title2=$D=0$}

For two <genetic loci> with two <alleles> each, <linkage equilibrium> is <independence> of their states on sampled <haplotypes>. It concerns association between <genetic loci>; <Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium> concerns pairing of <alleles> into <diploid> <genotypes>.

= Standardized linkage disequilibrium
{parent=Linkage disequilibrium}
{title2=$D'=D/D_{\max}$}

For positive <linkage disequilibrium> $D$, take $D_{\max}=\min(p_A(1-p_B),(1-p_A)p_B)$; for negative $D$, take $D_{\max}=\min(p_Ap_B,(1-p_A)(1-p_B))$ and retain its negative sign in $D'$. Thus $|D'|\le1$. Its magnitude measures the fraction of the largest disequilibrium possible at the specified allele frequencies. High $|D'|$ need not imply high <allelic correlation>, so it does not alone guarantee good marker prediction.

= Linkage-disequilibrium decay under recombination
{parent=Linkage disequilibrium}
{title2=$D_{t+1}=(1-r)D_t$}

<Genetic recombination> erodes <linkage disequilibrium> by reshuffling <alleles> between <haplotypes>, while preserving marginal <allele frequencies> in the idealized model. Let $f_{AB,t}$ denote the <haplotype frequency> in the <gamete> pool of generation $t$, let $p,q$ denote its marginal <allele frequencies>, and let $r\in[0,1/2]$ be the constant <recombination fraction> between two autosomal <genetic loci>. Assume <random mating> within one population, nonoverlapping generations, fair <Mendelian segregation>, equal reciprocal <gamete> production, the same $r$ in both sexes, and a deterministic population large enough to neglect <genetic drift>. Exclude <natural selection>, <mutation> and <gene flow>. Then
$$
f_{AB,t+1}=(1-r)f_{AB,t}+rpq=f_{AB,t}-rD_t,
\qquad D_{t+1}=(1-r)D_t,
\qquad D_t=(1-r)^tD_0.
$$
Here $p$ and $q$ remain constant: recombining changes which <alleles> occur together, not their total numbers. The other <haplotype frequencies> change by $+rD_t$ for $Ab,aB$ and $-rD_t$ for $ab$. Consequently the <joint probability distribution> stays within its allowed bounds and approaches the product of its <marginal distributions> when $r>0$.

For the original positive example $p=0.60,q=0.40,D_0=0.12$, a <recombination fraction> $r=0.10$ gives $D_1=0.108$ and $D_{10}\approx0.04184$. The negative example $D_0=-0.12$ has exactly the opposite sequence: <genetic recombination> reduces the magnitude without reversing the sign. With $r=0$, the initial <linkage disequilibrium> persists in this model; even <independent assortment> at $r=1/2$ only halves $D$ each generation. A population association does not vanish after one generation merely because the <genetic loci> are unlinked.

\Image[linkage-disequilibrium-ideal-recombination-decay.png]
{title=Idealized <linkage-disequilibrium decay under recombination> at four <recombination fractions>, with faster decay of squared <allelic correlation> when marginal <allele frequencies> remain fixed}

The <geometric sequence> gives a generation scale: for $r>0$, the continuous interpolation reaches $|D_0|/2$ at $t_{1/2}=\log(1/2)/\log(1-r)$. Actual generations are integers; the first at or below half is $\lceil t_{1/2}\rceil$. For small $r$, $t_{1/2}\approx(\log 2)/r$. Because the denominator of <allelic correlation> stays fixed, $r_{\mathrm{LD},t}^2=(1-r)^{2t}r_{\mathrm{LD},0}^2$; its decay is faster than the magnitude of $D_t$.

A short derivation uses <random mating> to assign frequency $2f_{AB}f_{ab}$ to the double <heterozygote> $AB/ab$ and $2f_{Ab}f_{aB}$ to $Ab/aB$. Relative to $r=0$, each loses or gains an $AB$ <gamete> fraction $r/2$, respectively. Their net contribution is $r(f_{Ab}f_{aB}-f_{AB}f_{ab})=-rD$. The other <genotypes> produce no net change in $f_{AB}$, proving the recurrence and, by iteration, its solution.

This temporal model does not identify elapsed generations from physical separation alone. The <recombination fraction> depends on <genetic recombination>, including <recombination hotspots>, while <population stratification>, <natural selection>, <gene flow> or <genetic drift> can create or maintain <linkage disequilibrium>. Repeated sampling across time would also require accounting for <sample size> and <haplotype estimation>. A cross-sectional plot of association against distance between <genetic loci> is therefore a different observation from the idealized trajectory plotted here.

= Allelic correlation
{parent=Linkage disequilibrium}
{title2=$r^2=D^2/[p_A(1-p_A)p_B(1-p_B)]$}

For two segregating biallelic loci, encode the allele on a random population <haplotype> by indicator variables $X,Y$. Their <covariance> is the <linkage disequilibrium> coefficient $D$. The squared <correlation> is $r^2=D^2/[p_A(1-p_A)p_B(1-p_B)]$, between zero and one. It measures how well one marker predicts the other and is undefined if either marker is monomorphic.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Population stratification
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

Population stratification is systematic variation in allele frequencies between subpopulations. If those subpopulations also differ in outcome causes, <genetic variant> associations can exhibit <confounding>, violating <instrumental-variable independence> in <Mendelian randomization>.

= Hardy-Weinberg principle
{c}
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki=Hardy–Weinberg_principle}
{title2=$p^2,2pq,q^2;\quad p+q=1$}

= Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium
{c}
{synonym}

= HWE
{c}
{synonym}

= Hardy-Weinberg proportions
{c}
{synonym}

The <Hardy-Weinberg principle> describes the <genotype> proportions produced by independently combining two parental <gametes> from the same <allele> pool. For <alleles> $A,a$ with <allele frequencies> $p,q$, where $p+q=1$, the <Hardy-Weinberg proportions> are
$$
P(AA)=p^2,\qquad P(Aa)=2pq,\qquad P(aa)=q^2.
$$
The two ordered <heterozygote> outcomes contribute $pq+qp$. These proportions sum to $(p+q)^2=1$, and their implied $A$ <allele frequency> is $p^2+(2pq)/2=p$. Reshuffling copies therefore preserves their frequencies.

For stable <Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium> across generations, use a large population with <random mating>, equal <allele frequencies> in the parental pools, ordinary <Mendelian segregation>, and no <natural selection>, <mutation>, <gene flow> or <genetic drift>. Negligible <genetic drift> is an idealization of large population size. The <one-generation random-mating equilibrium> needs independent union of <gametes>; persistence additionally requires processes that change <allele frequencies> to be absent.

At $p=0.70,q=0.30$, the predicted <genotype> proportions are $0.49,0.42,0.09$. For 100 sampled individuals the <expected values> of the counts are $49,42,9$, rather than the $25,50,25$ of a specified $Aa\times Aa$ cross. The latter fixes both parental <genotypes>; <Hardy-Weinberg proportions> average over population mating.

\Image[hardy-weinberg-proportions.png]
{title=Hardy-Weinberg genotype proportions as allele frequency varies, highlighting p = 0.70}

An observed population need not have these proportions. For example, $70\ AA$, no $Aa$, and $30\ aa$ among 100 individuals still gives $p=0.70$, but has no <heterozygotes>. <Random mating> predicts $0.42$ <heterozygosity> in the next generation under the stated model. A finite sample can also depart by chance, so a discrepancy is a starting point for investigating <inbreeding>, <population stratification> or <genotyping error>, rather than proof of one cause.

Hardy's 1908 note made the conceptual payoff explicit: a dominant <phenotype> does not by itself make its <allele> spread. <Complete dominance> describes expression; changes in frequency require a separate mechanism such as <natural selection>. See https://www-leland.stanford.edu/~huatang/gene244/readings/HardyWeinberg.pdf[Hardy, Mendelian Proportions in a Mixed Population, Science 28 (1908), 49–50].

= Multiplicative penetrance preserves Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium
{parent=Hardy-Weinberg principle}
{title2=$\pi_i^*=\pi_i\psi_i/\sum_u\pi_u\psi_u$}

For ordered alleles $I,J$ independently drawn with probabilities $\pi_i$, suppose disease <penetrance> is $c\psi_i\psi_j$. Conditional on disease, the allele draws remain independent with probabilities $\pi_i^*=\pi_i\psi_i/\sum_u\pi_u\psi_u$. This follows by normalizing the product $c\pi_i\pi_j\psi_i\psi_j$. Unordered homozygote probabilities are $(\pi_i^*)^2$ and heterozygote probabilities are $2\pi_i^*\pi_j^*$. Unaffected controls do not generally share this factorization: their genotype weights contain $1-c\psi_i\psi_j$.

// Target: biology.bigb

= One-generation random-mating equilibrium
{parent=Hardy-Weinberg principle}
{title2=$p=u+v/2,\quad(p^2,2p(1-p),(1-p)^2)$}

If parental <genotype> frequencies are $(u,v,w)$ and the two parental gametes are independently sampled from the same <allele> pool, <Mendelian segregation> gives allele frequency $p=u+v/2$. Their independent union has the displayed <Hardy-Weinberg proportions>, irrespective of parental genotype proportions. Equal allele frequencies in the two sexes and no transmission or viability distortion are part of this conclusion.

= Allele-frequency estimator under Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium
{parent=Hardy-Weinberg principle}

From genotype counts in $n$ independent diploid individuals, the unbiased and maximum-likelihood estimator of the frequency of allele $B$ is
$$
\widehat\theta=\frac{2n_{BB}+n_{AB}}{2n}.
$$
It is the sample proportion among the $2n$ sampled alleles, so it is consistent and
$$
\sqrt n(\widehat\theta-\theta)
\xrightarrow{d}N\left(0,\frac{\theta(1-\theta)}2\right).
$$

= Genetic variant
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

A genetic variant is a heritable difference in a DNA sequence relative to a chosen reference. Genetic variants can be used as <instrumental variables> in <Mendelian randomization> when the required causal assumptions are credible.

= Single-nucleotide polymorphism
{parent=Genetic variant}
{wiki}

= SNP
{synonym}
{c}

A single-nucleotide polymorphism is variation at one nucleotide position in sampled genomes. Under an <infinite sites mutation model>, the number of such variable positions in a sample equals the number of mutations on the genealogy below its most recent common ancestor.

= Tag SNP
{parent=Single-nucleotide polymorphism}
{wiki}

A tag SNP represents other nearby <single-nucleotide polymorphisms> whose <alleles> are strongly correlated through <linkage disequilibrium>. Tagging reduces the number of markers needed for <association mapping>; it does not establish that the tag itself causes the phenotype.

// Target: biology.bigb

= Quantitative genetics
{parent=Genetics}
{wiki}

Quantitative genetics studies how genetic and environmental variation contribute to continuously varying traits.

= Genetic risk score
{parent=Quantitative genetics}
{title2=$G_{\rm score}=\sum_j w_jG_j$}

A genetic risk score combines allele counts from several <genetic variants>, usually using specified weights. In <Mendelian randomization> it can act as a combined <instrumental variable> for an <exposure>; interpretation still requires <instrument relevance>, <instrumental-variable independence>, and the <exclusion restriction>.

= Heritability
{title2=$h^2$}
{parent=Quantitative genetics}
{wiki}

Heritability is the fraction of phenotypic variance in a specified population and environment attributed to genetic variation. It is a population parameter, not the genetic fraction of one individual's trait.

= Twin study
{parent=Heritability}
{wiki}

A twin study compares trait resemblance between twins with different degrees of genetic relatedness to separate genetic and environmental variance components.

= Monozygotic twin
{title2=MZ twin}
{parent=Twin study}

This is the single-zygote subtype of a <twin>.

Monozygotic twins develop from one zygote and are modeled as sharing essentially all additive genetic variation.

= Dizygotic twin
{title2=DZ twin}
{parent=Twin study}

This is the two-zygote subtype of a <twin>.

Dizygotic twins develop from two zygotes and, absent assortative mating and other complications, share one half of additive genetic variation on average.

= ACE model
{c}
{parent=Twin study}
{wiki}

The ACE model decomposes trait variation into additive genetic effects $A$, common environmental effects $C$, and unique environmental effects $E$.

= Falconer's formula
{c}
{parent=ACE model}
{wiki}

Falconer's formula estimates narrow-sense heritability from monozygotic and dizygotic twin correlations:
$$
h^2=2(r_{\rm MZ}-r_{\rm DZ}).
$$
It relies on equal relevant environments and a dizygotic additive-genetic correlation of one half.

= Assortative mating
{parent=Quantitative genetics}
{wiki}

Assortative mating is nonrandom mating in which partners are more or less similar than random pairs for a trait or correlated characteristic. Positive assortment can increase genetic similarity among siblings.

= Pathogen
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= RNA splicing
{c}
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

Processing of a precursor <RNA> that removes an <intron> and joins the flanking <exons>. The <ribozyme> experiments show one self-splicing mechanism; <RNA splicing> need not always be autonomous.

= Intron
{parent=RNA splicing}
{wiki}

A segment represented in a precursor <RNA> that is removed by <RNA splicing>; the corresponding segment of a <gene> is also called an <intron>. An <intron> can interrupt <ribosomal RNA> as well as <messenger RNA>.

= Group I catalytic intron
{parent=Intron}
{wiki}

= Group I intron
{synonym}

A class of <introns> with a conserved <RNA> catalytic core and a two-step <RNA splicing> mechanism initiated by external <guanosine>. Some <group I introns> self-splice under suitable conditions; others require <protein> assistance. The <ribozyme> body explains the Tetrahymena example.

= Exon
{parent=RNA splicing}
{wiki}

A segment retained in the mature <RNA> after <RNA splicing>, or the corresponding segment of a <gene>. An <exon> need not encode a <protein>: the <ribosomal RNA> <exons> in the Tetrahymena <ribozyme> experiment are an example.

= Guanosine
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

A <nucleoside> comprising <guanine> attached to <ribose>. Its free $3'$ <hydroxyl group> initiates <group I intron> <RNA splicing>; <guanosine> is incorporated into the excised <intron>, rather than acting as a regenerated <catalyst>.

= Gel electrophoresis
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

Separation of charged <molecules> through a gel in an <electric field>. Denaturing conditions suppress folded structures, helping compare <RNA> lengths; matching migration alone does not establish an identical <nucleotide sequence> or identical <covalent bonds>. In the <ribozyme> experiments, labelled <RNA> was detected after separation by exposing photographic film.

= SDS-PAGE
{c}
{parent=Gel electrophoresis}
{wiki}

= Sodium dodecyl sulfate polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis
{synonym}

An analytical form of <gel electrophoresis> using an anionic <surfactant> and a <polymer> gel to separate <proteins> predominantly by chain size under denaturing conditions. <Western blot separation> often uses <SDS-PAGE>; the resulting apparent <mass> and subsequent <antibody> binding provide complementary, incomplete evidence about identity.

= ELISA
{c}
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay
{synonym}

= Physiology
{parent=Biology}
{wiki}

= Homeostasis
{parent=Physiology}
{wiki}

<Homeostasis> is the maintenance of usable internal conditions despite disturbances. It concerns selected <regulated physiological variables>, rather than keeping every part of an organism unchanged: <core body temperature> may remain near its usual range while <skin> <temperature>, <sweating> and <blood pressure> change. Regulation makes a relatively dependable environment for <biological cells> out of a changing external world. <Anatomy and Physiology 2e by J. Gordon Betts and others/1.5 Homeostasis>[§1.5, opening paragraphs and Negative Feedback: normal ranges and compensatory responses]{ref}

A useful causal description separates a <physiological sensor>, a <physiological integrator> and a <physiological effector>. The return from the <physiological effector>'s action to the sensed <regulated physiological variable> closes a <feedback control> loop. In <negative feedback>, that return opposes the disturbance. The <insulin> and <glucagon> articles already trace the <blood glucose> example; <synaptic integration> explains how several inputs can jointly influence a <neuron>. These are implementations and ingredients, not a requirement that every regulatory loop use one anatomical control center. <Anatomy and Physiology 2e by J. Gordon Betts and others/1.5 Homeostasis>[§1.5, Negative Feedback, Figure 1.10: sensor, control center and effector]{ref}

\Image[homeostasis-temperature-feedback-circuit.png]
{title=<Homeostasis>: sensing, integration and action, with a synthetic disturbance response}
{description=Original qualitative <thermoregulation> circuit above an explicitly synthetic plot from the <homeostatic temperature model>. The solid return represents <negative feedback> from <core body temperature>; the dashed <skin> input can anticipate a core change. The lower curves use invented <dimensionless variables> and arbitrary time units. They are neither experimental measurements nor fitted human responses.}

<Homeostasis> is useful precisely because internal activity can change: a nearly steady <temperature> can conceal a large increase in <heat transfer>. Conversely, observing a flat trace alone does not identify a regulatory mechanism. The environment might simply be constant, or the measuring instrument too insensitive; a perturbation and an independent response readout provide stronger evidence. The <skin-temperature perturbation experiment> below makes that distinction tangible.

The historical motivation was the puzzle of stability in changing living material. In his 1932 https://www.panarchy.org/cannon/homeostasis.1932.html[The Wisdom of the Body], <Walter Bradford Cannon> emphasized that an organism exchanges material with its surroundings and continually replaces its own structure, yet maintains relatively steady internal conditions. This authored excerpt presents <homeostasis> as active compensation for external and internal disturbances, rather than a static body. <The Wisdom of the Body by Walter Bradford Cannon/Homeostasis (excerpt from The Wisdom of the Body)>[Homeostasis excerpt, paragraphs beginning The wonder increases, Resistance to changes and In short; hosting page identifies book and 1932 date]{ref}

= Regulated physiological variable
{parent=Homeostasis}

A <regulated physiological variable> is a specified internal quantity whose departures elicit actions tending to restore an appropriate range. Examples include <core body temperature>, <blood glucose> <concentration>, <blood pressure>, and the <pH> and <osmolality> of <extracellular fluid>. Specify the compartment and readout: <skin> <temperature> and <core body temperature> are different quantities, and an <insulin> <concentration> is a regulatory message rather than the <blood glucose> it helps control.

= Blood pressure
{parent=Regulated physiological variable}
{wiki}

= Osmolality
{parent=Regulated physiological variable}

<Osmolality> counts dissolved particles per mass of <solvent>; it characterizes a <chemical solution>, rather than the total amount of <water> in an organism.

= Physiological sensor
{parent=Homeostasis}

A <physiological sensor> detects a relevant condition and changes a signal. In <thermoregulation>, temperature-sensitive <neurons> supply information about internal and <skin> <temperature>. Sensing a disturbance and sensing its internal consequence need not be the same operation.

= Physiological integrator
{parent=Homeostasis}

A <physiological integrator> combines regulatory inputs to select a response; it need not perform the mathematical <integral action> used in <control theory>. In <thermoregulation>, the <hypothalamus> participates in combining internal and <skin> signals. The term describes a role, which can be distributed across interacting <biological cells>, rather than a mandatory single comparator.

= Hypothalamus
{parent=Physiological integrator}
{wiki}

= Physiological effector
{parent=Homeostasis}

A <physiological effector> performs the action that changes the <regulated physiological variable>. In <thermoregulation>, <sweat glands>, changes in <skin> blood flow, and <skeletal muscle> <shivering> contribute different actions. The command, the action and the resulting <temperature> are three distinct readouts. <Anatomy and Physiology 2e by J. Gordon Betts and others/1.5 Homeostasis>[§1.5, Figure 1.10 and thermoregulatory examples following it]{ref}

= Effector saturation
{parent=Physiological effector}

<Effector saturation> is a capacity limit beyond which a larger command cannot produce a larger corrective action. In the <homeostatic temperature model>, take $u=\min(4x,1/2)$ during the sustained unit disturbance. Before reaching the cap, $x=(1-e^{-5t})/5$. The cap is reached at $t_c=\log(8/3)/5$, when $x=1/8$. Thereafter
$$
x(t)=\frac12-\frac38e^{-(t-t_c)},\qquad u(t)=\frac12.
$$
The lasting displacement is now $1/2$, compared with $1/5$ without the cap. The <negative feedback> command still asks for more cooling, but the action cannot supply it. This illustrates a capacity limit without predicting a human <temperature> limit. Increasing <feedback gain> cannot remove a binding cap; <integral action> cannot overcome inadequate physical capacity either.

= Physiological set point
{parent=Homeostasis}

A <physiological set point> is a reference around which regulation is organized, analogous to a <reference signal>. An observed average is not automatically a separately encoded target. A range of acceptable values and different response thresholds can be more informative than a single exact number. <Negative feedback> describes a causal sign; neither perfect constancy nor complete restoration follows from that sign alone.

For <core body temperature>, <circadian rhythms> and <fever> can change the defended level. Thus a response toward the current <physiological set point> need not restore yesterday's <temperature>. A drifting target and imperfect tracking of a fixed target are different explanations for a changing readout. <Fundamental Concepts of Human Thermoregulation and Adaptation to Heat: A Review in the Context of Global Warming by Chin Leong Lim>[§2.3.1, Figure 1 and following paragraphs: circadian and pyrogen-related changes of the temperature set point]{ref}

= Homeostatic temperature model
{parent=Homeostasis}
{title2=$\tau\dot x=d-x-u$}

Here is an explicitly synthetic <mathematical model> of a warming disturbance and its compensatory cooling. It is a teaching analogy for <thermoregulation>, not a measured human heat budget. Write $x=(T-T_r)/\Delta T$, where $T$ is a lumped internal <temperature>, $T_r$ is a fixed reference and $\Delta T>0$ is a chosen <temperature> scale. Let $d$ be an additional heating rate and $u$ an additional cooling rate, both scaled to the same reference rate. All three are <dimensionless variables>, while $\tau>0$ is a <time constant> in the chosen time units. The <ordinary differential equation> is
$$
\tau\dot x=d-x-u,\qquad u=kx,\qquad k\geq0.
$$
The term $-x$ idealizes increased passive <heat transfer> with increased internal <temperature>, around the baseline. The term $-u$ opposes warming through <proportional control> with <feedback gain> $k$. This local signed model does not treat a negative $u$ as negative <sweating>: it would represent decreased cooling relative to baseline. Changes of the environment and multiple body compartments are omitted.

For a constant disturbance $d=D$ and <initial condition> $x(0)=0$, the <exponential function> solution is
$$
x(t)=\frac{D}{1+k}\left(1-e^{-(1+k)t/\tau}\right),\qquad x_*=\frac{D}{1+k},\qquad u_*=\frac{kD}{1+k}.
$$
Substitution checks the <ordinary differential equation> directly. Increasing $k$ reduces the lasting offset and, in this instantaneous model, shortens the response <time constant> to $\tau/(1+k)$. It does not make $x_*$ zero for a nonzero disturbance and finite $k$: a persistent cooling command requires a persistent displacement under <proportional control>. At the <equilibrium point>, the extra heating is balanced by passive loss $x_*$ plus active cooling $u_*$. Zero change of <temperature> means balanced rates, rather than zero rates, consistent with <conservation of energy>.

The first diagram uses $D=1$, $\tau=4$, and a pulse from $t=2$ to $t=10$, comparing $k=0$ with $k=3$. The latter approaches $x_*=1/4$ during the pulse, compared with $1$ without active <feedback control>. After the pulse ends, each displacement decays as $x(10)e^{-(1+k)(t-10)/\tau}$. These are computed curves, not physiological measurements. The $k=0$ curve still has passive loss; it is not a claim that an unregulated body warms without limit.

Real <physiological effectors> take time to respond. To isolate that issue, set $\tau=1$ and replace the instantaneous command by another <ordinary differential equation>:
$$
\dot x=1-x-u,\qquad \dot u=4x-u,\qquad x(0)=u(0)=0.
$$
Here the disturbance remains $1$ from $t=0$ onward and the effector <time constant> is also $1$. The <equilibrium point> is still $(x_*,u_*)=(1/5,4/5)$, but the <characteristic polynomial> is $(\lambda+1)^2+4$. Its <eigenvalues> $-1\pm2i$ give decaying <oscillations> and <asymptotic stability>. The exact response is
$$
x(t)=\frac15+e^{-t}\left(-\frac15\cos(2t)+\frac25\sin(2t)\right),\qquad u(t)=\frac45+e^{-t}\left(-\frac45\cos(2t)-\frac25\sin(2t)\right).
$$
In particular, $\dot x=e^{-t}\cos(2t)$, so the first maximum occurs at $t=\pi/4$, with $x=1/5+(2/5)e^{-\pi/4}\simeq0.3824$. The response exceeds its eventual displacement while remaining above its baseline. The return has the same negative sign as before; the extra state changes the timing. A finite effector <time constant> is distinct from the fixed time lag in the <delayed negative feedback model>.

\Image[homeostasis-feedback-lag-capacity.png]
{title=Synthetic <homeostasis> responses: offset, lag and limited effector capacity}
{description=Original computed plots from the <homeostatic temperature model>, all in arbitrary units and explicitly labelled synthetic. Upper panel: displacement under the same sustained disturbance. Lower panel: corresponding cooling command. Instantaneous <proportional control>, a finite effector <time constant>, and <effector saturation> give different responses despite opposing the same disturbance. No experimental data or clinical limits are shown.}

= Thermoregulation
{parent=Homeostasis}
{wiki}

<Thermoregulation> regulates body <temperature>. <Skin> signals can anticipate a change of <core body temperature>, so a response need not wait for an internal error. <Cortical, thalamic, and hypothalamic responses to cooling and warming the skin in awake humans: A positron-emission tomography study by Gary F. Egan and others>[Introduction, paragraphs beginning The reflex regulation and In humans, the contribution from skin temperature: thermoregulatory effectors and feedforward skin input]{ref}

These responses have costs and environmental limits. <Shivering> consumes metabolic resources; cooling through <sweating> removes <water> that must eventually be replaced. High <humidity> restricts <evaporation>, so secreted sweat and successful cooling are not interchangeable. Maintaining <core body temperature> also depends on maintaining circulation: depleted blood volume can compromise <skin> blood flow and <heat transfer>. Seeking shade or reducing work changes the disturbance itself, whereas a larger regulatory command merely attempts to counter it. <Fundamental Concepts of Human Thermoregulation and Adaptation to Heat: A Review in the Context of Global Warming by Chin Leong Lim>[§2.2.4, Evaporation: sweat secretion versus evaporation and blood-volume depletion; §2.3.1: behavioral heat conservation and dissipation]{ref}

= Core body temperature
{parent=Thermoregulation}
{title2=$T_c$}

<Core body temperature> refers to internal <temperature>, distinguished from <skin> <temperature>. Different measurement sites are proxies with their own limitations, not interchangeable readings of a perfectly uniform interior. <Fundamental Concepts of Human Thermoregulation and Adaptation to Heat: A Review in the Context of Global Warming by Chin Leong Lim>[§2.1.1, Core Temperature: internal sites and indirect measurement]{ref}

= Skin-temperature perturbation experiment
{parent=Thermoregulation}

Twelve resting volunteers wore a water-perfused suit. Tubes covered the torso, upper arms and legs; the right forearm remained uncovered for laser Doppler blood-flow measurement. Six <thermocouples> estimated mean <skin> <temperature>; a sublingual <thermocouple> measured oral <temperature>. A dry-air capsule measured local sweat output from outgoing air moisture. <Cortical, thalamic, and hypothalamic responses to cooling and warming the skin in awake humans: A positron-emission tomography study by Gary F. Egan and others>[Methods, Temperature-Control Procedures: garment, uncovered forearm, blood-flow probe, ventilated capsule and thermocouples]{ref}

After two control scans near $34\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ <skin> <temperature>, the suit cooled and then warmed the <skin>. Mean oral <temperature> was $36.6\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ during both cooling and early warming; mean <skin> readings were $30.5$ and $38.2\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. Cutaneous vascular conductance fell during cooling and rose during warming. The inference is that compensatory blood-flow responses can precede an appreciable internal <temperature> change. <Cortical, thalamic, and hypothalamic responses to cooling and warming the skin in awake humans: A positron-emission tomography study by Gary F. Egan and others>[Methods, Experimental Protocol; Results, Body Temperature and Hemodynamic Responses; Table 1, SC1 and H1]{ref}

Repeated baselines controlled comparison within participants; closed mouths reduced oral-probe cooling artifacts. A later control with reduced oral <temperature> was excluded. Oral <temperature> is a proxy, local flow is not whole-body <heat transfer>, and the fixed condition order leaves time effects possible. Brain imaging measured regional blood flow, not direct <neuron> firing or causal necessity. <Cortical, thalamic, and hypothalamic responses to cooling and warming the skin in awake humans: A positron-emission tomography study by Gary F. Egan and others>[Methods, Temperature-Control Procedures, Experimental Protocol and Imaging Procedures; Results, exclusion of C3; Table 1]{ref}

